{"_type":"item","id":"019f817b0f0e7b9882aece4b37c893a9","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"PCB plugin: components need a polarity / orientation indicator (pin-1 / polarity mark) for correct placement","created_at":"2026-07-20T21:42:33","updated_at":"2026-07-20T22:34:24","priority":2,"tags":["component-model","orientation","pcb","placement","plugin","polarity"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019fa9624b5a7f9ca70c089d921f7675","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"No-worktree gate runs race with concurrent sibling agents on the same checkout","created_at":"2026-07-28T15:40:19","updated_at":"2026-07-28T15:40:19","tags":["concurrency","epoch-z","gate-integrity","gd-tests","pcb"],"value":"Unit Z1 (019fa83e8310) ran without a worktree per its brief. Mid-run, pcb/worker/tests/test_kicad_cli_boundary.py, test_rotation.py, tools/mutants/run_sweep.py were modified and a new tests/test_mutation_harness.py appeared (timestamps 08:34-08:36, during my pytest gate run) — not written by this unit. A concurrent sibling agent was editing pcb/worker/ in the SAME shared checkout at the same time. Consequence: the worker pytest gate result (1497 passed/13 failed/1 skipped) is NOT a clean-8ad726f measurement — it ran against a moving tree. Also separately observed: pcb/tests/gd/test_pcb_canvas_input_probe.gd reported \"0 total assertions\" in one full 30-suite run but passed cleanly standalone (33/33) immediately after — 1654+33=1687 matches the campaign's stated baseline total exactly, so this is a pre-existing flake (likely docket-DB/MCP-subprocess contention between sequential godot suite processes), not caused by any fix. Lesson: when a brief says \"no worktree\" for a unit whose gate touches shared mutable state (pcb/worker, pcb/tests/gd), treat any gate numbers gathered mid-wave as provisional and flag concurrent-edit evidence (mtimes) rather than trusting the raw pytest/gd summary.","component":"pcb-gd-tests","key":"shared-checkout-concurrency","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"019fc504c69e74dfb37b65574967b419","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Go tests that mutate committed text fixtures break on Windows CI (CRLF)","created_at":"2026-08-03T00:27:32","updated_at":"2026-08-03T00:27:32","tags":["ci","crlf","fail-closed","testing","windows"],"value":"GitHub windows-latest checks out text files as CRLF (git's Windows core.autocrlf default). Go os.ReadFile is byte-exact, so strings.Replace(fixture, \"...\n\", ...) matches NOTHING there and silently returns the UNMUTATED fixture — the test then asserts against the wrong input and reads a correct success as a regression (real case: pcb TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_ExportAssembly, run 30773825956). Python is immune: pathlib.read_text() normalizes newlines, and dict-level mutations never touch text. FIX BOTH WAYS: (1) .gitattributes `*.yaml text eol=lf` in the plugin dir; (2) a helper that normalizes \\r\n->\n AND returns an error when the pattern matches nothing — a no-op mutation must fail closed, never pass through the original (see pcb/main_test.go replaceOnceLF). Repro on Linux: rewrite the fixture with CRLF on disk and run the test.","component":"go-tests","key":"crlf-fixture-mutation","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"PCB assembly advisory sends the whole resolved board by value — exceeds the 64 KiB IPC cap on an ordinary 36-component board","description":"## What is actually wrong\n\nThe 64 KiB limit itself is CORRECT and is not the bug. `PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:157` sets `MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES := 65536`, and the plugin's Go side deliberately refuses at 60 KiB to stay clear of it (`internal/board/yaml.go:15-19`, citing gap register A-8). Both sides agree, the headroom is intentional, and it fails CLOSED (`status: \"indeterminate\"`, not a false pass) — which is the right failure mode and matches the plugin's own fail-safe policy.\n\n**The defect is that the payload exists at all.**\n\nThe board was loaded by `path` — it was read SERVER-SIDE and the bytes never needed to travel. The assembly advisory then serialises the whole RESOLVED board (116,260 bytes, roughly 6x the 20 KB source because pin geometry and resolved footprints are expanded) and pushes it back through a 64 KiB pipe. It is round-tripping data that is already on the other end.\n\n## Why this is severity 2 rather than a nitpick\n\nThe threshold is reachable by an ORDINARY board. 36 components on a 90x100mm two-layer board is small by any standard, and it is ~1.8x over the cap. That implies the assembly advisory is effectively unavailable for essentially any real board — it stops working precisely when boards become substantial enough to need checking.\n\n## Suggested fixes, cheapest first\n\n1. **Read the board server-side by handle** rather than passing it by value. This is the real fix — the panel already has the board.\n2. Return only the assembly FINDINGS rather than echoing the board back.\n3. Chunk the payload.\n\n(2) and (3) treat the symptom. Raising the 64 KiB cap is explicitly NOT proposed: it is a host transport constraint, it belongs to Minerva core rather than this plugin, and there is no evidence the cap is wrong — only that one consumer uses it wastefully.\n\n## Related\n\nDiscoverability of this failure in the GUI is filed separately — it is independently verifiable and independently fixable.","created_at":"2026-08-16T00:21:26","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:09","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","ipc","load_board","payload","pcb","scaling"],"resolution":"The panel now projects boards to their canonical wire form (panel_tools.canonical_wire_board) at all three worker-channel seams, dropping render enrichment the worker re-derives from the library chain: 122,370 -> 13,259 bytes on smart-remote-v2, 20% of the 64 KiB broker cap. Prerequisite fix: the board_health completeness census is resolve-first, so canonical wire input yields an identical ledger (bug 01a01b6bc649). The assembly advisory and board_health now run on real boards for the first time. Uncommitted on the minerva-plugins dev tree; running via a dev-lane install.","environment":"Minerva PCB plugin panel (pcb_panel). Board: smart-remote-v2, 36 components / 29 nets, 90x100mm 2-layer, at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml (511 lines, ~20 KB source). Observed 2026-08-15.","repro_steps":"1. minerva_pcb_load_board(editor_name=\"pcb_panel\", path=\"/home/imran/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml\")\n2. Read the reply's `assembly` field.\n\nACTUAL:\n  \"assembly\": {\"error\": \"Payload too large: 116260 bytes (limit: 65536 bytes)\",\n               \"status\": \"indeterminate\"}\n\nThe board loads fine (success:true, component_count:36, net_count:29) but the assembly advisory never runs."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a007f21ce37be183d0ff4757dfcc4f","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"PCB panel: a load-time check that returns indeterminate is invisible in the GUI — the human sees a clean load","description":"## The defect\n\nThe load reply is HONEST — it reports `status: \"indeterminate\"` with the reason, and the plugin's stated policy is that indeterminate is never a pass. The reporting is not the problem.\n\nThe problem is that this honesty exists ONLY on the MCP surface. On screen, a board whose assembly advisory silently failed to run is indistinguishable from one that passed.\n\nThis matters more than usual here because **the owner works from the GUI and does not call MCP tools**. A check that only announces its own unavailability through a JSON field is, for that user, a check that fails silently — which is the exact failure mode the fail-closed design exists to prevent. Fail-closed at the data layer plus invisible at the presentation layer nets out to fail-open for the human.\n\n## Why this is separate from the payload bug\n\nIndependent in both directions:\n\n- Fixing the payload size (bug 01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046) makes THIS instance go away but does nothing for the NEXT check that cannot run for some other reason — worker unavailable, timeout, resolution failure.\n- Fixing the surfacing does not fix the payload.\n\nEach has its own acceptance test, so each can pass or fail on its own. Filing them as one item would produce something that can only ever come back half-passed.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe fix should be general — driven by \"a load-time check reported indeterminate\", not special-cased to the assembly advisory — otherwise the same gap reopens for the next check.","created_at":"2026-08-16T00:21:42","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:04","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["discoverability","fail-closed","gui-parity","load_board","pcb","ui"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. pcb_load_checks.gd holds any explicit indeterminate load-time check as a status lead (general, not per-check).","environment":"Minerva PCB plugin panel (pcb_panel). Reproduced with board smart-remote-v2 (36 components) on 2026-08-15, where the assembly advisory returned indeterminate due to an oversized payload.","repro_steps":"1. Load any board large enough that a load-time check cannot complete (smart-remote-v2 at ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/ reproduces it via the assembly advisory).\n2. Observe the MCP reply: it honestly carries {\"assembly\": {\"status\": \"indeterminate\", \"error\": \"...\"}}.\n3. Now look at the PANEL.\n\nACTUAL: the panel shows a normally-loaded board. Nothing indicates that a check did not run. A GUI-only user has no way to learn the advisory was skipped."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a00e8f4a6c7c34bbdec458cf105374","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"cad CI red since June: the runtime bundle was written to internal/runtime/bundle while go:embed reads internal/cadruntime/bundle","description":"FOUND 2026-08-16 while watching CI after the install-lane push. Pre-existing: every cad workflow run since at least 2026-06-23 failed at the same step for the same reason.\n\nCAUSE. 1c8291b (refactor(shared): extract plugin-agnostic bridge + runtime from cad, 2026-06-10) renamed cad/internal/runtime → cad/internal/cadruntime and moved the go:embed directives with it, but left BUNDLE_OUT_DIR in cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock pointing at the old path. That lock line was the LAST reference to internal/runtime anywhere in cad/ — grep confirms nothing else mentioned it. The destination directory's own .gitignore had been sitting unused in internal/cadruntime/bundle/ since the day of the rename.\n\nWHY IT HID FOR TWO MONTHS. The failure is not in the bundle step, which succeeds — it writes the tarball, lists the directory, and exits 0. The break surfaces one step later in `go build`, phrased as a go:embed pattern miss, which reads like a source problem rather than a path-config problem. codetools survived the same class of bug because its package is still internal/runtime, so its identical lock line is still correct.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. No cad release artifact could be produced for two months; the published cad-v0.1.2 assets were stale that whole time. Anyone installing cad from the marketplace got the pre-June binary.\n\nWORTH KEEPING: a build step that \"succeeds\" while writing to a path no consumer reads is invisible to exit codes. The binary-size assertion in cad.yml exists to catch an EMPTY embed; nothing asserted that the bundle landed where the embed looks. A cheap guard would be to have the bundle step verify its output directory matches the embed directory rather than just listing it.","created_at":"2026-08-17T07:11:06","updated_at":"2026-08-17T07:11:06","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["build","cad","ci","go-embed","packaging"],"resolution":"Fixed in d1f6c9d. cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock now sets BUNDLE_OUT_DIR=\"internal/cadruntime/bundle\"; cad.yml's two diagnostic `ls -la` lines and one stale comment updated to match. Verified end to end before pushing, not by inspection: the bundle script wrote a 237M tarball + sha256 into internal/cadruntime/bundle/, `go build ./` then succeeded, and the binary came out 240MB — above the 100MB floor CI's binary-size assertion uses to catch an empty go:embed. `go test ./...` green. CI run 31970922537 green on all three platforms, first green cad run since June, and the cad-v0.1.2 release assets refreshed for the first time since then.","repro_steps":"Before the fix: any push touching cad/ → GitHub Actions \"cad\" workflow → job \"cad (linux-x86_64)\" → step \"Build (native)\" fails with: internal/cadruntime/embed_linux_amd64.go:10:12: pattern bundle/runtime-bundle-linux-x86_64.sha256: no matching files found. Identical on macos-universal and windows-x86_64. The preceding step \"Build runtime bundle (single arch)\" reports SUCCESS — it wrote to a directory nothing embeds. Locally: go build -C cad -o /tmp/x ./ reproduces it on a tree whose bundle has not been built into the new path."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01b4ae78472329a9e308a3d820690","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Panel to_board_dict is ~9x canonical size; worker channels re-resolve anyway","created_at":"2026-08-19T18:31:28","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:28","tags":["assembly","ipc","payload","pcb"],"value":"pcb_component.gd to_board_dict() appends a render-detail tail (pads, graphics, local_bounds, properties, color) making the panel's board dict ~9x the canonical YAML size (smart-remote-v2: 137KB vs 15KB). The pcb.assembly_check and pcb.board_health channels DON'T need it: both inject withLibraryChain and land in methods._assembly_tri_state, which is resolve-first — it re-attaches courtyards/pads from the library itself. Sending the enriched dict is what blows the 64 KiB broker cap (bug 01a007f1dd02). Wire projections to worker channels should send canonical core + pins only (+pads only for non-library custom parts).","component":"pcb","key":"panel-board-dict-enrichment","retrieval_count":3,"research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01b6bc6497b1e938712d2c1263e5a","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"board_health completeness census is not resolve-first — canonical-source callers get a false `complete: true`","description":"FOUND 2026-08-19 by the corpus-equivalence step of the 01a007f1dd02 verification ladder — the projection experiment tripped over it, but it is reachable TODAY with no projection involved.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY. The board_health ledger has two halves with different input contracts:\n  * assembly half: methods._assembly_tri_state is RESOLVE-FIRST by documented contract — it calls _resolve_mapped(board, tolerant=True) itself, so a raw canonical board is enriched before measurement.\n  * completeness half: _completeness_keys reads the given dict VERBATIM. Pin copper is located from resolve-attached pad geometry; a canonical (unresolved) component with no inline pins contributes no pins to the census at all.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. _board_health_method's own docstring says \"params: {board: <canonical board dict>} (anything _load accepts)\" — but feed it actual canonical source and the census under-reports in the FAIL-OPEN direction. Measured on tests/testdata/gerber_boards/quadlayer.yaml (components carry ONLY library footprint refs, no inline pins):\n\n  board_health_method({yaml: quadlayer}) TODAY -> {\"complete\": true}\n  same board, enriched first                   -> {\"complete\": false,\n                                                   \"partial\": [{\"net\":\"SIG\",\"pin_groups\":2}],\n                                                   \"indeterminate\": [{\"net\":\"GND\",\"reason\":\"zone_copper\"}]}\n\n\"complete: true\" on a board with a split SIG net is a false pass — the exact vocabulary the tri-state census exists to make impossible. The Go channel description advertises canonical input verbatim, so any MCP-era caller sending {board:<canonical>} or {yaml:...} hits this.\n\nWHY IT HAS HIDDEN. The one production caller (PCBPanel load path) happens to send the deserialize-ENRICHED dict, so the census has always seen pads in practice. The contract and the caller disagree; the caller's accident masks the method's gap.\n\nFIX (2 lines, mirrors resolve_best_effort:461): _board_health_method should _resolve_mapped(board, tolerant=True, layers=_layer_params(params)) and run _board_health over the resolution, falling back to the raw board only when resolution errors — exactly what _assembly_tri_state already does internally. This is also the enabling prerequisite for the 01a007f1dd02 payload fix (lean canonical wire): once the census resolves-first, the panel may send canonical-core boards to BOTH channels.\n\nVERIFIED: with resolve-first simulated, board_health + assembly verdicts are dict-identical between enriched-dict input and lean canonical input across all 7 corpus boards (smart-remote-v2, gerber spike, coworking-unrouted/routed, assembly_fixture, drilltest, quadlayer).","created_at":"2026-08-19T19:07:23","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:17","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["board-health","completeness","fail-open","parity","pcb","worker"],"resolution":"_board_health_method now resolves the board tolerantly (library chain) before the completeness census, exactly as the assembly half always did — canonical and enriched input yield the identical ledger, proven across the 7-board repo corpus plus a render-tail poison test. Fix + 13-check regression suite in worker/tests/test_board_health_resolve_first.py.","environment":"pcb worker, minerva-plugins main (2026-08-19). Found while running the verification ladder for payload bug 01a007f1dd02.","repro_steps":"cd ~/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.')\nfrom pcb_worker import methods\nr = methods._board_health_method({'yaml': open('tests/testdata/gerber_boards/quadlayer.yaml').read()})\nprint(r['result'].get('complete'))  # True — should be False (SIG in 2 pin_groups, GND zone_copper indeterminate)\n\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a01bbfd2827b0c94bfb79b9672955b","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Dev-lane install: app_userdata dir goes stale; data_directory is what runs","created_at":"2026-08-19T20:39:11","updated_at":"2026-08-19T22:04:30","tags":["dev-lane","gotcha","install","pcb","plugin"],"value":"After minerva_plugin_install on a DEV TREE manifest, app_userdata/plugins/<id>/ can keep the OLD files (stale binary + ui/) — do NOT read it to check whether a fix is live. The registry entry's data_directory points at the dev tree and that is what the host runs. Verify with /proc: `for p in $(pgrep -f <plugin>-plugin); do readlink -f /proc/$p/exe; done`. For pcb the dev binary (~11MB) has zero-byte runtime-bundle embeds so it falls through to worker/.venv (editable install) = live Python source; the release binary (~76MB) embeds its own frozen worker and can never pick up a source fix. ALSO: minerva_plugin_install may TIME OUT on the MCP call yet succeed — the dev lane runs the manifest setup stanza; check minerva_plugin_list instead of retrying.","component":"plugins","key":"dev-lane-install-truth","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0223ec9e271269fd664fcf90dd20b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb.route (and serialize/deserialize) payloads exceed the 64KiB broker cap — move board out of the capped pipe via board_path + sha256 digest","description":"Observed on smart-remote-v2 (37 components, U1S = 44 THT pins, 29 nets): minerva_pcb_workspace_propose and minerva_pcb_apply_route_hints both fail with \"Payload too large: 153578 bytes (limit: 65536 bytes)\". Routing ONE 2-pin hint fails identically to routing everything — two attempts differed by 84 bytes — because PCBPanel.route_board (PCBPanel.gd:5370-5421) inlines _board_for_route_request() = _data.to_board_dict(), i.e. the ENTIRE draft-composed board, as obstacle context. The request dies at PluginScenePanelBroker.handle_scene_request step 6 (PluginScenePanelBroker.gd:609-620, MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES := 65536 at line 157; PluginWebviewBroker.gd:42 has the same cap).\n\nTHREE VICTIMS, not one: the same cap kills pcb.serialize export (documented at PCBPanel.gd:4804-4807) and large inline pcb.deserialize loads. Cap is one-directional (panel→broker requests only; replies and push_to_panel are uncapped) and platform-reserved channels (host.fs.watch, host_owned_save.response) already bypass it by early return.\n\nCHOSEN FIX (#2 of 6 graded candidates, weighted 94/105 on owner rubric durability→DRY→reliability→well-factored→readability→cost; table in session record 2026-08-20): board-by-path + digest. Panel snapshots the draft-composed board dict to a session temp file, sends {board_path, board_digest(sha256), route_hints, selection} — O(hints) not O(board). Python worker's board_model.load_board (board_model.py:25-49) grows a third arm: params[\"board_path\"] → read file, verify sha256 against board_digest (refuse loudly on mismatch), parse. load_board is the single resolver for route/drc/serialize consumers so the arm fixes all three victims; Go HandleRouteChannel forwards verbatim — zero Go changes; broker untouched — zero cross-plugin impact. Draft-composed board is panel-memory-only, so it must be SNAPSHOTTED, not referenced from the original YAML.\n\nRejected runners-up: broker blob-handle channel (76), manifest per-channel budget (60), worker session cache + deltas (56, cache-coherence trap), raise the cap (52, moves the cliff and masks the regression test), chunk/compress (52, wrong layer). Explicitly NOT doing raise-the-cap as interim. Spatially windowing the board is a correctness hazard — router fails closed on a board it cannot fully compile.\n\nRisks to cover in tests: temp-file lifecycle/cleanup, digest mismatch refusal, path encoding between Godot and Python worker, all three victim channels.","created_at":"2026-08-21T02:55:35","updated_at":"2026-08-21T03:49:47","priority":2,"tags":["broker","payload-limit","pcb","regression","router"],"resolution":"Board-by-reference shipped for route (plain + draft), serialize (both directions), deserialize, and the promote gate; snapshot + consumption helpers in panel_tools; worker + Go verified-digest arms. Follow-ups filed: sibling channels (01a0227029da), corpus-policy dct prose (01a0225bb250)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0225bb25078d19b181d7fa4c11fb4","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"corpus-policy: committed plugins.dct comment quotes the banned private-board path construction verbatim","description":"Pre-existing at HEAD (predates work item 01a0223ec9e271269fd664fcf90dd20b; verified by grepping `git show HEAD:docs/plugins.dct` — exactly one match, in committed comment id 27 on item 01a007f1dd027c82ba9aebcb1e6da046). The comment says the policy \"greps for exactly the <private-checkout>/smart-remote path construction\" — quoting the construction is itself a match. Comment rows are history; fix options: (a) a docket-native comment redaction/edit verb, (b) teach _PATH_REFERENCE to ignore .dct prose the way MINERVA_ROOT's docket is exempted (the scope comment in test_corpus_policy.py already reasons this way for Docs/minerva.dct), or (c) accept .dct in the repo-level exemption list. Option (b) matches the test's own stated rationale.","created_at":"2026-08-21T03:27:09","updated_at":"2026-08-21T04:09:29","severity":3,"tags":["corpus-policy","docket","pre-existing"],"resolution":".dct exemption added to the path-literal corpus-policy test; suite 13/13."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0227029da7b8b988ab93528925356","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Sibling board-carrying channels still ride the capped pipe inline: mask_view, fab_preview, board_health, assembly_check, draft_check","description":"Census from the fix cold review (finding 4). board_health/assembly_check are partially mitigated by the canonical wire-form projection (01a007f1dd02), but mask_view/fab_preview send the full board on exactly the class of board that blew pcb.route — WYSIWYG previews on smart-remote-v2-class boards will still die payload_too_large. Each is a one-line wiring through PCBPanel._payload_by_ref (their worker methods already resolve via the _load wrapper → board_model.load_board, which has the board_path arm); draft_check needs a look at whether {board, candidates} together clear the cap after the board goes by-ref. Add each wired channel to pcb/tests/gd/test_board_by_path.gd Section 1 and re-pin EXPECTED_SUITES.","created_at":"2026-08-21T03:49:31","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:14","priority":3,"tags":["broker","follow-up","payload-limit","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed (draft_check was already by-ref)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a022ab356c77e2a838e81641533334","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"\"Proposal\" means ghost trace only: direct span-propose without a minted intent, task-scoped router bridge, vocabulary pass","description":"MOTIVATING SESSION EVIDENCE (smart-remote-v2 VBAT first leg, 2026-08-21): (1) owner asked \"don't make a proposal — ghost trace only\"; the only path was deleting the intent annotation after propose, which orphaned the surviving candidate — workspace_reroute_route then refused source_hints_missing because the panel router bridge can only scope a run by hint selection. (2) A placement nudge to straighten the route forced a full reject/delete/re-intent/re-propose cycle instead of a reroute. (3) Rejected-task residue: tasks whose hints are deleted linger open and unactionable.\n\nDESIGN SKETCH (three legs):\n(a) TASK-SCOPED ROUTER BRIDGE: scope router runs by task terminal sets (pin refs), not hint ids. The eager RouteTask already carries net + terminals and is the natural scoping object; hint ids become one way to select tasks, not the bridge's input contract. This also fixes orphaned-candidate reroutes and lets stale hint-less tasks be actionable or reapable.\n(b) DIRECT SPAN-PROPOSE: workspace_propose (or sibling arg/verb) accepts spans:[{source_pin, dest_pin, width_mm?}] — mints TASKS only, no annotation, lands ghost candidates. Intents (pcb_route_hint) remain the durable/citeable channel for corridor steering, width authority, and cross-session persistence — they become optional, not a toll booth.\n(c) VOCABULARY PASS: \"proposal\" = ghost candidate everywhere (status bar \"N proposals\", docs, tool descriptions); the dashed-pink annotation is labeled/rendered \"intent\" and never called a proposal. Canvas legend distinguishes intent (ask), ghost (proposed answer), copper (committed).\n\nCONTEXT: C4b already retired proposal ANNOTATIONS (propose lands candidates, writes nothing) — the object model is 80% there; the gaps are the mandatory intent mint, the hint-only bridge scoping, and mixed labels. Cold-review finding from work item 01a0223ec9e2 noted the bridge limitation independently.\n\nSTATUS: proposed; awaiting option scoring (owner rubric: durability → DRY → reliability → well-factored → readability → cost) with independent fable review before designing.","created_at":"2026-08-21T04:54:00","updated_at":"2026-08-21T05:53:30","tags":["pcb","proposal-vocabulary","routing","ux","workspace"],"resolution":"B+C+D shipped: spans one-call span-propose (union, atomic, minted_hint_ids), hint-less reroute fallback (same-task supersession, endpoints carried, honest degrade + steering refusal), vocabulary (Intents header, docs, MCP manifest). Open: owner veto window on the UNION ruling; worker-side warning wording follow-up (comment 31)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a022b1b7d571d594e8f6ddfb3c8bce","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Routing sidecar zero-payload rule loses task-owned corridor constraints for unanswered intents across save/reload","description":"Found by the independent design review of DCR 01a022ab356c (P5). Live data loss today, independent of the DCR's fate: author intent with corridor → save → reload → corridor gone, next propose runs unguided with no warning. Fix direction: the zero-payload rule must count durable tasks (or at least tasks carrying a routing_constraint) as payload. Blast radius: test_workspace_persistence.gd pins the sidecar round-trip — grow it with an unanswered-constrained-intent case proven red first. This bug also gates any future task-native ontology work (the deferred legs of DCR 01a022ab356c): tasks minted without annotations cannot survive a session until this is fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-21T05:01:07","updated_at":"2026-08-21T05:01:07","severity":2,"tags":["data-loss","pcb","persistence","routing-workspace","sidecar"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a026ddd3ee72a9b45ec33ec9cf23d9","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"EPOCH SR2FAB — clear the plugin-side defects between smart-remote-v2 and a gated promote","description":"PURPOSE: clear every defect standing between the live smart-remote-v2 board and a gated promote -> gerbers/BOM/CPL, and only what is cheap to carry alongside that chain.\n\nAuthored by Fable from source (v0.4.2, main @ f74db1b). Opus implements.\n\nREVISION NOTE: supersedes the first draft (14 stations, \"SR2-FAB\"). Rewritten after two owner corrections — Godot syntax checking is exempt from testex, and the board's data is safe.\n\n== ENVIRONMENT FACTS (owner, 2026-08-21) ==\n- LINUX DESKTOP, not the Mac laptop.\n- The drive plugin syncs Minerva projects to the drive service. BOARD DATA CANNOT BE DESTROYED. Fable's original framing of S1 as an urgent \"board rescue\" is WRONG — S1 stays first because it closes a real capability gap and needs no godot invocation, not because the board is at risk.\n- Godot's editor is closed; Minerva is shut down.\n- Canonical YAML on disk is unchanged since 80c829c (traces: [], keepouts only). Tonight's copper — Q1 rotation+freeform position, 11 traces, 4 vias, the GND pour — lives in the Minerva project (drive-synced). Two untracked sidecars exist: .annotations.json (6.7KB) and .routing.json (58KB, workspace candidates/tasks, board_revision 51). The sidecars hold the WORKSPACE, not committed copper.\n\n== EXECUTION MODEL (fixed) ==\nOpus implements S0-S12 -> Fable single end-of-epoch review -> <=3 fix rounds -> Codex review via docket question (this project) -> <=3 fix rounds -> testex (FIRST execution of any test, pytest included).\n\n**SERIAL EXECUTION ONLY (owner ruling).** No station runs in parallel with another. No parallel subagents implementing. One station at a time, in order.\n\nRULES:\n- Tests AUTHORED but NOT EXECUTED until testex. Owner ruling; inverts the standing \"tests must RUN when written\" rule deliberately.\n- Godot SYNTAX checking exempt and continuous (S0 governs how).\n- Python has NO execution exemption. OPEN QUESTION FOR OWNER: extend the allowance to `python -m py_compile` only (pure parser, no imports, no pytest) — the exact analogue of Godot --check-only. Would convert S2/S3/S11's \"syntax unchecked\" rows to ~0.\n- COMMENTS: new comments minimal, functional only. No status, owner-request, or docket/DCR references in NEW comments. Forward-only — existing decision-trail comments untouched, including hunks that merely brush them. No stripping sweep.\n- DELIVERY: merge to main only. No version bump, no marketplace cut. (Whenever a release happens: main.go serverVersion and manifest.json version move in lockstep — that mismatch forced the 0.4.2 re-cut at a007ffb.)\n- OPERATIONAL: Python worker changes deploy via backend restart (the worker is the Go plugin's subprocess; _start_and_verify_backend, ui/PCBPanel.gd:4803-4808). The GD panel and in-memory board are untouched by a worker restart. Panel GDScript hot-reloads from the dev-lane data_directory.\n\n== STATIONS (serial) ==\n\nS0 — Pinned-safe syntax-check invocation. Prerequisite. S.\nEstablish ONE verified-safe per-file GDScript parse check (candidate: godot --headless --check-only --script <file> against a DISPOSABLE scratch project, never --path at any Minerva project). Record the incantation as the epoch's standing rule with explicit prohibitions: no --path <minerva project>, no --import, gd-runner only with --preflight-only (a FLAG, not an env var). No station runs any godot invocation before S0 closes.\nTests: none (operational prerequisite).\n\nS1 — minerva_pcb_export_yaml. Closes M1's practical harm, F3. S. Deps: none.\nGD-only verb reusing the export button's plumbing verbatim — _on_export_yaml_pressed (ui/PCBPanel.gd:4823-4860), _payload_by_ref (ui/PCBPanel.gd:4817) — as minerva_pcb_export_yaml {path?}: dispatch entry (ui/panel_tools.gd:100-292), manifest.json tools entry, reply labelled draft:true. Bump PLUGIN_BUILD (ui/PCBPanel.gd:77) in the same edit — stale since it landed, by its own design-note admission (ui/PCBPanel.gd:57-76).\nWHY FIRST (corrected): not a rescue — the drive plugin protects the data. First because it closes the only gap that makes a gate-blocked board unwritable to canonical YAML, needs no godot invocation (panel hot-reloads), and gets the board's copper into ccsandbox git where the rest of the epoch can reference it.\nTests: gd (headless, fake IPC) for dispatch + refusal shapes, mirroring tests/gd/test_board_by_path.gd's by-ref assertions.\nRisk: syntax ~0; behaviour unverified until testex — reviewer reads the by-ref wrap and _unwrap_channel_reply layering (ui/panel_tools.gd:5259-5279) for envelope parity with the button path.\n\nS2 — E1: model zone_connect. Closes E1, missed-9. THE BLOCKER. M. Deps: none.\nCapture the (zone_connect n) VALUE in the parser (footprints.py:342, footprint_def.py:_raw_marker at :324-326). Rewrite DefaultCapabilityPolicy.is_blocking (compile_board.py:278-291): values 0/1/2 non-blocking with per-value warnings — v1 fill is SOLID-connect only (zone_fill.py:35-36, 838-845), so 0/2 are modeled exactly and 1 is electrically-connected-but-solid, advisory. Value 3 or unparsable blocks ONLY WHEN RELEVANT: replace board-wide _declares_copper_pour (compile_board.py:301-320) with pad-scoped _pour_could_touch — non-keepout zone, same net, on a copper layer the pad occupies.\nOn this board U2's EP is TOP and the pour is BOTTOM: under the gate the marker is not even relevant. That is the epoch's unblocking event.\nTests: 4-quadrant pytest matrix (0/1/2/3 x relevant/irrelevant pour) + a fixture encoding this board's shape (thermal-pad QFN top, GND pour bottom -> compiles; value-3 same-net same-layer -> blocks). Homes: worker/tests/test_compile_board.py, test_footprint_def.py.\nRISK: HIGHEST-DANGER STATION. Python, syntax AND behaviour inspection-only. An error here silently fail-OPENS the one genuinely corrupting case (value 3). MANDATED SUBSTITUTE: a written truth table (value x pour-relevance -> verdict) mapped line-by-line in the review request, plus a post-testex operational replay (compile + board_check) before any promote is attempted.\n\nS3 — D3: pad layers in the connectivity projection. Closes D3. S/M. Deps: none in code; reviewed alongside S2.\nconnectivity_board emits SMD pads' copper layers (ir_connectivity.py:92-101 currently emits bare pins); carry `layers` through the pad-source fallback (pad_source.py:748-752, 789). drc._Pad.occupies already consumes them (drc.py:208-218); permissive-when-undeclared stays for TH pads by physical correctness.\nVOCABULARY TRAP: _harvest_pads folds KiCad spellings (\"F.Cu\", via kicad_to_canon at drc.py:299-300), so the projection must emit KiCad names, NOT the canonical \"top\" the rest of ir_connectivity speaks (ir_connectivity.py:123).\nTests: pytest in test_drc.py — F.Cu-only SMD pad + bottom segment at its center -> 2 pin groups; + through via -> 1; projection emits layers for SMD, none for TH.\nRISK: HIGH. Flips census verdicts BOTH ways; a false-partial would block legitimate promotes. Review must reason about the TH-permissive branch and the *.Cu spanning case (drc.py:295-303). Expect NEW TRUE FINDINGS on this board post-fix; that counts as success.\n\nS4 — Optional-tier DRC floors become real. Closes missed-2. S. Deps: none.\nJSON-only edit to library/profiles/v1-fab-conservative.json (currently ten floor keys): add min_silk_width_mm, min_silk_to_pad_mm, min_hole_to_copper_mm WITH PUBLISHED JLC FIGURES CITED IN THE PROFILE'S `source` STRING.\nCORRECTION vs the first draft: min_hole_to_edge_mm STAYS ABSENT. No house publishes one, and the K21 doctrine (design minima only from a sanctioned, cited origin — compile_board.py:233-247) forbids invented numbers. drc_geometric.py:2032-2039 documents this deliberately.\nTurns gc9/gc10 zeros from \"the profile said nothing\" into real verdicts (drc_geometric.py:1110-1114).\nTests: pytest asserting the loaded profile carries the three floors and that gc9/gc10 produce findings on a crafted violating fixture.\nRisk: LOW. JSON provable by reading; the load path (manufacturer_profile.load_rule_profile, eager at import — compile_board.py:250-259) is Python and unexecuted, so a typo'd key fails module import at testex, loudly.\n\nS5 — B2 minimal: never overwrite a declared segment width. Closes B2. B1 deferred. S. Deps: none.\nmethods.py:2323 seg[\"width_mm\"] = width -> seg.setdefault(\"width_mm\", width). The overlay's precedence (ir_candidates.py:368-370) already prefers the segment's value. Add subject_widths_mm to gc2 finding rows (drc_geometric.py:1736-1790) so any future phantom is self-diagnosing.\nTests: pytest — a candidate segment declaring 0.5 under a 1.0 run width yields the same gc2 verdict as a standalone 0.5 check. Kills the phantom-violation AND the symmetric FALSE-CLEAN direction.\nRisk: LOW — one line, arguable from the ir_candidates precedence chain on the page.\n\nS6 — A4: draft-check un-broken. Closes A4, heals B3. S/M. Deps: S1.\nGD half: check_draft wraps its payload by-ref before emit (PCBPanel.gd:5711 inline board, :5741 emit; copy the route_board precedent at :5465) and returns the broker failure dict instead of collapsing to {}, so _workspace_check (panel_tools.gd:7986, error at :8029) can name draft_check_refused / draft_check_timeout / draft_overlay_drifted instead of one conflated draft_check_no_reply.\nPython half: _draft_check (methods.py:3087-3088) gains the ten-line board_path+digest inflation copied from _promote_check (methods.py:859-869, via board_model.load_board, board_model.py:45-71), fail-closed on BoardParseError.\nWHY IT SURVIVES THE CUT: mhroute owns final routing, but the human+agent finishing pass (pour edge, keepouts, GND stitching) happens in-editor, and committing unverifiable ghosts is how boards get wrecked.\nTests: gd by-ref assertion for pcb.draft_check (the exact hole in test_board_by_path.gd's coverage); pytest draft_check with board_path; gd test for the de-conflated error names against tests/gd/test_workspace_check.gd's fake-panel scaffold.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour (live broker hop) unverifiable until testex. Reviewer traces the envelope through all three unwrap layers and checks by-ref key symmetry (board -> board_path/board_digest) against both consumers. Python half LOW (verbatim pattern copy).\n\nS7 — D2: degenerate copper guards. Closes D2. S. Deps: S1.\nGD: _commit_preflight (pcb_routing_workspace.gd:2129-2151) drops consecutive duplicate points within COPPER_COINCIDENT_EPS_MM and refuses segments collapsing under 2 distinct points (ERR_UNMODELABLE_SEGMENT, named); same filter at candidate ingest (pattern exists: _bus_drop_duplicates, panel_tools.gd:8168-8180).\nPython: compile_board.py:1866 degenerate-trace message gains the trace ID beside the ordinal, so a stranded board is repairable via delete_traces.\nTests: gd preflight test with a start==end segment — a mutation that survives today's entire suite; pytest for the id-bearing message.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0, behaviour low (pure model-path validation, no broker); Python trivial.\n\nS8 — M4: mounting-hole read verb + placement advisory. Closes M4. S. Deps: S10 for the snap helper (or land the helper here if S10 trails).\nGD: minerva_pcb_list_holes reading pcb_data.gd:108 mounting_holes [{position, diameter, plated}] (serialize refs :2608/:2918), snapped; dispatch + manifest.json entry; reply includes a placement advisory naming coincident or >=3-collinear holes — the exact shape of the prior silent-rewrite incident, which NO gc check catches (gc11 proximity never runs, drc_geometric.py:2032-2039; gc10 undeclared pre-S4; gc6 only on near-collision).\nTests: gd — verb reply shape; advisory fires on a 4-holes-same-y fixture, silent on the rectangle pattern.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour low (pure read).\n\nS9 — M3: declared-intent honesty surfacing. Closes M3's residual foot-gun. S. Deps: none.\nPython: in _promote_check's advisory block (methods.py:923-938) add a stage-incongruence advisory (\"stage defers routing but N traces exist\").\nGD: board_check/promote replies headline complete_by_declaration instead of a bare complete:true; get_layout_state (panel_tools.gd:5101) gains fabrication_stage.\nNO GATE CHANGE — the granular-promotion and declared-intent rulings stand.\nTests: pytest for the advisory tri-state; gd for the layout-state key.\nRisk: Python LOW (additive advisory), GD ~0/low.\n\nS10 — F4: millimetre quantization at the API boundary. Closes F4. S. Deps: before S11.\nGD: one _mm(v) = snapped(v, 0.0001) helper at every mm-emitting reply (_get_pin_position panel_tools.gd:430-438 is the worst offender; get_components, describe_component, get_selection, candidate geometry, spatial_query echoes — _list_vias :3757-3758 is the existing correct pattern) AND at authored-input ingest (add_trace/add_via), so what mhroute reads and writes back land on one grid.\nWHY CORE: the manufacture route is external Manhattan routing consuming pin positions over MCP; float32 residue (75.4000015258789) is DRC noise and S11 false-positives waiting to happen.\nTests: gd — pad at x=75.4 serializes as 75.4; ingest snap round-trip.\nRisk: GD syntax ~0; behaviour low but WIDE — reviewer's job is completeness of the sweep; a missed emitter re-opens the class silently.\n\nS11 — D1: gc12 trace-direction rule. Closes D1. M. Deps: S10 (tolerance must sit above the snap grid), S4 (same profile-extension pattern).\nPython: new gc12_trace_direction in drc_geometric.py (registry :2281-2295, dispatch :2423-2434): per segment, angle to nearest allowed axis <= tolerance; findings carry measured_angle_deg + endpoint.\nCORRECTION vs the first draft: profile-gated and ABSENT-BY-DEFAULT via a NEW profile (v1-fab-manhattan) or board-level design_rules opt-in. The shared conservative profile must NOT silently impose Manhattan on every board (selection path: compile_board.py:240-247). smart-remote-v2's YAML opts in — a cross-repo operational step in ccsandbox, not a plugin change.\nTests: angle unit vectors — exact-H, exact-V, 45deg, the real 0.9deg-off-vertical case, and a conforming segment with sub-grid jitter.\nRisk: Python, inspection-only, MEDIUM — new check class; fail direction must be \"spurious finding\", never \"missed diagonal\" (the module's cardinal rule, drc_geometric.py:11-25).\n\nS12 — F1: static-warning aggregation. Closes F1. S. Deps: none; last because everything else changes what the warnings ARE.\nPython: at the _promote_check/board_check reply boundary, group the repeated compile diagnostics (feature_omitted per marker, captured_geometry_not_emitted per component — attach points methods.py:213-220, 299-307) into {code, count, refs[:N]} rows + a static_warnings_digest; full flat list behind verbose_warnings:true. Sort before hash.\nWHY CORE: the DRC-clean push runs board_check dozens of times; this is the single largest context cost of coworking on the manufacture loop.\nTests: pytest — grouped shape, digest stability across identical calls, verbose passthrough.\nRisk: Python LOW-MED — the panel status line consumes these replies; reviewer checks both consumers by reading.\n\n== ORDERING RATIONALE ==\n1. S0 -> S1 first. S1 is GD-only and hot-reloads, needing no godot invocation.\n2. S2 (E1) is the keystone, immediately after: every compile-consuming capability is dead until it lands. It is also the most dangerous inspection-only change, so it gets maximum soak time before review.\n3. S3 with S2: together they make the promote gate's two halves honest; reviewing them as a pair keeps the fail-closed story coherent.\n4. Cheap provable-by-inspection work next (S4, S5) per the testex rule — near-zero review cost, and they de-risk everything measured later.\n5. S6-S9 are the GD-heavy middle, moved earlier than pre-addendum: syntax risk is now ~0, leaving only behaviour reasoning.\n6. S10 before S11 (non-obvious): an angle rule authored before reply/ingest quantization would need its tolerance chosen against float32 noise; snapping first lets the tolerance be a fab-meaningful number.\n7. S12 last: it summarizes warnings whose content S2/S4 change.\n\n== GATES ==\nLeave any station: code + authored tests in the diff; per-station inspection note written; GD files pass the S0-pinned parse check; NO execution of any authored test.\nLeave S2 specifically: the truth table (value x relevance -> verdict) written and mapped to lines; value-3-relevant provably still blocks by reading.\n\nEPOCH EXIT (after testex):\n1. All authored suites green (pytest + gd) within the <=3 + <=3 fix-round budget.\n2. Operational replay on the board: YAML on disk and adopted; compiles with the pour present; board_check geometric verdict DETERMINATE with zero unsupported_feature; gc9/gc10 running against cited floors; connectivity census honest per S3 (new true findings count as success).\n3. Fab-preview raster inspected via the existing live path (set_view show_fab_preview + get_image — ui/PCBPanel.gd:454, 4175-4185); gerbers/drills/BOM/CPL emit without identity refusals.\n4. EXPLICITLY NOT an exit criterion: the 23 unrouted nets. The epoch delivers a VERIFIABLE PATH TO PROMOTE; routing completion is the mhroute campaign's job, with GND required trace+via-complete for the census (the CPN1 narrowing, drc.py:797-805) until the pour-modeling epoch lands.\n\n== CUT LIST ==\nB1 full per-hint width provenance -> Workspace-ergonomics. mhroute owns final routing; S5 removes the correctness hazard.\nC1/C2/C3 reroute blast radius, constraint survival, task orphaning -> Workspace-ergonomics. Off this board's manufacture path.\nA1/A2/A3 proposed-via / selection geometry / spatial visibility -> Workspace-ergonomics. Coworking polish, not fab-gating.\nF2 live-editor drc_geometric/drc -> Workspace-ergonomics. board_check (+S12) covers the live board.\nE2 rotate direction swap -> Workspace-ergonomics. Needs a visual confirmation that mutates the live board; not fab-gating.\nM2 fill-aware pour connectivity -> Pour-modeling. Real engineering on the fill/census seam; the interim rule (GND trace-complete) suffices for promote.\nMissed-1 floating-pour detection -> Pour-modeling. Falls out of M2's fill-aware census.\nM9 BOM order_qty -> Assembly epoch if the workaround chafes. Zero-code workaround exists NOW: model U1S as two grouped components (_bom_rows qty = ref count, assembly_outputs.py:331; groups are \"one physical part\"). Operational modeling task on the board, not plugin code.\nM6 vector-level fab preview over MCP -> Assembly/preview epoch. Raster path suffices.\nMissed-3/-7/-8 (thermal-refusal UX, keepout asymmetry surfacing, fiducials) -> Pour-modeling / assembly. Advisory-grade; the fiducial decision is the owner's board-level call.\n\n== FABLE'S END-OF-EPOCH REVIEW CHECKLIST (step 2) ==\n1. Register closure map — each claimed item traced to its diff hunk; nothing closed by assertion.\n2. Seam symmetry — by-ref pairs complete on both sides (S1, S6: GD wrap <-> worker inflation, key names byte-equal); profile keys (S4, S11) byte-equal between JSON and their Python readers.\n3. Fail-closed audits — S2: quadrant table vs code, value-3-relevant blocks, unparsable blocks; S3: TH and *.Cu pads stay permissive; S11: fail direction is spurious-finding.\n4. Test-authorship audit BY READING (nothing ran): for every authored test, would it fail against pre-fix code? Any test passing both sides is returned as a defect. Fixture reachability reasoned per the mutate-to-find-fixture-gaps rule.\n5. COMMENT SALIENCE (named dimension): new comments minimal, functionality ONLY, absent where the code speaks; ZERO status/owner-request/docket/DCR references in new comments; FORWARD-ONLY COMPLIANCE BOTH DIRECTIONS — no stripping or rewording of existing docket/DCR/ruling comments anywhere in edited files, including hunks that merely brush them. Violations in either direction are review findings.\n6. Diff hygiene — no version bump, no manifest.json version change, no drive-by reformatting; dispatch/manifest/tool-schema entries consistent for every new verb.\n7. Behaviour-reasoning notes present — each GD station's \"what could only be verified by running\" note exists and is specific (envelope layer, dispatch route, hot-reload assumption).\n\n== CANNOT-VERIFY-UNTIL-TESTEX REGISTER ==\nS1, S6-gd, S7-gd, S8, S9-gd, S10 — syntax ~0 (S0-pinned checker); behaviour: broker envelope unwrapping, dispatch wiring, reply shapes, hot-reload assumptions; S10's sweep completeness.\nS2 — syntax UNCHECKED (Python); behaviour: the whole adjudication truth table. HIGHEST DANGER; inspection substitute mandated.\nS3 — unchecked; census verdict flips in BOTH directions.\nS5, S6-py, S9-py — unchecked; LOW (pattern copies and one-liners).\nS4 — JSON readable; profile import path fails loudly at testex import.\nS11 — unchecked; MEDIUM (new check class geometry math).\nS12 — unchecked; reply-shape change with two consumers.\n\n== CODEX REVIEW REQUEST (step 4) ==\nFile as a docket QUESTION against this project (plugins.dct — the repo's only .dct, established home of pcb DCRs), self-contained: registers summarized, station table, the S2 truth table inline, commit SHA pinned. Owner shuttles the verdict.","created_at":"2026-08-22T00:27:47","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:09","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["drc","epoch","fabrication","pcb","smart-remote-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a026ec0d807d1c81dea0011ae28b61","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Safe per-file GDScript syntax check that never opens a Minerva project","created_at":"2026-08-22T00:43:19","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:07","tags":["gdscript","godot","pcb","syntax","testex","tooling"],"value":"pcb/scripts/gd-syntax-check.sh <file.gd>... | --all   (exit 0 clean, 1 parse error, 2 harness). ~0.35s/file; whole tree (78 files) 27s.\n\nIt runs `godot --headless --path <disposable scratch> --check-only --script res://../../minerva-plugins/<rel>`, so it NEVER touches a Minerva checkout — unlike scripts/run-gd-tests.sh, which uses `--path <minerva>/src` and kills a running Minerva.\n\nLoad-bearing fact (measured, Godot 4.6.2): parsing precedes resolution and ABORTS before it. A file with both a syntax error and an unresolvable base class prints ONLY the syntax error. So a file whose diagnostics are all resolver-family is proven free of parse errors — no host stubs needed, and stubs would invent false errors of their own.\n\nResolver-family noise in the scratch project (all seven observed across the tree): Could not find base class / Could not find type / Could not resolve script / Cannot infer the type of X (variable|constant) / Identifier X not declared / Failed to compile depended scripts / Preload file X does not exist. All are caused by Minerva class_name types (MinervaPluginPanel, AnnotationKind) and res://test/helpers preloads that only exist in Minerva's project.\n\nOne is re-promoted to a real defect: a missing preload whose path points back into minerva-plugins. That catches typo'd in-repo preload paths, the usual way new dispatch wiring fails.\n\nDoes NOT prove the file resolves, loads or behaves. That needs the real host.\n\nPython analogue: python3 -m py_compile <files> — worker tree compiles in 4.5s; __pycache__ is gitignored.","component":"pcb","key":"tooling.gd-syntax-check","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":3,"research_cost":8}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0274b0e9a7f188057e28306d73027","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"CODEX REVIEW — EPOCH SR2FAB @ 0aa3d96: 13 stations, nothing executed","description":"REPO github.com/imrans-lab/minerva-plugins · BRANCH epoch/sr2fab · PINNED SHA 0aa3d96f4bf776d6c269e995d02c36bf48678c5c · DIFF `git diff main...0aa3d96` (17 commits).\n\nTHE ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE READING ANYTHING ELSE: **nothing in this branch has been executed.** By owner ruling the epoch authored tests and never ran them. The only gates that ran are `python -m py_compile`, a per-file `godot --check-only` (pcb/scripts/gd-syntax-check.sh, added by this branch), and `go build ./...`. No pytest, no gd suite, no live board. So every behavioural claim below is reasoned from reading, and the review that matters most is the one that asks \"would this actually do that\".\n\nThat is not hypothetical. Fable's review found `math.isfinite` used in compile_board.py with no `import math` — a NameError that py_compile passes happily and that would have made the whole of station S11 inert. Assume there are more of that class.\n\n== WHAT THE EPOCH IS FOR ==\nsmart-remote-v2 is a real board being pushed to fabrication. Every compile-consuming capability on it was dead: one `(zone_connect 2)` marker on a VQFN exposed pad refused the entire board whenever it declared a copper pour, taking geometric DRC, the routing IR and promotion with it. The epoch clears that and the defects standing beside it.\n\n== STATIONS ==\nS0  build   safe per-file GDScript syntax check that never opens a Minerva project\nS1  feat    minerva_pcb_export_yaml — live board as YAML text, ungated, writes nothing\nS2  fix     zone_connect judged by VALUE then by REACH  <- the unblocking event\nS3  fix     connectivity projection emits pad copper layers\nS4  fix     a zero count now says whether the rule was MEASURED (planned body CANCELLED)\nS5  fix     a segment keeps a width it declared for itself\nS6  fix     draft check goes by-ref and names its own faults\nS7  fix     a segment whose two ends are one point is not copper\nS8  feat    minerva_pcb_list_mounting_holes + placement advisory\nS9  fix     say which kind of \"complete\" a board reached\nS10 fix     millimetre values leave the surface on one grid\nS11 feat    GC12 trace direction, board-gated, absent by default\nS12 perf    collapse repeated compile warnings, with a stable digest\n+3 fix rounds against Fable's review (f9771e3, d0e336c, 0aa3d96).\n\n== S2's ADJUDICATION TABLE, THE HIGHEST-DANGER CHANGE ==\nRelevance := some non-keepout zone, on the pad's net, on a copper layer the pad occupies.\n\n  value        | relevance              | pad kind | verdict | why\n  -------------+------------------------+----------+---------+------------------------------\n  2            | any                    | any      | ALLOW   | v1's native solid connect\n  3            | relevant               | surface  | ALLOW   | 3 == 2 on an SMD/connect pad\n  3            | relevant               | thru     | BLOCK   | 3 == 1 on a through-hole pad\n  1            | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | spokes; solid fill = cold joint\n  0            | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | isolation inverted by solid fill\n  unreadable   | relevant               | any      | BLOCK   | uninterpretable instruction\n  0/1/3/unread | irrelevant             | any      | ALLOW   | nothing for the style to act on\n  any non-2    | context unreadable     | any      | BLOCK   | <-- MUST NEVER FAIL OPEN\n  any non-2    | footprint-level marker | pad=None | board-wide relevance\n\nCode: compile_board.py `_zone_connect_blocks` / `_pour_could_touch` / `_pad_occupies` / `_pad_net_name`. THE PLAN SAID 0 AND 1 SHOULD WARN; I made them block, because v1's fill connects same-net copper SOLID and nothing else, so 0 (isolate) and 1 (thermal spokes) are both silently overridden — and zone_fill._refuse_thermal already REFUSES the identical intent at zone scope. Warning at pad scope while refusing at zone scope would make strictness depend on where the author typed it. Tell me if that is wrong; it is the epoch's most consequential judgement call.\n\nNet effect is a LOOSENING: pre-branch, any zone_connect value blocked board-wide whenever any pour existed.\n\n== FIVE PLACES I DEPARTED FROM THE PLAN ==\n1. S4's body CANCELLED, not deferred. It called for writing JLCPCB's published silk/hole-to-copper figures into v1-fab-conservative.json — a profile whose own `source` says it is \"not a specific board house\". That falsifies its provenance or asserts JLC is a cross-house floor, and there is no second source to triangulate (OSH Park publishes neither figure). It would also have retroactively re-verdicted pcb/fab/coupon-001, already ordered and DFM-passed. Shipped instead: `not_evaluated`, which names every rule whose floor the profile does not declare, so a count of 0 says whether it was measured. Closes the class for every profile including permanently-silent ones.\n2. S11 gated on BOARD state (design_rules.allowed_trace_angles_deg), not on a rule profile, for the same reason: no board house requires orthogonal routing.\n3. S10's ingest-side snap NOT implemented. Measured: `snapped(v, 0.0001)` stored back into a Vector2 returns the float32 unchanged, so an ingest snap is dead code shaped like a guarantee — and snapping an author's off-grid input would silently move their copper. The grid only holds at the reply boundary.\n4. S8's verb is `list_mounting_holes`, not `list_holes` — pad drills and via barrels are holes too.\n5. PLUGIN_BUILD bumped once at S10, not per station.\n\n== WHERE I MOST EXPECT TO BE WRONG ==\n* **The NameError class.** F1 proves our gates cannot see it. Sweep for names used but not imported/defined, Python and GDScript, across the whole diff.\n* **S10's sweep completeness.** Three rounds, three different enumeration methods, and each found emitters the previous missed (candidate geometry as bare `[x,y]` arrays; then `_pin_info`'s position, one screen from the one already fixed; then `_move_relative`'s snap-disclosure `requested`). Assume a fourth exists.\n* **Tests that pass for the wrong reason.** Three were caught this way — one deleted (a via credit that the cross-layer coincident-endpoint rule was actually providing), one fixture that could not see a reorder bug it was written to catch, one whose duplicate pair sat in the position the pre-fix code already skipped. Fable's checklist rule 4 says any test passing both sides is a defect; audit for more.\n* **Hand-derived assertion pins.** test_draft_composer.gd 91->95 and test_pcb_panel_layout.gd 149->150, both derived from counting added assertions, never from a run.\n\n== THREE THINGS READING COULD NOT SETTLE (Fable's list, and I agree) ==\n1. Run the mutation proof rather than trusting the walkthrough: execute tests/gd/test_mounting_holes_verb.gd at this SHA AND with the any-pair guard reverted, and confirm cell (f) actually goes red pre-fix.\n2. Wire-level residue: read live MCP replies (pin_info, move_component, list_mounting_holes) and confirm the snapped values serialize clean through GDScript's JSON float printing and the Go/broker path — that nothing downstream reintroduces residue or re-truncates through a float32.\n3. Consumer audit ACROSS REPOS: the quantize-replies / verbatim-model boundary is only safe if no consumer computes copper against the verbatim surfaces. Does mhroute (ccsandbox tools/mhroute/) or any agent workflow read journal coordinates, `requested`, or feed reply positions back as authored geometry? That spans repos neither of us read.\n\n== CONTEXT ==\nPlan of record and all owner rulings: this project, item 01a026ddd3ee72a9b45ec33ec9cf23d9 (description = the plan; comment 32 = owner rulings; comments 33-44 = per-station notes stating every deviation and its argument).\n\nCI NOTE worth knowing: a description mismatch between pcb/manifest.json and internal/tools/worker_tools.go fails the Go test step BEFORE the Python suite runs, so the job goes red while pytest never executes. This branch touched that pair; all 19 backend-executor tools were verified byte-identical on both description and input_schema.\n\n== NOT IN SCOPE ==\nThe board's 23 unrouted nets. The epoch delivers a verifiable path to promote; routing completion belongs to the mhroute campaign.","created_at":"2026-08-22T02:27:05","updated_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:29","directed_to":"codex","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["codex","epoch","pcb","review","smart-remote-v2"],"resolution":"APPROVED at 085e8cf. Three rounds: 4 findings, then 2 residuals, then approve. No blocking code-review findings remain. Approval covers CODE REVIEW ONLY and explicitly does not waive the operational exit gate — compile, board_check, fab preview and gerbers/BOM/CPL still need replay against the live smart-remote-v2 board. One non-blocking hygiene item (_arr_pair's dead quantization) filed as chore 01a028512e26 rather than edited post-approval."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a027a233f179a78e8274ed5a276d93","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Run the pcb worker's pytest with pcb/worker/.venv/bin/python, not the ambient interpreter","created_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:16","updated_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:16","tags":["environment","pcb","pyclipper","pytest","testing","venv"],"value":"    cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests -q --ignore=tests/oracle\n\nThe ambient interpreter (/home/imran/anaconda3) LACKS pyclipper; pcb/worker/.venv has pyclipper 1.4.0. Running with the ambient one produces a ~116-failure zone-fill cascade across test_zone_fill, test_coupon_board, test_gerbers, test_silk_projection, test_mask_projection, the gc7/gc9/gc10/gc11 checks, test_cutouts, test_ir_parity, test_determinism_gate and test_hermetic_check.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH A HINT rather than being obvious: the cascade looks exactly like a real regression. It is 116 confident-looking failures with plausible names, on a branch that touches DRC and geometry. During SR2FAB I ran it on the ambient interpreter, saw 116 failures on the BRANCH, ran the merge base, saw the same 116, and concluded \"pre-existing, not mine\" — a correct conclusion reached from a broken measurement. Codex diagnosed the real cause by running both worktrees under identical dependencies and getting main 2605 passed / branch 2650 passed, both green.\n\nTwo lessons beyond the command:\n- A baseline comparison protects you from misattributing failures, but NOT from a bad environment: both sides were equally wrong, so the diff looked clean.\n- tests/oracle additionally needs kicad-cli and is environment-sensitive on top of this; exclude it or expect one more failure.\n\nAlso non-hermetic, and NOT caused by any branch: `go test ./...`'s TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly lets check_libraries consult the ambient Minerva plugin-data directory, so it fails when that lacks R_0805/C_0805/TH_TestPoint even though the repo's own pcb/library/footprints/ has them. `go test ./internal/...` is clean.\n\nRelated: [[pcb/test.baseline-before-branch-switch]]","component":"pcb","key":"test.use-the-worker-venv","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":3,"research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a028512e2671a2b2f98fdf95f705ed","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: _arr_pair quantizes an internal polyline — dead, and a landmine for the boundary rule","description":"panel_tools._arr_pair applies _mm() to coordinates whose only consumer is _route_polyline -> rec[\"polyline\"], which is read for pts.size() / waypoint_count and nothing else. The raw segments remain the ingest/materialization geometry, so the quantization is DEAD: it changes no behaviour today.\n\nRaised by Codex at the SR2FAB approval (question 01a0274b0e9a comment 50) as non-blocking hygiene, and NOT fixed in that branch on purpose — the approval is pinned to 085e8cf and a post-approval edit, however small, is an unreviewed delta.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH DOING ANYWAY, and why this is filed rather than dropped: SR2FAB produced two regressions from exactly one confusion — applying the reply-boundary quantization rule to something that is not a reply. One crashed a validator by snapping a null sentinel (width_mm in _validate_route_intent); the other moved a collision body by 0.00005mm before a polygon intersection (_other_ghost_targets). A third site that quantizes an internal path and happens to be harmless is a standing invitation for the next reader to conclude the rule applies to internals. Dead code that models a wrong rule is worse than dead code.\n\nFIX: pass the pair through verbatim (float(), no _mm) and say at the site that the polyline is internal and size-only. Confirm _route_polyline's consumers still only read length; if a later change ever puts polyline coordinates in a reply, quantize THERE.\n\nRelated: the not-snapped boundary is documented at each deliberate site in panel_tools (model writes, %-formatted display values, the change journal, camera state).","created_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:24","updated_at":"2026-08-22T07:13:24","priority":3,"tags":["hygiene","pcb","quantization","sr2fab-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02873cad3747ba8cc279f5b98fbf0","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"GC7 reports phantom zone-clearance violations on any pour that declares no clearance_mm","created_at":"2026-08-22T07:51:12","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:24","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["drc","false-positive","gc7","pcb","smart-remote-v2","zone-fill"],"resolution":"Landed in the pcb 0.5.0 merge 6462202 (\"GC7 fix\"); four commits cite it. Docket cleanup 2026-08-25.","environment":"Found during the SR2FAB operational replay on the live smart-remote-v2 board, plugin_build 0.2.0+sr2fab. PRE-EXISTING — neither gc7 nor the filler was touched by that epoch. It became visible only because SR2FAB's S2 made this board compile for the first time, so geometric DRC could run on it at all.</environment>\n<parameter name=\"repro_steps\">A board with a copper pour that does NOT author zone.clearance_mm, and foreign-net copper inside the pour region. smart-remote-v2 is the live instance: GND pour on bottom, SW_NODE trace + via_3 on bottom inside it.\n\nminerva_pcb_board_check reports verdict \"violations\", promotable false, refusals [\"geometric DRC reports 3 finding(s)\"], all three gc7_zone_clearance with measured_mm 0.0 and required_mm 0.2.</repro_steps>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">No findings. The pour IS correctly carved: the filler used 0.2 mm and there is no copper closer than 0.2 mm to anything.</expected_result>\n<parameter name=\"findings\">MEASURED, not inferred. I reproduced gc7's own intersection offline against the exported board and printed the overlap AREAS:\n\n  segment:6308a9fc...   18,103 nm^2   (0.000000018 mm^2)\n  segment:6f0add2b...    1,858 nm^2   (0.000000002 mm^2)\n  via:48cf7c55...       24,927 nm^2   (0.000000025 mm^2)\n\nFor scale, one square micrometre is 1,000,000 nm^2. The largest of these, squared off, is about 158 nm on a side. It is not copper; it is the difference between two polygonal approximations of the same arc.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is a coincidence of two correct decisions:\n1. zone_fill._clearance_mm: `floor = zone.clearance_mm`, and when that is None it falls back to board.design_rules.minimums.min_clearance_mm. This pour declares none, so it carves at 0.2.\n2. drc_geometric._check_gc7_zone_clearance: `required = _effective_min_clearance(global_min, ...)` = 0.2, with no net classes to raise it.\n\nGC7's docstring argues it is not circular because \"the FILLER carves by the ZONE's authored clearance_mm ... while this check uses _effective_min_clearance\". That is true when the zone AUTHORS a clearance. When it does not, both sides resolve to the identical number by design — zone_fill._clearance_mm's own docstring says \"Same rule resolution the geometric DRC applies, so the fill obeys the rule it will be judged against\". The two boundaries are then mathematically coincident.\n\n3. Both sides approximate round joins polygonally (PyclipperOffset(2.0, 5000), JT_ROUND) but through different call paths, so the approximations differ by sub-nanometre amounts and the intersection is a sliver rather than empty.\n\n4. GC7 has NO AREA GUARD: `overlap = clipper.Execute(...)` then `if not overlap: continue` and straight to a finding. Any non-empty result, however degenerate, is a violation.\n\nBLAST RADIUS: this is the DEFAULT configuration. A pour that authors no explicit clearance_mm is the common case, and any foreign copper inside it will trip this. It makes promote impossible on such a board, and the refusal names a clearance violation that does not exist.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE NUISANCE: it is a FALSE POSITIVE on the check that guards the largest piece of copper on the board. A reviewer who learns that gc7 cries wolf on every default pour is being trained to wave through the one time it is right.</findings>\n<parameter name=\"description\">SUGGESTED FIX, smallest first:\n\n(a) Add a minimum-area guard to gc7 — ignore an overlap whose total area is below a sliver threshold. The threshold has to be argued, not invented: it should be tied to the offset arc tolerance (5000 nm) rather than picked. An overlap that cannot represent real copper at the fabrication grid is not a violation. This is the narrow fix and it does not weaken the check: a genuine under-carve produces an overlap many orders of magnitude larger (a 0.1 mm under-carve on a 0.8 mm trace is ~10^5 mm^2 scale, not 10^-8).\n\n(b) Alternatively, have gc7 inflate by `required - EPS` so a boundary-coincident carve cannot intersect at all. Cheaper but it silently loosens the rule by EPS, which is worse on the honesty axis.\n\n(a) is preferred, with the threshold DERIVED and stated at the site.\n\nTESTS: the natural fixture is exactly this shape — a pour with no authored clearance_mm and a foreign trace inside it, asserting zero findings; plus a genuine under-carve (zone authoring a clearance BELOW the board minimum) asserting the finding still fires. The second is what stops the guard from being a blanket exemption. Both are cheap and neither exists today, which is why this shipped.\n\nNOT FIXED IN THE SR2FAB BRANCH. That branch is Codex-approved at 085e8cf and this is a pre-existing defect in code it did not touch; folding an unreviewed DRC change into an approved branch is the wrong trade. Filed for its own cycle.</description>\n"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02881647e7c6d9d1fc4dacaf6a167","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Most of the zone model is UNREACHABLE — clearance/thermal/priority have no MCP and no GUI path","created_at":"2026-08-22T08:06:03","updated_at":"2026-08-22T08:06:03","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["ergonomics","parity","pcb","smart-remote-v2","unreachable-property","zones"],"environment":"Found during the SR2FAB operational replay on the live smart-remote-v2 board, plugin_build 0.2.0+sr2fab. Independently verified by Fable against manifest.json, panel_tools.gd and PCBPanel.gd. Pre-existing; nothing in SR2FAB touched the zone surface.</environment>\n<parameter name=\"repro_steps\">Ask for the GND pour's clearance to be set to 0.25 on smart-remote-v2. There is no way to do it from either surface.\n\nAGENT: the eight zone verbs are create_zone, delete_zone, describe_zone, list_zones, propose_zone, set_zone_layer, set_zone_net, set_zone_outline. None accepts clearance_mm in its manifest.json input_schema. create_zone's parameters are {editor_name, kind, layer, net, outline}. _describe_zone returns exactly {zone_id, kind, net, layer, point_count, outline} (ui/panel_tools.gd:4366-4373). `grep clearance_mm ui/panel_tools.gd` returns zero zone hits.\n\nHUMAN: the zone property surface is _build_zone_rows (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1919-1968) — Kind as a read-only label, a Net dropdown, a Layer dropdown. Nothing else, and no dialog elsewhere (`grep clearance_mm ui/*.gd` hits only model files).</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"expected_result\">A property that the canonical schema documents (docs/board-yaml.md:189), that the model stores, and that decides both how the pour carves and whether GC7 passes should be settable by at least one of the two surfaces.</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"findings\">THE REACHABLE ZONE SURFACE IS: kind, net, layer, outline. That is all.\n\nUNREACHABLE, the whole tail of ResolvedZone (worker/pcb_worker/resolved_board.py:1043-1054):\n  clearance_mm      — decides the carve AND whether gc7 passes\n  min_thickness_mm\n  priority\n  connect_mode\n  thermal (thermal_gap_mm / thermal_bridge_width_mm)\n\nTHERMAL IS WORSE THAN UNREACHABLE. Authoring it makes zone_fill._refuse_thermal (zone_fill.py:838-845) refuse the fill outright — so the only way to reach that field is to hand-edit YAML, and doing so breaks the board. A property whose sole authoring path leads to a refusal is a trap.\n\nWHY clearance_mm IS URGENT RATHER THAN MERELY MISSING: it is the ONLY knob that breaks the gc7 coincident-boundary tie (bug 01a02873cad3). Measured on this board — raising board min_clearance_mm from 0.2 to 0.25 or 0.3 does NOT clear the phantom findings, because gc7's requirement rises with it; a net-class minimum does the same. Only a per-zone clearance that DIFFERS from the effective requirement works. So the documented workaround for a P1 that blocks promote is unreachable from both surfaces.\n\nONE ASTERISK ON THE READ SIDE: zones are held VERBATIM in the model (ui/model/pcb_data.gd:110-131), so minerva_pcb_export_yaml returns any authored clearance and an agent can READ it by parsing that document. There is no write path at all, from either surface.\n\nOWNER-PARITY NOTE: the owner is GUI-only. This is therefore not the usual \"MCP has it, the GUI does not\" parity gap — neither side has it, so no human instruction and no agent action can author the value. The only author is a text editor outside the application.</parameter>\n<parameter name=\"description\">SUGGESTED FIX, small and in two halves:\n\n1. AGENT: accept `clearance_mm` on minerva_pcb_create_zone, return it from minerva_pcb_describe_zone, and add minerva_pcb_set_zone_clearance mirroring set_zone_layer/set_zone_net (same no-op-on-unchanged, one-journalled-step, named-refusal shape). Decide deliberately whether to expose the rest of the tail; priority and min_thickness_mm are cheap, and connect_mode/thermal should stay closed until the pending v1 solid-vs-spokes owner ruling (accumulator R-d) is made, since authoring thermal today only produces a refusal.\n\n2. HUMAN: one more property row in _build_zone_rows (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1919-1968) beside Kind/Net/Layer. The owner is GUI-only, so this half is not optional — an MCP-only fix would leave the person who owns the board unable to set it.\n\nWHAT NOT TO DO, and this is why the workaround was refused during the replay: minerva_pcb_load_board with a hand-modified YAML reaches the field but is a DESTRUCTIVE whole-board replace. Its inline `yaml` form \"adopts nothing\" — annotations and the routing-workspace sidecar (ui/model/pcb_routing_sidecar.gd:145-235) are restored only on the `path` form with a sidecar beside the file — and undo/journal history is destroyed unconditionally. The delta honesty report covers entity counts, NOT session state. On a board whose only truth is the live editor plus drive sync, swapping the whole board to inject one field the app refuses to expose is a hazard, not a workaround.</parameter>\n</invoke>\n"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a029392ab57fb1966f4ab1a228b586","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb/test.mutation-testing-hygiene","created_at":"2026-08-22T11:26:47","updated_at":"2026-08-22T11:26:47","value":"Mutation-testing the worker: (1) COMMIT FIRST — a `git checkout pcb_worker/` restore helper silently wipes uncommitted source edits, and every row after the first then measures HEAD, not your work. (2) Disable bytecode caching: PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, `python -B`, `-p no:cacheprovider`, and rm -rf __pycache__ between rows. Editing a single-digit constant (GC7_SLIVER_WIDTH_NM = 2 -> 4) leaves the FILE SIZE UNCHANGED, so stale .pyc reuse gives a non-monotonic matrix (thresholds 2/3/5 \"failing\" while 10/50 \"passed\"). (3) grep-verify each mutation landed in the file before running. Both failure modes produced confident wrong matrices in one session.","component":"pcb","key":"test.mutation-testing-hygiene","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02adda22c72bfa835df5a7bb988e2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb/release.verify-artifact-contents","created_at":"2026-08-22T19:06:03","updated_at":"2026-08-22T19:06:03","value":"To prove a pcb release actually SHIPS a worker change, do NOT grep the pcb-plugin binary — the Python worker is copied into site-packages inside a go:embed'd zstd bundle (internal/pcbruntime/bundle/runtime-bundle-<target>.tar.zst), so source strings are compressed and absent from a plain grep. The release tarball has NO worker/ dir either; the pack step only copies pcb-plugin, manifest.json, ui/, library/, libraries.lock.json, NOTICE.md. To verify: scan the binary for the zstd magic 28 B5 2F FD (there are 2 frames — the SECOND, ~offset 7.5M, is the runtime bundle), slice from that offset, `zstd -d -c | tar xf - ./lib/python3.12/site-packages/pcb_worker/<file>.py`, then diff against the repo source. The bundle is rebuilt per package job from the checked-out tree (scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh copies worker packages), so it always matches the commit CI ran on; only the PBS CPython download is cached.","component":"pcb","key":"release.verify-artifact-contents","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720","type":"dcr","status":"approved","title":"Ratsnest as routing instruction: pad-scoped route target over MST/island airwires","description":"## Problem (owner, 2026-08-22, smart-remote v2)\n\n\"The ratsnest is too ratty — I can't tell what pins/pads want to connect.\" Then, more precisely: **\"What do I route this pin/pad to? I route pins one by one, and I need to know the two endpoints of the trace to figure out how to draw it.\"**\n\nThe second statement is the real requirement and it reframes the feature. The designer is not asking to browse a connectivity graph; they are asking for the other end of the trace they are about to draw.\n\n## Design direction — CONFIRMED by the owner 2026-08-22\n\nCodex's recommendation (child discussion 01a02ba3fd1e, comment 55) is the adopted design for this feature.\n\n**Core principle: do not make the designer interpret a graph while routing; turn the graph into one immediate routing instruction.**\n\nStarting a trace on a routable pad activates a transient, pad-scoped **route target**:\n\n- dim unrelated airwires; highlight the source copper island;\n- show exactly ONE prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island on that net;\n- mark and label the destination (refdes.pad, net, distance);\n- **lock that target for the duration of the routing gesture** — recompute only on commit or cancel;\n- for an offscreen destination, show an edge arrow plus \"frame both endpoints\", with NO automatic camera movement.\n\nComponent selection may still reveal all incident one-hop connections as an overview, but pad focus is the primary hand-routing interaction.\n\n## What this SUPERSEDES\n\nThis DCR was originally filed as *selection-scoped reveal* — select a component, its airwires brighten, the rest fade; pin-inspect a pad for the single-pad version anchored on the existing INSPECT_PIN tool. Two owner rulings changed it:\n\n1. **Pin inspection is rejected as the trigger** — \"not obvious; I wouldn't think to use pin inspection for this case.\" The routing gesture itself activates the focus.\n2. **Connectivity-depth levels are dropped from the routing story.** Dimming other airwires plus endpoint highlighting \"removes the need for zoom levels.\" A degree definition was developed and is retained for the separate, unscheduled *comprehension* story — \"how does this section connect\" — which must not be conflated with routing. It lives in the child discussion, section 2.\n\nThe earlier rejection of a 0-3 depth knob with a HUD stepper, a source lock on `L`, and state in `pcb_set_preference` stands, on the original grounds: `ui/pcb_prefs.gd` is registry-gated and plugin-scoped so a per-board view anchor violates its charter (the real channel is `minerva_pcb_view_state` over the `_VIEW_FLAGS` family, currently bool-only); `L` is already lock-selected-components and `N` already toggles the ratsnest; and 2-3 hops answer no demonstrated routing question.\n\n## Trigger dependency — RESOLVED 2026-08-22\n\nRoute-start activation was recorded as depending on the trace tool hit-testing pads. Measured, and the answer is stronger than assumed: **the trace tool REQUIRES a pad to start.** `_handle_trace_click` calls `_trace_pad_at` on every click including the first; `_start_trace` refuses an empty hit with \"Start a trace on a pad — that is where its net comes from,\" and refuses a pad on no net.\n\nSo at the instant a routing gesture begins the tool already holds the source pad ref, its position in board mm, and its net — the whole input the focus needs, with no new hit-test, no new mode, and no INSPECT_PIN dependency. There is no \"started in empty space\" case to design around. The earlier objection that pads are not selection entities in SELECT mode does not apply, because route-start never goes through SELECT. Full measurement in the child discussion, comment 58.\n\n## Why the renderer is the prerequisite\n\nFiltering the current renderer would only make arbitrary pairings less visually noisy. `_draw_ratsnest` chains pins in raw declaration order, subtracts nothing for copper already placed, and does not quiet power nets — so no amount of scoping makes it answer \"what do I route this to.\" **The MST edge incident to a pad IS the routing instruction**, and it only exists once islands are computed from real copper. See the child work item.\n\n## Build order (accepted verbatim from Codex, comment 55)\n\n1. Copper-island-aware MST renderer (child work item).\n2. Route-start pad focus with one locked destination.\n3. Correct the already-connected and pour-covered states.\n4. Offscreen target arrow and \"frame both endpoints\".\n5. Tap-anywhere / constrained-net metadata.\n6. Destination loupe or unrouted worklist — ONLY if testing shows remaining need.\n\n## Rules the implementation must honour\n\n- **Islands are physical**: built from pads, traces, vias and pours — not netlist membership.\n- **Exactly one suggestion.** No ranked list, no picker (owner ruling). Tie-break DETERMINISTIC (lowest refdes or coordinate), never random — a coin toss makes the same board answer differently between sessions.\n- **Ship named-pad targets first.** Mid-trace T-taps only for nets explicitly marked tap-anywhere; constrained nets (feedback/Kelvin, differential, high-speed, high-current) must require a named endpoint. Defaulting to pad-endpoints-only fails in the harmless direction. Rationale and the failure taxonomy are in the child discussion, section 4.\n- **A pad served by a pour is \"nothing to route\"**, not an airwire to a distant pad. A pad already joined by copper disappears entirely.\n- **Rails are dimmed, never silently hidden**, and labelled by UNRESOLVED count (\"GND · 27 unresolved\"), not net size.\n- **Do not rely on `is_power_net`** — measured False on every net of this board, including GND.\n- **Guidance, never enforcement**, unless a topology constraint exists on that net. There is a precedent in the same file: `_finish_trace_on_pad` carries an explicit owner ruling that the trace tool does not enforce same-net — a trace onto a foreign pad is named out loud as a short and drawn anyway, because \"DRC is the correctness net, not the drawing tool.\" Suggesting a target must never obstruct drawing elsewhere.\n- If hover is ever added as a second trigger, it must state its hit radius. Route-start inherits `TRACE_PAD_SNAP_MM` (1.27) for free; the inspector's default is 5.0, and a hover on the wider radius would fire on pads the trace tool refuses to start on.\n- Do not add view state to `pcb_prefs`; use `view_state` if persistence is needed. Do not rebind `L` or `N`. Register any new draw-affecting state in `CAPTURE_MIRRORED_FIELDS` (`ui/pcb_canvas.gd`) or agent screenshots silently disagree with the screen.\n\n## Deferred alternative (not chosen)\n\nUnrouted worklist panel — a sidebar listing inter-island airwires ordered by length or proximity, grouped by net. Reframes to \"what should I route next near here\" and is the only option that scales past what canvas overlays show. Now build-order step 6, gated on evidence.\n\n## Falsifier\n\nShip steps 1-3 and route a session by hand. If the designer still has to hunt for the other endpoint — or the single suggested target is wrong often enough that they want the list back — then one-answer-no-picker is the wrong ruling and the ranked set returns.\n\n## Status\n\nDesign confirmed by the owner; no implementation started. Child work item (MST renderer) is the blocker and must land first. A related defect — deleting committed copper leaves the routing workspace reporting the span as routed (bug 01a02bf97224) — corrupts the \"what is still unrouted\" answer this feature is built on, and should be weighed before or alongside step 1.","created_at":"2026-08-22T21:18:40","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:32:36","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","ratsnest","selection","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02b5763e87b84a9318b28f7437fa7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Ratsnest renderer draws a raw pin-list chain, not MST airwires","description":"## The mis-design\n\n`ui/pcb_canvas.gd:2629-2656` (`_draw_ratsnest`): for every net with >= 2 pins it draws a dashed chain in **raw pin-list order** — `pin[i] -> pin[i+1]` — at alpha 0.6 in the per-net colour. Three defects compound:\n\n1. **Not a spanning tree.** Chain order is the order pins happen to sit in the net's pin list, so lines criss-cross the board arbitrarily instead of connecting near neighbours.\n2. **No routed-pair subtraction.** Despite the \"unrouted net connections\" comment, pads already joined by committed copper still get airwires drawn between them. Airwires never shrink as routing lands.\n3. **No power-net quieting.** A 36-pin GND net contributes 35 lines, drawn in `Color.BLACK` (GND's default, `ui/pcb_net.gd:228`).\n\nNet effect on smart-remote v2: the ratsnest is unreadable, which is the owner-reported \"too ratty\" complaint. This is a renderer defect, not a missing filter feature — no amount of filtering fixes wrong geometry.\n\n## Intended behaviour\n\n- **Per-net Euclidean MST** instead of the chain: each unconnected copper island gets exactly one airwire to its nearest island.\n- **Island union**: union pads already joined by committed copper into islands and draw airwires only *between* islands, so lines disappear one by one as copper lands. The pad-snap tolerance logic in `PcbAnnotationHost.pin_info` (`:1013-1022`) is the seam to reuse for endpoint-to-pad association.\n- **Power-net quieting**: draw power-net airwires heavily dimmed (or nearest-neighbour only) unless a member is selected. `is_power_net` exists (`ui/pcb_net.gd:25`, contract `internal/board/minpcb.go:56`) but is not guaranteed set on agent-created nets — needs the name-heuristic fallback pattern already used by `generate_color_for_name:223`.\n\nThis is what KiCad does, i.e. the baseline the owner is unconsciously comparing against.\n\n## Why this is a work item, not a DCR\n\nIt corrects an existing mis-design in the renderer rather than proposing a new design direction (owner convention). The new-direction half — selection/pin-scoped reveal — is the parent DCR.\n\n## Cost and risk\n\nSmall-to-medium: one function rewrite plus union-find. The island endpoint-tolerance math is the only real risk (getting \"is this trace endpoint on this pad\" wrong either merges islands that aren't connected, hiding a needed airwire, or fails to merge, leaving a phantom one).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Register any new draw-affecting state in `CAPTURE_MIRRORED_FIELDS` (`ui/pcb_canvas.gd:9735`) or agent screenshots silently disagree with the screen.\n- `N` stays bound to ratsnest toggle (`:4102`); do not rebind.\n\n## Verification\n\nSmart-remote v2 with its 36-pin GND / 20-pin +3V3 nets is the natural fixture: GND should read as short local hops rather than a black hairball, and airwires between C12.1 and its VIN neighbours should have vanished now that `trace_8`..`trace_11` are committed.\n\n## Status\n\nFiled for the record only — no work started.","created_at":"2026-08-22T21:19:02","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:38:23","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","ratsnest","renderer","ux"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02ba3fd1e76c6816cabaa1f2408c0","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Degree, tap legality, and what silently breaks a board","description":"# 0. Decisions taken (owner, 2026-08-22)\n\n1. **Codex's UX story wins.** The primary interaction is a transient, pad-scoped **route target** activated by the routing gesture itself: dim unrelated airwires, highlight the source island, show exactly one prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island, label the destination.\n2. **Pin inspection is REJECTED as the trigger** — \"not obvious; I wouldn't think to use pin inspection for this\". Starting a trace activates the focus directly. (This overrides the earlier suggestion to anchor pad scope on the existing INSPECT_PIN tool; that suggestion rested on pads not being selection entities in SELECT mode, but the trace tool must already hit-test pads to start a trace at all — verify before building.)\n3. **Depth / connectivity-zoom levels are NOT NEEDED for routing.** Dimming other airwires plus endpoint highlighting does the job. Degree remains defined (section 2) and available for the separate *comprehension* story — \"how does this section connect\" — which is unscheduled and must not be conflated with routing.\n4. **MST/island renderer is the prerequisite for everything.** Filtering the current chain would only make arbitrary pairings less visually noisy.\n\nBuild order (from Codex, accepted): (1) copper-island-aware MST renderer; (2) route-start pad focus with one locked destination; (3) correct already-connected and pour-covered states; (4) offscreen target arrow and Frame Both; (5) tap-anywhere / constrained-net metadata; (6) destination loupe or unrouted worklist ONLY if testing shows remaining need.\n\n**Core principle**: do not make the designer interpret a graph while routing; turn the graph into one immediate routing instruction.\n\n# 1. Why this exists\n\nThe DCR asks how to make the ratsnest legible. The conversation behind it moved the question twice, and both moves change what should be built:\n\n- from \"filter the ratsnest\" to \"what is a degree, such that it survives power rails\";\n- from \"show connectivity\" to **\"what do I route THIS pad to, right now\"** — because routing happens one pad at a time, and the designer needs two concrete endpoints to draw a trace.\n\nThe second reframe is the load-bearing one: it makes the MST/island renderer (child work item) the primary feature rather than a cosmetic fix, because a correctly computed airwire IS the routing instruction.\n\n# 2. What is a degree\n\n*(Retained for the comprehension story, which is separate from routing — see decision 3.)*\n\n## The problem with the obvious definitions\n\n- **Shared-net adjacency** degenerates: GND and +3V3 touch nearly everything, so all parts are 1 degree apart and the measure carries no information.\n- **Copper adjacency** is empty on an unrouted board — which is exactly when the ratsnest is needed — and it changes whenever the router runs, so degree would be a property of the routing session rather than of the design.\n- **\"Power vs signal\"** is the wrong axis. The battery path J5 -> F1 -> Q1 -> U4 (nets VBAT -> VBAT_F -> VIN) is power but is SHAPED like a signal chain: series elements, low fanout, direction. The designer wants to walk it.\n\nThe axis that works is **distributes-to-many vs connects-a-few-in-series**.\n\n## Measured facts (Fable, against the live board YAML; 37 components, 29 nets)\n\n- **`is_power_net` is False on EVERY net of this board, including GND.** Not \"sometimes missing\" — empirically worthless as a gate. No definition may depend on it.\n- **Component fanout** (distinct components touching a net, NOT pin count) separates cleanly: GND 29, +3V3 18, then a hard gap to VIN 5, everything else <= 4. Any threshold in 6..17 gives an identical partition.\n- Pin count and component fanout differ (GND is 36 pins / 29 components); component fanout is the one that means \"distribution\".\n\n## The definition\n\n> Degree between two components is the length of the shortest chain of shared LOCAL nets between them, where every component is one node, every local net makes all components touching it mutually adjacent, and a net is local unless it touches more components than the rail threshold — in which case it is a distribution rail: shown, but never walked through.\n\nComputation: node = component; edge = for each local net, all its components pairwise adjacent at cost 1; **rails contribute ZERO edges**; undirected BFS; built from the netlist, never from copper. Rail test = component_fanout > T (default 8; auto-lowered to max(4, ceil(N/2)) below 16 components). Per-net override beats the auto test.\n\n## Why \"rails contribute zero edges\" rather than \"do not cross this net\"\n\nThis is the clause that neutralizes **decoupling capacitors**, which would otherwise silently reconnect the graph. C7 has one leg on VIN and one on GND. Refusing to *cross* GND is not enough if the component still conducts between its nets. Because a rail contributes no adjacency at all, C7 has no onward edge to offer and is a leaf by construction — the walk enters and stops. No special-casing of capacitors.\n\nTwo independent derivations converged on this rule:\n- structural (component-node BFS, rails contribute no edges);\n- the owner's **breadboard model** — a degree is one jumper wire you must place; the rails are pre-wired strips you *terminate on but never transit through*. Every part is one wire from the rail, and that wire is termination, not transit.\n\nThe breadboard model also cleanly separates two questions that were being conflated: **what a rail MEANS** (terminate-only — structural, fixed) from **how a rail is DETECTED** (fanout, flag, or explicit override — swappable). A wrong detector then degrades the view instead of corrupting the definition.\n\n## Worked results\n\n- **Synthetic line** (36 parts daisy-chained, all sharing GND and +3V3): U1 to U36 = **35**. Matches designer intuition exactly.\n- **This board**: J5 -> F1 -> Q1 -> U4 walks as degrees **1, 2, 3**. GND is never hopped. C7 appears in the VIN shell then terminates. R1 is a hop (U1S -> R1 = 1, U1S -> U2 = 2).\n\n## Resolved sub-questions\n\n- **Series passives are a hop, not transparent.** Every component is a node uniformly. Transparency would need part-type metadata this board has already proven absent, and a series capacitor is transparent to AC but open to DC with no structural test to tell them apart. Offer transparency as a view toggle, never as the definition.\n- **Multi-pin nets**: all members mutually 1. A net is one equipotential; distance through it is 1 regardless of pin count.\n- **Undirected.** Pin-direction metadata is less reliable than the flag that measured all-False; buses are bidirectional; the line example yields 35 either way.\n- **Unrouted boards**: fully defined, because degree is a function of the netlist. Identical before and after routing.\n- **Connectors pass through by default** (enter J2 on I2C, leave via its +3V3_SW pin). NOTE: this is contested — component-as-node treats every multi-pin part as internally bonded, which is electrically false for all of them; it is right as a model of FUNCTIONAL flow, and connectors are where functional flow and electrical reality diverge most. A \"connectors terminate walks\" toggle exists; whether it defaults ON is open.\n\n## Known failure modes of the definition\n\n- **Mid-fanout legitimate signals.** A clock/reset/enable tree touching 9+ components auto-classifies as a rail and truncates the walk. The honest insight: fanout measures *distribution shape*, not rail-ness — a reset tree is distribution-shaped signal exactly as VBAT_F is chain-shaped power. The heuristic has the right axis and is a lossy measurement of it. Mitigation is UI, not a cleverer threshold: never drop a net silently; render it grayed with its unresolved count and a one-click override.\n- **Hub components.** U1S touches 20 of 29 nets, so selecting almost anything puts it at degree 1-2, and from U1S half the board is degree 1. Truthful, but visually it is the hairball this feature exists to kill. The shell view must survive a 15-part shell.\n- **All-rail parts.** A decoupler with both legs on rails is degree infinity from everything. Word it \"rail-only part\", never \"unconnected\", or it reads as a DRC error.\n- **Tiny boards.** Below ~16 components the relative threshold is least trustworthy; overrides matter most there.\n\n**Falsifier for the whole structural approach**: a board in the corpus where must-walk nets and must-block rails OVERLAP in component fanout — a clock tree touching more components than a local supply rail on the same board — so no threshold separates them. Here the gap is 5 vs 18. If that gap closes elsewhere, rail-ness must come from curated per-net metadata and this becomes a different product.\n\n# 3. The routing primitive — THE PRIMARY FEATURE\n\nRouting happens one pad at a time, so the operative question is not \"show me connectivity\" but **\"what is the other endpoint of the trace I am about to draw?\"**\n\n## What the interaction does\n\nTriggered by **starting a trace** on a routable pad (decision 2):\n\n- dim unrelated airwires;\n- highlight the source copper island;\n- show exactly ONE prominent connection to the nearest unconnected island on that net;\n- mark and label the destination — refdes.pad, net, distance;\n- **LOCK that target for the duration of the routing gesture** so it does not jump as the cursor moves; recompute only after commit or cancel;\n- for an **offscreen destination**, show an edge arrow plus a \"frame both endpoints\" affordance — and do NOT move the camera automatically.\n\nComponent selection may still reveal all incident one-hop connections as an overview, but **pad focus is the primary hand-routing UX**.\n\nThe target lock and the offscreen handling both come from Codex and are load-bearing: an unlocked target flickers mid-gesture and makes the feature worse than none, and at routing zoom on a board this size the destination is usually offscreen.\n\n## Consequences for the computation\n\n- **Degrees above 1 are comprehension, not routing.** You never route to something three hops away. Keep the two features distinct.\n- **The destination is an ISLAND, not a pad.** For a multi-pin net the answer is never \"the other 35 pads\"; it is the nearest copper on this net not already joined to you. The MST edge incident to the pad IS the routing instruction. This is why the current renderer cannot answer it: its pairs come from pin declaration order, so they are arbitrary rather than nearest.\n- **Islands are built from pads, traces, vias AND pours** — physical connectivity, not netlist membership.\n- **The destination may be a POINT on that island, often mid-trace.** The owner's model: once U1.1 -> U2.1 is routed, that trace becomes a \"wide pin component\", and U3.1 connects to a virtual point on it. Geometrically this is point-to-segment nearest-point (a T-junction tap), not point-to-point. **Ship named-pad targets FIRST; mid-trace taps only for nets marked tap-anywhere** (see section 4).\n- **Airwires re-aim as copper lands.** After U1-U2 exists, U3.1's target becomes the nearest point on that new trace — likely closer and in a different direction.\n- **Exactly ONE suggestion. Owner ruling: no ranked list, no picker.**\n- **Tie-break must be DETERMINISTIC, not a coin toss.** Lowest refdes or lowest coordinate. A coin toss makes the same board answer differently between sessions, and the view flickers for no actionable reason.\n- **Two states the answer must get right or it becomes untrustworthy**: a pad served by a pour is \"nothing to route — the plane covers this\", not an airwire to a distant pad; and a pad already joined by copper to its net disappears entirely rather than showing a line already drawn.\n- **The suggestion is guidance, not enforcement** — unless a topology constraint exists on that net.\n\n## Rails in the resting view\n\nDim or collapse high-component-fanout distribution nets, but **never silently hide them**. Label with the UNRESOLVED count, not the net size — \"GND · 27 unresolved\" tells the designer work remaining; \"GND · 29 parts\" tells them nothing actionable. Selecting one of their pads must still reveal its single next instruction.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nThe accretion process the owner described is Prim's algorithm: grow one island, always take the nearest thing not yet joined. This exposes a fork worth choosing deliberately:\n\n- taking the **globally** nearest pair minimizes total copper;\n- taking the nearest thing **to where you are already working** minimizes hand movement and avoids jumping across the board between traces.\n\nThese give different orders and different totals. Hand routing wants the second. Recommendation: default next-suggestion to locality; let total length lose.\n\n# 4. When is a T-tap bad\n\n**The core answer: geometric DRC passes all of these.** DRC checks clearance, width, annular ring, hole-to-copper. Every failure mode below is a TOPOLOGY failure. The board fabricates perfectly and behaves wrong. (Evidence: this board's geometric DRC returns clean while 24 nets are entirely unrouted — that is what it is and is not measuring.)\n\n1. **Sense / feedback lines.** Must connect at one specific point; tapping anywhere convenient silently regulates the wrong node. **Live on this board**: U4.1 sits on +3V3 and on a fixed-output buck that pin is feedback sense, not the power outlet (READ FROM THE NETLIST, NOT THE DATASHEET — confirm against the AP63203 datasheet before relying on it). A tool suggesting a tap into the nearest +3V3 copper near the inductor would sense the wrong point and lose regulation under load. Nothing objects until the bench.\n2. **Stubs on fast edges.** A tap leaves a stub; a stub reflects once it is a meaningful fraction of the edge rate's wavelength. Requires rise-time knowledge, which is not in the netlist. Nothing on this board is fast enough to care.\n3. **Shared current paths.** Tap a branch off a power trace and the shared segment carries the sum of both loads. Requires current expectations, also not in the netlist.\n4. **Matched or paired nets.** Tapping one leg of a differential pair breaks the match. Detectable only if the tool knows they are a pair.\n\n## Consequence for the tool: a THIRD net category\n\n- **Rails** — terminate on, never transit; tap anywhere; usually a pour.\n- **Ordinary signals** — tap anywhere.\n- **Constrained nets** — topology carries meaning (feedback/Kelvin, differential, high-speed, high-current). The tool must REFUSE to suggest a mid-trace tap and insist the endpoint be a named pad; it may also forbid branching entirely.\n\nThis classification cannot come from fanout or any structural test; it is design intent. But the tool need not guess it to be safe: **defaulting to pad-endpoints-only for any net not explicitly marked tap-anywhere fails in the harmless direction**, and pours already cover the case where tapping matters most. This is also why named-pad targets ship before mid-trace taps.\n\n## How a designer would actually find out\n\n- **Before** — encode intent as a per-net constraint (\"Kelvin, connect only at C10\"; \"max stub 5mm\") and check it. The gap is that nobody authors these; they live in datasheets and in heads.\n- **After** — the scope or the bench. Expensive.\n- **Practical middle** — the datasheet's own layout-guidance section. Every buck datasheet has one; it states where FB connects and how tight the input loop must be. Closest thing to an oracle available today.\n\n# 5. Other failure classes that pass DRC and still break a board\n\nRaised by the agent, not by the owner. All are invisible to geometric DRC.\n\n1. **No reference plane / undefined return path.** The GND pour on this board was deleted (deliberately). Every high-speed or switching signal's return current then takes an arbitrary path, raising loop area and EMI. On a 2-layer board the bottom pour normally IS the return path; routing signals as though a plane exists when it does not is a silent, board-wide defect.\n2. **Return-path discontinuity.** Even with a plane, routing a signal across a split or a slot in the reference forces the return current around it. Classic EMI failure; DRC is silent.\n3. **Shared ground between analog and digital.** This board has an audio amp (U2) and a mic/codec (U3) alongside a switching regulator. Amp return currents through shared ground inject noise into the mic path. A topology question (star vs daisy vs partitioned plane), invisible to any geometric check.\n4. **Buck input loop area.** Cin -> VIN -> SW -> GND is the high-di/dt loop; its physical area is what radiates. C7 (VIN) and C12 (VIN bulk) must sit tight against U4's VIN/GND pins. Placement-and-routing coupled, checked by nothing.\n5. **Trace width vs current.** Geometric DRC checks a MINIMUM width, never whether width suits the current. See section 6 — this is live on this board right now.\n6. **Via current capacity.** A single via carries far less current than the trace feeding it. Power nets crossing layers need multiple stitched vias; one via is a thermal and reliability defect that passes every check.\n7. **Antenna keepout.** U1S is an ESP32-class module with an integrated antenna. Copper under or near the antenna detunes it and kills range. This board carries two keepout zones (top and bottom, 4 points each) which are plausibly for exactly this — routing through them fabricates fine and produces a radio that does not work.\n8. **Thermal relief vs solid pad connection.** Solid connections to a plane wick heat during hand soldering and cause cold joints; thermal reliefs restrict current. Wrong choice either fails assembly or overheats in service.\n9. **Floating CMOS inputs.** An unconnected high-impedance input oscillates and draws current. Nets exist for pins that are NC by design; nothing distinguishes \"intentionally NC\" from \"forgotten\".\n10. **Via-in-pad without fill.** Solder wicks down the via during reflow, starving the joint.\n11. **Acid traps / acute copper angles.** Manufacturing defect class; some fabs check, this DRC does not.\n12. **Mounting-hole clearance.** Screw heads and standoffs short nearby copper. A mechanical constraint expressed nowhere in the netlist.\n\n# 6. Live finding on this board (needs its own item)\n\nThe four VIN traces committed during this session landed at **0.25 mm** (`width_source: board_rules`), while every pre-existing VIN trace on the board is **0.8 mm** (measured via `minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry`). VIN is the input path to a 2 A buck. A 0.25 mm input trace is plausibly undersized and is certainly inconsistent with the rest of the net.\n\nSeparately, that same export now reports 18 traces with only ONE of the four committed traces present. The commit reply reported four trace ids (trace_8..trace_11). The discrepancy is unexplained and must be diagnosed before anything else is committed to this board — do not treat the commit report as ground truth.\n\n# 7. Open questions\n\n1. Does the \"connectors terminate walks\" toggle default ON or OFF? *(Comprehension story only — does not block routing work.)*\n2. What does the shell view do about hub components (U1S at degree 1 from half the board)? *(Comprehension story only.)*\n3. Does the rail threshold survive a second board, or do the fanout ranges overlap there?\n4. Who authors the constrained-net list, and where does it live so both the panel and agents read one copy?\n5. Does the trace tool already hit-test pads (needed for decision 2)? Verify before building step 2.","created_at":"2026-08-22T22:42:42","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:19:33","tags":["degree","design-intent","pcb","ratsnest","routing","ux"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bc4f8007e0daa1f3a65267ab3c1","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Proposed routes take the board default width, ignoring the net's established width","description":"WHAT IS WRONG\nA route proposed without an explicit width takes the board default rather than the width already established on that net, producing a trace far narrower than the copper it joins. On a power net this is a current-carrying defect that no gate catches.\n\nWHERE\n`minerva_pcb_workspace_propose` / `workspace_commit`; the candidate records carry `width_source: \"board_rules\"` when no width is supplied on the span. Measured on smart-remote v2 via `minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry`: routes proposed onto VIN committed at **0.25 mm**, while every pre-existing VIN trace on that board is **0.8 mm**. VBAT/VBAT_F are 1.0 mm, BST is 0.4 mm — so the board clearly carries per-net width intent that the propose path does not read.\n\nCONSEQUENCE\nSilent and physical. VIN is the input path to a 2 A buck (U4, AP63203); a 0.25 mm input trace is plausibly undersized for it. Geometric DRC passes because GC1 checks a MINIMUM width, never whether the width suits the current — so nothing objects at propose, commit, DRC or fabrication. The board is manufacturable and wrong. This is failure class 5 in the child discussion of DCR 01a02b570e4d (\"trace width vs current\").\n\nThe second-order harm is worse than the first: an agent proposing routes onto power nets will keep producing undersized copper, and each instance looks identical to a correct one in every report the tool emits.\n\nACCEPTANCE\nA route proposed onto a net that already carries copper adopts that net's established width, or refuses and says why. The observable: propose a span on a net whose existing traces are 0.8 mm with no width argument, and the candidate reports 0.8 mm — not 0.25 mm with `width_source: board_rules`.\n\nThe criterion a lazy fix fails: simply raising the board default would make this span correct and every thin-signal-net span wrong. The width must come from the NET, not from a different global.\n\nTRAPS\n- A net with mixed existing widths has no single answer; decide whether that is \"widest wins\", \"nearest segment wins\", or a refusal, and say which.\n- A net with no existing copper has nothing to inherit — the board default is genuinely correct there, so the fix must not make a fresh net an error.\n- `width_source` already exists as a field and reports honestly; the defect is which source is consulted, not the reporting.\n\nLINKS\nSurfaced while dispatching DCR 01a02b570e4d. Related failure taxonomy: discussion 01a02ba3fd1e section 5.","created_at":"2026-08-22T23:18:43","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:38:11","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","power","routing","silent-defect","width"],"resolution":"A route proposed onto a net that already carries copper now adopts that net's established width, widest-wins on a mixed net, composed as a max with the net-class minimum. Both halves were needed: the worker resolved from the board default, and the panel ignored the worker's resolved width entirely when creating copper. Scoped worker suites 396/0. Pushed in 562c770."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bc53fcf71b08a1dfca90db0d807","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"NOT A DEFECT — workspace_commit reported four trace ids correctly; the copper was deleted by hand four minutes later","description":"## Verdict\n\n**`minerva_pcb_workspace_commit` behaved correctly.** All four traces were written to the board. The export disagreed because the copper was removed by hand in the UI between the two measurements. The defect was in the method that filed this bug, not in the tool.\n\n## Evidence\n\n`minerva_pcb_get_change_journal` on editor \"Smart Remote v2\" (27 entries, the board's full forward log) reconciles the two surfaces exactly:\n\n```\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_8   (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_9   (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_10  (2 segments)\n13:49:00  add_trace    VIN  trace_11  (4 segments)\n          ... 4 minutes ...\n13:53:00  remove_trace VIN  trace_8\n13:53:03  remove_trace VIN  trace_10\n13:53:05  remove_trace VIN  trace_9\n13:53:07  remove_trace VIN  trace_11\n13:53:12  rotate_component  C12  90° -> 0°\n13:53:14  move_component    C12  (66.0,62.5) -> (65.5,64.5)\n13:53:23  add_trace    VIN  trace:b015f142...  (1 segment)\n13:53:28  move_component    C12  -> (65.0,63.5)\n13:53:29  move_trace   trace:b015f142...  +0.05mm\n13:53:31  remove_trace trace:b015f142...\n   ... two more hand-drawn/adjusted/discarded single-segment traces ...\n13:54:28  add_trace    VIN  trace:ea3544a9...  (1 segment)  <- the only VIN 0.25mm\n                                                              trace in the export\n```\n\nThe four `add_trace` entries carry the exact segment counts of the four committed candidates (cand_3=2, cand_5=2, cand_7=2, cand_9=4). The commit reply's ids and counts were accurate at the moment it returned.\n\nThe removals are interleaved with `rotate_component`, `move_component`, and `move_trace` nudges of 0.05 mm, spaced 2-3.5 seconds apart. That is an interactive editing session — a person deleting the four routes, re-orienting C12, and hand-drawing replacements — not a tool path.\n\nCode review agrees. In `RoutingWorkspace._commit_batch` (`ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd:2375-2410`) each `board.add_trace` is followed by a `board.get_trace(tid) == null` check that rolls the whole batch back if the board accepted a trace and then could not find it. A commit that returns `ok` has already proved every trace is present.\n\n## Where the original report was also wrong\n\nIt claimed the surviving 0.25 mm VIN trace \"matches candidate cand_3\". It does not. cand_3 is a two-segment L — (66.0,63.975) -> (70.5,63.975) -> (70.5,63.45). The surviving trace `trace:ea3544a9...` is a single segment (70.5,63.45) -> (66.475,63.4787), drawn by hand at 13:54:28. **No committed candidate's geometry survives on the board.** The \"one of four survived\" framing was a coincidence of net, width and rough position — not an identification.\n\n## The real lesson\n\nTwo surfaces disagreed and I reached for a defect in the one that reported success, without consulting the append-only log that records what happened between them. The change journal was available the whole time and answers the question in one call. A discrepancy between two readings taken minutes apart on a board a human is actively editing is not evidence of a lying tool.\n\nSeverity 1 was assigned on the reasoning that \"a commit reply is the evidence an agent uses to report this landed.\" That reasoning was sound; the premise it rested on was unmeasured.\n\n## What this DOES corroborate\n\nThe four deleted traces were 0.25 mm on VIN — the exact defect in bug 01a02bc4f800. The owner deleted all four within four minutes and hand-routed a replacement. That is independent confirmation that the width defect produces copper a designer rejects on sight.\n\n## What it left behind\n\nOne real defect, filed separately: deleting committed copper leaves the routing workspace believing it is still routed. The four candidates still report `disposition: committed` with `committed_trace_ids: [trace_8..trace_11]`, and their tasks still report `closed`, though none of that copper exists.\n\n## Consequence for the board\n\nNo new commits were blocked on the wrong grounds for long, but the standing instruction \"do not commit anything else to that board until explained\" is **lifted** — the board is in the state the owner hand-edited it into, and the tool is trustworthy.","created_at":"2026-08-22T23:19:02","updated_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:06","priority":1,"severity":4,"tags":["commit","diagnosed","method-failure","not-a-defect","pcb","routing"],"resolution":"Not a defect. The change journal shows all four traces were written by the commit and removed by hand in the UI four minutes later, interleaved with a C12 rotate/move and hand-drawn replacements. The commit reply was accurate; the export was taken after unrelated interactive edits. Real residue filed separately as the workspace-staleness bug."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02bf972247712b3f472fde5dbd52f","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Deleting committed copper leaves the routing workspace believing the span is still routed","description":"## What is wrong\n\nRemoving a trace from the board does not tell the routing workspace. A candidate whose committed copper has been deleted keeps `disposition: committed`, keeps `committed_trace_ids` naming trace ids that no longer exist, and its routing task stays `closed`. The workspace reports the span as routed when there is no copper.\n\n## Measured\n\nOn editor \"Smart Remote v2\", `minerva_pcb_workspace_list include_terminal:true net:VIN` currently returns:\n\n- cand_3 — `disposition: committed`, `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_8\"]`, task `VIN|ann_0021` state `closed`\n- cand_5 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_9\"]`, task closed\n- cand_7 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_10\"]`, task closed\n- cand_9 — `committed_trace_ids: [\"trace_11\"]`, task closed\n\n`minerva_pcb_export_trace_geometry` on the same editor returns 18 traces and **none of trace_8..trace_11 is among them**. The change journal shows all four removed at 13:53 on 2026-08-22.\n\nThe `validation` field did go `stale` on all four — but that reports the DRC verdict's freshness, not whether the copper exists. Nothing distinguishes \"this route is committed and on the board\" from \"this route is committed and someone deleted it.\"\n\n## Where\n\n`PCBData.remove_trace` (`ui/model/pcb_data.gd:819`) erases the trace and emits `trace_changed` / `data_changed`. It does not notify the bound routing workspace. `PCBData.bind_routing_workspace` (`:2487`) exists but its only use is workspace snapshotting for undo.\n\n`RoutingWorkspace.uncommit` (`ui/model/pcb_routing_workspace.gd:1424`) is the function that would repair this — it reverts the candidate to its prior disposition, clears the copper ids, and reopens the task, with a docstring saying \"the copper is gone → the task reopens\". **It has no caller.** Grep of `ui/` and `tests/gd/` finds only its definition and comments describing when it ought to fire. Undo does not call it either: undo restores the whole workspace layer from the snapshot attached at commit (`_restore_workspace_snapshot`, `pcb_data.gd:2813`), which is a coherent design and explains why the gap went unnoticed — the undo path is covered, the plain-deletion path is not.\n\n## Consequence\n\nQuiet and cumulative. The workspace is the surface that answers \"what still needs routing\", and the DCR 01a02b570e4d ratsnest work is going to lean on exactly that answer. A closed task over deleted copper is an unrouted span that no surface reports as unrouted. It fails in the reassuring direction: the board looks more finished than it is.\n\nIt also mints unreachable state. A candidate stuck at `committed` cannot be re-committed — `_commit_batch` refuses with \"already committed — its copper is on the board; undo the commit (or uncommit it) before committing again\" (`:2167`), and since nothing calls `uncommit`, there is no way to take that advice. The span cannot be re-routed through its own candidate.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nDeleting a trace that a committed candidate owns reopens that candidate's task and stops the workspace reporting the span as routed. The observable: commit a candidate, delete its trace, then `workspace_list` — the task must not still read `closed` with `committed_trace_ids` naming a trace the export cannot find.\n\nThe criterion a lazy fix fails: filtering dead ids out of `committed_trace_ids` at read time would make the listing look consistent while leaving the task closed and the candidate unre-committable. The task state is the part that matters.\n\n## Traps\n\n- Undo already works via snapshot restore. A fix that also fires on undo would double-apply — establish which path is running before compensating.\n- Partial deletion is a real case: a candidate can own several traces and lose only one. Decide whether that reopens the task or reports a partial state, and say which.\n- `uncommit` may be the right seam or may be the wrong shape for this; it was written for a caller that never arrived. Do not assume calling it from `remove_trace` is correct without checking its reentrancy guard against the batch-commit transaction flag.\n\n## Links\n\nSurfaced by the investigation of bug 01a02bc53fcf, which turned out to be no defect at all — this is its only real residue. Relevant to DCR 01a02b570e4d, whose renderer work depends on an honest answer to \"what is still unrouted\".","created_at":"2026-08-23T00:16:03","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:38:13","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","routing","staleness","trust","workspace"],"resolution":"Deleting copper a committed candidate owns now retires that commit and reopens its task, asked from outside against the board rather than hooked to trace removal (which is also the rollback path's tool and runs with the commit transaction open). Any missing owned id retires the commit; surviving copper is left alone. Wired into the delete tools' own history step so undo and redo move copper and commit together. Scoped GD suites green. Pushed in 562c770."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c4740907858a6e148a8c7c04364","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Codex re-review, round 4: ring area at scale, canonical witnesses, via-free tie preference","description":"## What this is\n\nFourth round on the ratsnest connectivity model, repairing the three findings in comment 62.\n\nRepository `minerva-plugins`, branch `main`. **Read `git diff 9454c8c..HEAD`** for round 4 alone — `9454c8c` is the commit you reviewed. Ignore `docs/plugins.dct`.\n\n## Evidence\n\nSuite: **95 passed, 0 failed** under Godot 4.6.2, re-pinned in the manifest from that run. Independently re-run here after the commit, same result.\n\nAgainst the previous model: **90 passed, 5 failed** — the five failures are exactly the three defects' new checks, and all 80 pre-existing assertions still pass. Canvas suites re-run: input probe 123, candidate canvas 281, zero failures.\n\n## Per finding\n\n**1. Ring area.** Coordinates are now collected into double-precision arrays and the area judged on them, before any reduction to the engine's vector type. Every coordinate is taken relative to the ring's first point, so both the shoelace terms and their float noise scale with the ring's own extent rather than with its distance from the origin. The cutoff itself is unchanged. Only after the verdict is the ring reduced for geometry use.\n\nYour four-point collinear ring is pinned as a solver-level false-merge regression — the assertion is on `remaining`, not on a helper's return.\n\n**2. Witness canonicalisation.** Every segment is handed to the closest-points primitive with its endpoints in a fixed lexicographic order. A segment is the same point set either way, so the pair the primitive picks out of a continuum becomes a function of the geometry. All six segment-measurement call sites route through the one helper, boolean touch tests included, so touching and aiming are measured with the same instrument.\n\nYour overlapping top/bottom case is pinned with all four flip spellings.\n\n**One measurement worth your attention:** flipping only the *top* trace happened not to move the witness on this engine build; flipping the bottom one did. All spellings are pinned regardless, so the invariant holds whichever branch of the engine's parallel-segment handling a future build takes. If that suggests the canonicalisation is only masking a build-specific behaviour rather than removing the dependence, say so.\n\n**3. Tie-break order.** Now length, then witness points, then layer change with no-change winning, then refs. Your coincident SMD-plus-through-hole island is pinned, run with both reference orderings so the result cannot come from spelling.\n\n## What to check\n\n- Is relative-to-first-vertex enough, or does a ring whose own extent is large relative to the cutoff still admit a degenerate case?\n- Does fixing endpoint order genuinely remove the continuum dependence, or only relocate it — is there a configuration where the primitive's choice still varies with something other than the geometry?\n- Does preferring no-layer-change before refs introduce a case where the chosen join is worse on some other axis?\n- Anything the four rounds have collectively missed.\n\n## Standing exclusions\n\nBugs 01a02bc4f800 and 01a02bf97224 remain out of scope and green. Closed and not to be re-litigated: the coincidence tolerance, the quieting thresholds, pad shape modelling, pad-local rotation, whole-fill rejection, and the structural fill validation.\n\n## Known and tracked, not for this review\n\nFill reaches the panel from nowhere (work item 01a02c754dc5), so real boards take the no-connection path. `corner_rratio` is dropped by the pad codecs (01a02c7568a0). The canvas render ignores pad-local rotation (bug 01a02c596899).\n\n## Note on convergence\n\nThis is the fourth implementation round and the third review. Severity has fallen each time and every finding you have raised has been real and reproducible. If what remains after this pass is narrow enough to ship as tracked known defects rather than blockers, say so explicitly — that is a useful answer and the owner will decide on it.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:02","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:15:58","directed_to":"Codex","priority":2,"tags":["connectivity","cross-provider-review","pcb","ratsnest"],"resolution":"Round 4 implemented and ready for Fable review. See Codex comment 65.","answer":"Round 4 implementation completed in the working tree; see Codex comment 65. The three 9b14ad1 repairs are sound. I fixed one additional in-scope fail-open case where finite Float64 fill coordinates overflowed to infinite Vector2 geometry and could erase an airwire. A/B evidence: 95/1 before the guard, 96/0 after; manifest re-pinned to 96; no commit. Broader duplicate-pad modeling defect filed separately as 01a02cd46ac1 and left untouched for separate scope."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c47aa7478f6a38ac23acf6d7a08","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Do code comments carry ticket IDs? The task-cycle standard and the codebase disagree","description":"## The conflict\n\nThe task-cycle skill's review standard states: comments must describe mechanism or a non-obvious code constraint, and **must not contain dates, requesters, ticket IDs, review history, or project rationale**.\n\nThe pcb plugin's existing comments violate that pervasively and deliberately — `docket 019fd0ab5af8`, `Codex review P1`, `HITL-7a (019fe0391d06)`, `owner ruling this round`, `Epoch NLC C4 (1135 -> 1136)`, `SR2FAB S6`. These are load-bearing to the extent that they let a reader find why a non-obvious choice was made.\n\nThe cold review of batch `e45e1ff..c2de619` flagged this and explicitly declined to demand a fix, because the new comments match the surrounding code exactly.\n\n## Why it needs deciding\n\nThe cost of rewriting one batch's comments to the standard is that the new code becomes the odd one out, which is worse for a reader than either convention applied consistently. So this cannot be settled inside a diff; it is a repo-wide call.\n\n## The options\n\n1. **Standard wins, repo-wide.** Strip identifiers from comments over time. The rationale moves to commit messages and the tracker, where it is already duplicated. Cost: a large mechanical sweep, and a reader loses the in-file jump-off point.\n2. **Convention wins; amend the skill.** Permit an identifier when it points at reasoning a comment cannot restate in a sentence. Cost: the standard's teeth were aimed at comments that are pure archaeology, and that boundary would need stating.\n3. **Split the rule.** Ban dates, requesters and review history; permit a tracker ID. Cost: a reviewer has to judge the boundary every time.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nOption 3. What the standard is really protecting against is a comment that records WHO wanted something and WHEN, which rots immediately and helps nobody. A tracker ID is a stable pointer to reasoning too long for the comment, and this codebase has demonstrably used it that way for a long time. Dates and reviewer names in comments are the part actually worth banning.\n\nNot acted on either way. Batch `e45e1ff..c2de619` follows the existing convention.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:29","updated_at":"2026-08-23T01:47:27","directed_to":"owner","priority":3,"tags":["comments","convention","owner-decision","task-cycle"],"answer":"OWNER RULING, 2026-08-22: **Option 1 — the standard wins.** Comments must be salient and describe only how the code works. Ticket IDs, review history, dates, requesters and project rationale do not belong in source.\n\nMy recommendation of option 3 (permit a bare tracker ID as a stable pointer) was rejected.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n1. **Existing comments get fixed later.** The repo-wide sweep of the pre-existing convention — `docket 019fd0ab5af8`, `Codex review P1`, `HITL-7a`, `Epoch NLC C4` and the rest — is acknowledged as work to be scheduled, not done now, and not a reason to leave new work non-conforming in the meantime.\n\n2. **New work conforms immediately.** The comments introduced by batch e45e1ff..HEAD are being rewritten now rather than deferred with the rest. The cold review's finding was correct as written.\n\nThe rationale itself is not lost — it moves to commit messages and to this tracker, where it was already duplicated."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c47c6587be1be1f27ce58086344","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"get_pad_world_transform rotates the pad offset the opposite way from every other pad reader","description":"## What is wrong\n\n`pcb_component.get_pad_world_transform()` rotates the pad offset by `deg_to_rad(+rotation)`. `get_transform()` and the canvas pad render both use `deg_to_rad(-rotation)` — the KiCad convention, defended at length in a comment in the same file.\n\nTwo \"where is this pad\" readers in one file with opposite signs.\n\n## Why it has not bitten\n\n`get_pad_world_transform()` currently has **zero callers**. It is latent, not live.\n\n## Consequence when it is called\n\nSilently wrong pad positions on any component rotated 90° or 270°, in a function whose name promises the authoritative answer. Nothing would fail loudly; copper would simply land in the wrong place, and the wrongness is invisible on 0°/180° parts, which is most of a typical board — so the first caller is likely to test on parts that cannot reveal it.\n\n## Found by\n\nThe ratsnest implementer (work item 01a02b5763e8), who needed pad geometry and chose the render path deliberately. The rotated-part fixture in `test_pcb_ratsnest.gd` pins that choice, so the correct convention now has a test behind it even though this function does not.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nThe two readers agree, or the divergent one is deleted. Deleting is worth considering seriously given it has no callers — an unused function with a subtly wrong convention is a trap with no benefit.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:36","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:29","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["geometry","latent","pcb","rotation"],"resolution":"Moot: get_pad_world_transform was removed (PLG 01a03805b8b6), so no pad reader rotates the opposite way. Docket cleanup 2026-08-25."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c47f5017bbc8e0910a67b0fc5a4","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Re-committing a partly-lost candidate can lay duplicate copper over the survivors","description":"## What is wrong\n\nAfter the copper-loss reconcile (bug 01a02bf97224) retires a candidate that lost only SOME of its traces, the candidate returns live carrying geometry that partly still exists as copper on the board. `_commit_preflight` does not refuse on stale validation, so committing it again lays a second copy of the surviving segments on top of the first.\n\n## Reachability\n\nThis path already existed via a manual `uncommit` then `commit`. The reconcile does not create it, but it makes it ordinary: partial loss is exactly the \"deleted one leg and redrew it by hand\" case, and the natural next move is to re-commit.\n\n## Consequence\n\nOverlapping same-net copper. Geometric DRC will not object — coincident copper on one net is not a clearance violation — so it reaches fabrication as extra copper that does no harm electrically but is wrong in the artifact, and any length or area report double-counts it.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nCommitting a candidate whose net already carries copper matching part of its geometry either refuses with a reason, or emits only the missing spans. The observable: commit, delete one of several traces, re-commit — the board gains only what was missing.\n\n## Traps\n\n- Refusing on ANY overlap would block the legitimate case of two candidates that share an endpoint pad; the overlap that matters is a segment, not a point.\n- The candidate's stale verdict is a signal here, but validation staleness and geometry overlap are different questions — do not conflate them.\n\nFiled by the task-2 implementer of batch e45e1ff..c2de619, not fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:48","updated_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:48","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["duplicate-copper","pcb","routing","workspace"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c480d507e37a28b9fce67e02ad8","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"A hard-coded 0.25mm literal is still the last-resort width on the copper-creating path","description":"## What is wrong\n\nBoth panel paths that turn a route reply into copper fall back to a literal `0.25` when the reply carries no width at all — an older worker, or a path that skipped the width attach.\n\nBug 01a02bc4f800 removed the case where this fired routinely: the width now comes from the router's own resolved stamp, which itself consults the net's established copper. But the literal remains as the last resort, and it is an invented number on the one path that writes physical copper.\n\n## Consequence\n\nNarrow. It only fires on a reply with no width stamp, which the current worker always provides. The harm is that it is silent: a board would gain 0.25mm copper with nothing in any report saying the width was invented rather than resolved.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nThe copper-creating path fails closed when it has no width to use, naming what it could not resolve, rather than inventing one. Consistent with the fail-closed stance taken elsewhere in the same fix.\n\nFiled by the task-1 implementer of batch e45e1ff..c2de619, not fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:41:54","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:03","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["invented-default","pcb","routing","width"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. No invented width on the copper path: reply stamp → hint → record → net copper → design_rules, else unmodelable_segment naming the net; default-sourced stamps never beat an authored hint."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c4d5bcf7a4ebf9a77bd14dfcd06","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Sweep pre-existing comments to the salience standard — they cause real defects","description":"## What\n\nComments must describe only how the code works. No ticket identifiers, no reasoning about rejected alternatives, no review history, no dates, no requesters, no project rationale. New work conforms from now on. Existing comments are this item.\n\n## Why this is priority 1, not tidy-up\n\n**Non-salient comments have cost this project months of work.** The mechanism, stated by the owner: an agent reads a comment as ground truth, cross-checks it against the code, finds something that does not match, drifts off the actual behaviour, and then delivers an error confidently. Bug IDs and embedded reasoning are the worst offenders precisely because they invite that cross-check — they look like a citation, so they get trusted and followed.\n\nThis is a correctness problem with a long fuse, not a readability problem. A stale comment does not confuse a reader into asking a question; it persuades a reader into making a wrong change.\n\n## Scale\n\nThe pcb plugin carries this pervasively and by long habit: `docket 019fd0ab5af8`, `Codex review P1`, `HITL-7a (019fe0391d06)`, `Epoch NLC C4 (1135 -> 1136)`, `SR2FAB S6`, `owner ruling this round`, `HITL-5 lesson mechanized`, plus long passages arguing why one approach was chosen over another. It is in source, tests, and data files such as `tests/gd/EXPECTED_SUITES`. Large sweep.\n\n## The test to apply\n\nIf a sentence could become FALSE without any code changing — it describes an intention, a comparison, a decision, or a history — it goes. If it can only become false when the code changes, it can stay.\n\nKeep: what a function does; what inputs and outputs mean; what a fallback means when it fires; which of two similar functions is authoritative; what invariant a comparator must satisfy; a behavioural constraint stated flatly. Cut: identifiers, round names, people, dates, \"chosen because\", \"X would be worse\", and any weighing of rejected alternatives.\n\n**Prefer deleting to rewriting when unsure.** A missing comment costs a reader a minute; a wrong one is what this item exists to stop.\n\n## Traps\n\n- **The dangerous comments are the mixed ones.** Much of this codebase's load-bearing explanation arrived attached to a review round. A regex pass would delete the behavioural fact along with the label, and that loss is itself a defect source. This is judgement per comment.\n- **No behaviour may change.** Verify the diff is comment-only rather than asserting it.\n- Slice it into reviewable commits. One repo-wide comment diff cannot be reviewed and will be rubber-stamped, which defeats the point.\n\n## Related\n\nRuling recorded at question 01a02c47aa74. Batch e45e1ff..HEAD was corrected under it at the time of the ruling.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:47:42","updated_at":"2026-08-23T01:53:01","priority":1,"tags":["cleanup","comments","convention","deferred"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c4d7f0e75ce8c075f2e46e3f6f2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Ratsnest re-extracts and re-hashes the whole board on every redraw frame","description":"## What\n\nThe ratsnest solve is cached, and the cache key is a hash of the solver's complete input. That choice is deliberate and good: there is no second hand-written summary of \"what counts as a change\" to drift from what the solver actually reads, and it catches a live drag, which moves component positions without bumping the board revision.\n\nThe cost is that computing the key requires extracting the whole board's copper and hashing it, and that runs on **every redraw frame** — including every frame of a pan or a zoom, when nothing changed.\n\n## Consequence\n\nNot a correctness defect; the picture is always right. It is a frame-time cost proportional to board size, paid continuously during the interaction where smoothness matters most. Unmeasured — it may be negligible on real boards, or it may be the reason a large board feels heavy to pan.\n\n## What to do\n\nMeasure first, on a real board of representative size. If the cost is real, the fix must not reintroduce the failure the hash was chosen to avoid: a cheaper key that summarises the board by hand can go stale in exactly the case the current key catches. A revision counter that also ticks on live drags would be a sound cheaper key; a revision counter that does not is the trap.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nEither a measurement showing the per-frame cost is immaterial and this is closed as not worth it, or a cheaper key that provably invalidates in every case the current one does — including a live component drag.\n\nFound by cold review of batch e45e1ff..c2de619.","created_at":"2026-08-23T01:47:51","updated_at":"2026-08-23T01:47:51","priority":3,"tags":["canvas","pcb","performance","ratsnest"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c596899763f927b5734947a9cc9","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Pad local rotation is emitted by the worker and dropped by the panel decoder","description":"## What is wrong\n\nA resolved pad carries a local rotation, distinct from its component's rotation. The worker emits it at `pcb/worker/pcb_worker/resolve.py:446-451`. The panel's component decoder at `pcb/ui/model/pcb_component.gd:891-899` does not read it, so the field does not survive into the panel model. Every panel-side consumer of pad geometry therefore composes only the component transform.\n\nMeasured by probe: `pad_local_rotation_survives_decode = false`.\n\n## Consequence\n\nThe panel's idea of a pad's shape differs from the fabricated copper whenever a footprint rotates a pad locally. A 2.0 x 0.5 mm SMD pad with local rotation 90 degrees is vertical on the board and horizontal in the model.\n\nBoth directions are wrong and both are silent:\n- copper touching the phantom horizontal end reads as connected when it is not;\n- copper landing on the real vertical end reads as unconnected when it is.\n\n## Why it is now urgent\n\nIt was latent while nothing consumed pad geometry for connectivity. The ratsnest rewrite (work item 01a02b5763e8) calls the pad-node builder on every solve, so an actively-used connectivity answer is now derived from the wrong shape. That is the reason this is priority 1 rather than a curiosity.\n\nIt is a DIFFERENT defect from the zero-caller sign mismatch in `get_pad_world_transform()` (bug 01a02c47c658). That one is dormant; this one is on a live path.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nA pad with a non-zero local rotation decodes with that rotation preserved and round-trips, and pad polygons compose pad-local rotation with component rotation. The observable: build a footprint with a 2.0 x 0.5 mm pad at local rotation 90, decode it, and the pad's long axis is vertical in the panel model.\n\n## Traps\n\n- Composition order matters and is easy to get backwards. Pin it with a fixture whose correct answer differs under the two orderings, not with a square pad that cannot tell them apart.\n- The component-rotation sign convention in this file is deliberate and defended; do not \"fix\" it while adding the pad term.\n\n## Found by\n\nCodex cross-provider review of batch e45e1ff..HEAD, finding 3. Reproduced by direct decode probe, not inferred.","created_at":"2026-08-23T02:00:52","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:25","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["decode","footprint","geometry","pcb","rotation"],"resolution":"Fixed in 7e66487; the panel now carries pad rotation, which is what exposed the still-open PLG 01a0380585 (rendering ignores it). Docket cleanup 2026-08-25."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c754dc5750195b2acc1dd5da5f1","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Zone fill regions never reach the panel, so no real board gets pour connectivity","description":"## What\n\nThe ratsnest now treats a pour as its compiled fill: each separately filled region is its own conductor, and a pour whose fill is unavailable contributes no connection at all. That is the correct model and the safe fallback.\n\n**No channel currently delivers fill from the worker to the panel.** The solver reads `zone[\"fill\"]` — an array of regions in the outline's own point encoding — and zone dicts pass through `_zones_from_list` verbatim, so the key would survive if it were ever set. Nothing sets it.\n\n## Consequence\n\nEvery real board today takes the fallback: pours contribute nothing, so a pad genuinely served by a plane still shows an airwire. Quieting collapses most of that noise on a distribution net, but the parent DCR's rule that \"a pad served by a pour is nothing to route\" is not yet delivered — it is merely not violated in the dangerous direction.\n\nThis is the deliberate interim, not a defect. It becomes a defect if it is forgotten, because the ratsnest will quietly overstate remaining work on every poured board and nobody will see a wrong answer to investigate — only more airwires than necessary.\n\n## What to do\n\nCarry `ResolvedZone.fill` through to the panel's zone dicts. The consumer already exists and is tested; this is a plumbing task, and the tests in `test_pcb_ratsnest.gd` section 7 already pin the behaviour for both the one-region and severed-into-two cases.\n\n## Traps\n\n- **The fingerprint.** `pcb_routing_sidecar.gd`'s projection excludes a zone's `fill`. Once fill rides the zone dict, a fill-only change will not stale routing candidates. Fill is derived from the outline, so this is probably right — but decide it deliberately rather than discovering it.\n- Fill is derived state. Decide whether the panel stores it, recomputes it, or treats it as cache, before wiring it — a stale fill is a wrong connectivity answer in the dangerous direction, which is the whole thing the current fallback avoids.\n\nSurfaced by the round-2 implementer of work item 01a02b5763e8.","created_at":"2026-08-23T02:31:20","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:13","priority":1,"tags":["connectivity","fill","pcb","ratsnest","zones"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02c7568a070d59df7f3cd16935864","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Pad codecs drop corner_rratio, raw_shape and the margin fields the worker emits","description":"## What\n\nThe panel's pad codecs carry number, type, shape, position, size, drill, layers and now rotation. The worker emits more: `corner_rratio`, `raw_shape`, and the solder-mask / paste margin fields. Those are dropped on decode.\n\n## Consequence today\n\nThe ratsnest models a roundrect land by its inscribed stadium — the maximum-corner-radius roundrect, which is contained by every roundrect of that size. That is deliberately conservative and cannot claim a connection the copper does not make, but it under-reports the land, so copper genuinely touching a squarer roundrect's corner can read as not touching.\n\nThat errs in the safe direction (a redundant airwire, not a missing one), which is why it was accepted rather than fixed. `corner_rratio` would let the shape be modelled exactly.\n\nThe margin fields matter for mask and paste output rather than connectivity; they are listed here because they are the same gap.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`corner_rratio` survives decode and round-trip, and a roundrect land is modelled at its true corner radius. A fixture whose correct answer differs between the inscribed stadium and the true shape is what proves it.\n\nSurfaced by the round-2 implementer of work item 01a02b5763e8.","created_at":"2026-08-23T02:31:27","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:08","priority":3,"tags":["decode","footprint","geometry","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Pad codec keeps corner_rratio/raw_shape/margins; roundrect at true radius."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02cd46ac173769bee66e43131f398","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Ratsnest conflates duplicate-number pad geometry and layers","description":"## What is wrong\n\nThe ratsnest collapses one logical pin to exactly one physical pad geometry even though the PCB pipeline explicitly supports footprints with duplicate pad numbers.\n\n`PCBComponent` preserves every entry in `pads`, but its pin map holds one position per number (the last matching pad). `pcb_ratsnest._pad_geometry` returns the first matching pad. `_pad_node` therefore combines geometry/type/layers from the first physical land with the logical pin center from the last and ignores every other land body.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe worker contract deliberately supports this:\n\n- `pcb/worker/tests/test_footprint_def.py::test_definition_local_ids_disambiguate_duplicate_pad_numbers` preserves two number-1 lands as distinct source ids.\n- `pcb/worker/pcb_worker/compile_board.py` states that duplicate pad numbers are the same electrical pad and applies the same pin override to each.\n\nA canonical board round-trip preserves both number-1 lands and leaves the pin position at the last one.\n\n### False-unrouted case\n\nTwo top-side 1×1 mm rectangular SMD lands share number 1 at local positions (0,0) and (10,0). A 0.2 mm trace genuinely overlaps the second land at (9.55,0.4) and runs to another pad.\n\nThe node contains the first rectangle and only a zero-width point at the second center. `nodes_touch` returns false at the second land body and the full solve reports one join remaining, although the real copper is routed.\n\n### False-complete case\n\nA square through-hole number-1 land is first at (0,0); a top-only rectangular SMD number-1 land is second at (10,0). A bottom trace runs from (10,0) to a bottom-side pad.\n\nThe first land gives the compound node every copper layer, while the rect path also inserts the last pin center at (10,0). The bottom trace under the top-only SMD is therefore credited as connected. The full solver returns `remaining_total=0`, even though the trace touches no copper at (10,0). This is a dangerous missing-airwire result.\n\n## Cause\n\n`pcb/ui/model/pcb_ratsnest.gd::_pad_geometry` returns the first match, while `_pad_node` assumes one geometry and one uniform layer set per logical pin. Duplicate lands can have different positions, shapes, and layer sets, so neither first-match nor a node-wide union/intersection of layers is correct.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nRepresent every physical land matching a logical pin, with each land retaining its own shape, transform, and layer set, while preserving the intended internal electrical equivalence of equal pad numbers.\n\nPin both directions:\n\n1. copper overlapping the body of a non-first duplicate land closes the logical pin;\n2. bottom copper under a top-only duplicate land does not connect merely because another same-number land is through-hole elsewhere.\n\nA fix that merely unions all duplicate shapes under one combined layer set fails case 2.","created_at":"2026-08-23T04:15:13","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:26:19","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["connectivity","duplicate-pad","pcb","ratsnest","silent-defect"],"resolution":"Per-land geometry/layers with internal logical-pin union implemented; 104/104 ratsnest assertions pass."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02ce73867765e9ef7330cfb8f3ab4","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"An unplated hole is modelled as copper on every layer","description":"## What\n\n`pcb/ui/model/pcb_ratsnest.gd::_physical_pad_node` gives `np_thru_hole` the same layer set as `thru_hole` — the full copper stack:\n\n    if pad_type in [\"thru_hole\", \"np_thru_hole\"]:\n        layers = _layer_set(stack)\n\nAn `np_thru_hole` is a drilled hole with **no plating**. It has no barrel and no annulus, so it conducts on no layer at all. Modelling it as copper on every layer means a top run and a bottom run that both reach it can be unioned into one island through a path that carries no current — two pads reported as joined when nothing joins them, which is the direction that reaches fabrication.\n\n## Status: NOT REPRODUCED — do not fix on this description alone\n\nRaised as a note by cold review of the duplicate-land round, on the reasoning that the new pin-group union adds a pathway for an unplated land to join siblings. The reviewer flagged it as suspected-by-reading, and noted that unplated pads normally carry number `\"\"`, which would keep them out of any net.\n\n**An attempt to demonstrate it failed.** A fixture with a top trace into an `np_thru_hole` land and a bottom trace out of it, all on one net, reported one join still owed — the same answer with and without a change zeroing the unplated land's layers. So either the land is already excluded from copper somewhere upstream, or that fixture does not construct what it appears to.\n\nA speculative fix was written and **reverted** rather than shipped, because it changed behaviour with nothing to show it changed an outcome.\n\n## What is actually needed first\n\nEstablish whether an `np_thru_hole` land ever reaches the solver as a net member with real geometry. Check the decode path (`pcb_component.load_pad_geometry`), whether such a pad is admitted to a net at all, and what layer set it ends up carrying in practice.\n\n- If it never reaches the solver on a net, this is dead defensive code and the honest fix is a comment saying so, or a guard that fails loudly.\n- If it can, build the fixture that shows two pads wrongly joined through it, and only then change the layer set.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nA board where copper on two layers meets at an unplated hole reports the join as still owed. Pinned by a test that FAILS against the current model — a test that passes both before and after proves nothing, which is exactly how the first attempt went wrong.\n\n## Note\n\nThe model already excludes keepouts on the grounds that a keepout is an instruction and not a conductor. An unplated hole is the same category of thing and is currently treated as its opposite.","created_at":"2026-08-23T04:35:45","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:57","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["connectivity","needs-repro","npth","pcb","ratsnest"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Reproduced via MountingHole_3.2mm_M3 (numbered np_thru_hole with *.Cu); NPTH lands are no-copper nodes on both sides, never anchor an airwire."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02cf3f8d8764fba0e3cae09df22da","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Route-start pad focus: one locked destination while a trace is being drawn","description":"## What\n\nBuild-order step 2 of DCR 01a02b570e4d. Step 1 — the copper-island MST renderer — is done (work item 01a02b5763e8). This is the step that turns that answer into a routing instruction.\n\nStarting a trace on a pad activates a transient, pad-scoped focus:\n\n- unrelated airwires recede;\n- the source pad's copper island is highlighted;\n- exactly ONE connection is shown prominently, to the nearest unconnected island on that net;\n- the destination is marked and labelled — refdes.pad, net, distance;\n- that target is LOCKED for the duration of the gesture and recomputed only on commit or cancel.\n\n## Why this and not a filter\n\nThe owner's requirement, in their words: \"What do I route this pin/pad to? I route pins one by one, and I need to know the two endpoints of the trace to figure out how to draw it.\" The renderer alone does not answer that — it shows every remaining join at once, and a dense board cannot be read. The MST edge incident to the source pad IS the instruction; this step surfaces it.\n\n## Trigger is already available\n\nMeasured: the trace tool refuses to start anywhere except on a pad. `_start_trace` rejects an empty hit with \"Start a trace on a pad — that is where its net comes from,\" and rejects a pad on no net. So at the instant a routing gesture begins, the tool already holds the source pad reference, its position in board mm, and its net. No new hit-test, no new mode, no INSPECT_PIN dependency, and no \"started in empty space\" case.\n\nPin inspection was explicitly REJECTED by the owner as the trigger: \"not obvious; I wouldn't think to use pin inspection for this case.\"\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Locked target.** An unlocked target flickers as the cursor moves and makes the feature worse than none.\n- **Recede, do not hide.** Other airwires must stay visible enough to keep context. Rails are dimmed, never silently removed.\n- **Guidance, never enforcement.** Drawing somewhere other than the suggested target must stay legal and unobstructed. Precedent in the same file: `_finish_trace_on_pad` carries an owner ruling that the trace tool does not enforce same-net — a trace onto a foreign pad is named out loud as a short and drawn anyway, because \"DRC is the correctness net, not the drawing tool.\"\n- **Transient.** The focus exists only for the gesture and leaves nothing behind on commit or cancel.\n- **Deterministic.** The same board and the same source pad give the same destination every session.\n- **Idle is unchanged.** With no gesture in progress the board must render exactly as it does now.\n- Do not rebind keys. No view state in plugin preferences. Any new draw-affecting state must be registered so agent screenshots do not silently disagree with the screen.\n\n## Not in scope\n\nOffscreen destination arrow and \"frame both endpoints\" (step 4). Correcting already-connected and pour-covered states (step 3). Tap-anywhere and constrained-net metadata (step 5). Destination loupe or unrouted worklist (step 6).\n\n## Acceptance\n\nStarting a trace on a pad with unrouted copper on its net shows one marked, labelled destination and recedes the rest. The target does not move while the gesture continues. Cancelling or committing restores the resting view.\n\n## Falsifier\n\nRoute a session by hand. If the designer still hunts for the other endpoint, or the single suggested target is wrong often enough that they want a ranked list back, then one-answer-no-picker is the wrong ruling and the DCR's exactly-one rule must be revisited.","created_at":"2026-08-23T04:49:41","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:14:43","priority":1,"tags":["canvas","pcb","ratsnest","routing","ux"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a02b570e4d7072800fe51b70c45720"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02d51716172acbfade8875cd0f406","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Real-worker gd suites test an extracted runtime snapshot, not the working tree","description":"## What is wrong\n\n`sharedruntime.PythonPath` resolves tier 1 to an extracted runtime whenever one has ever been extracted:\n\n    ~/Library/Application Support/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/<version>/bin/python3\n\nThat tree contains a **copy** of `pcb_worker`, not the `.venv` editable install pointed at the working tree. So every gd suite that proves `real_worker_used=true` runs the worker code in that snapshot, not the code in the repository.\n\nThere is no environment override to force the development tier.\n\n## Measured\n\nOn this machine the extracted bundle was behind HEAD by a whole feature: it did not contain `route_bridge.established_net_widths`, which landed earlier in this batch. The real-worker suites — `test_pcb_drc_propose`, `test_pcb_explicit_propose`, `test_pcb_hint_refine_loop`, `test_pcb_single_trace_tool` — were therefore certifying a stale snapshot while reporting green.\n\nFound while measuring the zone-fill transport, where the discrepancy surfaced because the new worker method simply did not exist in the bundle.\n\n## Consequence\n\n**This is a false-pass generator, which is why it is severity 1.** A real-worker suite is the strongest evidence this project has that a worker change works end to end, and the `real-worker` flag in `EXPECTED_SUITES` exists specifically to make that evidence trustworthy. If the flag can be satisfied by a snapshot, then:\n\n- a worker change can be green in CI and absent from the artifact under test;\n- a worker REGRESSION can be invisible, because the snapshot predates it;\n- and the failure is silent and in the reassuring direction — nobody investigates a pass.\n\nEvery real-worker result recorded before this is discovered is of unknown provenance. That includes results this batch relied on.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nA real-worker gd suite exercises the worker source in the working tree, or refuses to run and says why. The observable: change a worker method so it would fail, run a real-worker suite, and see it fail. Today it can pass.\n\nThe criterion a lazy fix fails: re-syncing the bundle by hand makes today's run honest and leaves the mechanism intact, so the next stale bundle produces the same silent false pass. The tier resolution is what needs to change, or the runner needs to verify the bundle matches the tree and abort when it does not.\n\n## Traps\n\n- The bundle is a legitimate production path — the fix must not break the shipped runtime, only the test path's use of it.\n- \"Abort when stale\" is safer than \"silently prefer the tree\": a suite that cannot tell which worker it ran should refuse rather than guess.\n- Whatever the fix, it must be observable from the suite's own output. `real_worker_used=true` should mean the worker in the tree, or a different flag should say which worker ran.\n\n## Note\n\nThe measurement in the zone-fill task was made honest by hand-syncing the bundle before running. That workaround is not a fix and is not committed.\n\nSurfaced by the implementer of work item 01a02c754dc5.","created_at":"2026-08-23T06:31:47","updated_at":"2026-08-23T06:31:47","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["false-pass","pcb","runtime","testing","trust"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a02d519f4e71ba824bdbfe1398a70c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"The panel's board projection hardcodes schema v1 and emits no board id","description":"## What\n\n`PCBData.to_board_dict()` hardcodes `\"version\": 1` and emits no board `id`. Every board the panel sends the worker therefore arrives as schema v1, and its entity ids are **minted during the compile** rather than carried in — so a compiled entity's id is not the id the panel holds for the same entity.\n\n## Consequence\n\nAny worker reply that refers to a board entity by id cannot be matched against the panel's model. Discovered while building the zone-fill transport: matching the reply by compiled zone id silently matched nothing.\n\nThat transport now works around it by echoing the caller's id back and cross-checking layer and kind, falling through to no match rather than a wrong one. The workaround is sound but it is a workaround, and every future worker verb that answers per-entity will hit the same wall and invent its own.\n\nMore broadly, sending v1 when the model may hold v2 concepts is a lossy projection asserted as a version number, which is the kind of claim that is believed rather than checked.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nThe projection states the schema version the model actually holds and carries stable entity ids across the boundary, so a worker reply can be matched by identity without an echo.\n\n## Traps\n\n- The compiler currently mints ids; changing the projection without changing that just moves the mismatch.\n- Existing consumers may depend on ids being minted. Establish who reads compiled ids before changing where they come from.\n- The zone-fill echo workaround should be removed as part of this, not left as a second mechanism.\n\nSurfaced by the implementer of work item 01a02c754dc5.","created_at":"2026-08-23T06:31:59","updated_at":"2026-08-23T06:31:59","priority":2,"tags":["identity","pcb","serialization","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03509e1f577b8a441cd14fe851939","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb canvas Trace tool cannot start or end a trace on a via","description":"The Trace tool's endpoint resolution is pad-only at both ends, so a via — which is real, net-carrying copper — cannot anchor a trace. Multi-layer routing needs this constantly: a run that changes layer arrives at a via and must continue from it.\n\nObserved while hand-routing smart-remote-v2. Every hand-drawn trace in that session landed off its intended anchor by a sub-0.1 mm drift, because a click near a via falls through to the generic waypoint path, which grid-snaps to the cursor rather than resolving the via centre. One GND trace committed at x=63.75 against a via at x=63.625 — 0.125 mm short of the via it was meant to reach, leaving copper that looks connected and is not.\n\nCurrent behaviour, all in pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:\n\n- _start_trace (7765) refuses any first click that is not a pad hit: \"Start a trace on a pad — that is where its net comes from.\"\n- _trace_pad_at (7676) resolves through host.pad_at only; vias are never consulted.\n- _handle_trace_click (7720) sends a non-pad click to _trace_append_point(_author_point(world_pos)), so clicking a via places a grid-snapped waypoint near it rather than a point on it.\n- _finish_trace_on_pad (7798) is the only terminator, so ending on a via means double-clicking, which commits at the cursor.\n\nThe canvas already owns the hit test this needs: _via_at(world_pos) (4866) delegating to data.get_via_at(world_pos, VIA_HIT_RADIUS_PX / zoom), used today by the selection path. The gap is that the trace tool does not call it.\n\nThe MCP verb minerva_pcb_add_trace is unaffected — it takes exact coordinates, which is why agent-drawn traces land on via centres and hand-drawn ones do not. That asymmetry is itself the defect: a click and a call should be able to author the same copper.","created_at":"2026-08-24T18:30:35","updated_at":"2026-08-24T18:54:58","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["canvas","pcb","routing","trace-tool","via"],"resolution":"The canvas Trace tool now resolves a click to a kind-tagged anchor (pad, else via) rather than to a pad only. A first click on a via arms the trace and inherits the via's net; a click on a via while drawing ends the trace on the via's centre. Pad wins ties, so every click that previously resolved to a pad still does and pad behaviour is unchanged by construction. A netless via is refused by name, matching the existing netless-pad refusal. Endpoints land on the anchor's centre, which is what makes a click and minerva_pcb_add_trace author identical copper.\n\nKnown limitation, deliberate and documented: a via start locks no ratsnest destination, because PcbRatsnest.focus resolves by pad ref. The call is guarded rather than changed — ratsnest focus was out of scope. A follow-up in pcb_ratsnest.gd would be needed to focus from a via.\n\nAlso re-pinned test_pcb_trace_tool.gd in EXPECTED_SUITES from assertions=31 to 54; the added tests would otherwise have failed the suite-count gate."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0351d54927e1695abfeb3bcf62205","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"Double-click finishing a trace overwrites its own success confirmation with a refusal","description":"A physical double-click delivers two presses. Press 1 arrives with is_double_click=false, finishes the trace, commits it, and resets the gesture buffers. Press 2 then arrives with is_double_click=true and calls _commit_trace() a second time — but the buffers are now empty, so data.trace_author_error(\"\", \"\", 0) returns \"A trace needs at least 2 points (0 placed).\" and that refusal is emitted over the \"Added trace...\" status the user was meant to read.\n\nThe trace itself is committed correctly. Only the status message is wrong, but it tells the user their action failed when it succeeded, which is the worst kind of wrong for a transient status channel.\n\nAffects pad and via finishes alike. Pre-existing; found while implementing via anchoring (PLG 01a03509e1f577b8a441cd14fe851939) and deliberately not fixed there under file-don't-fix.","created_at":"2026-08-24T18:51:49","updated_at":"2026-08-24T19:28:55","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["canvas","pcb","status-message","trace-tool"],"resolution":"The Trace tool's double-click branch now commits only while at least 2 points are held.\n\nCORRECTION TO THIS ITEM: the stated mechanism was right, the stated repro was wrong. Double-clicking EMPTY SPACE — the filed repro — is the one path that already worked: press 1 appends a waypoint, press 2 commits a >=2-point trace. The defect needs press 1 to END the gesture, which happens when it lands ON a pad or a via: it commits and resets, so press 2 re-enters _commit_trace on an emptied buffer and the point-count refusal lands over the confirmation. A third case the item never mentioned is fixed too: double-clicking a pad to START answered \"(1 placed)\" over the start line.\n\nGuarded at the call site rather than inside _commit_trace, because the Enter key shares that path and must keep reporting the point count — pressing Enter IS a request to commit, so the count is news there. Below 2 points the only sentence _commit_trace can produce is the point-count refusal, so the guard suppresses no message a user needed.\n\nCopper unchanged by construction: press 2 becomes a pure no-op, and the tests compare the serialized trace entity and change-journal length either side of it.\n\nTests: test_pcb_trace_tool.gd section 7, 10 assertions over four legs (pad finish, via finish, double-click-to-start, and empty-space as the regression guard). Oracles are the serialized board and the change journal; the refusal is recognised by asking the model for its own empty-buffer output rather than by a copied string. Suite re-pinned 54 -> 64.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main.","repro_steps":"1. Open a PCB editor, select the canvas Trace tool.\n2. Click a pad to start a trace, click one or more waypoints.\n3. Double-click empty space to finish.\n   Observed: the trace is added, but the status reads \"A trace needs at least 2 points (0 placed).\"\n   Expected: the status reads the \"Added trace ...\" confirmation."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0351d86cc7b9bb2bc565ceea3f5da","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"trace_author_error refusal still says traces start on a pad, though vias now start them too","description":"trace_author_error around pcb_data.gd:2350 refuses a netless trace with: \"A trace must name a net — start it on a pad that has one.\"\n\nSince vias became valid trace anchors (PLG 01a03509e1f577b8a441cd14fe851939), that sentence names only half the places a trace can legitimately start. It is not wrong about the rule — a trace does need a net — but it points the reader at pads only.\n\nThe string is shared with the minerva_pcb_add_trace MCP verb, which is why it was left alone by that canvas-scoped work under file-don't-fix rather than edited from a task that had no business changing an MCP-facing refusal.\n\nReachability note: the canvas no longer reaches this string for a netless anchor — it refuses earlier, in its own words, naming the pad or via. So this text is now reached through the MCP verb path.","created_at":"2026-08-24T18:52:02","updated_at":"2026-08-24T19:29:10","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["mcp","pcb","trace-tool","wording"],"resolution":"trace_author_error's netless clause now reads: \"A trace must name a net — the one carried by the pad or via it starts on.\" The rule clause is unchanged; only the pointer half moved.\n\nMEASURED: this rule is stated in FOUR places, not two — the model string, both of _start_trace's refusals in pcb_canvas.gd, and pcb/docs/tools.md. Only the model string still named pads alone, so this stayed a one-string edit and nothing is left disagreeing. Every claim in the item, including its reachability note, checked out: the canvas cannot reach this clause (an empty buffer hits the point-count clause first, and _start_trace refuses a netless anchor before the buffer arms), so the only caller reaching it is minerva_pcb_add_trace, whose net_name is caller-supplied text.\n\nThe minimal edit (\"start it on a pad or a via that has one\") was rejected deliberately: an agent cannot \"start on\" anything — it passes net_name — so an imperative gesture is not actionable text for the surface that actually reads this. Naming where the value comes from is true for both surfaces and mirrors the canvas's own untouched wording.\n\nTests: test_pcb_trace_tool.gd section 8, 13 assertions. The oracle is the canvas's ANCHOR_PAD/ANCHOR_VIA constants, looped over — never a quoted sentence — so a third anchor kind, or one that stops anchoring, fails it without anyone remembering which strings to update. The MCP leg compares the verb's note byte-for-byte against the model's own output, and closes a real gap: the verb path had no coverage for an EMPTY net at all (test_direct_copper_verbs.gd group 6 covers one-point and undeclared-net only). Suite re-pinned 64 -> 77.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0352f8e9e711a8030d72bdd6f5b7f","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"The Via and Eraser canvas tools ignore the double_click flag, so a double-click fires them twice","description":"pcb_canvas.gd's left-press dispatch hands every click-per-point tool the double_click flag (_handle_trace_click, _handle_zone_click, _handle_cutout_click, _handle_bus_click, _handle_corridor_click all take it and guard on it). Two tools do NOT receive it at all:\n\n  ui/pcb_canvas.gd:3625  _handle_via_click(world_pos)\n  ui/pcb_canvas.gd:3641  _handle_eraser_click(world_pos)\n\nA physical double-click is TWO press events. Both tools are one-click-one-action, so both presses run the action:\n\n  VIA: _handle_via_click (ui/pcb_canvas.gd:5947) resolves a target, add_via, end_batch, done. Press 2 runs the whole thing again at the same point. Unverified which of the two outcomes lands — resolve_via_target may refuse the second because the first via now occupies the point, or it may snap the second onto the junction the first just inserted — but either is a board mutation the user did not ask for, and either way it is TWO undo steps for one gesture.\n\n  ERASER: _handle_eraser_click (ui/pcb_canvas.gd:5981) resolves _entity_at and deletes it. Press 2 resolves again at the same point and deletes whatever is now on top — plausibly the entity UNDERNEATH the one the user meant to erase.\n\nThis is the same family as PLG 01a0351d5492 (the trace tool's double-click second press) but a GEOMETRY defect rather than a status-channel one, so it was left alone under file-don't-fix while that item was fixed.\n\nFix shape, if confirmed: pass event.double_click into both handlers and return early on it, the guard _handle_bus_click already carries for its PICKING phase (\"the picking grammar has no double-click verb\").\n\nNot measured by running the app — read off the dispatch and the two handlers.","created_at":"2026-08-24T19:11:44","updated_at":"2026-08-25T02:12:53","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["canvas","double-click","eraser-tool","pcb","via-tool"],"resolution":"NOT A DEFECT AS FILED — both legs refuted, for two different reasons.\n\nERASER: mechanism reproduced exactly (remove_via then remove_trace, presses 0.56s apart, NET_CHAIN left broken), but the OWNER RULED IT CORRECT. The eraser is one-click-one-action and its grammar carries no double-click verb, so two presses erasing two entities is what the gesture means. The trace tool differs because there a double-click TERMINATES a gesture and press 2 has no work of its own. No double_click guard goes into _handle_eraser_click. One caveat recorded for whoever reads this next: the second deletion is not guaranteed — an off-centre press 2 removed only the via, because _entity_at resolved nothing under it.\n\nVIA: the suspected geometry defect does not exist. Measured via_count 5 -> 6 with ONE add_via journal entry and no second batch; via_author_error refuses press 2 before begin_batch, in both the empty-board and snapped-to-trace cases. The item's \"two placement attempts ... and two batches on the history stack\" is wrong. What IS real is a status-channel defect — press 2's refusal overwrites press 1's confirmation — refiled as PLG 01a036aeed3670f083a1b200e7ce8635 with the measured evidence and a fix shape.\n\nMETHOD NOTE, since this is the second time in one day: this item and PLG 01a0351d5492 were both filed from reading the dispatch, and both had their repro or their classification corrected once someone ran it. Reading pcb_canvas.gd is enough to find a candidate defect and not enough to characterise one.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main.","repro_steps":"1. Open a PCB editor, select the canvas Via tool.\n2. Double-click empty board.\n   Expected: one via, one undo step.\n   Suspected: two placement attempts — either two vias or a via plus a refusal message, and two batches on the history stack.\n3. Repeat with the Eraser tool over two overlapping entities: double-click erases the top one AND then whatever was beneath it."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a036aeed3670f083a1b200e7ce8635","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"Double-clicking the Via tool overwrites its own success confirmation with \"A via already sits at...\"","description":"A physical double-click delivers two presses, and _handle_via_click (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:5947) is one-click-one-action, so both presses run it. The via tool does not receive the double_click flag at all — the left-press dispatch calls _handle_via_click(world_pos) at pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:3625 without it, unlike _handle_trace_click / _handle_zone_click / _handle_cutout_click / _handle_bus_click / _handle_corridor_click which all take and guard on it.\n\nThe BOARD is safe, which is what distinguishes this from the geometry defect originally suspected in PLG 01a0352f8e9e. Press 2 resolves the same grid-snapped author point, and via_author_error (pcb/ui/model/pcb_data.gd:2094-2104) refuses it because press 1's via now sits within the claim radius (max(size*0.5, 0.05); 0.4mm at the tool's 0.8mm VIA_TOOL_SIZE_MM). That refusal returns through resolve_via_target BEFORE data.begin_batch(), so press 2 mutates nothing and journals nothing — one via, one undo step.\n\nWhat press 2 DOES do is emit trace_tool_message with the refusal, landing it over press 1's confirmation. The user is told their action failed when it succeeded, on a transient channel they cannot scroll back — the same class of defect as PLG 01a0351d54927e1695abfeb3bcf62205 (fixed as f6d9d71), in a second tool.\n\nThe near-trace case is covered by the same guard: resolve_via_target snaps onto the trace centreline and then re-runs via_author_error on the SNAPPED position (pcb_data.gd:2176), which is exactly where press 1's via sits, so press 2 is refused there too. Board still safe, message still clobbered.\n\nFIX SHAPE, per the precedent set by f6d9d71: guard at the CALL SITE, not inside _handle_via_click. Pass event.double_click into the handler from the dispatch at :3625 and return early on it. Do not move the guard into via_author_error or resolve_via_target — the same refusal is the correct and necessary answer for minerva_pcb_place_via and for a deliberate second single-click, both of which genuinely are requests to place a via where one already is. Only the second press of one physical gesture is noise.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: the Eraser tool's identical missing flag. That was measured in the same HITL run and the owner ruled it CORRECT — the eraser is one-click-one-action with no double-click verb, so two presses deleting two entities is what the gesture means. See PLG 01a0352f8e9e comment for the ruling.","created_at":"2026-08-25T02:10:28","updated_at":"2026-08-25T02:12:41","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["canvas","double-click","pcb","status-message","via-tool"],"resolution":"NOT A DEFECT — OWNER RULING 2026-08-24, and the item was wrong on principle, not on measurement.\n\nEvery fact in this item is confirmed: one via, one batch, press 2 refused before begin_batch, refusal text landing after the confirmation. What was wrong was calling that a defect.\n\nThe Via tool is ONE-CLICK-ONE-ACTION, exactly like the Eraser. Under the ruling on PLG 01a0352f8e9e — each press of a physical double-click is an independent action — press 2 genuinely IS a request to place a via where one already sits, and \"A via already sits at (x, y).\" is the correct and informative answer to it. Nothing is being overwritten; the user asked twice and got two truthful answers.\n\nTHE ERROR IN THIS ITEM'S REASONING, recorded because it is the reusable part: it accepted press 2 as a legitimate independent action for the Eraser and simultaneously called press 2 \"noise\" for the Via tool. Two rules for two tools of the same class. The rule is one rule — for a one-click-one-action tool, press 2 is real work and deserves a real answer. The trace tool is the ONE genuine exception (PLG 01a0351d5492, fixed as f6d9d71), because there a double-click TERMINATES a gesture, so press 2 has no action of its own and its point-count refusal was answering a question nobody asked.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR FUTURE WORK: do not pass event.double_click into _handle_via_click or _handle_eraser_click. The missing flag on those two handlers is correct by design, not an oversight, and pcb_canvas.gd:3625/3641 should stay as they are.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main. Measured live on macOS 2026-08-24, owner driving the canvas, agent measuring over MCP.","repro_steps":"1. Open a PCB editor, select the canvas Via tool.\n2. Double-click empty board, clear of any trace.\n   Observed: exactly ONE via is placed (correct), but the status line reads\n   \"A via already sits at (15.240, 7.620).\" instead of the\n   \"Via <id> placed at (x, y) — standalone and unassigned.\" confirmation.\n   Expected: the status line reads the placement confirmation.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred: via_count 5 -> 6, one add_via journal entry, no second\nbatch. Also reproduces on a via that snaps to a trace (the \"snapped to trace ...\njoined to net ...\" confirmation is overwritten the same way)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a036c3dbca7f2398afea3f1ba9d022","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus routing does not work: the picking phase renders nothing, and a commit lands no copper","description":"READ OFF THE CODE, corroborated by the owner's observed symptom; the falsifier in repro_steps has NOT been run.\n\nThe picks are registering. The canvas is drawing nothing.\n\n_handle_bus_pick_click (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:8120) resolves the click through _bus_net_at -> _trace_pad_at, appends the net to _bus_nets and the pad ref to _bus_net_refs, emits a bus_tool_message, and calls queue_redraw(). All of that works.\n\nThen _draw_bus_preview (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:8285) opens with:\n\n    if not _bus_drawing:\n        return\n\n_bus_drawing is FALSE for the entire PICKING phase — it only becomes true in _start_bus_draw, which is reached by pressing ENTER. So every redraw the picking phase requests bails on line 8286 before drawing a single pixel. The queue_redraw() calls at :8136 and :8144 are the tell: the code intends to show something on pick, and the draw path refuses.\n\nTHE AFFORDANCE IS MODELLED AND NEVER RENDERED. _bus_net_refs exists precisely to record WHICH pad each net came from (its own doc at :788 says so) — the exact data a pad highlight would need. Grep shows it is written at :8140, removed at :8134, cleared at :8255 and :8271, and READ BY NOTHING. It is accumulated only to be discarded.\n\nSO THE ONLY FEEDBACK IS TRANSIENT. bus_tool_message is wired to _show_transient_status (PCBPanel.gd:1159), which calls _set_status and then clears it on a 2.0-second timer (PCBPanel.gd:4691-4697). A user who clicks a pad, looks at the canvas for the highlight that never comes, and looks back has already lost the only evidence the pick happened. Two seconds is the entire lifetime of the tool's feedback.\n\nThe failure therefore compounds into the reported \"nothing works at all\": picks are invisible, so the user never learns picking succeeded, so they never press ENTER, so the DRAWING phase — which DOES render, richly — is never reached, so no copper is ever committed. Every downstream stage is fine and unreachable.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE HERE, filed separately as the model mismatch: the owner's expected grammar (select source pads, waypoint, select TARGET pads) is not what this tool does even when working. It picks NETS and draws a SPINE whose parallel offsets become the traces; it never selects targets.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not applied — owner said file only): narrow the guard at :8286 so it gates only the spine/ghost section that genuinely needs a spine, and give the PICKING phase its own rendering — highlight the pads in _bus_net_refs and show the ordered net list. The data is already there. Consider also whether a phase whose entire feedback is a 2s transient should be using a transient channel at all.","created_at":"2026-08-25T02:33:20","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:04","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","data-loss","hitl","pcb","silent-failure","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed in the bus pin-to-pin cycle (e29b5aa \"show the bus tool's picked nets, and the armed-but-empty spine\", range cb4ceca..bd269a7). Docket cleanup 2026-08-25; owner has since driven the bus tool live.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main. Owner-driven from the live panel on macOS, 2026-08-24, board Smart Remote v2 (private, ccsandbox). The second half was retried WITH the visibility patch live (pcb plugin reloaded). The pcb WORKER was unavailable throughout the session — minerva_pcb_board_check returned worker_unavailable on every attempt.","repro_steps":"1. Open a PCB editor with a board that has pads on nets.\n2. Select the Bus tool (Draw flow).\n3. Click pads to pick nets.\n   OBSERVED (pre-patch): nothing appears to happen — no pad highlight, no\n   persistent sign a net was picked.\n4. Press ENTER.\n   OBSERVED (pre-patch): also no visible action. Owner ran this as the filed\n   falsifier; it refuted the original prediction and exposed a SECOND mute\n   window (armed-but-empty).\n5. With the visibility patch live (plugin reloaded), attempt a bus across pins.\n   OBSERVED: the status line updates to indicate routing happened. Nothing\n   appears on the canvas at any point. Afterwards the board contains NO bus\n   copper, and the change journal shows traces added and then removed with no\n   delete or undo by the user.\n\nEXPECTED: picks visibly register; a committed bus produces copper that stays\nand is drawn.\n\nSEVERITY RATIONALE: a surface reporting success while producing nothing is the\nworst failure mode for an authoring tool — the user believes work is saved.\nCopper withdrawn with no attributable user action is worse still."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a036c453d97e64af967d9834cacc8d","type":"rca","status":"detected","title":"RCA: why the Bus tool's UX doesn't work — the human-only phase is the one phase nothing could verify","created_at":"2026-08-25T02:33:51","updated_at":"2026-08-25T02:34:13","priority":2,"tags":["bus-tool","hitl","pcb","rca","test-coverage","ux"],"detected_at":"2026-08-24","why_chain":"OBSERVED: \"Bus routing doesn't seem to work at all — no pins get selected, no traces get created.\"\n\nWHY 1 — Why does clicking a pad with the Bus tool appear to do nothing?\nBecause the PICKING phase draws nothing. _draw_bus_preview (pcb_canvas.gd:8285) returns on line 8286 whenever _bus_drawing is false, and _bus_drawing is false for the whole picking phase. The pick itself succeeds — the net lands in _bus_nets and queue_redraw() is called at :8136/:8144 — but the draw path bails before touching a pixel. State changes; the screen does not.\n\nWHY 2 — Why is there no other visible sign the pick registered?\nBecause the only other channel is transient. bus_tool_message -> _show_transient_status (PCBPanel.gd:1159) clears itself on a 2.0-second timer (:4691-4697). And _bus_net_refs — the array whose stated purpose (:788) is recording WHICH pad each net was picked from, i.e. exactly what a highlight would need — is written at :8140 and read by NO drawing code anywhere. The affordance was modelled in state and never rendered.\n\nWHY 3 — Why did a two-phase tool ship with one phase unrendered?\nBecause the preview function was scoped to the second phase's job. It exists to draw the spine polyline and the N offset ghost polylines, and neither means anything before a spine exists. So \"there is no spine yet\" got implemented as an early return over the WHOLE function rather than a guard around the spine/ghost section only. \"Nothing to preview yet\" and \"draw nothing at all\" were conflated.\n\nWHY 4 — Why did tests and review not catch it?\nBecause everything they cover is genuinely correct. test_pcb_bus_geometry.gd pins the offset/pitch geometry (83 checks, per the header at pcb_canvas.gd:40-48), and the canvas tool and minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct share bus_plan/bus_commit_plan as the single model path (:45-48), so the plan and the committed copper are right. The picking state machine is right too. What no test asserts is that a human can SEE that a pick landed — a headless suite cannot fail on \"zero pixels changed\", and a code review reads _draw_bus_preview as a correct spine renderer, which it is.\n\nWHY 5 (ROOT) — Why was rendering-of-state outside the definition of done?\nBecause the tool was specced, built and judged as an AUTHORING path — pick nets, plan, commit the right copper — and the shared core being right was treated as the tool being done. But the two surfaces are not symmetric: the MCP verb takes its net list as an ARGUMENT and has no picking phase at all. Picking is the one part of the canvas tool with no agent-side twin, so it is the one part the shared-core design could never exercise and the shared-core tests could never reach. The architecture that made the tool cheap to build is exactly what hid its human-only phase from verification. Feedback rendering is not a polish layer on an authoring tool; for the phase that exists only for a human, it IS the feature.","contributing_factors":"1. GUARD PLACEMENT. A single early return at the top of _draw_bus_preview (:8286) covers a function that has two audiences. Narrowing it to the spine/ghost block would have left the picking phase free to render.\n\n2. TRANSIENT CHANNEL FOR PERSISTENT STATE. The picked-net list is durable state that survives until Enter or Esc, but its only report is a 2.0s status flash. State that persists deserves a display that persists.\n\n3. WRITE-ONLY STATE AS A LATENT SIGNAL. _bus_net_refs being written and never read is a detectable smell — the code carries the highlight's data and no highlight. Nothing flags write-only members.\n\n4. THE HINT TEXT IS ALSO TRANSIENT-ADJACENT. PCBPanel.gd:6295 documents the real grammar (\"Click pads/traces to pick nets (2+), Enter to draw the spine...\"), and the tooltip at :1547 says it too, but a user who has already concluded the tool is dead does not go hunting in tooltips.\n\n5. MENTAL-MODEL MISMATCH, INDEPENDENT OF THE BUG. The owner expects source pads -> waypoints -> TARGET pads. The tool picks NETS and draws a SPINE whose parallel offsets become the traces; it never selects targets. Fixing the rendering makes the tool visible but still not the tool the owner reached for. Filed separately."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a036c49dd37489aeeccd0f8111e0be","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Canvas Bus tool cannot route pin-to-pin, though the router already can — the button is wired to the spine tool, not the bus-hint path","description":"SEPARATE from PLG 01a036c3dbca (the picking phase rendering nothing). That one makes the tool invisible; this one is about what the tool does once you can see it.\n\nThe owner reached for \"bus routing\" expecting a MULTI-NET TRACE TOOL: pick the source pads, route a path, land on the target pads — i.e. the single-trace grammar times N, with the pin pairs naming both ends. What shipped is a GEOMETRY AUTHORING tool: pick which nets participate, draw one spine, and the tool emits N parallel polylines at computed offset/pitch from that spine. The endpoints of those polylines are wherever the spine starts and stops, not pads the user chose.\n\nTwo concrete consequences:\n\n1. NO TARGETS. There is no phase in which the user names where the bus should END. _handle_bus_click's DRAWING branch (pcb_canvas.gd:8111-8116) states this outright: the tool has no pad-to-finish shortcut, \"since it is not itself copper on a net\". The single-trace tool finishes ON a pad; the bus tool cannot.\n\n2. ENTER IS THE HIDDEN HINGE. The PICKING -> DRAWING transition is a keypress, not a click (_start_bus_draw via the Enter branch at :4204-4208). A user working the canvas with a mouse has no reason to expect a keyboard verb mid-gesture, and it is documented only in the tooltip (PCBPanel.gd:1547) and the transient status hint (:6295).\n\nWorth deciding rather than assuming: whether the right answer is to teach the existing grammar better (persistent picked-net panel, an explicit \"Draw spine\" button beside the tool so the hinge is clickable, spine endpoints that can optionally snap to pads) or to accept that \"bus routing\" names the pin-pair model in most people's heads and the spine tool wants a different name (e.g. \"Bus Spine\") with a genuine pin-pair bus router filed as its own work.\n\nNot a defect in the geometry — pcb_bus_geometry.gd and bus_plan do exactly what they say. This is a mismatch between the tool's model and the name it is offered under.","created_at":"2026-08-25T02:34:10","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:22","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","pcb","regression","routing","ux"],"resolution":"Four commits cite it, ending 3cdbfae \"measure clearance between whole bus routes\"; the canvas bus tool routes pin-to-pin and PLG 01a03a26 built on it. Docket cleanup 2026-08-25.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main. Owner-reported 2026-08-24.","repro_steps":"Owner's stated expectation, verbatim: \"I select bus routing tool. I select the pins/pads starting the bus. I then waypoint the bus, then select target pins/pads.\"\n\nActual grammar: click pads/traces to pick 2+ NETS -> press ENTER -> click spine vertices -> ENTER or double-click to commit (Shift for propose). Target pads are never selected; each net's copper is a parallel offset of the spine."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0377ba26874c5936ed64c82fd9c66","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Pad hit testing ignores pad size canvas-wide — a click well inside a large pad reads as clear board","description":"pcb_component carries pad_width_mm / pad_height_mm, so the model knows how big each pad is. The hit test does not consult them. pad_at answers \"which pin centre is nearest, if within radius_mm\", and every consumer passes the same 1.27mm constant — trace anchors, bus source picking, bus target picking.\n\nThe consequence scales with pad size. On a small SMD pad the radius covers the copper and the approximation is invisible. On a large pad the accepted region is a 1.27mm disc floating in the middle of a much bigger land, and every click on the surrounding copper reads as empty board. The failure is silent in the direction that matters: the tool does not refuse, it proceeds with a different interpretation of the gesture.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, and why the symptom pointed elsewhere. A cold review of the bus batch reported that _bus_target_at (centre-snap) disagreed with _trace_pad_at (believed to be a real hit test), so a click inside a large pad would be a legal target wrongly refused with a message claiming it was that net's own source pad. Measured, that specific defect is UNREACHABLE: both paths use the same metric and the same radius, so they agree by construction and the refusal branch only runs for a genuinely illegal pad. The reviewer's suggested repair would have been a no-op and would have cost the anti-shadowing property the target picker documents. But the reviewer was right that something is wrong with pad hit testing — it is one level down, in pad_at itself, and it affects the trace tool at least as much as the bus tool.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT FIXED HERE: it is a canvas-wide contract change with several consumers, found mid-batch on a bus-scoped task, and the fix belongs at PcbAnnotationHost.pad_at rather than in any one tool. Filed under file-don't-fix.\n\nWORTH DECIDING WHEN THIS IS TAKEN UP: whether pad_at grows a true shape test (rect/roundrect/oval per the pad's own geometry, which the model already carries), or whether radius_mm becomes per-pad — derived from the pad's half-diagonal with the existing constant as a FLOOR, so small pads keep today's generous snap while large pads stop lying. The second is cheaper and strictly better than today; the first is what \"click the copper you see\" actually means. Note also that StubPadHost mirrors the centre-distance rule, so whichever is chosen, the stub must move with it or the suites will keep passing against the old contract.","created_at":"2026-08-25T05:54:04","updated_at":"2026-08-25T09:19:25","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","hit-test","pads","pcb","trace-tool"],"resolution":"Fixed at 3e40519, with the tie-break half at f80e262.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main. Found by measurement while resolving a cold-review finding on the bus pin-to-pin batch, 2026-08-24.","repro_steps":"1. Open a board carrying a physically large pad — a connector land or a big SMD pad more than ~2.5mm across.\n2. With the canvas Trace tool, click INSIDE that pad's copper but more than 1.27mm from its centre.\n   Observed: the click is treated as clear board and becomes a plain waypoint,\n   rather than anchoring on the pad.\n   Expected: a click on a pad's copper resolves to that pad.\n3. Same click with the Bus tool during source picking: it is treated as clear\n   copper and starts the path phase.\n4. Same during bus target picking: the click resolves to nothing and only\n   re-prints status.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred: PcbAnnotationHost.pad_at (pcb/ui/PcbAnnotationHost.gd:952) is a NEAREST-PIN-CENTRE-WITHIN-RADIUS test, not a shape hit test. Callers pass TRACE_PAD_SNAP_MM (1.27mm). The test rig's StubPadHost does the same, so the suites cannot see this."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03798feae794f83fd467e9cf44c64","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"bundle_routes emits self-overlapping copper for a single net on a folded-back spine","description":"Found while fixing the two P1 cross-net breakout defects (the whole-route clearance gate added to bundle_routes). The new gate is PAIRWISE across nets only; it says nothing about one net's route against itself.\n\nA spine may legally turn back alongside its own start — _spine_shape_error only refuses diagonals and 180-degree reversals, so (0,0) -> (100,0) -> (100,100) -> (0,100) is accepted. On such a spine the source station-0 leg and the target station-0 leg can land on the SAME line (both ends' first point share x = 0 there), and a net's own breakout leg can run back across its own lane. Measured example, module at 0cfc3f5 with the gate applied: net A routes as\n\n  (-10,150) (0,150) (0,-0.25) (100.25,-0.25) (100.25,100.25) (0,100.25) (0,90) (-10,90)\n\nwhose second segment (x = 0, y from 150 to -0.25) contains its own seventh segment (x = 0, y from 100.25 to 90). Same net, so it is not a short — but it is a trace that doubles back over itself, which the fab sees as one blob and which no downstream DRC gate in this plugin will catch.\n\nNot reachable through the product today: panel_tools.bus_plan refuses a bus with fewer than 2 nets, and with 2+ nets the new cross-net gate fires on this shape first. So this is a contract hole in the model, not a live defect.","created_at":"2026-08-25T06:26:08","updated_at":"2026-08-25T06:26:08","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus","drc","geometry","pcb"],"repro_steps":"BusGeom.bundle_routes(\n  spine  [(0,0), (100,0), (100,100), (0,100)],\n  nets   [\"A\"],\n  src    [(-10,150)],\n  tgt    [(-10,90)],\n  widths [0.2], clearance 0.3)\n-> ok == true, polylines[0] overlaps itself on the line x = 0."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a037a0963273febc5494eae4f8b87b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Bus clearance gate measures to pad CENTRES — a lane passing a pad at exactly pitch can still short on the pad's flash","description":"bundle_routes measures every pair of finished routes centreline-to-centreline and requires that pair's pitch_between, which is derived from the two TRACE widths plus clearance. That is exact for trace-against-trace: traces are emitted through add_trace_line with a Circle aperture (pcb/worker/pcb_worker/gerber.py:1096), so copper is the centreline dilated by w/2 and centreline gap minus (w_i + w_j)/2 IS the copper gap.\n\nIt is NOT exact where a route ENDS. A route's last point is a pad centre, and a pad's flash is its own aperture — a rectangle, roundrect or oval whose extents routinely dwarf a 0.2mm track. Measuring that endpoint as if it carried the track's own width understates the copper there by roughly (pad extent - track width) / 2 in every direction.\n\nWHY THE GATE CANNOT FIX THIS ITSELF: pad dimensions are not inputs to bundle_routes. The module is deliberately pure — plain numbers in, plain numbers out, no board model — and it is handed points, widths and a clearance. The pad's geometry never crosses that boundary. So the fix is either at the tool layer (which HAS the board and can gate on real pad extents after the plan returns) or by feeding per-endpoint extents into the geometry alongside the widths.\n\nRELATED, and probably the same root theme: PLG 01a0377ba268 records that pad HIT TESTING ignores pad size canvas-wide (pad_at is nearest-centre-within-1.27mm and never consults pad_width_mm/pad_height_mm, which the model carries). Two different surfaces both treating a pad as a point. Worth deciding once whether pad extents become a first-class thing the canvas and the geometry both consult.\n\nNOT BLOCKING the pin-to-pin batch: the reviewer that found it said so explicitly. The gate added at 3cdbfae is a strict improvement over what preceded it (which measured nothing metric at all), and this window is narrower than the hole it closed.","created_at":"2026-08-25T06:34:26","updated_at":"2026-08-25T06:34:26","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","clearance","drc","geometry","pads","pcb"],"environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 3cdbfae. Found by cold review of the whole-route clearance gate added in that commit.","repro_steps":"Not reproduced as a board; established from the model by review.\n\n1. Author a bus whose spine folds back, so a lane from a distant arm passes near another net's PAD.\n2. Place that pad so its CENTRE sits exactly pitch_between(w_i, w_j, clearance) from the lane's centreline.\n   The gate passes: measured centreline gap meets the requirement for two TRACK-width envelopes.\n   But the pad's aperture is much wider than the track, so the pad's own copper overlaps the lane.\n   Result: a short between two nets, committed as real copper.\n\nThe narrower the tracks relative to the pad, the wider the window."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a037ee82707da1b842c96ac35bd71a","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus target candidates are invisible until the TARGETS phase, which cannot be entered without hitting one","description":"OWNER'S REPORT, verbatim: \"I don't know what pads to hit with a bus. Each trace in a bus really only wants one target -- but I don't know what they are. I tried clicking one of the ones I thought was a target, and either I was wrong or there's a bug.\"\n\nMEASURED. The legal-target rings ARE drawn, correctly, from the same list the click picks from — but only once the tool is ALREADY in TARGETS. _draw_bus_preview gates the call:\n\n    if _bus_phase == BusPhase.TARGETS:\n        _draw_bus_targets()\n\n(pcb_canvas.gd:8655-8656). The click that ENDS the PATH phase, however, happens IN the PATH phase — _handle_bus_path_click resolves a pad through _bus_target_at and only then sets _bus_phase = BusPhase.TARGETS (pcb_canvas.gd:8186-8211).\n\nSo the user must successfully hit a legal target pad in order to be shown which pads were legal targets. The information needed to perform the gesture is released only as a reward for having performed it. That closed loop IS the owner's reported problem.\n\nWHAT MAKES THE FIX CHEAP: _bus_target_candidates (pcb_canvas.gd:8314-8337) depends only on _bus_nets and _bus_net_refs, both of which are fully populated the moment the SOURCES phase ends. Nothing about the candidate list requires the TARGETS phase to have started. The phase gate is the whole defect.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF, and why the rings alone are not the whole answer: the owner's belief that \"each trace really only wants one target\" is FALSE in the data model. _bus_target_candidates returns every pad on a picked net EXCEPT that net's own source, so a 3-pad net (a line through a series resistor, a multi-drop strobe) has several legal targets, and the TARGETS phase deliberately supports set/replace/clear per net (_assign_bus_target, pcb_canvas.gd:8283-8300). A display that names ONE target per net would be lying on exactly the boards where the guidance matters most.\n\nThe precedent for saying \"here is the likely one\" WITHOUT claiming it is the only one already exists in this codebase: PcbRatsnest.focus (pcb_ratsnest.gd:1031) computes nearest-unjoined-copper for a pad, and the single-trace tool already draws it (_trace_focus, pcb_canvas.gd:786, 7919). A per-net focus airwire drawn OVER the visible candidate rings is a suggestion layered on the full truth, which is the honest shape.\n\nRELATED AND SEPARATE: PLG 01a0377ba268 (pad hit testing ignores pad size). That one explains the \"or there's a bug\" half of the owner's report — on a pad larger than ~2.5mm a click on visible copper reads as clear board and silently appends a spine vertex instead of transitioning. The two are independent and BOTH must land for the tool to be drivable; neither substitutes for the other.\n\nREJECTED, recorded so it is not re-proposed: proximity auto-entry into TARGETS (owner's original proposal — enter the phase when the bus comes within ~1cm of any target pin). Refused because candidates are scattered across the board on any net with a mid-board part, so a corridor routed PAST a legal candidate would flip the mode unasked; and because bundle_routes refuses a target pad short of the spine's end, proximity would fire preferentially on pads that would be refused at commit anyway. Ruling: geometry may REVEAL a candidate, only an explicit gesture may change the mode.","created_at":"2026-08-25T07:59:32","updated_at":"2026-08-25T18:20:19","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","discoverability","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed at 22094fa, with the landed-target ring correction at a4bdf43.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at bd269a7. Owner-reported from live use 2026-08-25 after a full Minerva restart and a clean pcb plugin reinstall.","repro_steps":"1. Open a board with at least two multi-pad nets. Select the Bus tool.\n2. Click one pad per net to pick 2+ nets.\n3. Click clear of the pads to begin the path. Place a vertex or two.\n   OBSERVED: nothing on screen indicates which pads would be accepted as\n   targets. The user must guess.\n4. Click a pad you believe is a target.\n   If it is legal AND within 1.27mm of its centre: the phase advances and the\n   rings appear — showing, after the fact, the information needed to make the\n   click.\n   Otherwise: a 2-second status message, or (on a large pad) a silently\n   appended path vertex.\n\nEXPECTED: the pads eligible to end the path are visible from the moment the\nnet list is complete, before the click that needs them.\n\nORACLE for a fix: with 2+ nets picked and the tool in PATH, the draw path must\nemit candidate geometry for every entry in _bus_target_candidates. A test that\ndrives the tool into PATH and asserts the rendered candidate set equals the\npicked-from-list set would fail today and pass after."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a037eeca5079d59b7ffd9723473792","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"The Bus tool's phase is reported only by a 2-second transient — the modes are hard, but mute","description":"The Bus tool already has exactly the three hard modes the owner proposed building: enum BusPhase { SOURCES, PATH, TARGETS } (pcb_canvas.gd:793). The owner re-derived that architecture from the outside while driving the tool, which is the finding — the phases are not missing, they are ILLEGIBLE.\n\nWHERE THE PHASE IS REPORTED TODAY, in full:\n  * bus_tool_message -> _show_transient_status (PCBPanel.gd:1159), cleared on a 2.0s timer (PCBPanel.gd:4691-4697).\n  * A canvas teach line anchored to the most recent pick (_draw_bus_picks, pcb_canvas.gd:8586-8598). This one DOES persist until the state it describes changes — it is the good half — but it hangs off the last picked pad, which may be off-screen, under the cursor, or nowhere the user is looking while placing a distant vertex.\n\nThere is no fixed, always-present indicator of which phase the tool is in. A user who looks away for three seconds has no way to recover that fact except by acting and inferring from what happens — and the two ways it can go wrong (an illegal pad, a pad whose centre was missed) both produce either a message that has already expired or no message at all.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN ITS SEVERITY SUGGESTS: every phase's verb is different, and two of them are the same physical gesture with different meanings. A click clear of the pads ENDS the SOURCES phase, EXTENDS the path in PATH, and is inert in TARGETS. A click on a pad picks a net in SOURCES, ends the path in PATH, and assigns a target in TARGETS. The tool is unusable without knowing the mode, and the mode is the one thing it does not durably say.\n\nFIX SHAPE, not prescription: a persistent phase indicator that is always on screen while the tool is armed. Whether it belongs on the toolbar beside the tool button, pinned to the canvas, or on the cursor is open. What is NOT open, per the review that produced this item: it must not expire on a timer, and it must not require the user to find the last-picked pad to read it.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: changing any phase's entry or exit gesture. This item makes the existing state machine legible; whether the grammar itself changes is gated on the owner driving the tool once the two visibility defects — PLG 01a037ee8270 (candidates invisible until TARGETS) and PLG 01a0377ba268 (pad hit testing ignores pad size) — are fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-25T07:59:51","updated_at":"2026-08-25T09:19:33","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","discoverability","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"Fixed at 1e67ebc.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at bd269a7. Established from the code during the design review of the owner's hard-modes proposal, 2026-08-25.","repro_steps":"1. Select the Bus tool. Pick two nets. Click clear of the pads to start the path.\n2. Wait three seconds without clicking, then look at the screen.\n   OBSERVED: the status bar has cleared. The only remaining phase signal is a\n   small teach line beside the most recently picked pad, which may be far from\n   where the path is being drawn.\n3. Ask: is the tool in PATH or TARGETS? There is no fixed place to look.\n\nORACLE for a fix: an indicator whose presence does not depend on elapsed time\nor on any pick's screen position. A test that advances the tool through all\nthree phases and asserts the indicator reports the current phase at each step,\nwith no timer advanced, would fail today."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a037ef247f7270acede1ed114e92bc","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Non-axis bus spines (45-degree snap, or snaps off) — deferred, and it is new geometry rather than a flag","description":"DEFERRED BY THE OWNER, 2026-08-25, at the moment of proposing it. Filed so the measurement behind the deferral is not re-derived later.\n\nTHE ASK: the canvas Bus tool's path phase gains an override from Manhattan to either 45-degree snaps or free angle (\"snaps off\").\n\nWHY IT LOOKS CHEAP AND IS NOT. offset_polyline in pcb_bus_geometry.gd is genuinely angle-agnostic — it does proper miter/bevel joins with a MITER_LIMIT, and that module exists precisely BECAUSE the router's route_bus rigid-translates its perpendicular at bends and collapses pitch to zero. So the lane-offsetting core would survive a diagonal spine. Everything downstream of the lanes would not:\n\n  1. _spine_shape_error refuses a segment with non-zero dx AND dy at EXACT zero, and the module documents why: exactness is what lets _axis_unit return a unit vector with no rounding, so every emitted segment is axis-aligned in float rather than to within an ulp. Relaxing this is not deleting a check; it is giving up the property the emitted geometry is built on.\n\n  2. The breakout construction (pad --parallel--> corner --perpendicular--> lane), _station_point and _departure_stations all work in each end's perpendicular frame and COPY coordinates rather than recompute them. All of it must be re-derived for rotated frames.\n\n  3. THE CLEARANCE GATE BREAKS, and this is the decisive one. _segment_separation's fast path states its own precondition: every segment reaching it is axis-aligned, an axis-aligned segment IS its own bounding box, so \"the boxes overlap\" and \"the segments share a point\" are the same statement. For diagonals that equivalence is FALSE — two diagonal segments with overlapping boxes and no intersection would report separation 0.0, a phantom short. It fails closed, so a 45-degree bus would be effectively UNCOMMITTABLE until the separation math is rewritten. And this gate is load-bearing: the module authors copper with no DRC gate downstream of it, so the measurement IS the gate.\n\n  You cannot carve out \"diagonal spine, axis-aligned legs\" to dodge this — bundle_routes measures whole polylines, legs included.\n\nSo the true cost is roughly four functions of new geometry plus re-proving the pitch invariant at 135-degree bends, in the one module its own header calls \"the whole risk in a bus tool\".\n\nTHE STANDING RULE THIS RUNS INTO: this owner routes Manhattan — every segment exactly horizontal or vertical. mhroute exists in ccsandbox because the plugin autorouter could not hold that line. Every output of a \"snaps off\" mode would be forbidden by the owner's own rule the moment it was produced.\n\nRECOMMENDATION carried in from the review that produced this item: reject free angle outright; consider 45-degree ONLY if a real board demands it, and if so do it geometry-first — rewrite _segment_separation and the station frames, pin them with plain-number tests, and only then let any UI reach them. Do not couple this to bus UX work; it would chain a days-long UI fix to a geometry campaign.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:00:14","updated_at":"2026-08-25T08:00:14","priority":3,"tags":["bus-tool","deferred","geometry","manhattan","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0380585b472e4b764bf7fbc5ecc26","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Pad rendering ignores a land's own rotation, so rendered geometry and hit-test geometry now disagree for rotated lands","description":"_draw_component_pads (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:3168) draws every land with pad_rot = -comp.rotation and IGNORES the land's own `rotation` field, which the model does carry (pcb_component.gd:294). A footprint with a land rotated within its own footprint therefore renders at the wrong orientation.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW THAN IT DID YESTERDAY. Both pcb_ratsnest and the new copper-extent hit test (pcb_component.pin_copper_distance) honour the land's rotation. Before that change, the hit test was a disc drawn from the pin centre and was equally wrong for every shape, so nothing disagreed with the renderer in a way a user could see. Now the hit test is CORRECT and the renderer is not, which means for exactly those lands the copper you can see is not the copper you can click.\n\nThat inverts a rule this canvas states about itself in several places — \"rendered geometry == hit-test geometry\", written in the bus tool's candidate region as the reason a ring the user can see is a ring the click can land on. This is the one place it no longer holds.\n\nNOT FIXED HERE because it is a rendering defect found during a picking task, and repairing it means deciding how the land's rotation composes with the component's in the DRAW path — the same composition the hit test now does, but the draw path also swaps width/height at 90 degrees in places and the two conventions have to be reconciled deliberately rather than in passing.\n\nWORTH DOING TOGETHER with the finding below it in this batch (get_pad_world_transform), since both are about the same unsettled question: what the canonical land-to-world transform is, and which code owns it.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:24:41","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:58","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["canvas","pads","pcb","rendering","wysiwyg"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Renderer and hit test share get_pad_world_transform (which also lost a mirrored offset sign and a -90 modulo defect).","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 3e40519. Found under file-don't-fix while moving pad hit testing onto copper extents (01a0377ba268).","repro_steps":"1. Load a board with a footprint whose pad entry carries a non-zero `rotation`\n   (a land turned within its own footprint, not the whole part turned).\n2. Look at the pad on the canvas: it is drawn at the component's rotation only.\n3. Click the visible copper at the end of the land's TRUE long axis.\n   With the copper-extent hit test in place the click resolves to the pad\n   correctly, while the drawn rectangle says the copper is elsewhere.\n\nThe two disagree, and the drawing is the wrong one.\n\nORACLE for a fix: place a land with rotation 90 inside an unrotated part, then\ncompare the rendered rectangle's corners against\npcb_component.pin_copper_distance's zero-distance region. They must coincide."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03805b8b67365b9288348c49d84cd","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"get_pad_world_transform has no caller and disagrees with the live rotation convention in two ways","description":"pcb/ui/model/pcb_component.gd:559 get_pad_world_transform() has NO caller anywhere in the repo, and it disagrees with the convention every live path uses:\n\n  1. It rotates by deg_to_rad(rotation) where get_transform() — and the worker, and KiCad — use deg_to_rad(-rotation). The sign is inverted.\n  2. It swaps width and height at 90 degrees, which the draw path does not.\n\nSo it is wrong in two independent ways AND uncalled. Reported rather than deleted, per the standing rule that no current caller does not mean dead code: a consumer may simply be sequenced later. That is exactly the hazard here — the next person who needs a land-to-world transform will find a function with the right name, no callers to contradict it, and two baked-in sign and axis errors.\n\nRESOLUTION IS A DECISION, not a repair: either correct it to match get_transform's convention and leave it for its future caller, or delete it and let that caller be written against get_transform directly. Deleting is cheaper and loses nothing, since nothing calls it. What must NOT happen is leaving it as-is under a name that promises correctness.\n\nRELATED: PLG 01a0380585b4 (pad rendering ignores a land's own rotation). Both are the same unsettled question — what the canonical land-to-world transform is, and which code owns it. The copper-extent hit test now answers it for PICKING; the draw path and this function have not been reconciled with that answer.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:24:54","updated_at":"2026-08-25T19:15:27","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["dead-code","geometry","pads","pcb","trap"],"resolution":"get_pad_world_transform no longer exists in pcb_canvas.gd (0 occurrences at 7092f13). Docket cleanup 2026-08-25.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 3e40519. Found under file-don't-fix while moving pad hit testing onto copper extents (01a0377ba268).","repro_steps":"Static, no board needed.\n\n1. grep the repo for get_pad_world_transform — the definition at\n   pcb/ui/model/pcb_component.gd:559 is the only hit.\n2. Compare its rotation term against get_transform() in the same file: one uses\n   deg_to_rad(rotation), the other deg_to_rad(-rotation).\n3. Compare its width/height handling at 90 degrees against the draw path in\n   pcb_canvas._draw_component_pads, which does not swap.\n\nORACLE for a fix: for any component rotation, the transform this returns must\nplace a land's corners at the same world points get_transform composed with the\nland's offset and rotation does. Today it does not, for any non-zero rotation."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03811b4e37b6690b1cc7c1770e0fc","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"An agent cannot see the bus tool at all — no MCP verb reads its target guidance, and get_image omits the whole preview","description":"Two independent mechanisms, one consequence: while a human is authoring a bus, an agent asked to help cannot see any of it.\n\n  1. NO VERB. bus_target_guidance() (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd) is now the single source of what the canvas claims about a bus in progress — per net: the source pad, every pad that may legally end it, which one the ratsnest suggests, and which target has landed. It is public and is exactly the shape an MCP verb wants. No verb exposes it.\n\n  2. NOT IN THE PICTURE EITHER. CAPTURE_MIRRORED_FIELDS (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:10578-10608) carries _trace_focus but neither tool_mode nor any bus-tool state, so a get_image capture taken mid-bus shows no picks, no spine, no ghost lanes and no target guidance. This looks deliberate — every tool preview is excluded, uniformly — but the block's own doc names the hazard it creates, and the guidance added this cycle is now part of what the human sees and the agent does not.\n\nWHY THIS IS FILED AS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A NICE-TO-HAVE: the standing rule for this product is that a capability has a GUI affordance AND an MCP verb, because the owner drives the GUI and agents drive everything through MCP. The bus tool now has a rich guidance surface on one side of that rule and nothing on the other. It is also the exact failure this tool has already been burned by once — the picking phase's original invisibility survived tests and review precisely because it was the one part of the tool with no agent-side twin, so nothing could exercise it.\n\nWORTH DECIDING TOGETHER rather than patching one: whether the answer is a read verb over the guidance, tool state in the capture mirror, or both. A verb alone leaves the picture wrong; the capture alone gives an agent pixels it must re-derive meaning from.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:37:59","updated_at":"2026-08-25T08:37:59","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","mcp","parity","pcb","ux"],"environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 22094fae. Found under file-don't-fix while making bus target eligibility visible.","repro_steps":"1. In the GUI, arm the Bus tool and pick two nets. Rings appear marking every\n   pad each net may end on, with a halo on the likely one.\n2. Ask an agent, over MCP, which pads the bus may end on.\n   OBSERVED: there is no verb that answers. The state exists only in the\n   canvas.\n3. Have the agent take a board image instead.\n   OBSERVED: the capture shows the board with no bus picks, no rings, no\n   spine, no guidance — as if no tool were armed.\n\nEXPECTED: an agent can read what the human is being shown, by verb or by\npicture.\n\nORACLE for a fix: with a bus part-authored in the GUI, an agent-side read must\nreturn the same per-net eligible set the canvas rings, sourced from\nbus_target_guidance rather than recomputed, so the two cannot drift."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03811e64379dab7cad4fdcdf417c9","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"The ratsnest cache walks the whole board every redraw just to build its cache key","description":"_ratsnest() (pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:2720) caches the result of PcbRatsnest.solve(), but builds its cache key by calling PcbRatsnest.extract(data) FIRST — every redraw, unconditionally. The surrounding comment labels extract() O(board) itself. So the cache spares the solve and not the walk, and while the ratsnest is shown the board is traversed once per frame during pan and zoom.\n\nThe cost is invisible on a small board and grows with the design, which is the shape of performance defect that gets discovered at exactly the wrong moment.\n\nNOT FIXED HERE because a correct fix is a cache-invalidation design question, not an edit: the key has to become something cheaper that still changes whenever the extraction would — a board revision counter, or a change-journal sequence number, both of which exist in the model in some form and neither of which has been checked for whether it covers every mutation extract() is sensitive to. Getting that wrong caches a STALE ratsnest, which is worse than a slow correct one.\n\nRECORDED AS PRECEDENT, since it shaped adjacent work: the bus target suggestion added this cycle deliberately does NOT follow this pattern. It asks the ratsnest once per net PICK and holds the answer, rather than re-extracting on the draw path — see _refresh_bus_suggestions and _bus_suggested_refs.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:38:12","updated_at":"2026-08-25T08:38:16","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["canvas","pcb","performance","ratsnest"],"environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 22094fae. Found under file-don't-fix while adding bus target guidance.","repro_steps":"Static, established by reading.\n\n1. pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:2720 — _ratsnest() calls PcbRatsnest.extract(data)\n   before consulting the cache, to derive the key.\n2. The call site is on the draw path, so it runs on every queue_redraw —\n   including every frame of a pan or zoom while the ratsnest is visible.\n3. The comment beside it names extract() as O(board).\n\nORACLE for a fix: with the ratsnest shown, a pan across a large board must not\nscale its per-frame cost with component count. A counter incremented inside\nextract(), read before and after a pan of N frames, would show N walks today\nand 0 after."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03812c6197ba2aa9a3f8242a040a4","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb GDScript syntax checks are safe while Minerva is running — use gd-syntax-check.sh, never the test runner","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:39:09","updated_at":"2026-08-25T08:39:09","tags":["gdscript","godot","pcb","static-gate","testing"],"value":"pcb/scripts/gd-syntax-check.sh <file.gd> [...] | --all\n\nSAFE TO RUN AT ANY TIME, INCLUDING WHILE MINERVA IS UP. It builds a disposable scratch project under $TMPDIR and runs `godot --headless --check-only --script` against a symlink to the plugins repo. It never reads or writes any Minerva checkout, so it never takes the project's single-instance lock.\n\nCONTRAST with pcb/scripts/run-gd-tests.sh, which EXECUTES suites via `godot --headless --path <minerva>/src`. That opens the Minerva project, takes its single-instance lock, and TERMINATES a running Minerva app, losing anything unsaved. Sequence those: shut Minerva down first.\n\nExit codes: 0 every file parsed, 1 at least one parse error, 2 harness problem (no godot on PATH, unreadable file, path outside the repo).\n\nWHAT IT PROVES AND DOES NOT. It reports parse errors only. Resolver-family diagnostics (\"Could not find base class\", \"Could not find type\", missing preloads of Minerva host paths) are expected in the scratch project and are filtered out — the plugin's files extend class_name types that only exist inside Minerva. Measured on Godot 4.6.2: when a file has BOTH kinds, the parser error is the ONLY one printed, since parsing precedes resolution and aborts it. So a file whose diagnostics are all resolver-family is proven parse-clean, which is the entire claim.\n\nONE resolver message IS re-promoted to a real failure: a missing preload whose path points back into minerva-plugins. A pcb file preloading a pcb file that does not exist is a typo, not a host-path absence.\n\nINVISIBLE HERE: type errors, wrong member names, signal wiring, and every runtime question. Those need the real host via run-gd-tests.sh.","component":"pcb","key":"gd-syntax-check","confidence":"measured","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03820df787ea28bbd192d4048bad7","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Transient status timers are never cancelled — a second message is wiped early by the first message's timer","description":"_show_transient_status (pcb/ui/PCBPanel.gd:4817) creates a fresh 2-second SceneTreeTimer for each message and never cancels the one before it. The timers are independent, so the FIRST timer to fire clears whatever text is on screen at that moment — including a later message that has been up for only a fraction of its own two seconds.\n\nTwo messages 0.1s apart therefore leave the second one visible for 1.9s instead of 2.0s. Two messages 1.9s apart leave the second visible for 0.1s.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ARITHMETIC: the messages most likely to arrive in quick succession are refusals, which is exactly when the user needs to read one. A rapid click that produces \"that pad is on no net\" followed by a considered click that produces a real explanation can leave the explanation on screen for a blink.\n\nThis is the same channel whose expiry made the bus tool's phase unreadable and drove the phase badge onto the toolbar button. Fixing the timer does not remove the need for durable surfaces for durable state — a two-second flash is the wrong channel for anything that persists regardless of whether the timer is honest.\n\nNOT FIXED HERE: found during a badge task, and the repair is a small ownership change (hold the pending timer and cancel or restart it) that deserves its own test rather than riding a UI commit.","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:54:33","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:00","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["canvas","pcb","status","ux"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Supersede-by-token on the transient status timer; real-time oracle in test_transient_status.gd.","environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 1e67ebc. Found under file-don't-fix while adding the bus phase badge.","repro_steps":"1. Trigger any two transient status messages less than 2 seconds apart — for\n   example two refused clicks in quick succession with a canvas tool armed.\n2. Watch the second message.\n   OBSERVED: it clears early, at 2.0s after the FIRST message rather than\n   after itself.\n\nORACLE for a fix: emit two messages T seconds apart for T < 2, and assert the\nsecond is still displayed at first_message_time + 2.0 + epsilon. Today it is\ngone."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03836f7d9747c8511236601a89aaf","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"codex exec cannot use its sandbox on this host — feed the diff on stdin instead of letting it shell out","created_at":"2026-08-25T09:18:41","updated_at":"2026-08-25T09:18:41","tags":["codex","cross-provider","review","tooling"],"value":"The `codex` CLI is on PATH (~/.nvm/.../bin/codex) and is how the cross-provider review seat gets spent WITHOUT Minerva running — it needs no MCP.\n\nTHE TRAP: every `codex exec --sandbox read-only` shell command dies during sandbox startup with\n\n    bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted\n\nCodex's Linux sandbox is bubblewrap and needs to create user namespaces, which this host does not permit. The failure happens BEFORE any command runs, so Codex cannot read the repo at all. It then (correctly) refuses to review rather than speculating — you get a polite non-answer after burning tokens and several minutes.\n\nWHAT WORKS: give it no shell to need. Pipe the material on stdin — Codex appends it as a `<stdin>` block — and tell it explicitly not to run commands:\n\n    codex exec --sandbox read-only \"<prompt>\" < input.txt\n\nBuild input.txt as the diff PLUS the surrounding code the diff does not show. A diff alone is not enough for questions like \"is this rotation sign right\", because the function it composes with is not in the diff. Roughly: `git diff BASE..HEAD -- <subdir>/` then `sed -n` excerpts of the functions the review questions name. ~110KB was fine.\n\nDo NOT reach for `--sandbox danger-full-access` to get the shell back: it gives an unsupervised agent write access to a repo mid-batch, which is a worse trade than assembling the input.\n\nIgnore the `rmcp::transport::worker ... 127.0.0.1:9315/mcp` errors it prints — that is Codex failing to reach a configured MCP server and it does not stop the review.","component":"codex","key":"cross-provider-review","confidence":"measured","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a039ac850c7324a0b89920c927b630","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"The installed runtime bundles its own pcb_worker copy, which shadows the repo source — worker changes are invisible until it is refreshed","description":"MEASURED, and reproducible with no Godot involved.\n\nThe pcb-plugin Go binary resolves its Python at startup and logs the choice:\n\n    pcb-plugin: worker dir=/home/imran/github/minerva-plugins/pcb/worker,\n                python=/home/imran/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/0.5.0/bin/python3\n\nThe worker DIR is the repo. The PYTHON is the installed runtime bundle — and that bundle carries its own COPY of pcb_worker in its site-packages, which shadows the repo tree the worker dir points at. So `python -m pcb_worker` runs the INSTALLED snapshot, not the source anyone is editing.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE, by timestamp:\n  installed  .../runtime/0.5.0/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pcb_worker/methods.py  2026-08-22 12:04  — no \"zone_fill\" in _HANDLERS\n  repo       pcb/worker/pcb_worker/methods.py                                      2026-08-22 23:46  — has it\n`zone_fill` entered _HANDLERS in 4ff8aa9 at 2026-08-22 23:31, ELEVEN HOURS after the installed snapshot was taken. The repo venv is an editable install and resolves correctly; it is simply not the interpreter the binary picks.\n\nREPRODUCTION, no test harness:\n    cd pcb && python3 scripts/e2e_route_stdio.py ./pcb-plugin <req>.json pcb.zone_fill\n    -> {\"error\": {\"kind\": \"internal\", \"message\": \"unknown method: 'zone_fill'\"}, \"ok\": false}\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. test_zone_fill_transport.gd is designated real-worker in EXPECTED_SUITES, so it refuses to accept its own canned fallback and fails the run — correctly. It is the ONLY failure in a 65-suite run (64/65 reporting, 6418 assertions passed). The suite's own printerr says it plainly: \"canned fallback engaged — do not trust the green assertions\". So the gate works; what it is reporting is a stale install, not a defect in the code under test.\n\nIT IS WORSE THAN ONE RED SUITE. Every worker change since 2026-08-22 12:04 is invisible to the running plugin and to every E2E path on this machine, whether or not a test covers it. That is the most likely explanation for the \"pcb WORKER was unavailable / worker_unavailable on every attempt\" reported during the 2026-08-24 bus session — the same shadowing, presenting as an absent capability rather than an old one.\n\nA PLUGIN REINSTALL DID NOT FIX IT. The owner reinstalled the pcb plugin on 2026-08-25 and the snapshot is still the 08-22 12:04 one — the version-pinned runtime/0.5.0 directory is reused rather than rebuilt, so reinstalling the plugin does not refresh the Python payload inside it.\n\nWORTH DECIDING, not assumed: whether the runtime bundle should carry pcb_worker at all. If the binary already resolves a worker DIR, a bundle that also ships the package guarantees exactly this shadowing whenever the two drift. The alternatives — bundle the interpreter only and let worker dir supply the package, or version the bundle so a stale one is detected rather than silently preferred — are a design call, not a repair.\n\nNOT OUR REGRESSION, recorded so the next full run does not re-litigate it: the batch ef974c8..55196d7 touches no worker or zone file (pcb_component.gd, PcbAnnotationHost.gd, pcb_canvas.gd, PCBPanel.gd, four gd suites, tools.md, EXPECTED_SUITES). The failure reproduces straight from the binary with no GDScript in the path.","created_at":"2026-08-25T16:06:42","updated_at":"2026-08-27T18:22:53","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["build","e2e","install","pcb","runtime","worker"],"environment":"Minerva pcb plugin, minerva-plugins main at 55196d7. Found by the first full gd suite run since 2026-08-23, on Linux.","repro_steps":"1. Note the binary's own startup line:\n     cd pcb && timeout 15 ./pcb-plugin < /dev/null 2>&1 | grep \"worker dir\"\n   It reports a repo worker dir and an INSTALLED-RUNTIME python.\n\n2. Ask that interpreter what it imports:\n     RT=~/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/0.5.0\n     $RT/bin/python3 -c \"import pcb_worker; print(pcb_worker.__file__)\"\n   It answers with the runtime's own site-packages copy, NOT the repo.\n\n3. Drive the real worker for a method added after that copy was taken:\n     python3 scripts/e2e_route_stdio.py ./pcb-plugin <req>.json pcb.zone_fill\n   OBSERVED: {\"kind\":\"internal\",\"message\":\"unknown method: 'zone_fill'\"}\n   EXPECTED: the method the repo source has registered.\n\n4. Confirm the repo venv is fine, so the fault is interpreter choice and not\n   the package:\n     pcb/worker/.venv/bin/python -c \"from pcb_worker import methods; print('zone_fill' in methods._HANDLERS)\"\n   -> True\n\nORACLE for a fix: for any method registered in the repo's _HANDLERS, the\nbinary's real-worker path must answer it rather than \"unknown method\". A\none-line probe over the set difference between repo _HANDLERS and whatever the\nbinary answers would fail today and is cheap to keep."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03a26c248748c984d76a93d8fb687","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Bus tool PATH phase: double-click ends the path, airlines show each net's target, refusals are loud","description":"OWNER'S REPORT, paraphrased: with 3 nets picked and a path drawn, double-click did not move the tool from the routing phase (PATH) to the endpoint phase (TARGETS). The first double-click made 3 grey traces appear; the second collapsed everything to one red trace carrying the drawn path, which read as single-trace routing mode. Expected: the double-click ends the path and shows airlines to the pads each net can end on.\n\nMEASURED (all pcb_canvas.gd at 5a8e292):\n- _handle_bus_click (:8195) makes double-click inert in PATH by design. The ONLY exit from PATH is a click on a legal target pad (_handle_bus_path_click :8280, which sets _bus_phase = TARGETS). The recorded ruling on PLG 01a037ee (\"only an explicit gesture may change the mode\") is satisfied by a double-click — it is a gesture, not proximity.\n- The grey traces are the per-net ghost lanes from bus_plan, drawn once the spine has ≥2 vertices (:8918). Each double-click's first press appended a vertex; the second press was ignored.\n- The red trace is the spine tinted BUS_REFUSAL_COLOR (:8898): bus_plan refused, so the ghosts vanish. The reason is drawn as small text beside the FIRST vertex (:8931) — easy to miss, and the 2s status transient is the only other channel.\n- 01a037ee was resolved (22094fa, a4bdf43) by drawing 5px, 45%-alpha rings on eligible pads plus an 8px halo + label on the ratsnest suggestion (_draw_bus_targets :8792). No airline. On a real board this did not register as guidance at all.\n\nTHREE TASKS, sequential, one commit each:\nT1. Double-click clear of the pads in PATH ends the path and enters TARGETS (spine must already have ≥2 vertices; otherwise the existing \"needs 2 points\" message). The double-click's own first press must NOT leave a stray vertex under the cursor. Keep the TARGETS double-click commit as-is. Esc ladder peels back to PATH.\nT2. From the moment the spine has ≥1 vertex, draw an airline from the spine's LAST vertex (or live preview point) to each net's suggested target pad, in that net's colour, dashed like _trace_focus. Rings stay. A landed target replaces its net's airline with a solid line to the landed pad.\nT3. A bus_plan refusal is loud: the reason goes to the status bar (not the 2s transient — hold until the state changes) and the phase badge tints to the refusal colour while refused. The small first-vertex label may stay.\n\nORACLE: a test drives the tool to PATH with 3 nets and 2 vertices, sends a double-click on clear board, and asserts _bus_phase == TARGETS and the spine still has exactly 2 vertices. A second test asserts airline geometry exists for every net in bus_target_guidance() while in PATH.\n\nReview: task-cycle, with a batch Codex review over the combined diff at the end. Codex is fed the diff on stdin (PLG 01a03836).","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:20:13","updated_at":"2026-08-25T18:56:39","priority":1,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"Landed on minerva-plugins main as 5a8e292..c43510a: fe3316e (T1 double-click ends PATH, drops only its own vertex), 9cf4533 (T2 per-net airlines from bus_target_guidance, dashed suggested / solid landed), 40a3737 (T3 refusal held in the status lead + badge tinted BUS_REFUSAL_COLOR; PATH teach names the double-click), c43510a (batch-review fix: press-appended flag, loud missing-net checks, measured pins). Reviews: Fable cold review must_fix→resolved; Codex must_fix→one Fable-judged fix round→Codex re-check approve. Scoped run with Minerva down, all green: test_bus_tool 148, test_pcb_panel_ui 200, test_pcb_ratsnest 136, test_workspace_tools 1189, test_pcb_single_trace_tool 98, test_pcb_trace_tool 77. Deferred full suite not yet run over this range. Codex staleness finding filed as PLG 01a03a43."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03a43f0f97183994f6e0246fe38f7","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Bus tool preview, held refusal and badge can go stale after an undo or external board edit mid-gesture","description":"Surfaced by the Codex batch check on PLG 01a03a26 (bus double-click / airlines / loud refusal). NOT fixed in that batch — it is the memo's pre-existing design, and widening its key is a separate decision.\n\n_bus_current_plan memoises bus_plan on [nets, spine, layer, target pins] only (_bus_plan_cache_key); board data revision is not part of the key. _announce_bus_refusal runs only from the bus gestures (clicks, Esc ladder, reset). So if the board changes underneath an in-progress bus — an undo, an MCP edit, a component move from another surface — that alters clearance or pad positions, the ghost lanes, the spine tint, the held status refusal and the badge colour all keep showing the previous answer until the next bus click.\n\nTo measure first: does set_data / the data changed signal already reset the bus tool (which would make this moot)? If it does, close as not-a-bug and say where. If it does not, the fix is to fold a data revision into the cache key and call _announce_bus_refusal from the data-changed path.\n\nOracle: start a bus, get a clean plan, move a target pad via the model so the lanes now violate clearance, do not click; assert bus_refusal() is non-empty and bus_refusal_changed fired.","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:52:06","updated_at":"2026-08-25T18:52:06","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03a500531781486f800dac3c5b53d","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus tool cannot be finished by the natural gesture — double-click on the last landed target is silently inert, and the PATH teach line never names the layer","description":"OWNER'S REPORT: \"no way to finalize the bus -- double-click after targets assigned doesn't complete the workflow\" and \"I don't have a great way to think about either F.Cu or B.Cu.\"\n\nMEASURED (pcb_canvas.gd at 5c5145d):\n- _dispatch_bus_click returns before dispatch when the double-click's second press lands on ANY pad (:~8300). That guard exists so the press that lands the last target cannot also commit. The cost: double-clicking the last target pad — the most natural \"I'm done\" gesture — does nothing and SAYS nothing. Commit is reachable only by double-click clear of the pads, or Enter, neither of which is named anywhere on the canvas in TARGETS (_bus_targets_status lists nets still needing a target; once all are landed it does not say how to commit).\n- _bus_layer is frozen from the toolbar's author layer at _begin_bus_path (:~8465) and never shown in PATH except in the small first-vertex label; the teach line says \"Path on top\" using the raw layer id, and nothing says the layer was fixed at the first click or how to get a bus on B.Cu (choose the layer BEFORE the path).\n\nEXPECTED:\n1. In TARGETS, once every net has a target, a double-click on a landed target pad commits (the landing press and the committing press are distinguishable: the pad already IS that net's target, so the re-assignment is a no-op and the second press can carry the commit). If the plan is refused the refusal is what the user sees, as today.\n2. The TARGETS teach/status text, once all targets are landed, says how to commit (\"double-click a target or clear of the pads, or Enter\").\n3. The PATH teach line names the layer in the user's terms (F.Cu/B.Cu display name) and says it was fixed when the path began.\n\nORACLE: drive 3 nets to all-landed, double-click the last landed pad → traces serialized == 3. Today: 0. And with a spine started, bus_teach_line() contains the layer's display name.","created_at":"2026-08-25T19:05:17","updated_at":"2026-08-26T01:02:45","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"Double-click on the last landed target commits (first press's clear is taken back); teach lines name F.Cu/B.Cu via PcbLayerStack.canon_to_kicad. minerva-plugins 731ecc0; Fable cold review + Codex final check passed; test_bus_tool 223/223.","environment":"minerva-plugins main at 5c5145d, manifest-lane side-load. Owner-reported from live use on smart-remote v2, 2026-08-25, after the PLG 01a03a26 batch."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03a5034c07257896673e358cdf785","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Bus commits even when its plan is bad, and can jump layers: refusals become DRC marks on real copper; a layer switch mid-path becomes a via station","description":"OWNER'S REPORTS: \"I can't via the bus to jump collisions\" and, expanding the scope: \"even bad bus routing commits on final double-click — because we can then perform corrections, which we can't do until we have a bus / traces to correct.\"\n\nPART A — COMMIT ANYWAY (owner's addition; sequenced FIRST because it unblocks correction of everything else).\nWHAT EXISTS: bundle_routes returns {ok:false, error} and NOTHING else on any refusal, and _commit_bus lands nothing when ok is false. The user is walled off from the copper they asked for, and the only remedy is redrawing the spine blind.\nDIRECTION: split refusals into two classes. (1) UNBUILDABLE — no geometry can exist: no nets, missing source/target/width, <2 distinct spine points, a diagonal segment. These stay refusals. (2) BAD BUT BUILDABLE — geometry exists and violates a rule: lane/pad clearance, lanes crossing at an end, a target short of the spine's end, a segment too short for its fan-outs, doubled-back spine, self-overlap. For these bundle_routes STILL returns the polylines it built, with ok:false and the error attached, and the final double-click / Enter COMMITS them as ordinary traces plus a DRC finding carrying the reason (the same channel other DRC violations use, so the user sees the mark on the board and can drag, delete or re-route a single trace). The live preview keeps its red tint and held status; what changes is that commit is not refused. The MCP verbs get the same behaviour with the finding in their result.\nORACLE A: the fixture spine that today refuses with a clearance error commits N traces and one DRC finding naming the two nets and the distance; an UNBUILDABLE spine still commits nothing.\n\nPART B — VIA STATION (original scope).\nWHAT EXISTS: a bus is ONE layer, frozen at _begin_bus_path, and pcb_bus_geometry.gd has no via concept at all. A collision with existing copper can only be avoided by redrawing the spine around it.\nDIRECTION: the spine becomes a list of segments each carrying a layer. A layer switch while in PATH (toolbar layer change, or a key) does not restart the bus; it marks the NEXT vertex as a via station. At a station bundle_routes emits, per lane, a via at that lane's offset on the station's perpendicular (the same cumulative_offsets that place the lanes), and the lane continues on the new layer. Lanes keep order and pitch; if the board's via rules need a wider pitch, the station widens locally (fan to via pitch and back) — the only new geometry.\nMinimal first form: straight spine across the station (a via, not a bend), one switch per bus, via rules from design_rules. A station within fan-out distance of either end, or a via pitch the lanes cannot widen to, is a BAD-BUT-BUILDABLE case under Part A (commit with the mark), not a refusal.\nORACLE B: a 3-net bus with one station commits 3 traces with two layer runs each and 3 vias on the station's perpendicular at the lane offsets; Manhattan preserved; adjacent-via clearance holds.\n\nNOT in this DCR: automatic collision detection choosing where to via; 45-degree spines (PLG 01a037ef); multiple stations.","created_at":"2026-08-25T19:05:30","updated_at":"2026-08-26T01:02:48","priority":2,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","geometry","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03b87473c7641b5a43b5b351adf3d","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Proposed bus/candidate vias ignore design_rules via size — make_via hardcodes 0.8/0.4 and _via_dimensions never rescues it","description":"RoutingWorkspace._create_candidate_for_route builds every ingested via with PcbRouteCandidate.make_via's hardcoded 0.8/0.4 defaults, so a candidate via never picks up design_rules.via_diameter_mm/via_drill_mm at proposal time. _via_dimensions only substitutes when the stamped value is 0, and 0.8 is never 0, so it never rescues. A proposed bus via therefore renders at 0.8/0.4 regardless of board rules while the direct-commit path (bus_commit_plan) honours them. Found while implementing the bus via station (DCR 01a03a5034 part B).</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T00:45:16","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:01","severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","pcb","routing-workspace","via"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. PcbViaDimensions is the one via-size rule (proposal, candidate, commit, bus, sidecar restore)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03b875507706ca90352b185ff8092","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Bus ghosts carry findings but validation stays \"unchecked\", and witnesses vanish when candidate rendering is off","description":"Two gaps in how a bad-but-buildable bus proposal (DCR 01a03a5034 part A) surfaces its findings on ghosts: (1) bus_propose_plan calls set_findings but never sets the candidate's `validation`, so the candidate list shows no violation badge even though the witness draws; setting it collides with the draft-check state machine's prior-snapshot logic, which needs a decision. (2) witness_draw_items is gated on _candidates_active() (show_route_candidates), so findings on ghosts are invisible with no hint whenever candidate rendering is off.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T00:45:20","updated_at":"2026-08-26T00:45:20","severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","drc","pcb","routing-workspace"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03b8785e47c1d899c06629239ec07","type":"hint","status":"validated","title":"Bus follow-ups: route_bus_direct dry-run, capped findings sentence, via-to-track clearance, batch history for bus commits, misnamed geometry test suite","description":"Discovered during DCR 01a03a5034 (bus commit-anyway + via station); filed, not fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-26T00:45:32","updated_at":"2026-08-26T01:02:53","tags":["bus-tool","follow-up","pcb"],"value":"(a) minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct has no dry-run — now that a bad bus commits, an agent's only way to see findings first is commit+undo; a `preview: true` arg returning bus_plan's findings would be cheap. (b) bus_findings_sentence joins every finding with no cap; a reversed 3-net bus yields ~7 findings and a very long status line — \"first N, +M more\" changes the message contract tests assert on. (c) Neither pcb_bus_geometry.gd nor _clearance_findings models via-to-track clearance, only track-to-track; the staggered station fan keeps margins by construction but nothing measures it. (d) bus_commit_plan uses bare save_to_history not begin_batch/end_batch, so a bus does not bump board_revision the way place_via does. (e) test_pcb_bus_geometry.gd covers only offset_polyline/pitch_between/cumulative_offsets while bundle_routes is pinned by test_bus_breakout_geometry.gd — names invert the split.","key":"pcb.bus.followups.dcr_01a03a5034","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ba65b3174378e57ca2d313fabd7","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus breakout legs run through foreign pads with no finding — four shorts at U3 that bundle_routes, the verb reply and connectivity DRC all missed","description":"Live MCP test on smart-remote v2 (minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct, nets I2S1_SD/WS/BCLK, U1S.GPIO6/5/4 → U3.7/3/6, via station at (45,86.5) onto top). The committed target legs run straight down U3's 1 mm LGA pad columns: I2S1_SD at x 44.05 from y 89.5 to 92.5 passes THROUGH U3.5 (GND) and U3.6 (I2S1_BCLK); I2S1_BCLK at x 44.05 from 88.6 to 91.5 passes through U3.5 (GND); I2S1_WS at x 45.95 from 89.05 to 91.5 passes through U3.4 (+3V3). Four net-to-net shorts. bundle_routes only measures track-to-track clearance and the bus's own pads, so its findings named the two trace crossings and nothing about the pads. Same construction at the source end would short a header column. Expected: a leg (and a lane) that crosses copper of another net — pad, via, trace — is a BAD-BUT-BUILDABLE finding naming the pad/net and the overlap, on both ends, so the commit says what it did. Visually obvious on the canvas (legs plunging through the upper pad rows); a via station in a pour also made geometric DRC indeterminate on the same commit (separate bug), so nothing automated caught it.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:19:13","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:24","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["bus-tool","drc","geometry","hitl","pcb","short"],"resolution":"63e99a0 (bus names every foreign pad/via/trace its copper lands on or crowds) + 16add04 (a station via's finding names the copper's own layer, from the Codex check). test_bus_tool 13/13b/13c, 251 passed.","repro_steps":"1. Open Smart Remote v2 in the pcb editor. 2. minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct {nets:[I2S1_SD,I2S1_WS,I2S1_BCLK], sources:[U1S.GPIO6,U1S.GPIO5,U1S.GPIO4], targets:[U3.7,U3.3,U3.6], points:[(57.6,48.6),(59,48.6),(59,82),(45,82),(45,86.5),(45,89.5)], layer:bottom, via_station_index:4, via_station_layer:top}. 3. Export trace geometry: the I2S1_SD top run (45.45,89.5)→(44.05,89.5)→(44.05,92.5) crosses U3.5 at (44.05,90.5) and U3.6 at (44.05,91.5); findings[] names only bus_end_crossing/bus_clearance between the bus's own nets."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ba6704c7d6bb344261d6b99f464","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Connectivity DRC misses a trace passing OVER a foreign pad mid-segment — only endpoints on wrong-net pads are flagged","description":"minerva_pcb_drc / minerva_pcb_board_check connectivity reported exactly the two same-layer trace crossings of the bad U3 bus and none of the four trace-through-pad shorts (I2S1_SD through U3.5 GND and U3.6; I2S1_BCLK through U3.5; I2S1_WS through U3.4 +3V3). wrong_net_pad keys off trace ENDPOINTS; a segment whose centreline passes through another net's pad is not examined. Geometric DRC would catch it (GC2) but was indeterminate on the same board because of pour islands, so the board had no automated short detection at all. Expected: connectivity check flags segment-over-foreign-pad (pad centre or pad copper intersecting the centreline) as wrong_net_pad or a new type, so the cheap always-available check catches the obvious short even when the geometric one cannot run.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:19:18","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:35","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["connectivity","drc","pcb","short"],"resolution":"0cb78c8 (connectivity DRC names a trace passing over a foreign-net pad). worker tests/test_drc.py green (94 passed in the scoped set). Narrowing follow-ups filed: 01a03c6b0f (nearest-pad only), 01a03c708d (dedup direction)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ba68f1b7891a3835e628cb3b4bb","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Via station inside a copper pour leaves 0.02 mm² pour islands between the vias, and geometric DRC goes indeterminate on the whole board","description":"After the U1S→U3 bus committed with its via station at (45,86.5) on the bottom GND pour, minerva_pcb_board_check's geometric DRC returned indeterminate/unresolved_geometry: zone fill 'broke into 2 region(s)' — islands at (44.525–44.675, 85.997–86.205) and (45.325–45.475, 85.998–86.205), 0.0219 mm² each, between the staggered fan legs and the 0.6 mm vias at 0.8 pitch. The schema has no zone min-thickness or island-removal rule, so the filler refuses rather than culls, and one bad bus silences copper DRC for the entire board (which is how the four shorts in the sibling bug went unreported).\n\nOWNER'S RULING: the ZONE FILLER owns this. Any two hand-placed vias at 0.8 pitch inside a pour reproduce it, so the fix is a board-level rule, not a bus-tool one: the filler culls islands below a minimum (thickness and/or area, declared in design_rules with a sane default) instead of failing the compile, and reports what it culled. The bus station stays as it is.","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:19:26","updated_at":"2026-08-26T03:31:53","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","drc","pcb","via","zone-fill"],"resolution":"Filler culls islands/slivers below authored zone_min_thickness_mm / zone_min_island_area_mm2 (defaults from profile min trace width and via land area), reports per pour; large orphans still refuse. minerva-plugins 77d5665. Worker tests green; batch review pending."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ba6a1c974bf9b4773e6f2b75d14","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus emits a 0.05 mm stub segment at a leg-to-lane corner — miter artefact survives _drop_duplicate_points","description":"Committed I2S1_BCLK bottom run on smart-remote v2 (route_bus_direct, spine starting (57.6,48.6)→(59,48.6)→south): (58.5,55.177)→(58.5,49.05)→(58.55,49.05)→(58.55,81.55). The 0.05 mm horizontal between the leg's top and the lane is a miter/offset rounding artefact; _drop_duplicate_points only removes points within _MIN_SEGMENT_MM of each other, and 0.05 mm is above it. The other two nets' corners are clean. Expected: a lane/leg join collapses to a single axis-aligned corner (snap the leg's x to the lane's x when they differ by less than the track width), so exported copper has no sub-width stubs for Gerber/DRC to trip over.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:19:31","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:29","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","geometry","pcb"],"resolution":"c6d2c59 (a bus leg lands on its lane's mitered corner, no sub-width stub) + 7ffc164/e7154c9 (review dropped a non-independent check). test_bus_breakout_geometry 100 passed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03bc51e0f77d5a38ae4f7d19b1ab5","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"Separate the working layer from the view filter: the F.Cu/B.Cu chooser sets where copper is authored, never what is shown","description":"OWNER'S RULING (live bus test on smart-remote v2): \"the F.Cu / B.Cu chooser also updates view — view filters and working layer should be separate.\"\n\nWHAT EXISTS: pcb_canvas.trace_layer_filter is one property doing two jobs. (1) VISIBILITY — _layer_visible() treats a specific filter as \"show only this layer\", overriding the per-layer eyes (hidden_layers). (2) AUTHORING — _author_layer() returns it as the layer the trace tool, zone tool and bus draw on (\"all\" falls back to TRACE_DEFAULT_LAYER = top), and since DCR 01a03a5034 part B its setter is also the funnel that arms a bus via station. The toolbar's single LayerOption dropdown, the View menu and minerva_pcb_set_view {trace_layer_filter} all write it. Consequence seen live: arming a via station onto F.Cu mid-path hides the bottom copper you are drawing, and choosing a working layer to draw on collapses the view to that layer.\n\nDIRECTION: two properties, two controls.\n- working_layer (canvas): the copper layer authoring tools draw on — trace, zone, bus, via-station arm. Always a declared copper layer, never \"all\"; defaults to top (or the first declared copper layer). The toolbar F.Cu/B.Cu chooser sets THIS and only this. Changing it never touches visibility. _author_layer() reads it; the bus station arms from ITS setter.\n- View: the per-layer eyes (hidden_layers) are the only visibility control, driven by the View menu and minerva_pcb_set_view. trace_layer_filter either goes away or survives as a view-only filter that the eyes compose with — but nothing that authors copper reads it.\n- MCP: minerva_pcb_set_view keeps its view semantics; the working layer is settable and readable through the view-state / preference surface (one new arg or verb, minimal), and the direct authoring verbs keep their explicit layer args.\n- Teach/status lines that name \"switching layer\" (bus PATH) name the working-layer chooser.\n\nORACLE: with the eyes showing both layers, choosing B.Cu in the toolbar draws a trace on bottom while top copper stays visible; hiding top through the eye does not change where the next trace lands; arming a bus via station by switching the working layer leaves the bottom run visible; minerva_pcb_set_view {trace_layer_filter} (if kept) changes what is drawn and not where copper goes.\n\nNOT in this DCR: a keyboard flip for the working layer (candidate follow-up DCR); the zone tool's own zone-layer OptionButton (leave, or fold into working_layer if trivially the same thing — implementer's call, reported).</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:52:49","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:19","priority":1,"tags":["bus-tool","canvas","layers","pcb","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03bc8b77f73e9a3922e7396efa1cc","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus teach/status lines mislead: \"REFUSED\" while it will commit, \"click clear of the pads\" when the spine must start behind them, and nothing says a two-layer bus must start on the target's opposite layer","description":"Three traps walked from the live U1S→U3 test, each a wrong or missing sentence rather than wrong geometry. (1) The panel's held status lead reads \"BUS REFUSED: <first finding>\" and the badge/spine tint says refused, but since DCR 01a03a5034 part A a bad-but-buildable plan COMMITS on the finish gesture — the wording now teaches users the commit is broken. It should say the bus will land with findings, and how many. (2) The PATH teach line says \"click clear of the pads to start the path\"; the geometry needs the first vertex BEHIND the pads (before them along the spine's first segment), and a spine started beside a pin column lands every pad \"inside the corridor\" with legs shorted together. The line should say where to start. (3) A bus is one layer plus at most one via station, so a bus from THT sources to SMD targets on the other side can only be drawn if the WORKING layer is set to the targets' opposite layer before the first path click; nothing says so, and the natural top-first attempt dead-ends at the second switch's refusal. Name it in the SOURCES→PATH teach line, or at least in the second-switch refusal.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T01:56:45","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:39","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","pcb","teach-line","ux"],"resolution":"5b0fc57 (bus teach/status words match what the tool does). Owner ruling 2026-08-25: the SOURCES \"start on the other side\" note is a designer preference, not a feature — removal filed as 01a03c728b. 4-layer second-switch advice defect filed as bug 01a03c6ad8."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03bf71d127737adbdcedadc23253b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Bus breakout legs always join the spine's FIRST segment, so a short lead-in segment makes a leg run past the bundle and the lane double back over it","description":"Live U1S→U3 bus (spine (57.6,48.6)→(59,48.6)→(59,82)…): I2S1_BCLK's leg runs 6.13 mm north from its pad at y 55.177 to the lane's corner at y 49.05 on the 1.4 mm first segment, and the lane immediately runs back south over the same x — 12 mm of copper a human would never draw ((56.43,55.177)→(58.55,55.177)→(58.55,81.55) is the obvious route). After the stub fix (PLG 01a03ba6a1) the two runs overlap exactly, leaving (58.55,49.05) as a zero-area spur; before it they were 0.05 mm apart. Cause: bundle_routes' departure construction meets the lane on the spine's first segment even when that segment carries essentially none of the bundle and the track's real run is the second. Fix direction: a leg whose pad projects onto a LATER segment of its own lane (and whose station-order invariant still holds) joins there, or the collinear spur is collapsed after construction. Construction change — needs its own oracle over the pinned breakout suite. Related: the teach-line bug 01a03bc8b7 (spine must start behind the pads) is what forces the short lead-in in the first place.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T02:47:25","updated_at":"2026-08-26T02:47:25","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","geometry","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c07a23a72ac8277180a70371d74","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Split the three PCB god files (pcb_canvas 11.5k, panel_tools 10.1k, PCBPanel 7k lines) into per-tool scripts so a task reads one tool, not the whole editor","description":"MEASURED: ui/pcb_canvas.gd 11,454 lines / 339 funcs; ui/panel_tools.gd 10,091 / 222; ui/PCBPanel.gd 7,025 / 225 — 28.5k lines across three files, plus pcb_data 3.8k and pcb_routing_workspace 3.9k. Every task-cycle implementer re-reads the relevant slab of all three before its first edit; across the last two batches that was 40–80 read/grep calls per task and the dominant share of 10–30 min wall time per task. The canvas already has section banners (selection, rotate handles, corridor draw, junction drag, routing workspace, zone/cutout/trace/bus authoring, ghost styling ×3, ratsnest, DRC witness, verbs, approximation notice, fab preview) — each is a natural script boundary that today shares one node's state.\n\nGOAL: a bus task reads the bus script and the canvas seam it plugs into, nothing else. Keep behaviour byte-identical; the pinned gd suites are the oracle (every EXPECTED_SUITES count unchanged, no test text edits beyond preload paths).\n\nDIRECTION (implementer verifies, owner rules on the shape):\n- pcb_canvas.gd → a thin canvas node (input dispatch, draw order, view state, selection set) plus per-tool scripts as RefCounted/Node helpers the canvas owns: bus_tool.gd, trace_tool.gd, zone_tool.gd, cutout_tool.gd, via_tool.gd, universal_select.gd (rotate handles + propose-move), corridor_draw.gd, junction_drag.gd, ratsnest_draw.gd, ghost_styling.gd, drc_witness.gd, fab_preview.gd. Tool scripts receive the canvas (or a narrow context object) and own their own state and _draw contribution; the canvas keeps the funnel functions (tool mode, phase accessors the panel pulls) as one-line forwards.\n- panel_tools.gd → split THE VERBS by domain: bus verbs + plan/commit/propose, workspace verbs, staged/draft verbs, view/prefs verbs, export/fab verbs, drc verbs. The dispatcher table stays in one file.\n- PCBPanel.gd → toolbar/sidebar construction per area (Draw flow toolbar, layer chooser, zone props, status/badges, proposals area) — consider scenes where a piece is static UI (owner preference: scenes over generated UI).\n- Constraint from feedback: off-tree plugins cannot use class_name for cross-script type references — use preload consts, as the codebase already does.\n- Do it in slices, one tool per commit, each slice green on its pinned suites before the next; bus first (it is the hottest file section right now), then trace/zone, then the panel.\n\nNOT in scope: any behaviour change, renaming public accessors the tests pin, or touching the Python worker.\n\nWHY (owner, 2026-08-25): \"why do these tasks take so much wall time per task\" — reading before writing dominates, and the read is these three files.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T03:05:28","updated_at":"2026-08-26T03:05:28","priority":2,"tags":["canvas","panel_tools","pcb","refactor","task-cycle-throughput"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c21263a71c39d6943421dfbab4c","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF — working-layer batch (6 commits on main, 880a0ac..1877952) is UNREVIEWED and its GD tests UNRUN: do the batch review, scoped run, re-pin, Codex, then close the six items","description":"PICK THIS UP FIRST on any machine. Owner pushed the batch to main unreviewed (2026-08-25 night) so the Linux box could pull it; the task-cycle's back half was not done.\n\nWHAT IS ON MAIN (minerva-plugins 880a0ac..1877952):\n- 9c1b2b2 working layer separate from view filter — DCR 01a03bc51e (status implementing → shipped when closed)\n- 63e99a0 bus names foreign pads/vias/traces its copper crowds — bug 01a03ba65b (active → resolved)\n- c6d2c59 leg lands on the lane's mitered corner, no 0.05 mm stub — bug 01a03ba6a1 (active → resolved)\n- 0cb78c8 connectivity DRC names trace-over-foreign-pad — bug 01a03ba670 (active → resolved); worker tests DID run green\n- 5b0fc57 bus teach/status words — bug 01a03bc8b7 (active → resolved)\n- 77d5665 zone filler culls sub-minimum islands/slivers — bug 01a03ba68f (already resolved); worker tests DID run green\n\nDO, IN ORDER (task-cycle skill in Minerva/.claude/skills/task-cycle):\n1. Fable cold review over `git diff 880a0ac 1877952 -- pcb/` as ONE body of work against the six goals above; no implementer context. Fold fixes into the responsible commit (fixup + autosquash) — main is already pushed, so a force-push is NOT allowed: land fixes as a \"review fixes\" commit instead.\n2. Scoped GD run with Minerva CLOSED, from Minerva/src (NOT the plugin dir): `godot --headless --path . --script res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/tests/gd/<suite>.gd` for test_bus_tool, test_bus_breakout_geometry, test_pcb_panel_ui, test_view_state, test_pcb_trace_tool, test_layer_stack, test_pcb_workflow_kinds, test_workspace_tools, test_candidate_canvas, test_pcb_ratsnest. Worker: `cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_drc.py tests/test_zone_fill.py tests/test_zone_seals.py -q`.\n3. MEASURE and re-pin tests/gd/EXPECTED_SUITES in its own commit. Implementers reported bus_tool 225, pcb_trace_tool 86, view_state 76, bus_breakout_geometry 101 — measure, do not trust.\n4. Codex serial final check on the whole diff (`git diff ... | codex exec --skip-git-repo-check -C <repo> -o out.md '<prompt>'`, tell it not to run commands).\n5. Transition the five open items (DCR → shipped; bugs → resolved with resolution text naming the commit), then this chore → done. Flush docket, commit the .dct.\n\nTWO OWNER JUDGEMENT CALLS still open — ask before closing: (a) task 1 changed the pour default from bottom to the working layer; (b) task 5's \"start on the other side\" SOURCES note appears on every ordinary THT→SMD bus (narrowing = show only once a station is armed).\n\nRelated open follow-ups filed from the same live test: bug 01a03bf71d (leg joins the spine's first segment / doubled-back run), work_item 01a03c07a2 (split the three god files), bugs 01a03b8747 / 01a03b8755, hint 01a03b8785.</description>\n<parameter name=\"component\">pcb","created_at":"2026-08-26T03:33:20","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:44","priority":1,"tags":["handoff","pcb","review-pending","task-cycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c6ad8ba70a88a62f4688b720b78","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Bus second-switch advice is wrong on ≥4-layer boards: recommends starting on the layer it says never to recommend, and omits the station switch","description":"From the cold batch review of the working-layer batch (880a0ac..1877952), finding F4.\n\npcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd `_arm_bus_via_station`: the refused second-switch advice computes `start_on` as `_bus_layer` whenever `layer != _bus_layer`. On a 4+ layer board, switching to in1 after a station already landed on bottom yields \"START on F.Cu... set the chooser to F.Cu, then redraw\" — the very start layer the surrounding comment says must never be recommended — and it never says the station must switch onto in1. Correct only for 2-layer boards.\n\nRepro: 4-layer board, bus from F.Cu pads, arm a via station to B.Cu, then switch the working layer to In1.Cu mid-PATH; read the refusal text.\n\nFix direction: derive the recommended start from the target of the SECOND switch (the layer the author is trying to reach), and name the station's layer change in the text.","created_at":"2026-08-26T04:53:50","updated_at":"2026-08-26T04:53:50","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["batch-review","bus-tool","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c6af6d57793a36ca7788e6c23cf","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"structure_changed rewrites the working layer, so an undo that drops the working layer from the stack arms a bus via station as a side effect","description":"From the cold batch review of the working-layer batch (880a0ac..1877952), finding F8.\n\npcb/ui/PCBPanel.gd `_rebuild_layer_option` writes `_canvas.working_layer` on every `structure_changed`, which includes undo/redo. If a bus is in PATH and an undo removes the current working layer from the stack, the working_layer setter funnels into the bus tool and arms a via station — an edit the user did not make, triggered by an undo.\n\nRepro: 4-layer board, bus in PATH with working layer In1.Cu; undo the set_board_layers that added In1; observe a via station armed.\n\nFix direction: on structure_changed, if the working layer disappears, cancel or hold the bus gesture rather than re-targeting it; or make the structure-driven write bypass the station funnel.","created_at":"2026-08-26T04:53:58","updated_at":"2026-08-26T04:53:58","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["batch-review","bus-tool","pcb","working-layer"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c6b0f95742180b4601c7715abb8","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Connectivity DRC endpoint pass reports only the nearest foreign pad; a second foreign pad within clearance of the same endpoint is reported by neither pass","description":"From the cold batch review of the working-layer batch (880a0ac..1877952), finding F7.\n\npcb/worker/pcb_worker/drc.py ~421-427: the endpoint pass names only the NEAREST foreign pad to each trace end, and the new along-the-run pass explicitly cedes every pad within `clr` of an endpoint to the endpoint pass. So two foreign pads both within clearance of the same endpoint produce one finding, not two. Pre-existing narrowing, now codified by the partition.\n\nSmallest fix: a sentence in the docstring stating the narrowing. Fuller fix: endpoint pass reports every foreign pad within `clr`, with a test where two foreign pads crowd one endpoint.","created_at":"2026-08-26T04:54:04","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:10","priority":4,"tags":["batch-review","drc","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed; also a correct own-net landing no longer hides a foreign pad at the same end."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c70762272adac9411f20bb4de4d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Zone-fill cull warnings are lost when a later region or zone refuses the compile","description":"Codex final check on the working-layer batch (880a0ac..8346cfa), note 2.\n\npcb/worker/pcb_worker/zone_fill.py + compile_board.py: CulledRegion rows are returned alongside the fill, so a zone holding both a culled sliver and an above-minimum orphan island raises ZoneFillError before its culls are returned; likewise zone A's culls vanish when zone B raises. That contradicts \"every cull is reported, never silent\" for the failing compile — the author sees the refusal but not the scrap that was also dropped.\n\nFix direction: accumulate culls eagerly (a collector passed into the filler) and emit their zone_fill_culled warnings even on the ZoneFillError path. Oracle: a two-zone board where zone B refuses; the diagnostics must still carry zone A's cull warning.","created_at":"2026-08-26T04:59:58","updated_at":"2026-08-26T04:59:58","priority":3,"tags":["codex-review","pcb","zone-fill"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c708db0746a929923a0efcbe023","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Connectivity DRC along-run dedup is direction-sensitive: reversed duplicate traces double-report a foreign pad","description":"Codex final check on the working-layer batch (880a0ac..8346cfa), note 4.\n\npcb/worker/pcb_worker/drc.py: the `seen_along` key for the trace-over-foreign-pad pass is built from the segment's endpoints in authored order, so two duplicate traces with reversed endpoint order produce two wrong_net_pad findings for one segment/pad pair, although the comment claims geometry-based dedup.\n\nFix: canonicalise the rounded endpoint pair (sort the two points) before forming the key. Oracle: a board with A->B and B->A copies over one foreign pad yields one finding.","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:00:04","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:08","priority":4,"tags":["codex-review","drc","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c7274d87d799d612577b3234325","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Go Validate: typed design_rules.zone_min_thickness_mm / zone_min_island_area_mm2 with validation and cross-language vectors","description":"Owner ruling 2026-08-25 on the working-layer batch review's F3: close the batch with the asymmetry documented (board-yaml.md \"Zone minima\"), and fix it here.\n\npcb/internal/board/board.go DesignRules carries the two zone-minima keys in the inline Extra map, so `validate` accepts `zone_min_thickness_mm: -1` or `\"abc\"` while `compile` refuses it with invalid_design_rule. The zone_fill docstring that shipped the rule named \"validate and compile disagree about a fabrication parameter\" as the reason the Go side matters.\n\nDo: typed fields on DesignRules; validate thickness > 0 and island area >= 0 (finite numbers); add vectors under pcb/spec/vectors/ so Go and Python assert the same codes; update the \"compile-time rules only\" paragraph in board-yaml.md.","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:09","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:34:33","priority":3,"tags":["batch-review","pcb","validate","zone-fill"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c728b41796a84388562f0a756f4","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Remove the bus SOURCES \"if this bus needs the other side, start it there\" note — a per-board designer preference, not a feature","description":"Owner ruling 2026-08-25 (working-layer batch handoff, question b; confirmed after reading the code): the note is \"really a designer preference on a specific board, not really a feature.\" Remove it.\n\nWHERE: pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd `_bus_off_layer_target_note`. The function has TWO branches — remove only the SECOND:\n\n1. KEEP — targets have NO copper on the working layer and all sit on one other layer (\"No target pad for these nets has copper on X — every one is on Y... set the Layer chooser to Y before the first click\"). That is a geometric fact: the bus as drawn cannot land.\n\n2. REMOVE — targets are on the working layer only and the THT sources also reach the other side (\"Every target pad is on X only... If this bus needs the other side, start it on Y and let the station switch onto X\"). This fires on every ordinary THT→SMD bus and speculates about how the designer wants to route.\n\nAlso drop the `_bus_source_copper_layers` walk if branch 2 was its only caller, adjust the comment block above the function (it describes both shapes), remove/adjust the test_bus_tool teach-line checks that pin branch 2's wording, and re-pin EXPECTED_SUITES from a run.","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:02:14","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:34:23","priority":4,"tags":["bus-tool","pcb","teach-line"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c9776d07ebe88b12c2498fcf830","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Never Save / minerva_save_editor a PCB tab onto board YAML — it writes the panel document over it","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:34","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:34","tags":["destructive","gotcha","pcb","yaml"],"value":"The pcb panel is save_mode host_owned, so Editor.save_file_to_disc (Minerva Editor.gd, PLUGIN_SCENE branch) calls the panel's _on_panel_save_request and writes the returned dict — the ~266KB render-enriched panel document — straight over editor.file, i.e. the canonical board YAML. It reports success and renames the tab to the file basename. To persist a board from the live editor: minerva_pcb_export_yaml (non-writing, returns canonical YAML text; by-ref past 64KiB) → write that text to the .yaml with minerva_doc_write → minerva_pcb_load_board to re-sync. Keep boards in git so a bad save is one checkout away.","component":"pcb","key":"save.destroys.yaml","confidence":"verified 2026-08-25 against Editor.gd + pcb manifest","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c9792bd78bda001ab112aff47b7","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Plugin release order: bump manifest → push → tag → CI release → THEN regen_registry.py","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:41","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:41","tags":["gotcha","marketplace","release"],"value":"scripts/regen_registry.py builds each tarball URL as <releases/download>/<LATEST PUBLISHED TAG>/<id>-<MANIFEST VERSION>-<target>.tar.gz — tag from the last published release, filename from the plugin manifest. Run it before the new tag exists and it emits e.g. releases/download/pcb-v0.3.1/pcb-0.4.0-linux-x86_64.tar.gz, which 404s at install. Sequence: (1) bump <plugin>/manifest.json, commit, push main; (2) git tag <plugin>-vX.Y.Z && git push origin <tag> — the per-plugin workflow (.github/workflows/<plugin>.yml, on tags <plugin>-v*) builds the platform tarballs and publishes the GitHub release; (3) only then regen_registry.py, commit, push. registry.json is the marketplace index other machines install from.","component":"release","key":"release.order","confidence":"verified 2026-08-25 against regen_registry.py:98-136","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c97b6d57e46b382ffcefadc9683","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb seed library: a footprint resolves ONLY via a footprints.lock.json entry keyed \"LibNick:Part\"; three bare keys are synthetic fixtures","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:50","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:42:50","tags":["footprints","gotcha","library","pcb"],"value":"Seed library = pcb/library/footprints/ + footprints.lock.json (schema 2). Resolution is by lock key \"LibNick:PartName\" (e.g. Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric) — the .kicad_mod file being present at that path is NOT enough. Three entries are keyed BARE on purpose: C_0805 and R_0805 point at *_SeedSynthetic.kicad_mod (736/744-byte synthesizer fixtures, \"deliberately NOT the official KiCad part\"), TH_TestPoint at its own file; do not re-key them to LibNick:Part or four test modules silently start resolving wrong pads. The official KiCad bytes sit at the canonical paths under their proper keys. All seed entries are bless:null — seed is exempt from the bless gate. Adding a seed entry touches three more places: EXPECTED_PAD_COUNTS (tests/test_footprints.py), _EXPECTED_SEED_MARKERS (tests/test_footprint_def.py), and `python3 pcb/scripts/gen_notice.py` (CI release gate diffs NOTICE.md against the lock).","component":"pcb","key":"seed.library.keys","confidence":"lock keys + synthetic paths verified 2026-08-25; the three-places rule not re-verified","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03c9800207791ad52f555fe9a02f5","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Rewriting board YAML with sed/awk: guard to the components: section, then reload and LOOK","created_at":"2026-08-26T05:43:09","updated_at":"2026-08-26T05:43:09","tags":["gotcha","pcb","scripting","yaml"],"value":"A `ref` captured by /^- ref: / stays set past the end of components:, and mounting_holes: entries are `- x_mm:` / `  y_mm:` at the same indent — so a bare `^  y_mm:` rewrite silently overwrites every mounting hole's Y with the last component's value. Guard: /^[a-z_]+:/ { inc = ($0 ~ /^components:/) } and only rewrite while inc. Nothing downstream catches a moved hole reliably (assembly check sees only courtyard overlap; connectivity ignores holes), so after any scripted YAML edit reload the board into the panel and look at the corners.","component":"pcb","key":"yaml.awk.section.guard","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca8f7a876edbcb5ae131294f619","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Bus tool: lanes wear their net, lane order is a visible choice, and any trace can be cut and finished pad-to-pad from its free end","description":"ORIGIN — owner live test 2026-08-25 (smart-remote-v2, I2S1 trio U1S -> U3 LGA): \"I couldn't tell what wire would go to what pad at target, and I had no way to say trace 1 to this pad, trace 2 to this pad, trace 3 to this pad. Being able to choose the trace and its route to pad after bus exit is a missing feature.\"\n\nPRINCIPLE (owner, 2026-08-26): a bus is a convenience for defining a group of traces that run parallel to each other. It is a GENERATOR, not an object: once committed, its traces are ordinary traces — one polyline per net, many segments — and everything that can be done to a trace can be done to each of them. No bus record persists and nothing is bus-specific after commit.\n\n# Design\n\n## 1. What the code does today (measured 2026-08-25)\n- Gesture: SOURCES → PATH → TARGETS → commit (pcb_canvas.gd 8224-8310). In TARGETS, clicking a legal pad assigns THAT net's target (_handle_bus_target_click 8568); re-targeting and clearing are allowed. \"Trace N to this pad\" is expressible today by clicking the pad — and nothing says so.\n- Rings are drawn for every candidate of every net in one style (_draw_bus_targets 9423); no ring or lane carries the net's name or colour. Lane order = pick order, changeable only by Esc-ing back to SOURCES.\n- A committed bus is ONE trace per net (7 segments each on the live board). A committed trace can be translated whole, re-widthed or deleted whole; there is no vertex editing and no cut.\n- The trace tool's anchors are pads and vias only (_trace_anchor_at 7922); a trace's free end is not an anchor.\n- A plan with complete == false (targets not all picked) is refused at commit.\n\nGaps: (A) lane↔net identity is invisible; (B) lane order is not a visible choice; (C) a bad tail cannot be cut and redone — the whole trace goes.\n\n## 2. Part 1 — identity and order in the gesture (A, B)\nA1. Every lane wears its net: ghost polylines, candidate rings and SOURCES pips take the net's ratsnest colour; the lane's target end carries a label \"SD → U3.7\"; landed ring solid, suggestion dashed, others thin.\nA2. The bus badge (PCBPanel _draw_bus_phase_badge 1542) lists one line per net in lane order: \"1 SD  U1S.GPIO6 → U3.7\", unlanded as \"→ ?\".\nA3. TARGETS teach: \"Click each net's target pad — lanes are numbered in the order you picked the nets; click a numbered pip to move that net outward.\"\nB1. Clicking a net's pip in SOURCES/TARGETS moves that net one lane outward (Shift+click inward), rotating _bus_nets/_bus_net_refs/_bus_net_points/targets together; replan through the existing cache key. The verb needs nothing — `nets` order is the same rule.\nB2. On a bus_end_crossing finding, add the advisory \"pick order N,M,… would leave the bundle clean\" (try ≤ 24 orders for n ≤ 4, else skip). Never re-sorts.\nMCP parity: bus_target_guidance() rows gain lane_index and color; workspace_propose_bus reply and the add_bus journal row gain per-net {net, lane_index, source, target}.\n\n## 3. Part 2 — every trace is cuttable and finishable (C)\nC1. Trace-end anchor. The trace tool gains a third anchor kind beside pad and via: {ref: trace_id, kind: \"trace_end\", position, net} — a click within TRACE_PAD_SNAP_MM of either free end of a trace (an end touching no pad, via or same-net copper). Net inherited from the trace; a netless trace is refused by name like a netless via. Finishing FROM a trace end EXTENDS that trace's polyline (same id, waypoints appended) so one net stays one polyline; finishing ON a trace end appends to it likewise. minerva_pcb_add_trace accepts `start`/`end` of the form {trace_id, end: \"start\"|\"end\"} with the same semantics. The netless-refusal vocabulary and the anchor label (\"Trace end trace:…\") cover the new kind.\nC2. Cut a trace at a vertex. Context menu \"Cut here\" on a selected trace (nearest vertex to the click; refused when the nearest vertex is an end); the trace keeps its id and the waypoints up to the cut; the tail is deleted. One journal row (cut_trace {trace_id, at_index, dropped_segments}), one undo step. MCP twin minerva_pcb_cut_trace {editor_name, trace_id, at_index | x_mm+y_mm}. A cut end is a free end — DRC reports it as dangling_endpoint until it is finished, which is the intended signal.\nC3. Open-ended bus commit. A plan whose targets are not all picked may commit: each unlanded net's lane ends at the spine's end as a free end. Status line says \"N lanes end open — finish them with the Trace tool from their free ends\"; the add_bus row records which nets landed open. The verb: `targets` becomes optional per net (empty string = open); the propose verb likewise.\n\nRedoing a bad bus exit is then: select the trace → Cut here at the last good vertex → Trace tool: click the free end, click the pad. Nothing bus-specific is involved, and the same moves fix any hand-routed trace.\n\n## 4. Not in this DCR\n- Drag/vertex editing of committed traces beyond the cut.\n- Automatic lane re-ordering (B2 is advisory).\n- Any persisted bus object (rejected by the principle above).\n- Same-column LGA pairs: still named by bus_pads_along_spine (bug 01a03ca933).\n\n## 5. Acceptance\nTests (authored, run at testex): test_bus_tool — pip click rotates lane order and the plan's offsets follow; badge text lists the mapping; add_bus row carries the per-net mapping and open nets; open-ended commit lands lanes ending at the spine end. test_pcb_trace_tool — a trace end starts and finishes a trace, net inherited, netless refused by name; finishing from a trace end extends the same trace id; minerva_pcb_add_trace agrees with the click. test_trace_identity_delete or a new test_trace_cut — cut keeps id and head waypoints, drops the tail, one history step, journal row; cut at an end refused. test_pcb_panel_ui — badge lines and the Cut menu item. Manifest — minerva_pcb_cut_trace registered; add_trace schema carries the trace-end form.\n\nHITL (after this DCR ships; combined with the batch-3 bus fixes at 10326d4): on the live board, Draw ▸ Bus the I2S trio starting beside the U1S column: (1) lanes and rings show net colours and labels; (2) click a pip to move BCLK outward and watch the lanes swap; (3) land two targets, leave SD open, commit — SD's lane ends free; (4) Trace tool: click SD's free end, click U3.7; (5) select WS, Cut here at its last bend, finish it again from the cut; (6) minerva_pcb_add_trace from a trace end and minerva_pcb_cut_trace reach the same board states.\n\n## 6. Stations (proposed order)\nS1 A1+A2+A3 · S2 B1+B2 · S3 C1 trace-end anchor (canvas + add_trace) · S4 C2 cut (model + menu + MCP) · S5 C3 open-ended commit · S6 docs (tools.md Bus and Trace sections).","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:01:41","updated_at":"2026-08-26T19:45:14","priority":2,"tags":["bus-tool","parity","pcb","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca9335b7c139d418f129783137f","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus fan-out through a pad column PARALLEL to the spine is unsupported and unnamed — legs run down the column through every neighbour; a horizontal exit from the same pads is clean","description":"Live test 2026-08-25, smart-remote-v2, nets I2S1_SD/WS/BCLK, sources U1S.GPIO6/5/4 (THT column at x=56.43, y 50.1/52.6/55.2), targets U3.7/3/6 (top LGA, columns x=44.05/45.95).\n\nEXPERIMENT RESOLVED THE EITHER/OR. Spine leaving U1S VERTICALLY (parallel to the pad column), e.g. (58.5,57.5)->(58.5,86)->...: every breakout leg runs DOWN THE PAD COLUMN from its pad to the spine's start row through the neighbouring pads (GPIO4/5/6 and EN), all three legs collinear -> 6 bus_foreign_copper + 3 bus_clearance at the source; connectivity DRC wrong_net_pad x7. Spine leaving U1S HORIZONTALLY, (58.5,52.637)->(66,52.637)->(66,86)->...: ZERO source-end findings; legs leave each pad sideways into their own lanes. So the leg geometry is right for the case it supports (pads spread ACROSS the spine's end-segment axis) and simply wrong for pads spread ALONG it — and nothing tells the author which case they are in.\n\nThe SAME failure repeats at U3 in every attempt: the last segment runs -x, U3's pads are spread along x (two columns) as well as y, and the drops stack on x=47.5-48.4 crossing each other (bus_clearance x3) before running along the pad rows through U3.3/U3.2.\n\nFIX: at each end, measure the picked pads' spread along the end segment's axis vs across it. Across -> today's fan-out. Along (spread along the axis exceeds one lane pitch) -> either fan out in the lane axis (legs leave the pad perpendicular to the column, join lanes that start at successive stations along the spine), or refuse by name: \"these pads lie ALONG the spine — leave the column sideways (draw the first segment away from the column)\". Named refusal is acceptable for the first cut; drawing legs through neighbours is not.\n\nAcceptance: test_bus_breakout_geometry hand-derived case with the live U1S column and a vertical first segment -> named finding or a clean lane-axis fan-out with no leg within pad radius + clearance of a foreign pad; the horizontal-exit case stays clean (regression pin). Copper from the experiment was deleted; the board is the owner's.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:01:56","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:37","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["bus-tool","geometry","live-test","pcb"],"resolution":"586302d: pads lying along the spine fan out sideways from their own rows (column-mate = |dperp| < track width, |daxial| >= pitch); a pad under its lane gets no reach; remaining sub-width runs named bus_sub_width_stub; two aligned columns named bus_pads_along_spine. test_bus_breakout_geometry 122 passed. Fable batch review must_fix (staggered pair) + Codex must_fix (sub-width reach) both folded and re-checked resolved."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca94e5f746ab2c863c30cb220f3","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus lands on a target pad that has no copper on the landing layer — an open by construction — with no finding","description":"Live test 2026-08-25: the owner bused I2S1_SD/WS/BCLK on B.Cu from U1S (THT) to U3 (MSM261S, layer top, SMD-only pads) with no via station. The traces landed under the pads on bottom; nothing connects. Connectivity DRC afterwards: dangling_endpoint x2. The bus tool reported nothing at commit.\n\nA target (or source) pad with no copper on the layer the leg lands on is not a correctable landing; it is a guaranteed open. Add a bus finding (e.g. bus_pad_off_layer) naming the pad, the pad's layers and the landing layer, and say the way out in the teach line at the target pick: \"U3 is top-only — switch the Layer chooser now to add a station before this pad\". The MCP verb returns the same finding. Refusal vs land-with-finding: owner policy is land-with-finding; the finding must at least exist.\n\nOracle: LGA fixture on a two-layer board, bus on bottom, targets top-only -> finding present in verb reply and status line; same bus with a station to top -> finding absent.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:02:03","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:38","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","live-test","pcb"],"resolution":"bcddd32 (+ b90cd84 for NPTH): bus_pad_off_layer names a source/target pad with no copper on the landing layer, its layers, and the station way out; through-hole never raises it; an unplated hole is on no layer at all. test_bus_tool 14/14b, 291 passed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca9724471c2a1af619cea58da47","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus spine-start/end message says the opposite of what the rule measures (\"start BEHIND the source pads\" flags a spine that does)","description":"Live test 2026-08-25. bus_pad_inside_corridor text: \"source pad is X mm past the start of the spine — a breakout leg runs from the spine back to its pad, so the spine has to start BEHIND the source pads, before them along its first segment.\" Following that literally — spine start at y=47.5, pads at y 50.1/52.6/55.2, first segment heading +y — flagged ALL three pads as \"past the start\". Starting at y=57.5 (pads BEFORE the start along the segment, i.e. outside the spine) cleared the finding. Same inversion at the target end (\"has to end PAST the target pads\" — an end at x=42.5 with pads at 44.05/45.95 along -x was flagged \"short of the end\"; an end at x=47.5 cleared it).\n\nThe rule is: the spine is the trunk BETWEEN the two fan-outs; source pads lie before its start and target pads beyond its end. The text should say that in the author's terms (\"start the spine on the far side of the pads from where the bus is going; the legs reach back to them\") and the measured number should be signed consistently with the words. Two readers (owner, agent) both did the opposite of the rule from the text. Part of the wording item 01a03bc8b7's scope; reopened as its own bug because that item is resolved.\n\nOracle: test_bus_tool teach/status check that a spine starting on the pads' far side yields no corridor finding and the message for a spine starting on the near side names \"far side\".","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:02:12","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:44","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","live-test","pcb","teach-line"],"resolution":"e7c33c8: corridor findings, BUS_PATH_START_HINT and the BUS teach say the spine is the trunk between the fan-outs — start past the source pads in the travel direction, end short of the targets; number signed with the words. Implementer corrected the coordinator's suggested phrasing against the measured rule."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca98e157d51a067271ef7c3511a","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus via station places vias legal-by-clearance but walling off a neighbouring pad (0.2 mm from C13) — no corridor rule, no finding","description":"Live test 2026-08-25, attempt 3: station at spine index 2 = (52, 86) dropped three 0.6 mm vias at x=52, y 85.55/86/86.45, about 0.2 mm from C13's pad edge. Clearance rule satisfied, so the foreign-copper pass stayed silent; the owner's read: \"via jumper too close to C13 (will block traces)\". The station strands the pad — nothing can reach C13.1 from that side any more.\n\nA station via needs a CORRIDOR rule beyond clearance: keep at least (trace width + 2 x clearance) of bare board between the via ring and any foreign pad so that pad remains routable, and report bus_station_crowds_pad {via, pad, measured, required} when it does not. Landing with the finding is fine; silence is not. The gesture's station preview should draw the corridor so the author can nudge the station along the spine before committing.\n\nOracle: LGA/0603 fixture with a pad 0.25 mm from the station column -> finding present; pad 0.7 mm away -> absent.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:02:20","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:47","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bus-tool","live-test","pcb","via-station"],"resolution":"e5c12c0: bus_station_crowds_pad when a station via sits within (board trace width + 2 x clearance) but >= clearance of a foreign pad; corridor is for the walled-off pad's own future trace at the board rule width (Codex's bus-width objection rejected, accepted on re-check). test_bus_tool 13e/13f. Follow-ups: 01a03cc8d6 (centre-only pads, own target pads; net-class width above the rule width is under-reserved)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03ca99f7c7365b12738f6d711ec1e","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Journal the bus findings that stood at commit, so the board can say what it was warned about","description":"Live test 2026-08-25: the owner's bus committed with shorts at both ends; the change journal holds only three add_trace rows, so after the fact nobody can tell whether the status line showed the foreign-copper findings and they were accepted, or nothing showed. A bus commit (gesture and minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct alike) should append one journal row carrying the finding types/count and the pads named, alongside the trace ids it created. Read-only consumers: minerva_pcb_get_change_journal, the panel's history.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:02:24","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:49","priority":4,"tags":["bus-tool","journal","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03cc8c2ef7556be183be7b625578f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Sideways bus pad with a row inside the spine skips the corridor finding and its row is ignored by the span check","description":"Cold review of the bus fix batch (task-cycle 3, commit d690fed), note F2. pcb/ui/model/pcb_bus_geometry.gd bundle_routes: a sideways (column-mate) pad whose axial coordinate is positive — its row lies inside or beyond the spine's first segment — skips bus_pad_inside_corridor, and span_src/span_tgt ignore its row when checking the spine is long enough for the fan-outs. A ladder lane starting at station 0 that lies in that pad's band is then crossed, and only the clearance measurement names it, with the generic short message rather than a placement hint. Fix: either give sideways pads their own corridor rule (row must be outside the spine's end segment) or include their rows in the span check. Oracle: a column pad with row past the spine start alongside a ladder pad -> a named corridor/along-spine finding, not only bus_clearance.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:36:25","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:36:25","priority":3,"tags":["batch-review","bus-tool","geometry","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03cc8d6b1747d99f713991dcfc05f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Station corridor check overstates bare board on centre-only pads and can name a bus net's own target pad","description":"Cold review of the bus fix batch (task-cycle 3, commit 42802b1), note F4. pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd _bus_station_corridor_findings: (1) comp.pin_copper_distance on a pad with no land geometry (centre_only) returns the centre distance, so the reported bare-board gap is overstated by the pad's half-size — mirror the foreign-copper pass's centre_only caveat in the message and treat centre-only pads conservatively; (2) the pass compares every station via against every foreign-NET pad, so bus net A's via can name bus net B's own target pad as crowded when the station sits near the target fan-out — exclude the bus's own source/target pads (the legs are supposed to reach them). Oracle: LGA fixture station within 0.5 mm of a target pad of another bus net -> no finding; centre-only foreign pad -> message carries the caveat.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:36:30","updated_at":"2026-08-26T06:36:30","priority":4,"tags":["batch-review","bus-tool","pcb","via-station"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03cd42d1c7558a8bdd17b23e9b3cc","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb_ratsnest treats an unplated hole as an all-layer node while the bus tool and CAM treat it as no copper","description":"Found by the Fable judge of task-cycle 3's Codex round. CAM emits no copper for np_thru_hole (gerber.py ~604, ir_parity.py ~1051) and, since b90cd84, the bus tool treats a pure-NPTH pin as no copper on any layer. pcb/ui/model/pcb_ratsnest.gd ~312 still treats NPTH as an all-layer node, so the ratsnest can report a connection through an unplated hole that the fab will never make. Align the ratsnest with the model: an NPTH land joins nothing. Oracle: a net whose only bridge between top and bottom is an NPTH pin reads as two islands.","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:48:53","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:13","priority":4,"tags":["batch-review","npth","pcb","ratsnest"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03d2c5bad790a958b4d1ea9bc1226","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Never git-stash or rebase across a dirty .dct while the docket app runs — it re-reads the transient file and flushes the OLD state back","created_at":"2026-08-26T08:25:12","updated_at":"2026-08-26T08:25:12","tags":["data-loss","docket","git","gotcha"],"value":"Every docket tool call re-reads a .dct whose contents changed on disk. During `git stash push docs/plugins.dct` + `rebase --autosquash` + `stash pop`, the file on disk is briefly an OLDER commit's version; the next docket call reloads that, and the next flush writes it out — items created since are gone from the live DB and the working tree (2026-08-26: plugins.dct lost 549 lines incl. an in-flight DCR; recovered because HEAD still had them: `git checkout HEAD -- docs/plugins.dct` then docket_reload, NOT flush). Rules: commit the .dct BEFORE any rebase/stash; if a rebase must run with a dirty .dct, flush+commit it first as its own commit; after any git operation touching a .dct, docket_reload and docket_get one recent item before writing anything.","component":"docket","key":"dct.stash.rebase.reload","confidence":"lived it 2026-08-26; recovery verified"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03d2d561a7022b332154f027a0abe","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"go test: TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly fails at minerva_pcb_check_libraries (pre-existing at 9932487)","description":"Found by the full-suite run before the DCR 01a03ca8f7 HITL (2026-08-26). `go test ./...` in pcb/: every internal package ok; the root package fails TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly, whose t.Errorf at main_test.go:808 names minerva_pcb_check_libraries (\"dispatch using ONLY its declared-schema keys …\" followed by the spike-board YAML dump; every other tool logs STDIO SMOKE PASS). Reproduced identically in a clean worktree at 9932487 (before the batch), so it is pre-existing. Not yet diagnosed: whether check_libraries needs the library lock / network / a fetched cache that this machine lacks, or whether its declared schema drifted from what the handler requires. Oracle: the smoke test passes on a clean checkout with no network, or the tool's schema is corrected so the smoke dispatch is clean.","created_at":"2026-08-26T08:26:16","updated_at":"2026-08-26T08:26:16","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["ci","full-suite","go","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03d2d68bb77ee969b0549f12fd1b0","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"Reinstall the pcb plugin binary on the Linux box — installed build (2026-08-13) predates zone_fill, so real-worker GD suites cannot pass","description":"~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/pcb/pcb-plugin is dated 2026-08-13; the worker's zone_fill method landed 2026-08-22 (4ff8aa9). test_zone_fill_transport (real-worker) therefore fails the gd runner with \"unknown method: 'zone_fill'\" and falls back to canned answers. Any other real-worker suite that depends on a post-08-13 worker method is in the same state. Do: build/install the current pcb plugin (minerva_plugin_install from the dev lane, or the marketplace release after the release-order sequence), then re-run test_zone_fill_transport and confirm real_worker_used=true. Owner action (install is an owner-visible change to the running app).","created_at":"2026-08-26T08:26:21","updated_at":"2026-08-27T18:23:00","priority":3,"tags":["environment","full-suite","install","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f6f56e678559d457f214d572cf2","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Bus lanes sit at exactly width + clearance, so geometric DRC reports every lane pair at 0.199997 mm vs 0.2 required","description":"Live test 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2 (I2S1 trio, 0.25 mm tracks, 0.2 mm clearance): a bus with zero bus findings and clean connectivity DRC gets 22 gc2_copper_clearance rows from minerva_pcb_drc_geometric, all kind copper_pair on bottom, all measured_mm 0.199997 vs required 0.2, all between the bus's own three lanes. bundle_routes places lanes at pitch = width + clearance exactly (0.45 mm), so the edge-to-edge gap is nominally 0.2 and the geometric kernel's float arithmetic lands a few nm short; the bus tool's own bus_clearance measurement passes because it uses a tolerance. Two rules disagree about the same copper, and the fab-facing one is red.\n\nFix options (pick one, say why): (a) bundle_routes adds a small margin to the pitch (e.g. clearance + 0.01 mm, or round the pitch UP to the authoring grid quantum) so a bus is never exactly at the limit; (b) the geometric kernel treats measured >= required - 1 nm as clean (it already quantises to nm). (a) is the honest one — a bus exactly at the limit is fragile after any transform. Oracle: route the fixture bus in test_bus_tool / breakout geometry and run the geometric DRC on the exported board: zero gc2 rows between the bus's own lanes; the pitch pin in EXPECTED points updates accordingly.","created_at":"2026-08-26T18:57:36","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:15:24","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bus-tool","drc","live-test","pcb"],"resolution":"Task-cycle 5 P1: main 3ed904a (+fold f86badc), pushed at f1f855c. lane_pitch_between = rule + LANE_PITCH_MARGIN_MM 0.01 on every laid spacing; measuring checks keep the rule. Worker pytest proves the kernel flags the bare rule and passes the laid pitch. Verify live: route a bus, minerva_pcb_board_drc {geometric:true} → zero gc2 rows between its lanes."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f99764e70e9b7840668c4070750","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb panel has no board-level undo on screen — no Undo/Redo button, no Ctrl+Z — although PCBData.undo/redo exist and every commit writes a history step","description":"Owner, live HITL 2026-08-26: accidental mouse drags moved three traces 0.2 mm (the journal recorded three move_trace rows in one gesture) and nothing on screen could undo it. PCBPanel.gd ~4141 states it: \"PCBPanel currently wires NO board-level undo either (no Undo button, no Ctrl+Z handler anywhere in this plugin)\"; Ctrl+Z is claimed only for route-hint revision undo when a hint is selected. PCBData.undo()/redo()/can_undo() exist (pcb_data.gd ~2879) and every tool commit calls save_to_history so the steps are there.\n\nDo: (1) Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z (or Ctrl+Y) on the panel Control → PCBData.undo/redo when no route hint is selected (the existing seam at _unhandled_key_input already leaves the key unconsumed for exactly this); (2) answer the host's undo/redo request (Minerva bug filed alongside: Editor ribbon Undo/Redo have no PLUGIN_SCENE branch) with the same calls; (3) status line names what was undone (the history label). MCP parity: a minerva_pcb_undo / minerva_pcb_redo verb pair (today only hint_undo exists), so an agent can revert its own bad step without delete-and-redraw. Oracle: test_pcb_panel_ui — after a bus commit, Ctrl+Z removes every trace and via of that step and the status names it; redo restores; with a hint selected Ctrl+Z still targets the hint. Also: a selection drag of 0.2 mm is easy to do by accident — consider a drag threshold (a few px) before a translate begins, filed as part of this or separately.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:43:37","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:15:25","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","parity","pcb","undo"],"resolution":"Task-cycle 5 P2: main 9087b4b (+fold f86badc), pushed at f1f855c. ui/pcb_board_history.gd; Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z / Ctrl+Y (a selected hint keeps the keys, now always consumed); host hook pair; minerva_pcb_undo/redo; status shows Undid/Redid \"<step>\"; DRAG_TRAVEL_PX 3 px before a selection drag goes live. test_pcb_panel_ui 226/226. Needs Minerva 5f440938 for the ribbon buttons."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9ad05379e6a8d74b74f2522049","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"A via whose copper is all on one layer (bottom→bottom after a leg is rerouted) is kept silently — nothing names a vestigial via","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26: a bus landed with a via station (top continuation); the agent then deleted the top continuations and drew the legs on the bottom instead, starting each at its station via. Those vias (via_13, via_15) then joined a bottom lane to a bottom run — a layer change to the same layer — and stayed on the board doing nothing. The owner found them by eye (\"I selected 4 vias that seem to do nothing\"); no verb, finding or status line said so.\n\nDo: connectivity DRC gains a finding (e.g. via_single_layer) for a via whose touching copper is all on one layer (or that touches no copper at all — an orphan via), reported like dangling_endpoint; minerva_pcb_list_vias carries a per-via `layers_touched` so an agent can see it; optionally the canvas draws such a via hollow. Oracle: the fixture above → one finding per vestigial via; a via joining top to bottom copper → none; deleting the via clears it.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:45:05","updated_at":"2026-08-26T19:48:45","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["drc","hitl","pcb","via"],"resolution":"Not a bug — filtered by the owner's \"arguable routing error\" test 2026-08-26. A via whose copper is on one layer is legal (stitching vias, a via placed before its trace); the vestigial pair was the agent's routing error after rerouting legs, not something the tool should refuse or flag. The one useful piece — list_vias / describe_region reporting layers_touched per via — is folded into work_item 01a03f9dd6."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9ae9117d91b574a7d076b9f51a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"minerva_pcb_drc / drc_geometric should accept editor_name and read the live board — export_yaml is 280 KB and overflows the MCP result cap on every DRC pass","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26: every DRC check on smart-remote-v2 by an agent was export_yaml (281 KB, exceeds the MCP tool-result cap so it lands in a file) → parse the file → call the worker's DRC locally, because minerva_pcb_drc and minerva_pcb_drc_geometric take only {yaml} or {board}. The live-board verbs already ride the by-ref path for oversized boards (export_yaml, draft_check).\n\nDo: both DRC verbs accept {editor_name} as an alternative to yaml/board, snapshot the live board through the same by-ref pipe export_yaml uses, and return the findings only (no board echo). Same for minerva_pcb_pcb_board_health / assembly_check if they share the shape. Oracle: minerva_pcb_drc {editor_name} on the live board returns the same findings as minerva_pcb_drc {yaml: export_yaml(...)}; a board past 64 KiB works.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:45:11","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:14:42","priority":3,"tags":["drc","ergonomics","hitl","mcp","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9d8fe57976b554d86234abc2df","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Every copper-creating verb returns the geometry the next verb needs — route_bus_direct/propose_bus reply carries per-net polylines, layers, and ready-to-use free-end anchors","description":"Agent routing pass 2026-08-26: minerva_pcb_route_bus_direct returns trace_ids, via_ids and open_nets only. To finish an open lane the agent had to call export_trace_geometry (64 segment rows on this board) and search for the trace ending at the spine end. place_via and cut_trace already return their coordinates; add_trace returns the grown polyline.\n\nDo: route_bus_direct and workspace_propose_bus replies gain `nets_detail`: per net {net, lane_index, traces:[{trace_id, layer, points}], via_id?, landed: bool, target?, free_end?: {trace_id, end, x_mm, y_mm, layer}} — the free_end object shaped exactly as add_trace's `start` argument takes it, plus its coordinates. Rule for the manifest text: a verb that creates copper returns the coordinates and ids an agent needs for the NEXT verb, never only ids. Oracle: test_bus_tool — an open-ended commit's reply free_end feeds add_trace start verbatim and the extension lands on the same trace id; a landed net reports its target; polylines equal the board's.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:48:05","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:14:38","priority":2,"tags":["bus-tool","ergonomics","hitl","mcp","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9da3237eb88f940678aa5f273d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"route_bus_direct dry_run — the full reply (findings, open nets, polylines, clean order) without touching the board","description":"Agent routing pass 2026-08-26: to read a plan's findings the agent landed copper and deleted it, three times, churning the journal and history. workspace_propose_bus exists (ghosts) but its name does not lead an agent there when it is holding route_bus_direct.\n\nDo: route_bus_direct accepts dry_run: true and returns the identical reply shape (findings, note, open_nets, clean_order, nets_detail per the sibling item) with trace_ids/via_ids empty and `dry_run: true`, writing nothing (board triple unchanged, no journal row). The manifest description of route_bus_direct points at dry_run as the first call and at propose_bus for a reviewable ghost. Oracle: test_bus_tool — dry_run reply equals the committing reply minus ids; board state and journal unchanged.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:48:10","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:14:39","priority":3,"tags":["bus-tool","ergonomics","hitl","mcp","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9dc12f7872bf99372edde58390","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pin_info answers approach_sides, and the bus says \"no pick order lands both — leave one open\" in verb form when that is the case","description":"Agent routing pass 2026-08-26: the agent derived U3's reachable sides by hand (0.6 mm pads on 1 mm pitch, 0.25 mm track, 0.2 mm clearance → column pads reachable only from the outer side) and then found, by two failed attempts, that the header's net order and U3's pad order are mirror images so no single bus lands SD and BCLK from the west. The tool named the crossing and gave lane-order advice that cannot be followed when the other end fixes the order (DCR 01a03ca8f7 comment 90).\n\nDo: (1) minerva_pcb_pin_info (and bus_target_guidance rows) carry approach_sides: the sides of the pad from which a board-rule-width trace can reach it clear of neighbouring pads on the same component (north/east/south/west), computed from the footprint's pads and the design rules. (2) In bus_plan, when clean_pick_order returns empty and the crossing pair is at the target end, the finding's advice becomes the next verb: \"no pick order lands both <A> and <B> from this side — leave one open: targets [..., \\\"\\\", ...] and finish it from its free end\". Oracle: pin_info on U3.6 reports approach_sides == [west]; the two-column LGA fixture's bus reply carries that sentence with a concrete targets array.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:48:18","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:14:45","priority":3,"tags":["bus-tool","ergonomics","hitl","mcp","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9dd6b8792f8e1cbb4ca1380a8a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"minerva_pcb_describe_region — one read for everything in a board rectangle: pads with nets, traces with free ends, vias, zone outlines, keepouts, components","description":"Agent routing pass 2026-08-26: understanding the ground around U3 took list_zones + two describe_zone + get_components (37 parts) + spatial_query + pin_info calls and hand cross-referencing. spatial_query already reports copper in a region but not pad nets, zone outlines or trace free ends.\n\nDo: minerva_pcb_describe_region {editor_name, x_mm, y_mm, width_mm, height_mm, layer?} returns {components:[{ref, pos, pads:[{ref, net, x, y, layers, approach_sides}]}], traces:[{trace_id, net, layer, points, free_ends}], vias:[{via_id, net, x, y, layers_touched}], zones:[{zone_id, kind, net, layer, outline}], keepouts, notes}. `layers_touched` per via (which layers have copper meeting the via) also lands on minerva_pcb_list_vias — folded here from the closed not-a-bug 01a03f9ad0, so an agent can see a via that joins nothing on one side without the tool judging it. Read-only. Oracle: test_workspace_tools or a new small suite — the region around the LGA fixture returns its pads with nets, the pour outline, the open lane's free end, and a station via's layers_touched.","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:48:23","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:16","priority":4,"tags":["ergonomics","hitl","mcp","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. minerva_pcb_describe_region + layers_touched on list_vias."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03f9e9ab5725ba7aedbb49c3d4de2","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"TASK-CYCLE 5 PLAN — HITL bugs + LLM ergonomics for the pcb bus/trace verbs (Fable/Fable + Codex)","description":"PICK THIS UP after compaction. Owner approved the shape 2026-08-26: one Fable implementer agent carried across stations by follow-up messages, one Fable cold batch review (split by dimension if the diff is large), Codex serial at the end; removal counts as much as addition; commit the .dct BEFORE any rebase (hint dct.stash.rebase.reload).\n\nTWO REPOS, TWO BASES (serial, Minerva first because the pcb undo station answers its hook):\n\nMINERVA (development, base = HEAD at dispatch):\n M1  bug 01a03f994f — Editor ribbon Undo/Redo: PLUGIN_SCENE branch that asks the panel through PluginScenePanelHost (_on_panel_undo_request / _on_panel_redo_request, mirroring _on_panel_save_request); buttons hidden when the panel declares no undo. Oracle: a test host panel with the hook receives the call; one without → buttons hidden. Scoped run: the Minerva editor/plugin-host GD suites that cover Editor chrome + PluginScenePanelHost.\n\nMINERVA-PLUGINS (main, base = HEAD at dispatch):\n P1  bug 01a03f6f56 — bus lane pitch gains a margin (round up to the authoring grid quantum or clearance + 0.01) so geometric DRC never reads 0.199997. Oracle: fixture bus exported → drc_geometric: zero gc2 rows between its own lanes; breakout/bus pins update.\n P2  bug 01a03f9976 — board-level undo: Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Shift+Z on the panel (hint-selected case untouched) → PCBData.undo/redo; answer the host hook from M1; status names the step; minerva_pcb_undo / minerva_pcb_redo verbs; a drag threshold (few px) before a selection translate begins. Oracle: after a bus commit, Ctrl+Z removes its traces+vias and redo restores; verb parity; a 2 px drag moves nothing.\n P3  work_item 01a03f9d8f — route_bus_direct / propose_bus reply nets_detail with per-net polylines, layers, free_end shaped as add_trace's start arg. Oracle: reply free_end feeds add_trace verbatim, same trace id extended.\n P4  work_item 01a03f9da3 — route_bus_direct dry_run: identical reply, nothing written.\n P5  work_item 01a03f9ae9 — minerva_pcb_drc / drc_geometric accept editor_name (by-ref snapshot), findings only.\n P6  work_item 01a03f9dc1 — pin_info approach_sides; bus advice in verb form when no pick order lands both (DCR 01a03ca8f7 comment 90).\n P7  work_item 01a03f9dd6 — minerva_pcb_describe_region incl. layers_touched per via (LAST; drop if the batch runs long).\n Scoped run at batch end (Minerva closed): test_bus_tool, test_bus_breakout_geometry, test_pcb_bus_geometry, test_pcb_panel_ui, test_pcb_trace_tool, test_trace_cut, test_pcb_pin_inspector, test_manifest_tool_registration, test_workspace_tools, test_direct_copper_verbs + worker pytest test_drc*/test_zone* if P5 touches the worker. Re-pin EXPECTED_SUITES from runs. Then push, close items, HITL script = redo the I2S bus on the live board using dry_run → route → free_end → add_trace, then Ctrl+Z.\n\nNOT in this batch: DCR 01a03ca8f7 (at reviewing, awaiting its own HITL); chore 01a03d2d68 reinstall (owner); bug 01a03d2d56 go smoke (pre-existing); netlist U1S.3V3_1 (owner).","created_at":"2026-08-26T19:49:14","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:14:49","priority":1,"tags":["ergonomics","hitl","pcb","task-cycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a03fd143d175e4adce423c89ab5a29","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"/plugin/panel-ipc-channel-may-be-backend-tool-name","created_at":"2026-08-26T20:44:34","updated_at":"2026-08-26T20:44:34","value":"A panel's ipc_channels entry may be any registered BACKEND tool name (e.g. \"minerva_pcb_drc\"), not only dotted pcb.* channels: PluginScenePanelBroker checks ui.panels[*].ipc_channels and ui.ipc_messages (steps 4/5) then dispatches by name to the registered tool. Use this to give a panel verb a live-board form of a headless backend verb WITHOUT flipping the backend tool's executor — panel-executor tools are rejected by PluginToolRegistry.gd:502 when editor_name is absent, so flipping would break CI/headless callers. Established by minerva_pcb_board_drc (task-cycle 5, 85959b2).","key":"plugin/panel-ipc-channel-may-be-backend-tool-name"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04085bdb7721f8c608e794fad7157","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Ratsnest and connectivity DRC ignore zone fill — a via or pad inside a filled GND pour still draws an airwire and counts as an unjoined pin group","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2: U3's three GND pads are wired — U3.1+U3.2 by trace to via_22, U3.5 to via_21, both vias inside the filled bottom GND pour; geometric DRC fills the zone (\"solid connect\") and is clean. The ratsnest still draws one airwire between the two U3 GND islands, the corner reads \"GND — 32 joins left\", and minerva_pcb_drc reports GND as indeterminate {reason: zone_copper} with approximate: true. The connectivity graph is trace/via/pad only; zone copper is not a node, so every pour-connected pin group on the board looks unjoined. Two surfaces disagree about the same copper, and the on-screen one is the wrong one.\n\nDo: the connectivity model treats a FILLED zone as a copper node on its net and layer — a via, pad or trace end whose copper lies inside the fill region (not just the outline; thermal/solid connect both count, islands culled by the fill do not) joins that node. Ratsnest airwires, the \"joins left\" count and minerva_pcb_drc pin_groups all read the same graph, so they agree with drc_geometric; drop the GND indeterminate row once the zone is folded in. Cheapest honest version: use the fill regions the geometric kernel already computes (zone_filled static row) rather than a second fill.\n\nOracle: the fixture above → ratsnest draws no GND airwire inside U3, \"joins left\" for GND drops by the count of pour-connected pads, minerva_pcb_drc lists GND as complete with no indeterminate row; an unfilled or keepout zone changes nothing; a via sitting in a culled island still reads as unjoined. Existing ratsnest hint: the unplated-hole case (bug 01a03cd42d) shares this code — do not regress it.","created_at":"2026-08-27T00:01:41","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:11","priority":3,"tags":["drc","hitl","pcb","ratsnest","zone"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Filled pour is copper in the worker census (fill recomputed per check; uncomputable → indeterminate with detail). Real board: dangling 4→0, GND 3 honest joins left."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a040c2351f7cd7a1bbfb8ca716298c","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_pcb_connect_net is additive with no disconnect — reassigning a pin to another net leaves it on both (a netlist short), is not an undo step, and the panel's nets disagree with export_yaml","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2: owner asked to swap BTN3/BTN4 between U1S.GPIO40 and GPIO41. Two connect_net calls (BTN3 ← SW3.A + GPIO41; BTN4 ← SW4.A + GPIO40) produced get_nets BTN3 [GPIO40, SW3.A, GPIO41] and BTN4 [GPIO41, SW4.A, GPIO40] — each GPIO on both nets. pin_info reports the first net that contains the pin (both GPIOs said BTN3). route_bus_direct then refused \"GPIO40 is on net BTN3, not BTN4\". No verb removes a pin from a net; the four connect_net journal rows are NOT history steps (minerva_pcb_undo instead undid the previous bus commit — recovered with redo). export_yaml meanwhile still carried the ORIGINAL netlist (BTN3 [GPIO40, SW3.A]), so the live nets and the serialized document disagreed. Workaround used: edit the two pin lines in the exported YAML and minerva_pcb_load_board it back (which also revealed the panel had no adopted document path, so promote refused no_target_path).\n\nDo: (1) connect_net MOVES a pin — a pin sits on exactly one net; connecting it elsewhere removes it from its old net and the reply names the move ({pin, from_net, to_net}); an explicit minerva_pcb_disconnect_net {pins} (or connect_net net_name:\"\") clears a pin. (2) Both are ONE board history step each so Ctrl+Z / minerva_pcb_undo revert them like every other commit. (3) get_nets, pin_info and pcb.serialize read the same pin→net map so export_yaml can never show a different netlist than the panel. Oracle: the swap above via two connect_net calls → get_nets shows each GPIO on exactly one net, pin_info agrees, export_yaml agrees, undo twice restores the original, and route_bus_direct accepts GPIO40 as a BTN4 source.","created_at":"2026-08-27T01:07:44","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:48","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","mcp","netlist","pcb","undo"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed (plugins main c4ac3ef). connect_net moves; disconnect_net; one undo step; NET CONFLICT lead; verbs validate before composing. HITL: redo the BTN3/BTN4 swap with Pin Select + move/swap."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a040f6d7097891938a209f75e1bc8e","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"delete_traces attributes freshly drawn copper to a stale sidecar route candidate on another net — reopens its task and reports a false \"committed by\" on the reply","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2, after minerva_pcb_load_board {path: ccsandbox/.../smart_remote_v2_board.yaml} restored an OLD routing sidecar (.routing.json: three VBAT tasks, cand_1..cand_3, all validation:stale, from a prior session on a board revision 3) onto a board whose trace/via ids had just been re-minted (\"migrated board source v1→v2: minted N persistent entity id(s)\").\n\nThen: minerva_pcb_delete_traces {trace_ids:[trace:77f010b7… a +3V3_SW bottom hop drawn by add_trace minutes earlier], via_ids:[two vias placed by place_via]} replied \"this copper was committed by 1 route candidate(s); their commits are retired and their routing tasks are OPEN again … reopened_candidate_ids:[cand_3]\". cand_3 is a VBAT candidate (net VBAT, width 1.0, bend_count 1) — a different net, never committed in this session, and its geometry is nowhere near (60,82)–(57,82). workspace_list afterwards shows cand_3 disposition:proposed, task_state:open, validation:stale.\n\nDiagnosis (unverified): the commit→copper ownership map in the restored sidecar is keyed by ordinal/board-namespaced ids that collided with the re-minted ids of today's copper (the geometric DRC's own static row warns \"trace/via/hole/zone ids are ordinal-derived … NOT stable under reorder/insert\"). A stale sidecar record should never be able to claim live copper it did not author.\n\nDo: (1) ownership records carry the candidate's net and the committed geometry digest, and delete_traces only retires a commit when id AND net match (and, cheaply, the copper lies within the candidate's bounds); a mismatch is logged, not acted on. (2) On sidecar restore, ownership records whose ids do not resolve to copper of the same net are dropped as stale rather than re-attached. (3) The reply's \"committed by N candidate(s)\" sentence names the candidate ids and nets so a human can see the mismatch. Oracle: the fixture above → delete_traces on today's hop reopens nothing and the reply carries no reopened_candidate_ids; a genuine commit-then-delete still reopens its task.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:05:13","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:51","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["hitl","ids","pcb","sidecar","workspace"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Persistent ids at every GD mint site (survive load_board); ownership id+net+bounds; foreign/missing sidecar claims dropped by reason; negative via dims dropped by name. HITL: delete_traces with a via id held from before a reload."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a040f731dd7e9bba04a238c0606c47","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Connectivity DRC flags a trace end as dangling_endpoint while pin_info reports that same trace touching the pad — the two surfaces disagree, and the false finding gates promote","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2: trace:7a5239dd (VBAT, width 1.0, top) runs (73.0,90.0)→(70.5,90.0). J5.1 sits at (70.25,89.75) and J5.2 at (72.75,89.75); minerva_pcb_pin_info for BOTH pads lists trace:7a5239dd in trace_ids (touching), and by geometry a 1.0 mm trace whose end is 0.35 mm from the pad centre overlaps the pad copper. Yet minerva_pcb_drc / board_drc {geometric:false} reports dangling_endpoint at (73.0,90.0) AND at (70.5,90.0) on VBAT, every run, and minerva_pcb_promote refuses promotion_gated on exactly these (plus one genuine GND stub at (63.5,9.457)). The pin inspector says joined; the DRC says open; the gate believes the DRC.\n\nDo: the connectivity endpoint pass uses the same pad-contact test the pin inspector uses (trace end within the pad's copper outline, or the trace's swept width overlapping the pad — not centre-within-radius), so pin_info and drc can never disagree about a pad/trace touch. Oracle: the J5 fixture → zero VBAT dangling findings, VBAT reads complete (J5.1, J5.2 joined by that trace; F1.1 by trace:9bffabca); the GND stub at (63.5,9.457) still reports; promote on this board is gated only by real findings.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:05:37","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:49","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["connectivity","drc","hitl","pcb","promote"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. One contact predicate per side (copper_contact.py / pcb_copper_contact.gd) pinned by 18 spec/contact vectors; J5 VBAT, U2 EP stubs and the JP2.1 pass-through all read joined; a 1 mm-short run still dangles. HITL: promote the board — no false dangling gate."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a040f7523e7c30b049f7de896bf30e","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"minerva_pcb_set_view {fit:true} on a narrow docked panel frames 106×176 mm around the 90×100 board — the board shrinks to a sliver and the owner reads it as \"the board disappeared\"","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26: panel docked bottom, width 554 px (minerva_pcb_get_layout_state mode:medium). minerva_pcb_set_view {fit:true} replied visible {x:-8.75, y:-38.2, width:105.9, height:175.7}, zoom 3.45 px/mm — a 90×100 board drawn ~310 px tall in the middle of a ~600 px viewport with 38 mm of empty margin above and below. The owner's next message was \"woah, board disappeared?\". set_view {region:{0,0,90,100}, margin_mm:3} gave a sane frame (visible width 96 mm, zoom 3.81) — so fit is not computing the tighter of the two axes, or is fitting to a stale/oversized canvas size. Earlier in the session, with the same panel, fit:true returned the identical 105.9×175.7 frame, so it is deterministic, not a race.\n\nDo: fit:true frames the board's bounds plus a small margin using the CURRENT viewport size (min of width-fit and height-fit zoom), exactly what region:{board} does; the reply's visible rect should be within ~10 % of the board on the constrained axis. Oracle: on a 554 px-wide panel, fit:true's visible.width_mm ≤ 100 for this board, and the human sees the board fill the panel.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:05:45","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:53","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["hitl","mcp","pcb","view"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. PcbViewFit: outline ∪ parts against the live viewport, shared with capture. HITL: fit:true on the narrow docked panel."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04106bd4d78f8a83bf208dff6d41a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Route hints can't express a layer hop, and proposed vias carry no net/owner — a human's \"F.Cu, duck under here, F.Cu\" intent arrives as an unroutable hint plus orphan via ghosts the agent must geometry-match","description":"Live HITL 2026-08-26 (amp +3V3_SW feed on smart-remote-v2): the owner drew pcb_route_hint ann_0005 {layer:F.Cu, single_trace, waypoints (67.2,74.9)→(85.3,75.1)→(85.3,38.7)→(67.9,39.0), dest C15.1} and then, because the path must pass under VBAT_F (x=75.4) and the buck output cluster (x≈85), proposed FOUR vias with minerva_pcb_propose_via at (72.2,75.0),(78.0,75.1),(85.2,65.3),(85.2,53.7). workspace_list shows those as cand_4..cand_7 with net:\"\", layers:[], task_id:\"\", validation:unchecked, source_hint_ids:[] — no net, no owner, no pairing. The hint itself is un-routable as written (F.Cu straight through VBAT_F). The agent recovered the intent only by matching via coordinates to hint segments by eye; a second hint nearby would make it ambiguous.\n\nDo: (1) a route-hint waypoint may carry `layer` (canonical copper id); the polyline changes layer at that waypoint and apply_route_hints / the router places the via there (via size from design_rules), so one hint expresses F.Cu→B.Cu→F.Cu. The hint editor's waypoint handle offers the layer chooser. (2) minerva_pcb_propose_via accepts optional `net` and `for_hint` (annotation id); a via ghost then lists under that hint's task with the net filled, and workspace_list groups it with the hint's candidate instead of as an orphan. A ghost with neither stays allowed but is labelled unowned in the reply. (3) get_selection / workspace_get_active on a via ghost report its hint and net. Oracle: the fixture above expressed as one hint with two layer-carrying waypoints routes clean with exactly two vias and no separate ghosts; a propose_via with for_hint shows source_hint_ids and net on its candidate row; the old four-ghost form still works but each row says owner:none.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:22:35","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:15","priority":3,"tags":["ergonomics","hints","hitl","mcp","pcb","via"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Layered waypoints → as-drawn hop vias; propose_via net+for_hint; unowned ghosts labelled. GUI layer chooser on the bend handle = 01a042fd8292."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0410c62c877158808e80bf6cef2a7","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"Pin Select tool in the selection area — click a pad to select it as a first-class object (pin info, net, approach sides, \"swap with…\" / \"move net to this pin\"), for the human and for get_selection","description":"Owner, live HITL 2026-08-26 (asking \"U1S.8/9 are on the east column but J2 is west — which free west pins can take I2C?\"): today a PIN is not selectable. Universal Select picks the component (U1S, 44 pins) or a trace; the Pin Info section only shows a pin after a hover/click dance, and get_selection never returns kind:'pad', so the human cannot point at a pad and say \"this one\". Pin-level questions — what net is this, what can reach it, swap this net onto that pin, route from here — are the co-working questions we ask most on a socketed devkit, and the agent answers them today by pin_info(ref) after guessing the ref from coordinates.\n\nDesign: a Pin Select tool beside Select in the selection area of the toolbar (keyboard: P). Clicking a pad selects THE PAD: highlight on the pad only, the Pin Info section fills (ref, net, net members, approach_sides, position, touching copper — the pin_info reply), get_selection returns {kind:'pad', ref, component, pin, net, position, approach_sides}. Shift-click adds a second pad (two-pad selection). With one pad selected the context menu / properties offer: \"Start trace here\" (arms the Trace tool at the pad), \"Show net\" (highlight every pad on its net), \"Move net to…\" (pick another pad on the same component: reassigns the net — depends on bug 01a040c235 so it is one undo step), and with two pads selected on one component \"Swap nets\" (the BTN3/BTN4 case done by hand today). Pins of a component filtered by net-free are listed in the properties pane (\"free pins on U1S: GPIO0, 38, 39, 45, 48, 43, 44…\") so \"what is available on the west side\" is a read, not a scan. Universal Select is unchanged; Pin Select is a sibling mode like Via/Trace. MCP parity: minerva_pcb_get_selection carries the pad rows; a minerva_pcb_select {refs:[...]} verb lets the agent point back at pads for the human (the deictic reverse).\n\nOracle (test_pcb_panel_ui + test_pcb_pin_inspector): P then click on U1S.GPIO38 → selection is exactly that pad, Pin Info shows net (unconnected) and approach_sides [west]; shift-click GPIO39 → two pads; \"Swap nets\" on two netted pads swaps them in one undo step and pin_info agrees; get_selection returns the pad rows; Select tool behaviour unchanged.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:28:45","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:34:39","priority":2,"tags":["dcr","hitl","pcb","pins","selection","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a041169f7d7859aa94086b7fb4cf4f","type":"chore","status":"in_progress","title":"HANDOFF — task-cycle 6 plan from the 2026-08-26 HITL: Minerva + pcb-plugin code work (bugs, ergonomics, Pin Select DCR) — for the Linux box; update as the HITL continues","description":"PICK THIS UP on the Linux box. Source: live co-working HITL 2026-08-26 on the Mac (owner + agent) exercising the pcb plugin on smart-remote-v2; the board is a side-effect and is NOT part of this work. Items below are all filed; this chore is the batch. Same shape as task-cycle 5 (chore 01a03f9e9ab5): one implementer carried across stations, one cold review, Codex serial at the end, dry_run/oracle per station, commit the .dct BEFORE any rebase (hint dct.stash.rebase.reload). Board fixture for oracles: the ccsandbox smart_remote_v2_board.yaml as of 2026-08-26 evening (resolved form, uncommitted on the Mac — owner will commit).\n\nMINERVA (development):\n M1  bug 01a0410a18 — annotation dock pane ≤ 50 % of editor height, opens ≈1/3, remembered per tab (verify ownership: editor chrome vs pcb panel drawer; mirror to plugins if it is the plugin's).\n\nMINERVA-PLUGINS (main), priority order:\n P1  bug 01a040c235 (sev 2) — connect_net MOVES a pin (one net per pin), add disconnect, both ONE undo step, get_nets/pin_info/pcb.serialize read one pin→net map so export_yaml never disagrees with the panel.\n P2  bug 01a040f731 (sev 2) — connectivity dangling_endpoint uses the same pad-contact test as pin_info (trace swept width vs pad outline); false findings stop gating promote. Fixture: J5 VBAT trace 7a5239 (1.0 mm, ends 0.35 mm off pad centres).\n P3  dcr 01a0410c62 — Pin Select tool: pad as first-class selection (P key), multi-pad shift-click, get_selection pad rows {ref, net, position, approach_sides}, per-component free-pins read with roles (strapping/uart_console/jtag/onboard_led/adc from the socket pin table), side/column filter, \"Move net to…\"/\"Swap nets\" (depends on P1), minerva_pcb_select verb. Comment #92 = owner scenario (\"see these pins? move them to the other side of U1S\").\n P4  work_item 01a04106bd — route-hint waypoints carry `layer` (router places the hop vias); propose_via takes net + for_hint and lists under its hint; unowned ghosts labelled.\n P5  work_item 01a04085bd — ratsnest + connectivity DRC treat a FILLED zone as a copper node (reuse the geometric kernel's fill regions); GND indeterminate row goes away; \"joins left\" honest.\n P6  bug 01a040f6d7 (sev 3) — delete_traces commit-ownership keyed by id AND net (+ bounds); stale sidecar records that don't resolve are dropped on restore, never re-attached.\n P7  bug 01a040f7523e (sev 3) — set_view fit:true fits the current viewport (min of the two axes' zoom); narrow docked panel oracle.\n P8  work_item 01a03f9dd6 — describe_region (carried from cycle 5).\n DECIDE AT PLANNING (unfiled, from nudge pcb-hitl/owe-list-2026-08-26): get_image ignores set_view framing and draws no annotations; load_board {path} silently drops live annotations when the sidecar switches; promote has no acknowledge-through and the panel has no adopted doc path until load_board {path}; 'footprint graphics not attached' warning on every resolved-export load; cycle-5 P6 follow-up (canvas status line lacks the leave-one-open sentence); annotations_add host_rejected still reason-less (01a01dbdc5).\n\nScoped run at batch end: test_pcb_panel_ui, test_pcb_pin_inspector, test_workspace_tools, test_direct_copper_verbs, test_manifest_tool_registration, test_board_by_path, test_bus_tool + worker pytest test_drc*/test_zone* if P2/P5 touch the worker; re-pin EXPECTED_SUITES; push; close items; HITL script back on the Mac = redo the BTN3/BTN4 swap with Pin Select + connect_net move, promote the board, fit:true on the docked panel.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:39:56","updated_at":"2026-08-27T20:32:22","priority":1,"tags":["ergonomics","handoff","hitl","pcb","task-cycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0411a930b74deab2668fc841ff108","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Canvas Trace tool cannot terminate on a same-net copper pour — a click inside the GND fill does not end the trace, the owner has to double-click and leave it \"incomplete\"","description":"Owner, live HITL 2026-08-26 on smart-remote-v2 after the bottom GND pour was extended west: drawing a GND trace on B.Cu from a pad/via toward the pour, a single click inside the filled pour does NOT finish the trace the way a click on a pad, via or same-net trace does; the only way out is a double-click, which commits the trace as a free end. Result: two traces (trace:78aeadff ending (27.0,9.375), trace:a3ea2e31 ending (26.625,12.0)) that are physically joined to the pour but that the tool, the status line and the connectivity DRC all treat as unfinished. Related: work_item 01a04085bd (the model does not know zone fill is copper) — this is the AUTHORING side of that gap.\n\nDo: the Trace tool's terminator set = pad ∪ via ∪ same-net trace ∪ same-net FILLED zone region on the working layer (the fill region the geometric kernel computes, not the outline). A click inside such a region ends the trace there (status: 'ended on GND pour'), it is journalled as one step like any landing, and the trace end is a join in the connectivity model (01a04085bd). Clicking inside a zone of a DIFFERENT net is refused by name (trace_author_error: zone_net_mismatch) instead of silently continuing. MCP parity: add_trace whose last point lies inside a same-net fill reports landed_on: {zone_id, net} in its reply. Oracle (test_pcb_trace_tool): GND trace from a via, click inside the GND pour → trace ends, no double-click, connectivity reports no dangling end; same click on a +3V3 trace → refused; add_trace reply carries landed_on.","created_at":"2026-08-27T02:44:15","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:50","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["ergonomics","hitl","pcb","trace-tool","zone"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. Trace tool terminates on a click inside a same-net pour fill (net-scoped, foreign pour is a waypoint). HITL: end a GND run in the bottom pour with one click."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0414891df716a95894fabc0e8a06f","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"Fab preview fails with payload_too_large on the smart-remote class of board — the View toggle sends the whole board by value, unlike export_yaml/board_drc which ride the by-ref path","description":"Owner, live HITL 2026-08-26 ~21:50 PDT: View > Fab preview on the smart-remote-v2 tab (44 components, 35 nets, ~115 traces, resolved YAML ≈ 326 KB) returns a 'payload too large' error and no overlay; minerva_pcb_view_state {show_fab_preview:true} reports the flag set with changed:[] and nothing renders. The MCP twin minerva_pcb_pcb_fab_preview takes {board:<dict>} or {yaml} BY VALUE, so the same board exceeds the 64 KiB broker/IPC cap. Sibling of bug 01a007f1dd (assembly advisory sends the whole resolved board by value); export_yaml, board_drc and draft_check already snapshot the live board by reference for oversized boards, so the pattern exists.\n\nDo: the panel's fab-preview refetch and minerva_pcb_pcb_fab_preview accept editor_name and snapshot the live board by reference (same pipe as export_yaml/board_drc) when the document is over the cap; the reply carries layers/unrendered as today. Same for minerva_pcb_pcb_mask_view (show_mask) if it shares the path — verify. The View menu toggle must surface the failure in the status line (today it silently leaves the flag on) and the view_state reply should say the overlay is NOT rendered (overlay_state: 'failed: payload_too_large') instead of just echoing the flag. Oracle: on this board, View > Fab preview renders every copper/mask/silk/edge layer or lists it in unrendered with a reason; view_state after a failed refetch reports it; a 20-component board still works by value.","created_at":"2026-08-27T03:34:30","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:13:55","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["by-ref","fab-preview","hitl","ipc","pcb"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. fab_preview/mask_view/zone_fill/board_health/assembly_check by-ref; overlay flags retract on failure with a visible lead + view_state.overlay_unavailable. HITL: View > Fab preview on smart-remote-v2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04150a898732493d5599755bee479","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb plugin authoring contract for agents after task-cycle 6 (supersedes the 2026-08-26 HITL gotcha list)","created_at":"2026-08-27T03:43:20","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:33:16","tags":["agent-contract","ergonomics","mcp","pcb"],"value":"State on minerva-plugins main after task-cycle 6 (commits 7d2ac0e..b57614c). What changed vs the HITL list:\n1. NETLIST: minerva_pcb_connect_net MOVES a pin (one net per pin); minerva_pcb_disconnect_net exists; minerva_pcb_move_net / minerva_pcb_swap_nets act on pad selections; each is ONE undo step. load_board reports double-booked pins as a NET CONFLICT lead. Ids (trace/via/zone/cutout/graphic) are persistent <type>:<32hex> and SURVIVE load_board — hold them across reloads. (Board id + mounting-hole ids still re-mint: 01a042fd4b7c.)\n2. FOOTPRINTS: a component whose YAML carries a `pads` list compiles from its own geometry with no library hit; `pads: []` = zero pads (graphics-only pseudo-components work). No `pads` key = library authority + inline pin overrides, and an unknown ref still fails footprint_unresolved. Indeterminate load-time checks are shown in the GUI status lead.\n3. SILK TEXT: minerva_pcb_add_silk_text {text, position, layer F.SilkS|B.SilkS, size_mm, rotation_deg?} and minerva_pcb_add_graphic / delete_graphic — board-level, fabricated, B side mirrored once about the text anchor. Do not hand-write stroke polylines any more.\n4. ROTATION: degrees apply as R(-angle) in the y-down frame: a WEST pad lands SOUTH at 90, NORTH at 270. move/rotate replies now carry the component's pad rows, so no pin_info round trip is needed. The word \"clockwise\" in the API is under review (01a042fd8292).\n5. CONNECTIVITY: one copper-contact predicate per side (swept trace width vs true pad land incl. EP, pass-through, rotated/roundrect; ratio-less roundrect = stadium; NPTH = no copper) pinned by pcb/spec/contact vectors. A FILLED pour is copper for the worker census (fill recomputed per check; uncomputable → indeterminate with detail). The Trace tool terminates on a same-net pour. pin_info.position is now {x_mm,y_mm}.\n6. BUS: unchanged — dry_run first; end the spine level with the last target's row/column (now in route_bus_direct's description).\n7. DOC PATH / PROMOTE: unchanged (adopt via load_board {path}; promote has no acknowledge-through).\n8. VIEW: set_view {fit:true} fits outline ∪ parts to the live viewport and refuses when the canvas has no rect. fab_preview/mask_view/zone_fill/board_health/assembly_check ride the by-ref path; view_state.overlay_unavailable names any overlay that failed. get_image still ignores set_view framing and draws no annotations (deferred).\n9. READS: minerva_pcb_describe_region {x_mm,y_mm,width_mm,height_mm,layer?} = pads with nets, traces with free_ends, vias with layers_touched (also on list_vias), zones/keepouts/cutouts/notes in one call. minerva_pcb_get_selection returns pad rows; minerva_pcb_select sets a selection; minerva_pcb_free_pins lists a component's unconnected pins with roles and a side filter. Pin Select tool = P key, shift-click.\n10. ROUTE HINTS: waypoints may carry `layer`; the as-drawn materializer places the hop vias (engine path warns and drops hops). propose_via takes net + for_hint. Via size comes from design_rules everywhere; a route with no resolvable width (reply → hint → net copper → design_rules.trace_width_mm) is refused, never given 0.25 mm.\n11. TESTING ON LINUX: the installed runtime bundle is stale (zone_fill missing); run-gd-tests.sh now refuses real-worker suites until it is refreshed (owner action 01a03d2d68bb).","component":"pcb","key":"authoring-gotchas-2026-08","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":6,"research_cost":40}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04150ccf17fa79f2cedad5eb860ec","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"smart-remote-v2 board state after the 2026-08-26 HITL (ccsandbox main) — the fixture for task-cycle-6 oracles","created_at":"2026-08-27T03:43:29","updated_at":"2026-08-27T03:43:29","tags":["fixture","hitl","pcb","smart-remote"],"value":"File: ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml (resolved 11k-line form; load with minerva_pcb_load_board {path} into a PCB tab). Fully routed: missing_copper empty; every net complete except VBAT (false-dangling pair at J5 — 01a040f731). Geometric DRC clean. 44 components, 35 nets, ~116 traces, 42 vias. Power: buck -> JP1 -> +3V3 (socket 3V3_2, C2, C4, C5, C6, R3) -> JP2 -> +3V3_Q2 (Q2.2, C3) -> Q2 -> +3V3_SW rail y=82 -> JP3 mic / JP4 amp / JP5 modules -> JP6 OLED. Six 1206 0R links (Fuse_1206 footprint, C17888) + TP1 GND pad pair at (8,30). RF window is a cutout 36..54 x 63.9..69.8. Bottom GND pour covers x 2..27.5 west + x 62.5..89.5 east + south band, notch keeps 5 mm off U1S. B.SilkS text/peace sign are 65 polylines in TP1's graphics. Known DRC-blind ends: 4 pour-terminated GND, 2 EP stubs on U2, 2 false VBAT. Stale VBAT candidates cand_1..3 in the routing sidecar. BOM = Minerva spreadsheet 'Smart Remote v2b DevKit Socketed'; cost sheet 'Smart Remote v2 JLC cost estimate' (~$25/board x5, estimate).","component":"pcb","key":"smart-remote-v2-board-state-2026-08-26","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0418dc657740ab1d347a3285615bd","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"pcb: silk-text and graphic authoring verbs — minerva_pcb_add_silk_text / add_graphic (stroke font, layer, mirrored for the B side)","description":"Origin: HITL 2026-08-26 (handoff chore 01a041169f7d comment #104). There is no silk/graphic authoring verb, and fp_text is 'not fabricated in v1' by the emitter, so board text (copyright line, board name) had to be hand-generated stroke geometry — an agent wrote a 5x7 vector font and 65 B.SilkS polylines into the YAML by hand.\n\nDesired outcome: an agent (via MCP) and the panel can place board-level text and simple graphics without hand-authoring polylines.\n- minerva_pcb_add_silk_text {text, position, layer (F.SilkS|B.SilkS), size_mm, rotation?, id?} — renders the string as stroke-font polylines on the named silk layer; B-side text is mirrored so it reads correctly from the bottom; returns the graphic id and its bounds.\n- minerva_pcb_add_graphic {layer, polylines|rect|circle, width_mm, id?} — raw stroke geometry on a silk or courtyard layer, same id/bounds reply.\n- Both are board-level graphics (not hung off a component), survive export_yaml → load_board round-trip, are fabricated by the emitter, are undoable, and are deletable by id.\n- Panel: the graphics render on the canvas like any other silk; a delete affordance by selection is enough for v1 (no in-panel text tool required).\n\nOracle: add_silk_text on B.SilkS → export_yaml carries the polylines on B.SilkS, mirrored (x reflected about the text origin) relative to the same call on F.SilkS; the fab emitter's Gerber for B.SilkS contains the strokes; load_board of the exported YAML reproduces identical bounds; the existing hand-authored TP1 polylines on smart-remote-v2 are unaffected. Not in scope: TrueType/outline fonts, text editing after placement, pseudo-components (that is the resolved-geometry item).","created_at":"2026-08-27T04:50:05","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:21","priority":2,"tags":["hitl","mcp","pcb","silk","task-cycle-6"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0418de7a0748abe1ae583cd861ed0","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb: resolved-geometry components stand on their own YAML — no footprint_unresolved for a component that carries full pad geometry; pads: [] means no pads","description":"Origin: HITL 2026-08-26 (handoff chore 01a041169f7d comments #99 item 1 and #104 item 2). Two failures of the same root:\n(1) The worker refuses to compile when a component's footprint ref is not in the seed/wip library layers (footprint_unresolved on 'Resistor_SMD:R_1206_3216Metric', 'TestPoint:TestPoint_Pad_2.0x2.0mm') — board_drc goes 'indeterminate' and every pin on that part reads net_pin_unresolved, even though the YAML carries full pad geometry.\n(2) A known footprint ref attaches the LIBRARY pads even when the YAML says pads: [] (LOGO1 with Fuse_1206 grew two pads at (0,0) → gc5 copper-to-edge). A graphics-only pseudo-component is therefore impossible.\n\nDesired outcome: a component whose YAML carries resolved geometry (pads and/or graphics) compiles and DRCs without a library hit; the library is consulted only for what the YAML does not carry; an explicit pads: [] yields zero pads. Keep the existing rule that the footprint is the geometry authority when the YAML does NOT carry geometry (hint: footprint-is-geometry-authority).\n\nOracle: smart-remote-v2 board with JP1..JP6 (R_1206 ref absent from the library) → board_drc determinate, no net_pin_unresolved, JP pins land at the YAML pad positions; LOGO1 with footprint Fuse_1206 and pads: [] → zero pads, no gc5; a component with NO inline geometry and an unknown ref still reports footprint_unresolved. Related: 01a03c97b6 (seed library hint).","created_at":"2026-08-27T04:50:14","updated_at":"2026-08-27T13:14:06","priority":2,"tags":["footprint","hitl","pcb","task-cycle-6","worker"],"resolution":"TC6 pushed. FULL (pads key present, list) vs PARTIAL (no key) rule; present-but-not-list, bad layer, null graphic refuse by name."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a041fd7a827c98bf6914b3ef960808","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"KiCad np_thru_hole pads declare *.Cu — that is a keepout, not copper","created_at":"2026-08-27T06:52:06","updated_at":"2026-08-27T06:52:06","tags":["connectivity","drc","kicad","npth","pcb"],"value":"A KiCad `np_thru_hole` pad line carries copper layers (`*.Cu` / `F.Cu B.Cu`), which says where copper must be kept AWAY, not where copper is. Any predicate that reads that layer list hands a drilled mechanical hole an all-layer land — a via whose barrel is the hole's full diameter. Witness in this repo's own library: library/footprints/MountingHole.pretty/MountingHole_3.2mm_M3.kicad_mod line 16, `(pad 1 np_thru_hole circle (size 3.2 3.2) (drill 3.2) (layers *.Cu *.Mask))` — NUMBERED, so it reaches both solvers (the worker's net-blind dangling credit and the panel's pad_nodes number match). ONE predicate now answers it: pad_source.is_unplated_hole(); copper_contact.pad_node and pcb_copper_contact.physical_pad_node both return a no-copper node for it. pad_source.has_copper() still says True for such a pad — do NOT use has_copper to test for plating.","component":"pcb-plugin","key":"npth-declares-cu-layers","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fcfe217946abdefb8ea4c886bd","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: refdes stroke font (stroke_font.py) is a GPL-2 Newstroke subset embedded in a proprietary repo — unify on the in-house board font or replace; NOTICE.md has no section for source-embedded third-party data","description":"Found while adding board-level silk text (DCR 01a0418dc6). pcb/worker/pcb_worker/stroke_font.py renders reference designators from a 26-glyph table extracted from KiCad's newstroke_font.cpp, which carries a GPL-2.0-or-later header; this repo ships under LICENSE.md (proprietary). The new board_font.py (95 printable ASCII, 5x7, authored in-house) does not share the problem. Options: (a) render refdes with board_font.py too — one typeface per board, but every committed refdes Gerber golden moves; (b) keep Newstroke and record it as a licensed dependency. Either way pcb/scripts/gen_notice.py inventories only the footprint lock, so no source-embedded third-party table appears in any licence inventory — add a section. Oracle: NOTICE.md names every third-party data table embedded in source, and either no GPL table remains or the licence obligations are recorded.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:11","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:47","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["fcib","licence","pcb","silk"],"resolution":"stroke_font.py deleted; refdes via board_font; NOTICE inventories embedded tables (allowlist empty, fail-closed on undeclared/missing/out-of-tree). Newstroke-DERIVED strokes remain in LOGO_Owl coupon footprint + payload_cap fixture — sev-2 follow-up 01a045e4da8e."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fd1c83705a8f178423d1cd6e80","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: a component on layer: bottom keeps F.Cu pads and un-mirrored pad offsets through resolve, and drc._harvest_pads does not mirror bottom-side footprints — bottom-mounted parts have their copper placed on top","description":"Two measurements from task-cycle 6 (stations P2 and P5). (1) resolve: R2 authored `layer: bottom` comes back with layers [\"F.Cu\",\"F.Paste\",\"F.Mask\"] and un-mirrored offsets, so every layer-aware consumer (contact predicate, ratsnest, DRC) puts a bottom part's copper on the top layer. (2) drc._harvest_pads (raw path) places U2 (bottom, rot 90) pad 4 at (33.08,12.38) while the IR path (geometry.PlacementTransform.point, which negates local y for bottom) puts it at (22.92,12.38); the projected path therefore reports 2 wrong_net_pad findings on parity_corners U2.2/U2.3 that the raw path does not — pre-existing, reproduced with the batch's land emission stripped. Related: ir_pads.pad_land/smd_shape use radians(+rotation) while the pinned convention is radians(-deg) — invisible at 0/90/180/270, mirrors a 45° pad. Oracle: parity_corners.yaml compiled via raw and IR paths yields identical pad positions for U2; a bottom-side part's pads carry B.* layers after resolve; a 45° pad's land matches geometry.rotate_local_offset.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:19","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:29","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["bottom-side","drc","geometry","pcb","worker"],"resolution":"drc._harvest_pads delegates to geometry.component_transform; rotation_radians pins the CW convention for ir_pads / drc_geometric. Resolve unchanged by design (footprint-local FULL geometry mirrored by compile_board). Remnants filed as 01a0457a53ea."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fd32ac7023af1fa7deeea90bd1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: worker pin→net readers disagree (drc._pin_net_map last-wins vs compile_board._pad_net_name first-wins) and no multi_net_pin finding exists — a double-booked pin compiles on one net and DRCs against another","description":"Found at task-cycle 6 P1 (the panel now enforces one net per pin and reports conflicts on load; the worker still does not). drc.py ~263 builds (ref,pin)→net last-wins; compile_board.py ~452 resolves the same pin first-wins. A doubly-listed pin therefore compiles onto one net and is checked against another, silently. Adding a `multi_net_pin` finding means a new key in run_drc's counts (breaks two exact-equality asserts in tests/test_drc.py) and board_validate.py is a strict Go-parity boundary (internal/board/validate.go + spec/vectors) where a new code needs a matching Go change. Oracle: a YAML listing one pin under two nets yields one named finding from validate (both validators, one spec vector) and both readers pick the same net.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:24","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:24","priority":3,"tags":["nets","parity","pcb","validate","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fd4b7c78059784f06d37c94c79","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: board id and mounting-hole ids are re-minted on every load_board (to_board_dict emits no board id, hardcodes version: 1, holes have no GD mint) — every load logs a spurious \"migrated v1→v2\" warning and the v2 write-gate never runs on panel-authored boards","description":"Left over from task-cycle 6 P6 (trace/via/zone/cutout/graphic ids now persist). to_board_dict() emits no `id` and hardcodes version 1, so Go's MigrateV1toV2 mints a fresh board:<hex> each load and re-mints mounting-hole ids; the v2 write-gate validation (unminted_persistent_id, duplicate-id) never runs on a panel-authored board. Do: mint + persist the board id and hole ids through PcbEntityId, emit version 2 once every persistent collection carries minted ids, and make a clean round trip produce zero migration warnings. Oracle: load_board of an exported board logs no \"migrated\" warning and the v2 validator runs on the exported YAML.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:31","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:31","priority":3,"tags":["ids","load_board","pcb","schema-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fd5f16776d8508ab1e9a2cd6f4","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: canvas delete gesture retires a stranger's route commit — _remove_entity / _delete_selection have no ownership pre-check, and retire_commits_owning_trace matches id-only internally","description":"Task-cycle 6 P6 guarded delete_traces/delete_via/_retire_commits_owning_trace at their MCP call sites with an id+net(+bounds) ownership prune BEFORE copper changes. The canvas delete gesture (pcb_canvas.gd _remove_entity via _delete_selection / _handle_eraser_click) has no pre-check and relies on the lazy _workspace_ctx reconcile that runs after the copper is gone, when ownership can no longer be tested; retire_commits_owning_trace itself still matches by id only. Do: one per-gesture prune (not per entity) in the canvas delete paths, and move the id+net check inside retire_commits_owning_trace so no caller can skip it. Oracle: draw SIG copper by hand, seed a GND candidate whose sidecar claims that id, delete via the canvas trash-can — the GND candidate stays committed.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:36","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:36","priority":3,"tags":["canvas","ownership","pcb","routing-workspace"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fd8292709fbedcda90e7a8291f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: Pin Select follow-ups from the DCR — \"Start trace here\" and \"Show net\" context actions, marquee pad selection, a layer chooser on the route-hint bend handle, and a rotation vocabulary fix (\"clockwise\" maps to +90 which reads counter-clockwise on the y-down screen)","description":"Left out of task-cycle 6 P3/P4 deliberately. (1) DCR 01a0410c62 asks for \"Start trace here\" (arm the Trace tool at the selected pad) and \"Show net\" (highlight every pad on the net) on the pad selection; only move_net / swap_nets shipped. (2) Drag/marquee pad selection while Pin Select is armed (shift-click only today); a pad selection also does not count toward selection_count(), so the trash-can batch cannot delete pads (same asymmetry as candidates). (3) Route-hint waypoints can carry a layer (work item 01a04106bd) but only via annotations_update kind_payload; hint_move_bend/_insert_bend preserve a layer but cannot set one — add a `layer` arg + a right-click item on BendHandleEditTool. (4) Vocabulary: panel_tools._rotate_component maps \"clockwise\" to +90 and pcb_component.rotate_clockwise() is rotation+90, while the transform is R(-angle) in a y-down frame, so a WEST pad lands SOUTH at 90 — which reads counter-clockwise on screen; the manifest description now states the concrete rule but still says CLOCKWISE. Decide the word, rename or re-document consistently (hint pcb rotate degrees:90 is COUNTER-clockwise already records the surprise). (5) _draw_fallback_pins still draws pin 1 at -comp.rotation — a second literal of the convention; route through get_pad_world_transform.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:45","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:45","priority":3,"tags":["ergonomics","pcb","pin-select","rotation","route-hints"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fda3df7b87ae88c0203ac81476","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: resolved-geometry follow-ups — canonical_wire_board pad-drop heuristic is invalidated (library-absent parts now compile from YAML but the wire projection strips their pads), resolve writes pads for footprints with BLOCKING parser markers, resolve attaches only F.SilkS/F.CrtYd graphics, inline_footprint coerces bad layers / negative drill quietly, panel round-trips pad geometry as float32","description":"Census from task-cycle 6 P9 (work item 01a0418de7): (a) panel_tools.gd canonical_wire_board: `worker_can_rederive_pads = footprint_resolved || footprint.contains(\":\")` — on a machine whose library lacks the part, assembly_check/board_health now receive a component with no pads and a ref they cannot resolve, so the verdict goes indeterminate for a board that carries its own geometry; keeping pads unconditionally re-blows the 64 KiB cap the drop exists for — needs a rule (e.g. keep pads only for components whose ref is not in the lock). (b) resolve.py _pads_from_parsed drops custom_primitives / chamfer / pad_drill_offset copper without recording it, so a board saved after such a resolve compiles on the inline path where the library path failed closed — the producer should refuse or record the markers (measured: feature_omitted 45→24 on smart-remote-v2). (c) resolve attaches only F.SilkS/F.CrtYd (_LEGACY_GRAPHIC_LAYERS), so a self-carrying board has no F.Fab graphics. (d) inline_footprint.py ~207-232 coerces unknown layers and negative drills instead of refusing by name. (e) pcb_component stores position/size as Vector2 (float32): U1S pad y 39.937001→39.937, drill 1.02→1.01999998 — sub-micron, but a panel round trip is not bit-exact. (f) durable hint \"footprint is the geometry authority, not board pins\" needs the full-vs-partial qualification (a `pads` key = board is authority).","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:31:53","updated_at":"2026-08-27T18:57:45","priority":3,"tags":["footprint","payload-limit","pcb","resolve","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fdc4c878289fd750ef855274be","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: connectivity follow-ups — wrong_net_pad (check A) still measures centreline vs pad CENTRE so an NPTH land trips a short; unknown-land fallback differs per side (worker: design clearance, panel: 0.3 mm); pour compile runs per run_drc / net_pin_group_count (methods.py calls it twice per route); three test_drc zone fixtures author `points` not `outline` so their pours never fill; run_drc can report complete:true beside a non-empty partial","description":"Census from task-cycle 6 P2/P5 and fix round 2. (1) drc.py check A (wrong_net_pad) is the last centre-only check: an unplated hole with no copper still produces {'type':'wrong_net_pad','pad':{'ref':'H1','pin':'1','net':None}} — move it onto the copper_contact predicate. (2) A pad with no stated size falls back to a coincidence disc on both sides but with different radii (worker: board clearance; panel FALLBACK_PAD_RADIUS_MM = 0.3) — not in spec/contact and not in its README's out-of-scope list; pick one. (3) Performance: a pour-bearing board compiles its fill once per run_drc and once per standalone connectivity_completeness; net_pin_group_count compiles per call and methods.py ~2093 calls it twice per route (~0.2 s each on smart-remote-v2); thread pours/pour_reason through methods._drc_for_routes and _attach_completeness. (4) tests/test_drc.py: three zone fixtures put the outline under `points`; the pour never fills and the tests now assert detail=invalid_zone_outline — fixing the key flips them to complete and duplicates test_zone_copper_drc; owner call whether to keep the \"unmeasurable pour\" case. (5) run_drc observed reporting complete:true with a non-empty partial on the pre-fix smart-remote board — not chased.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:02","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:02","priority":3,"tags":["connectivity","drc","pcb","performance","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fde8827d09a6d6a7e8c585a8ed","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: panel follow-ups — five *_check envelope normalizers drop error_code (payload_too_large is matched by prose), _refresh_zone_fill swallows failures silently, STALE overlay paths keep show_fab_preview raised, canvas draws every roundrect as a sharp rect, _center_view (Home) centres on world origin not the board, zoom_to_fit excludes copper outside the outline, tools.md documents no view verbs (set_view / view_state / get_image + 22 others)","description":"Census from task-cycle 6 P2c/P7/P9. (1) PCBPanel.gd's five *_check normalizations reach callers as {kind:\"worker_error\", message} — payload_too_large is recoverable only by matching prose (pcb_overlay_fetch._CAP_MARKERS; assembly_check already string-matches \"not running\"); one shared normalizer carrying `code` fixes all five. (2) _refresh_zone_fill: `if not reply.ok: return` — pours stay fill-less with zero words to the user; give it the overlay-lead treatment. (3) view_state.show_fab_preview stays true on the STALE paths (_invalidate_fab_preview, board moved mid-fetch) by design of record; decide whether \"true only when artwork exists\" should hold there too. (4) pcb_canvas _draw_roundrect_pad draws a sharp rectangle; the model now carries corner_rratio, so the drawn land can match the fabricated one. (5) _center_view (Home key) centres on world (0,0); on a board centred at (45,50) Home throws it off-screen. (6) zoom_to_fit content = outline ∪ components; copper outside the outline is not framed — decide. (7) pcb/docs/tools.md has no entry for set_view, view_state, get_image, place_via, update_via and ~20 other pre-batch tools. (8) pcb_data.to_dict() legacy panel-state path omits cutouts and board_graphics (no live caller); gerber._harvest loose-dict path has no board graphics (production uses _harvest_ir).","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:11","updated_at":"2026-08-27T18:24:25","priority":3,"tags":["docs","fab-preview","panel","pcb","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fe07037150946566a45f0c7d93","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: god-file census after task-cycle 6 — pcb_canvas 12.2k, panel_tools 11.4k, PCBPanel 7.4k, pcb_data 4.1k, pcb_routing_workspace 4.1k, compile_board.py 3.5k, drc.py 1.2k; widen the split item 01a03c07a23a to cover pcb_data and pcb_routing_workspace","description":"Task-cycle 6 put all new logic in new files (pcb_copper_contact, pcb_zone_copper, pcb_region_describe, pcb_pad_row, pcb_entity_id, pcb_copper_ownership, pcb_via_dimensions, pcb_trace_width, pcb_hint_waypoint, pcb_view_fit, pcb_overlay_fetch, pcb_load_checks, pcb_net_membership, pcb_undo_step, pcb_board_graphic, pcb_board_font, pcb_pin_select_tool, pcb_pin_selection_section; worker copper_contact, zone_copper, inline_footprint, board_font, board_graphics) and shrank pcb_ratsnest 1260→814, but the wiring still grew the old god files: pcb_canvas.gd 11788→12151, panel_tools.gd 10841→11425, PCBPanel.gd 7148→7434, pcb_routing_workspace.gd 3890→4143, pcb_data.gd 4046→4142, compile_board.py 3426→3482, drc.py 1024→1155. Split item 01a03c07a23a names only the first three; widen it. Also: pcb_ratsnest._canon() has no caller since via_span moved (leave until the split).","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:19","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:19","priority":3,"tags":["god-file","pcb","refactor"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a042fe7fe672bd84163028f52f7aa2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb GD suites: scoped run recipe (the runner has no per-suite filter)","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:50","updated_at":"2026-08-27T11:32:50","tags":["godot","pcb","runner","tests"],"value":"pcb/scripts/run-gd-tests.sh runs the WHOLE EXPECTED_SUITES manifest; RUN_GD_TESTS_SUITE_DIR is only the self-test sandbox, not a filter. To run named suites: with Minerva NOT running, once `godot --headless --path ~/github/Minerva/src --import`, then per suite `godot --headless --path ~/github/Minerva/src --script res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/tests/gd/<suite>.gd` and read the \"=== Results: N passed, M failed ===\" line (may carry a \"(real_worker_used=true)\" suffix; exit code alone lies). Worker pytest: `cd pcb/worker && MINERVA_PCB_ROOT=$(pwd)/.. .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/<file>`. Go: `cd pcb && go test -count=1 ./internal/board/`. The runner now refuses real-worker suites when the installed runtime bundle is stale (pcb/scripts/probe-worker-methods.py); run that probe first to know.","component":"pcb","key":"gd-scoped-run","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0430ff5f17ca0b1e6b4c9d49a033c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: Codex notes from the task-cycle 6 final check — PTH contact layers listed top/bottom only; via loops in dangling/pin-groups still centre-based; unknown-shape stadium not contained by a trapezoid; raw-dict KiCad emitter drops text; probe picks a site-packages by lexicographic sort; partly-lost committed candidate stays committed; compose cannot guarantee end_batch on a GDScript runtime error; trace-to-trace swept contact; logical-centre pseudo-land; multi-layer SMD pad row layer; overlapping overlay fetches; Pin Info readout stale after move/swap; rotation:0 dropped as present-only","description":"Each was judged a note (not must_fix) at the batch's Codex pass; recorded here so the next PCB planning session can pick them by value. 1. drc._harvest_pads builds a plated through-hole's contact node from its listed layers (often F.Cu/B.Cu only) — an inner-layer trace ending on the barrel reads dangling; a plated barrel spans the stack. 2. drc._check_dangling / _net_pin_groups via loops compare via CENTRES against clr and model pour↔via contact with a zero-diameter via — a trace overlapping a via's annulus off-centre reads disconnected (same family as check A's centre-based wrong_net_pad, 01a042fdc4c8). 3. copper_contact._sized_shape: the inscribed stadium is not contained by every unknown land shape (a tapered trapezoid excludes part of it) — spec/contact/README admits the token is unmodelled; a coincidence-point fallback for unknown tokens would be strictly conservative. 4. kicad._board_graphic_sexprs on the raw-dict path returns [] for kind:text (only the IR path expands text) — production is the IR path; refuse loudly on the raw path. 5. scripts/probe-worker-methods.py picks candidates[-1] of lib/python3.*/site-packages by sort — a bundle with 3.9 and 3.12 could inspect the wrong one. 6. pcb_routing_workspace.drop_unowned_commit_records: a committed candidate owning two traces with one MISSING drops the id but stays committed with its task closed (partial-loss policy belongs with 01a02c47f501). 7. PcbUndoStep.compose: GDScript has no try/finally, so a runtime error inside body leaks the open batch. 8. pcb_data.trace_end_is_joined trace-to-trace credit measures only the other trace's width (swept overlap of two 1.0 mm runs 0.8 mm apart reads free) — trace↔trace is out of contact-spec scope today. 9. pcb_copper_contact.physical_pad_node credits a logical pin centre as a land when it differs from the fabricated pad centre. 10. pcb_pad_row.layer_for_pin reports only the mounting layer for an SMD/custom pad on both faces or an inner layer. 11. PCBPanel._refresh_fab_preview/_refresh_mask_view: two overlapping fetches — an older failure can retract a newer success (request-generation token). 12. After move_net/swap_nets from the sidebar the adjacent Pin Info readout keeps the old net until the next click. 13. pcb_component PAD_OPTIONAL_KEYS drops an explicit rotation: 0 on round trip though board-yaml.md says rotation is present-only.","created_at":"2026-08-27T11:51:54","updated_at":"2026-08-27T12:59:52","priority":4,"tags":["codex-review","connectivity","panel","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0447a288c71b9a2e7b9e84f568f6b","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"PCB panel: Fab preview overlay is unreadable — the emitted-artwork overlay darkens the whole canvas so the board underneath falls below human-readable contrast, and its header text (\"10 layer(s) from the emitted artifacts…\") covers the top of the board","description":"Owner, live HITL 2026-08-27 on the coupon (jlc-coupon-1) after View > Fab preview was raised (by MCP view_state {show_fab_preview:true}): \"I can't see crap. There's an overlay saying '10 layers from emitted artifact…'. That overlay is so dark that the underlying coupon has a terrible contrast ratio, below human reading capability. I can see the coupon is present and rendered, but am unable to read/use/understand it.\" The agent's own capture (get_image, 1000x700) agrees: dark grey artwork on a near-black ground, header lines occupying the top ~50 px, mirrored B.SilkS text barely distinguishable from the pour. The overlay exists to show the fab what will be made; a human cannot check it in this state, which makes the WYSIWYG gate fail-open for the GUI-only owner. Do: render the emitted layers with the same palette/contrast as the live board (copper vs mask vs silk distinguishable, light strokes on the dark theme), keep the status header out of the board rect (status bar or a collapsible banner), and give the human a layer picker for the preview (one Gerber layer at a time, like the trace layer filter) instead of compositing all ten. Oracle: with Fab preview on, the same silk text and pad outlines that are legible on the live canvas remain legible in the preview at 554 px panel width; the header never overlaps the board outline; a screenshot passes a contrast check (WCAG-ish ≥ 4.5:1 for silk strokes against their ground).","created_at":"2026-08-27T18:27:31","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:42","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["contrast","fab-preview","gui-parity","hitl","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"Root cause: gerbonara black-on-transparent SVG at 96 dpi, not a dark overlay. Live palette recolour, view-scale raster (capped by dimension+area), banner outside the board rect, per-layer picker via view_state.fab_preview_layer. .gbrjob banner follow-up 01a045c6a615."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0448190a67494b46b5e66bee4ed94","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: HITL bench coupon — a linear, table-shaped board with one scenario per row (≤2 tests per row) for validating the task-cycle 6 fixes by hand and by MCP","description":"Owner ask 2026-08-27: the JLC coupon is a fabrication test and too busy for behaviour HITL. Build a bench board laid out as a table: fixed row pitch, one scenario per row, a silk label on the left of each row (\"R3 pad→pour\"), no nets shared between rows, real outline + design_rules so DRC/fab preview still run, never ordered. Modes: P = pre-built, the human inspects what tools report; I = the human performs the gesture; blend = one cell each. Expected answers per row live in ONE place (YAML comment + docket hint pcb/hitl-bench) so GUI and MCP checks read the same oracle. Master file: pcb/worker/tests/testdata/hitl_bench.yaml (a shared fixture the suites may adopt later); HITL loads a COPY from ~/pcb-hitl so sidecars never land in the repo.\n\nROWS (cell A | cell B | mode):\nR1 trace end on pad (01a040f731): 1.0 mm run landing 0.35 mm off a 1.3x4.5 pad centre → joined | same run 1 mm short → dangling | P\nR2 through-pad + EP (01a040f731 #97/#99): run passing THROUGH a small same-net pad → joined | 0.25 mm stub ending on an exposed pad → joined | P\nR3 pad→pour same net (01a0411a93, 01a04085bd): draw from the pad, single click inside the pour ends the run, no free end | DRC/ratsnest: pad joined via the fill, no airwire | I\nR4 pour foreign net (01a0411a93): draw from a NET_X pad, click inside the GND pour = waypoint, end stays free | second pour left UNFILLED → click is a waypoint | I\nR5 NPTH + dead via (01a02ce73867, 01a03f9dd6): net bridged top↔bottom only by a mounting hole → join still owed (3 islands) | via fed on top only → layers_touched [top] | P\nR6 swap / move net (01a040c235, 01a0410c62): P key, click pad, shift-click second, Swap nets → one Undo restores both | Move net to… from a third pad; export_yaml agrees with the panel | I\nR7 Pin Select on a socket (01a0410c62 #92): 2xN header with roles on 3 pins; free_pins side:west exclude_roles:[strapping] lists the right ones | minerva_pcb_select lights named pads; get_selection returns them | blend\nR8 rotation convention (#99.2): 2-pad part at rot 90 → pin 1 SOUTH | same at rot 270 → pin 1 NORTH; rotate reply carries pad rows | P\nR9 rotated land vs hit-test (01a0380585b4): land with own rotation 90 in an unrotated part — drawn copper == clickable copper | roundrect land: click near the corner, pin_info and canvas agree | I\nR10 silk text (01a0418dc6): add_silk_text F.SilkS reads normally | same on B.SilkS reads mirrored in fab preview; Delete + one Undo | blend\nR11 board graphic (01a0418dc6): add_graphic rect + circle on F.CrtYd, selectable, deletable | get_selection reports it after a click | I\nR12 inline geometry (01a0418de7): part with a pads: list and a footprint ref NOT in the library → compiles, DRC determinate | LOGO with pads: [] → zero pads, graphics only | P\nR13 indeterminate visible (01a007f21ce3): zone whose fill cannot compute (thermal relief) → status lead names it | after fixing the outline the lead clears | I\nR14 ids survive reload (01a040f6d7): note a via id, export_yaml → load_board {path} → delete_traces with the old id succeeds | hand-corrupted sidecar claim → warning on restore, copper untouched | blend — NOTE: needs the routing sidecar; runs on this bench\nR15 layered route hint (01a04106bd): hint with one waypoint on bottom → exactly one via at the hop, sized from design_rules | propose_via for_hint lists under the hint; bare propose_via shows unowned | blend\nR16 width provenance (01a02c480d50): route on a net with established 0.4 mm copper → candidate 0.4, source net_copper | (separate tiny board with no design_rules width and no copper → refused by name, no copper) | P\nR17 view fit (01a040f7523e): narrow docked panel, fit:true → whole board visible | Home key centres on the board — EXPECTED FAIL until 01a042fde882 (5) | I\nR18 fab preview (01a0414891, 01a0447a288c): View > Fab preview renders on a >64 KiB board (load smart-remote-v2 in a second tab) | contrast readable — EXPECTED FAIL until 01a0447a288c | I\nR19 status timer (01a03820df78): two refused clicks <2 s apart → second message stays its full 2 s | a held lead survives a transient message | I\nR20 annotation dock (01a0410a18, 01a0411e099a): 3 long annotations at ~554 px: opens ≈1/3, cannot drag past 50%, re-clamps on resize | every row's controls reachable; nothing scrolls at 1200 px; chevron closes | I\n\nOracle for the item: the bench YAML validates (both validators), compiles, and every P-row expected answer is reproduced through the worker's own entry points before the file is committed; each row has its silk label; a docket hint pcb/hitl-bench carries the per-row expectations; the owner walks rows 1–20 and records pass/fail per cell on this item.","created_at":"2026-08-27T18:35:36","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:52:28","priority":2,"tags":["bench","fixture","hitl","pcb","task-cycle-6"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044947d3b7011944adbed88c60cc3","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"HITL bench board — per-row expected answers (hitl_bench.yaml)","created_at":"2026-08-27T18:56:17","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:24:29","tags":["bench","fixture","hitl","oracle","pcb"],"value":"MASTER: pcb/worker/tests/testdata/hitl_bench.yaml (repo). ROW TABLE IN-REPO: pcb/docs/hitl_bench.md. HITL COPY: ~/pcb-hitl/hitl_bench.yaml — load the copy so sidecars never land in the repo.\n\nBOARD: v1, 60 x 286 mm, layers [top, bottom]. design_rules clearance 0.2 / trace 0.25 / via 0.8 / drill 0.4. Row n centre y = 8 + 12*(n-1) (R1 y=8 ... R23 y=272). Cell A ~x=22, cell B ~x=46. Left 10 mm of each row = a board_graphics F.SilkS text \"R<n> <name>\" at x=0.9, cap height 0.7. No net is shared between rows; every net is R<n>_<letter>. Bench-only footprint refs are named Bench_* — NEVER colon-shaped (a colon ref strips inline pads on the wire path).\n\nROW NUMBERS ARE STABLE, ROW SUBJECTS ARE NOT. A row keeps its slot; an answered question is retired and the slot re-used. R4 was \"foreign pour\" and is now \"npth route\" (R3 answers the foreign-pour question by itself now that its pour is on B.Cu). The SILK LABEL is the authority on what a row is about — do not read an old results sheet as a row index.\n\nWHOLE-BOARD BASELINE — RE-MEASURED 2026-08-27 after this revision, R5_A CORRECTED 2026-08-28 (task-cycle 8: an unplated hole is a non-pad in the raw census, so it is no longer an island) (worker, MINERVA_PCB_ROOT=pcb, pcb/worker/.venv python, methods via pcb_worker.methods.handle_request; pyclipper is REQUIRED or every pour reads indeterminate; RESOLVE FIRST — plating is a footprint fact, a raw-YAML census still counts the bores):\n- validate = 0 errors, 1 warning: trace width 0.05 < design_rules.trace_width_mm (R9 B's probe, deliberate).\n- Entities: 48 components, 29 nets, 18 traces, 5 vias, 5 zones, 23 board_graphics.\n- compile (V1_FAB_OUTPUTS) = ResolutionSuccess. Diagnostics: zone_filled x4, footprint_from_board x8, ordinal_ids x1, feature_omitted x9, captured_geometry_not_emitted x40. NO zone_fill_empty any more (R4's empty pour went with R4).\n- drc (connectivity): dangling_endpoint = 5 — R1_B (40.475, 4.75); R5_A (22.9, 56.0) and (27.1, 56.0); R22_A (22.0, 260.0); R23_B (46.0, 274.0). wrong_net_pad 0, crossing 0, layer_change_no_via 0. missing_copper [\"R15_A\", \"R4_A\"]. partial: R1_B 2, R3_G 2, R5_A 2, R16_A 3, R21_B 2, R23_B 2. indeterminate: none. (test_hitl_bench_baseline.py pins these.)\n- drc_geometric: exactly ONE finding, gc1_trace_width on R9 B's 0.05 mm probe.\n- zone_fill: Z3 bottom 1 region 104.0000 mm^2; Z13 top 1 region 112.0000; Z21A bottom 1 region 70.0000; Z21B bottom 1 region 70.0000.\n- board_health / check_bom / gerbers / fab_preview / generate / resolve_best_effort = ok. board_health canonical == enriched, and the render-tail poison test is unchanged (the corpus invariants in test_board_health_resolve_first.py hold).\n- STRICT resolve FAILS by contract: \"footprint ref 'Bench_RotLand_1P' is not in the seed library lockfile\". Inline pads do not exempt a component from the strict resolve surface. Use drc / board_health, never minerva_pcb_resolve, to prove this board healthy. The board LOAD path is tolerant, so the panel opens it normally.\n- Router projection (route_bridge.resolved_board_to_router) keeps all 29 nets and emits THREE exclusion warnings: R4_N/MH4.1 excluded, \"R4_N has fewer than two routable pads\", R5_A/MH5.1 excluded. test_route_npth_on_net.py pins len(board.nets) == 29 (was 25) and now also pins row 4's pair.\n\nPER ROW (A | B, mode):\nR1 trace end [P] — A: trace R1_A, 1.0 mm, ends (18.5, 5.0) = 0.354 mm off J1A.1's centre; JOINED, dangling 0, net complete. B: trace R1_B, 0.25 mm, ends (40.475, 4.75); cap edge x=40.6 vs pad edge x=41.6, gap exactly 1.0 mm; DANGLING, R1_B partial 2 groups.\nR2 through pad [P] — A: run (14,20)->(30,20) passes THROUGH TP2M's land; R2_A complete, 1 group, dangling 0. B: bottom run to a via-in-pad at (45.75, 20.25), top stub to (46.5, 19.75) inside U2B pad 17's 1.68x1.68 exposed land; complete, dangling 0.\nR3 pad pour [I] — THE POUR IS NOW ON B.Cu (the real GND-plane case), which folds the retired R4's lesson in: the terminator must be LAYER-aware as well as net-aware. Start: R3_G partial 2 groups (TP3A outside, TP3B + fill inside), no dangling, Z3 fills 1 region 104.0000 mm^2 on bottom. GESTURE: with the BOTTOM layer active, draw from TP3A and single-click inside the pour -> the run ends there, R3_G leaves `partial`, no new dangling. VERIFIED by simulation: a bottom run (16,32)->(24,32) gives R3_G 1 group, 0 dangling. FALSIFIER (verified): the same run on TOP gives 2 groups and dangling at (24.0, 32.0) — no copper up there, so the click is a waypoint.\nR4 npth route [I] — SLOT RE-USED for the router's NPTH exclusion (fix 2986f57). MH4 (MountingHole_3.2mm_M3 at (47,44)) is the SOLE member of net R4_N. TP4A (15,44) / TP4B (33,44) carry R4_A with zero copper (it is the second `missing_copper` entry). GESTURE: propose a span TP4A.1 -> TP4B.1 on R4_A. VERIFIED: ok true, ONE candidate on R4_A, trace_width 0.25 source `board_rules`; among the reply's warnings, one names R4_N and MH4.1 as excluded and one says R4_N has fewer than two routable pads. This call used to be refused with the WHOLE board unroutable, which is what blocked R15 and R16.\nR5 npth via [P] — A: R5_A partial, 2 pin groups {TP5T + top trace}, {TP5B + bottom trace}; MH5.1 is np_thru_hole and is NOT a pad on either census (task-cycle 8), so it is no island of its own — the top<->bottom join is still OWED; two deliberate dangling ends at (22.9,56) and (27.1,56). B: via V5 at (46,56) fed on top only — list_vias reports layers_touched [\"top\"]; dangling 0.\nR6 swap move [I] — three one-pin nets R6_A/B/C on TP6A/B/C, no copper. Swap R6_A<->R6_B: ONE Undo restores both. Move net from TP6C: export_yaml agrees with the panel. (Owner: net assignment is LLM/YAML only, so this is a VERB check, not a human-UX gate.)\nR7 pin select [blend] — J7 is DIP-6_W7.62mm_Socket (2x3, not 2x6): WEST x=18.0 pins 1,2,3 at y 77.5/80.04/82.58; EAST x=25.62 pins 6,5,4. roles: pin1 [strapping], pin2 [uart_console], pin3 [adc]; nets on the EAST column. free_pins side \"west\" = 1,2,3; + exclude_roles [\"strapping\"] = 2,3. select/get_selection return those pad rows with their roles; pin 6 answers roles [].\nR8 rotation deg [P] — R8A (R_0805, rot 90): pin1 (22.0, 92.95) SOUTH, pin2 (22.0, 91.05). R8B (rot 270): pin1 (46.0, 91.05) NORTH. Rotation is CLOCKWISE in the y-down frame.\nR9 rotated land [blend] — A (I, the ONE gesture left in this row): U9A (Bench_RotLand_1P) has ONE 2.0x0.6 land with its own rotation 90 in a part at rotation 0, so the copper stands tall x 21.7..22.3, y 103.0..105.0. Trace ends (22.0, 104.8) = 0.2 mm inside; dangling 0. FALSIFIER (verified): drop the pad's `rotation: 90` and that end dangles. B (P, INSPECT ONLY — pre-routed, the human does not draw): U9B (Bench_CornerLand_1P) 2.0x2.0 roundrect corner_rratio 0.25; the 0.05 mm trace ends (46.8, 104.8), 0.0757 mm INSIDE copper; dangling 0. The 0.076 mm margin is not a gesture a hand can aim — that is WHY B is inspect-only now. FALSIFIER for the agent walk: move the end to (46.9, 104.9) in the YAML -> dangling, R9_B partial 2. Source vectors: spec/contact/080 and 070.\nR10 silk text [blend] / R11 board art [I] — nothing pre-placed. F.SilkS near (20,116) reads normally, B.SilkS near (46,116) reads MIRRORED in fab preview, delete + one Undo. add_graphic rect + circle on F.CrtYd near (22,128); get_selection names the graphic id. Courtyard emits NO Gerber — expected.\nR12 inline pads [P] — A: U12A's ref Bench_Nowhere_1206 is in NO library yet compiles: two 1.2x1.8 lands, own silk, INFO footprint_from_board, DRC determinate. B: LOGO_R12 names a ref that DOES resolve and overrides it with `pads: []` — zero copper primitives, graphics only, no BOM/CPL line.\nR13 no fill [I] — SHIPPED FILLABLE ON PURPOSE (one refused pour takes every pour on the board down, R3's and R21's included). Start: Z13 fills 1 region 112.0000 mm^2. GESTURE: add BOTH thermal_gap_mm: 0.3 AND thermal_bridge_width_mm: 0.3 to Z13. After: the refusal names the zone by id (\"v1 fill implements SOLID connect only ... refused rather than mis-filled\") and drc turns R3_G indeterminate, reason \"zone_copper\", detail quoting the refusal. Only ONE of the two fields refuses earlier and differently. Remove both to clear.\nR14 id reload [blend] — the ONLY authored ids: trace:1400...0001 (top (16,164)->(22,164)), via:1400...0002 ((22,164) top<->bottom), trace:1400...0003 (bottom (22,164)->(28,164)). All three survive MigrateV1toV2 unchanged. R14_A complete before a delete, partial after. Do NOT quote an id out of a Python compile diagnostic — the compile re-derives ordinal ids on a v1 board (`ordinal_ids` INFO).\nR15 route hint [blend] — R15_A = TP15A (15,176) to TP15B (57,176), ZERO copper. Keepout K15 (x 34..38, y 171..181) blocks the top, so a bottom-waypoint hint is the only way through. Expect EXACTLY ONE via at the hop, 0.8 / 0.4 from design_rules; propose_via for_hint lists it under the hint, a bare propose_via shows unowned. (Unblocked by the R4 fix.)\nR16 width source [P] — R16_A = TP16A/B/C/D; a 0.4 mm trace joins A-B. Census: partial, 3 groups, dangling 0. Route C to D: candidate width 0.40, source `net_copper` — NOT `design_rules` (0.25). (Unblocked by the R4 fix.)\nR17 view fit / R18 fab preview / R19 status timer / R20 annotation dock [I] — no geometry, the silk label IS the row. R17: fit:true must show the whole 60x286; Home centres. R18: well over the 64 KiB IPC cap, so fab preview must ride snapshot-by-reference; contrast readable; since task-cycle 8 the preview pans/zooms with the board camera, drills and Edge.Cuts are visible, and every emitted layer is selectable in the picker (no INCOMPLETE banner on a complete emission). R19: two refused clicks <2 s apart, the second keeps its full 2 s; a held lead survives a transient. R20: three long annotations at ~554 px — opens ~1/3, cannot drag past 50%, re-clamps on resize; all controls reachable, no horizontal scroll at 1200 px, chevron closes.\nR21 via credit [P] — NEW, for fix 8f7d4cf. Both cells are the same experiment twice; only the via moves. Lands are inline SMD (F.Cu only) on Bench_Probe_2P: pads 1.25x1.75 at local (-1.4,0)/(1.4,0). A: U21A at (22,248); 0.5 mm top run (20.6,248)->(23.4,248) pad centre to pad centre; via at (22.0, 248.0) = the run's exact MIDPOINT, inside the R21_A pour on the BACK (Z21A, x 19..29, y 244.5..251.5); TP21C (TH land, (27,248)) stands in that pour. VERIFIED: R21_A pin_groups = 1, COMPLETE, dangling 0. FALSIFIER (verified): delete that one via -> 2 groups. B: U21B at (46,248), identical run, via at (46.0, 249.0) — 1.0 mm off the centreline. Copper reaches 0.25 (half run) + 0.40 (annulus) = 0.65 mm, so 0.35 mm of laminate is left. VERIFIED: R21_B partial, 2 groups, dangling 0 (both ends are on their own lands, so this is an OPEN, not a loose end). FALSIFIER (verified): move it onto the line -> 1 group.\nR22 add by ref [I] — NEW, for fix a59f723. Shipped: TP22A (16,260) and a 6 mm top stub ending in mid-air at (22.0, 260.0) — DANGLING on purpose, and one of the board's five. A: add `Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x02_P2.54mm_Vertical` at (22.0, 260.0) BY REF (MCP only since task-cycle 8 — the sidebar Add Part section is gone) and connect pin 1 to R22_A. That footprint's pad 1 is a 1.8x1.8 thru-hole land at its own local origin (pad 2 is 2.54 mm south), so it lands dead on the stub's end. Expect the add reply's geometry block = fabricable true / source \"library\" / pad_count 2; R22_A complete, 1 group; the (22.0, 260.0) dangling finding GONE; no new geometric finding. B: add a generic `HEADER` at (46.0, 260.0) — no colon, no library. Expect fabricable FALSE / source \"sketch\" plus the note naming the way out, the canvas unresolved badge, a status lead naming the part, and EVERY pour stops filling with the refusal naming the component by ref (compile_board emits footprint_unresolved \"component 'X': ...\"; zone_copper._first_error leads with it and appends \"(also refused: ...)\" for further offenders). THIS IS A GESTURE, NOT A SHIPPED STATE — same hazard as R13; while it stands, R3's and R21's answers go dark. Undo/delete to clear.\nR23 part on back [P] — NEW, for fix 5b9156b. Bench_BottomLand_2P: pads 1.6x1.0 at local (0,-2.0) and (0,2.0), layers F.Cu/F.Mask/F.Paste, silk on F.SilkS. A `layer: bottom` placement negates LOCAL Y and flips the layers, so pad 1 (authored NORTH) lands SOUTH on B.Cu. Since task-cycle 8 the PANEL draws and hit-tests the part mirrored too (one transform: get_transform + placed_pad_layers). A: BS23A at (22,272) -> pad 1 on B.Cu at (22.0, 274.0); the 0.25 mm BOTTOM run from TP23A (16,274) ends on its centre. VERIFIED: R23_A 1 group, complete, dangling 0. FALSIFIER (verified): set BS23A `layer: top` — the land moves to (22.0, 270.0) and the run's end reports dangling at (22.0, 274.0), R23_A 2 groups. B: BS23B at (46,272), same land on B.Cu at (46.0, 274.0), approached by a TOP run from TP23B (40,274). VERIFIED: dangling at (46.0, 274.0), R23_B partial 2 groups — copper on the other side of the laminate joins nothing without a via.\n\nRETIRED: the old R4 (foreign-net pour + a keepout-emptied pour with `fill: []`). R3's B.Cu pour answers the net-and-layer question on its own, so the slot went to the NPTH router row and the zone_fill_empty diagnostic left the baseline with it.\n\nSUPERSEDED GOTCHA (kept so nobody re-derives it): a `layer: bottom` component whose footprint pads state F.Cu USED to be flipped to B.Cu by the compiled IR and left on F.Cu by the loose-dict connectivity harvest, so the two DRC surfaces disagreed. Fix 5b9156b routes drc._harvest_pads through geometry.component_transform and the two now agree — R23 is the row that pins it. R5's TP5B stays a through-hole land anyway, so that row's answer never turned on it.","component":"pcb","key":"hitl-bench","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":5,"research_cost":47}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044a04f627ade8ac3b6bc8a7963fd","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"PCB panel: pour fills are never adopted live — after load or any edit, fill_region_count stays 0, so the Trace tool cannot end on a pour and the ratsnest keeps the airwire (HITL bench R3 fails)","description":"Owner, HITL bench R3, 2026-08-27: \"started a trace from TP3A, connected to the pour. 1. the click in the pour went to waypoint routing, so the pour didn't count as a connection; 2. even after double click, the ratsnest airwire continues to believe disconnected.\" Tried both copper layers. Measured by the agent: describe_region reports fill_region_count 0 for the R3 pour after load and 3 s after an MCP add_trace (which journals and should trigger _restate_zone_fill → _refresh_zone_fill); both trace ends inside the pour read free. The same was true on the coupon earlier (fill_region_count 0). The worker is NOT the problem: pcb.zone_fill through the live binary on the panel's own by-ref snapshot (user://pcb_snapshots/board-…-1.snap, wire form with the panel's zone ids) returns ok with fills whose ids match the panel's zones (R3 1 region). So the drop is one of _refresh_zone_fill's silent returns (token mismatch between the two to_board_dict() calls, reply.ok false through the IPC envelope, or adopt matching 0). Related: 01a042fde882 (2) \"_refresh_zone_fill swallows failures silently\" — this is the cost of that silence. Oracle: load the bench, wait, describe_region R3 → fill_region_count 1; draw from TP3A into the pour → the click terminates and R3_G leaves partial.","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:09:11","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:21:46","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["connectivity","hitl","panel","pcb","zone-fill"],"resolution":"pcb.zone_fill was absent from ui.panels[0].ipc_channels and ui.ipc_messages; the broker answered permission_denied and _refresh_zone_fill swallowed it. Fixed + pushed (plugins main 01024e8) with TestPanelIPCChannelsDeclaredInManifest (fails when either list drops a channel the panel sends on). Verified live on the bench: R3 pour fill_region_count 1; pad→pour trace completes R3_G."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044a664fc73f1b90ca2e74c2dc528","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: router refuses the WHOLE board when any net references a pad with no routable copper (an NPTH mounting hole on a net) and the panel reports it as route_worker_unavailable — proposing a span on an unrelated net fails","description":"HITL bench 2026-08-27, row R16: minerva_pcb_workspace_propose {spans:[TP16C.1→TP16D.1]} (net R16_A) fails with error route_worker_unavailable and detail {kind: unsupported_geometry, message: \"net 'R5_A' references pad placed-pad:… which carries no routable copper\"} — the pad is MH5, an np_thru_hole mounting hole deliberately on net R5_A (row R5's NPTH-bridge case). Two defects: (1) the router (route_bridge/board_to_router) treats one unroutable pad on ANY net as fatal for the board instead of excluding that net (or that pad) with a warning, so nothing can be routed while it exists; (2) the panel's error mapping labels a worker refusal as \"route_worker_unavailable — Router worker did not answer\", which sends the human to the wrong fix (the worker answered, with a refusal). Oracle: on the bench, propose R16 → candidate lands at 0.4 mm (net_copper) with a warning naming R5_A/MH5; a genuine worker outage still reads route_worker_unavailable.","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:15:50","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:34","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["error-mapping","hitl","npth","pcb","router"],"resolution":"route_bridge excludes the unroutable pad with a warning naming net+pad; panel reports worker refusals as route_worker_refused, route_worker_unavailable reserved for silence. Bench R4/R15/R16 unblocked."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044a67de87b56bdbf3a0dec44003f","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: pad-row `side` for a wide 2-column part ties corner pins to north/south, so free_pins side:west on a 2x3 DIP returns one pin of three — a two-column part should read its columns as west/east regardless of aspect","description":"HITL bench R7 (J7 = Package_DIP:DIP-6_W7.62mm_Socket, 7.62 wide x 5.08 tall): pcb_pad_row.side_for_pin resolves corner ties to the SHORTER axis, which for a tall socket is the column — the rule the owner's U1S case needs — but for a WIDE part the shorter axis is vertical, so pins 1 and 3 read north/south and only pin 2 reads west; free_pins {side:\"west\", exclude_roles:[strapping]} returns J7.2 alone where a human expects J7.2 and J7.3. Do: when a part's pads form exactly two columns (or two rows), name the sides by that structure (columns → west/east, rows → north/south) before falling back to the box heuristic; keep U1S's answer unchanged. Oracle: bench R7 side:west lists pins 1,2,3 (minus excluded roles); a 2x22 socket still lists its west column.","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:15:57","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:15:57","priority":3,"tags":["ergonomics","hitl","pad-row","pcb","pin-select"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044a6d96478cf839408ce2f47dfe8","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb panel: the GD contact predicate ignores an inline pad's own rotation and corner_rratio — R9 lands read FREE in pin_info/describe_region/trace_end_is_joined while the worker DRC reads them joined (panel/worker disagree on inline-pad geometry)","description":"HITL bench 2026-08-27 rows R9 A/B. Live board: U9A carries one inline pad 2.0x0.6 rotation 90 (copper x 21.7..22.3, y 103.0..105.0); trace R9_A ends at (22.0,104.8). U9B carries a 2.0x2.0 roundrect corner_rratio 0.25; a 0.05 mm trace ends at (46.8,104.8), 0.076 mm inside the true copper near the corner. WORKER (minerva_pcb_board_drc, connectivity): no dangling_endpoint on R9_A or R9_B, nets complete. PANEL: describe_region reports free_ends at (22.0,104.8) and (46.8,104.8); pin_info U9B.1 trace_count 0. The live export (export_yaml) shows the panel model DOES hold rotation: 90 and corner_rratio: 0.25 on those pads, so the decode is fine — the GD contact path (pcb_copper_contact.pad_nodes / physical_pad_node / pcb_component.pin_copper_distance for INLINE pads) is not applying the land's own rotation and/or the roundrect radius the way the worker's copper_contact does. The shared spec/contact vectors did not catch it because both runners build pads from a `pad` dict directly, not from a component's inline `pads` list as loaded by the panel. Oracle: on the bench, describe_region R9 reports no free ends for both traces and pin_info U9A.1/U9B.1 each list one touching trace; add a contact vector (or a GD check in test_copper_contact_vectors.gd) that builds the node from a loaded PCBComponent with an inline rotated/roundrect pad.","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:16:20","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:49:44","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["connectivity","hitl","inline-pads","parity","pcb"],"resolution":"Cause: pcb_component.load_from_board_dict read has_pad_geometry as a KEY (written only by resolve's library-success path), and pcb_copper_contact.pad_nodes gated on it, so inline-pad parts (Bench_* refs, any library-absent part with its own pads) had no lands and contact fell back to the 0.2 mm coincidence disc. The shape math (rotation, corner_rratio) was already right. Fix: _pads_from_list derives the fact from the lands (worker's has_resolved_pads rule); pad_nodes asks the list; load_pad_geometry delegates to the same deserializer (it had dropped PAD_OPTIONAL_KEYS — imported roundrects lost corner_rratio). Verified live on the bench after tab reopen: R9 A/B free_ends [], pin_info trace_count 1 each. Pushed on plugins main. GD check authored, to be run with Minerva down."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044abf2b170688147b6c041a7f735","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A new panel IPC channel must be declared in BOTH manifest lists, and the plugin must be REINSTALLED for the broker to see it — a missing entry fails silently as permission_denied","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:21:54","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:21:54","tags":["broker","gotcha","ipc","manifest","pcb"],"value":"Every \"pcb.<name>\" channel the panel sends (via _request_with_backend_ensure / request.emit / worker_check) must appear in pcb/manifest.json under ui.panels[0].ipc_channels (what PluginScenePanelBroker registers at panel open — the log line \"Registered panel 'pcb_panel' -> N channel(s)\") AND ui.ipc_messages (the allowlist ipc_channels must intersect). Missing → the broker returns PluginErrors.permission_denied, the panel's *_check normalizers turn it into {kind:\"worker_error\", message:\"Permission denied\"}, and callers like _refresh_zone_fill return silently — the feature just never happens (pours never filled from 2026-08-22 until 2026-08-27). Guard: pcb/manifest_panel_ipc_parity_test.go (go test ./ in pcb/). The channel list is parsed at INSTALL into the plugin record: minerva_plugin_reload does NOT re-read it; do minerva_plugin_remove {delete_data:false} + minerva_plugin_install, then reopen the tab, and check the log's channel count went up. Diagnose a suspect channel by replaying the panel's own by-ref snapshot (user://pcb_snapshots/*.snap + sha256) through the MCP twin of the channel: if MCP answers ok and the panel does not, it is the allowlist.","component":"pcb","key":"panel-ipc-channel-allowlist","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":2,"research_cost":25}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044c591867edd9d7b37a47d0e96e9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Panel: never gate on comp.has_pad_geometry as a dict key — resolve writes it only on a LIBRARY hit; the fact is \"pads list non-empty\" (worker's has_resolved_pads)","created_at":"2026-08-27T19:49:53","updated_at":"2026-08-27T19:49:53","tags":["connectivity","gotcha","inline-pads","panel","pcb"],"value":"resolve._resolve_component sets comp[\"has_pad_geometry\"] only when the footprint resolved from the library. A component carrying its own inline `pads` (library-absent ref, Bench_* fixtures, any P9-style self-carrying part) never gets the key, while the worker's real predicate pad_source.has_resolved_pads() is simply \"non-empty pads list\" and is what iter_pads / DRC / emitters use. Any GD code that reads the key (it did in pcb_component.load_from_board_dict and pcb_copper_contact.pad_nodes until 2026-08-27) silently drops those lands: contact falls back to the pin-centre coincidence disc, pin_info says no traces, describe_region says free, the canvas draws placeholder pads with an 'unresolved' badge. Rule now: PCBComponent._pads_from_list derives has_pad_geometry from the lands; ask the pads list, not the flag. Symptom signature: worker DRC says joined, panel says free, only on parts whose ref is not in the seed library.","component":"pcb","key":"has_pad_geometry-is-a-fact-not-a-key","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":3,"research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a044d1fe227390aae42ae4cefab071","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb panel: to_board_dict emits `pads: []` for every pins-only component, which the worker's full-vs-partial rule reads as \"zero pads\" — every panel-sent board with such a part is refused (\"pin '1' has no matching footprint pad\"), real-worker propose suites fell back to canned results","description":"Found by the deferred full GD run before release 0.5.1 (2026-08-27). pcb/ui/model/pcb_component.gd to_board_dict writes d[\"pads\"] = _pads_to_list() unconditionally; since the inline-geometry rule (work item 01a0418de7: a `pads` KEY = the board is the sole pad authority, `[]` = zero pads) the worker compiles a pins-only component as having no lands and refuses: {\"kind\":\"compile\",\"message\":\"component 'U1' pin '1' has no matching footprint pad\"} (also 'EXIST_ANCHOR'). Fell out as real_worker_used=false on test_pcb_explicit_propose, test_pcb_hint_refine_loop, test_pcb_single_trace_tool, test_pcb_drc_propose. The scoped run at the batch's close did not include those four suites. Fix: emit `pads` only when the component carries lands, keep an explicit authored `pads: []` (graphics-only pseudo-component) round-tripping via a pads_authored marker; tests on both sides. Oracle: the four real-worker suites report real_worker_used=true and green; the bench's LOGO_R12 still exports `pads: []`.","created_at":"2026-08-27T20:03:27","updated_at":"2026-08-27T20:13:21","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["inline-pads","panel","pcb","regression","release-blocker"],"resolution":"to_board_dict/to_dict emit `pads` only when lands exist or the source carried an explicit list (pads_authored); load_from_board_dict branches on the key. Not new in the batch: the unconditional emit dates from the model's port; the P9 key-trigger rule made it fatal. Four real-worker suites real_worker_used=true; GD + pytest checks on both sides. Pushed on plugins main."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04511e2dc7e1380cf41d09f785409","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb worker connectivity: a via under a trace's INTERIOR is not credited as a join (via↔segment credit is endpoint-only), so a test-point pair strapped to the plane by a mid-trace via reads as its own island — false `partial` on smart-remote-v2 (TP1)","description":"Measured 2026-08-27 on the live smart-remote-v2 board (pcb 0.5.1 marketplace build). drc._net_pin_groups credits \"a via joins every same-net segment ENDPOINT within clr of it\" — TP1.1 (6.6,30) and TP1.2 (9.4,30) are joined by a 0.5 mm top trace and a GND via (0.8 mm) sits at (8.0,30.0), the exact midpoint of that trace, inside the bottom GND pour. The via's centre lies on the segment interior, 1.4 mm from either endpoint, so no via↔segment union happens; the via↔pour union does, but the pair never reaches the via. Census: GND partial 3 groups where reality is 2 (the other island, C12.2/C7.2, is a genuine open). Same family as check A and the dangling pass measuring via CENTRES (01a0430ff5f1 note 2): the fix is to run via↔segment through the copper_contact predicate (a barrel node vs the segment's swept stadium — a via anywhere along a trace's copper is a join) on both the worker and the panel ratsnest. Oracle: on smart-remote-v2 GND reports 2 groups; a bench row with a via under a trace midpoint reads complete; a via 0.5 mm beside a 0.25 mm trace still reads unjoined.","created_at":"2026-08-27T21:13:15","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:32","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["census","connectivity","hitl","pcb","via"],"resolution":"Via credit goes through the copper-contact predicate on both sides; measured on the owner's board outside the corpus: GND partial 3 → 2 (TP1 joins), mutation → 3."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0451c3e7c7433a49a36777f561dc5","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: annotation dock still offers an (R) \"Route Hint\" button — superseded by the panel's Intents controls","description":"The annotation dock pane's Annotate strip shows an \"(R)\" toggle on a pcb tab. It is AnnotationToolbar's auto-abbreviation for the `pcb_route_hint` kind (display_name \"Route Hint\", no toolbar_icon) — the toolbar adds one button for every registered kind whose `author_ui()` returns non-null (Minerva `AnnotationToolbar.gd` ~L425-431). `pcb_route_hint_kind.gd::author_ui()` returns `WaypointRouteHintAuthorTool`, the generic click-waypoints-then-double-click hint tool. Route authoring now lives in the panel's Intents group (PCBPanel `_add_group_label(\"Intents\")` ~L1851, tools built by `_new_route_flow_tool` ~L3993), so the dock button is a duplicate entry point with the older, less specific UX.\n\nFound while co-working on the Smart Remote v2 board (0.5.1 marketplace build), 2026-08-27.","created_at":"2026-08-27T21:24:34","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:43","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["FnF","annotation-dock","dead-code","hitl-2026-08-27"],"resolution":"author_ui() null, WaypointRouteHintAuthorTool deleted; Intents tools untouched.","repro_steps":"1. Open a pcb tab (any board), open the annotation dock pane.\n2. Look at the Annotate strip: an \"(R)\" toggle is present next to the 2d_* kinds; tooltip \"Route Hint\".\n3. Press it and click on the board: the old waypoint hint tool runs — separate from the Intents tools."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0451de71179a4b28c3f2c02a4ac2f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb skill: teach Minerva's built-in annotations (arrows, text, regions) — LLMs misplace arrows and misuse the kinds","description":"The pcb plugin installs one skill, `minerva_pcb_design` (manifest `skills`). It covers board verbs but says nothing about Minerva's core annotation tools (`minerva_annotations_add/update/list/query/render_overlay/resolve_ref`, kinds `2d_arrow`, `2d_text`, `2d_region`, `2d_polyline`). LLMs working a board with it repeatedly place arrows wrong (anchor vs. target confusion, wrong coordinate space, arrow pointing from instead of at the thing) and pick the wrong kind.\n\nOwner request during the 2026-08-27 co-working session: the skill(s) shipped by the pcb plugin should also explain how to use the built-in annotations correctly on a pcb tab.","created_at":"2026-08-27T21:26:22","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:48","priority":3,"tags":["annotations","hitl-2026-08-27","skill"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0452996e27e5f9fb36e4bed952000","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: add_component makes placeholder parts the worker refuses — one such part turns DRC and the pour fill indeterminate for the WHOLE board, with no panel warning and no add-by-library-ref path","description":"The worker is hermetic by design (DCR 019f761e): a pad is fabricated only from the board's own `pads` list (FULL) or a sha-pinned library footprint (PARTIAL); anything else fails closed as `footprint_unresolved`. That rule is correct and must stay.\n\nThe panel side has not caught up. `minerva_pcb_add_component` (and the GUI Add) still take the generic enums (HEADER, RESISTOR, CAPACITOR, …) and create a part with ESTIMATED geometry and no `pads` key and a footprint name that is not a library ref. The panel renders it, rotates it, lets nets be connected — and says nothing. The first worker call then refuses the entire board:\n- `footprint_unresolved`: component 'TP2': footprint ref 'HEADER' is not in any of the 3 library layers\n- `net_pin_unresolved` for each of its pins\n- geometric board_drc → `verdict: indeterminate`, `unresolved_geometry`\n- connectivity board_drc → GND `indeterminate` (zone_copper: \"the pour fill could not be computed\") — the pour that every other GND pad depends on.\n\nTwo defects, nuanced:\n1. Blast radius: fail-closed for the ONE part is right; taking the pour and both DRCs of the other 44 parts down with it, silently, is not. The panel shows no unresolved-geometry state before a check runs.\n2. No affordance to do it right: the library already holds the footprint (`Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x02_P2.54mm_Vertical`) but nothing on the panel or MCP adds a part BY LIBRARY REF. The only agent workaround is footprint_report → hand-built `import_footprint_geometry` JSON; a GUI-only human has no path at all (adding a part is effectively YAML-only).\n\nFound 2026-08-27 placing a 1x02 test-power tap (TP2) on Smart Remote v2 under the 0.5.1 marketplace build.","created_at":"2026-08-27T21:39:08","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:38","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["add_component","hermetic-cam","hitl-2026-08-27","library"],"resolution":"add_component accepts a library ref (GUI Add Part + MCP) via the pcb.footprint_geometry channel; sketch enums kept and named in every reply's geometry block; unresolvable geometry visible at add time; pour refusal names every offending part. Wire pad-trim for by-ref parts left as owner decision 01a0460126f2.","repro_steps":"1. Load any FULL-geometry board (Smart Remote v2, or hitl_bench.yaml), confirm board_drc geometric = clean and the GND pour reports zone_filled.\n2. minerva_pcb_add_component {footprint:\"HEADER\", pin_count:2, pad_type:\"tht\", x, y}; connect_net its pins to two existing nets.\n3. board_drc {geometric:true} → indeterminate / unresolved_geometry naming the new part; board_drc (connectivity) → GND indeterminate, zone_copper \"pour fill could not be computed\". Panel shows no warning on the part.\n4. Look for a way to add the same part as `Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x02_P2.54mm_Vertical` from the panel or MCP: none. footprint_report + import_footprint_geometry (hand-typed pad list) is the only route; it also leaves `component_graphics_absent` (no silk/courtyard)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0453b13d87d58b46df396194ffc7c","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"DCR: gerber-plot plugin — turn a Gerber/Excellon set into 1:1 paper drawings and laser process drawings (colour = meaning)","description":"A NEW standalone Minerva plugin (not part of `pcb`; working id `gerber_plot`). Owner intent, restated and confirmed 2026-08-27:\n\nTake a Gerber (RS-274X) + Excellon drill set — what `minerva_pcb_gerbers` emits, but any set — and render it as PHYSICAL-OUTPUT drawings, not a screen preview:\n\n1. PAPER (ordinary printer): 1:1-scale colour-coded drawing of one or more layers so a printed sheet can be laid over/next to a real or blank board to see where holes and traces are. Holes = black circles at true drill diameter; copper (traces, pads) in a per-layer colour; outline a thin line. Layers overlay on one sheet with distinct colours, or print as separate sheets. Output PDF/SVG at exact scale with a registration mark + ruler so scale is verifiable after printing.\n\n2. LASER (diode/CO2 for DIY board making — ablating paint/resist, marking, cutting): a drawing where COLOUR MEANS PROCESS in the convention laser software (LightBurn-style) already uses — e.g. orange fill = raster/ablate this area, black circles = drill positions (mark or pierce), a distinct stroke colour = cut the outline. Export SVG (DXF later) with those colour assignments, 1:1.\n\nINPUT — the set can arrive MANY ways, and either the user or the LLM names the source:\n- a folder on disk (e.g. the directory pcb_gerbers just wrote),\n- a .zip (fab-house style bundle),\n- Minerva NOTES (a note carrying the files as attachments / the emitted artifact),\n- individual files picked in the GUI.\nLayer identification by filename convention (KiCad/Protel extensions, JLC names) with a manual override table.\n\nSINGLE-SIDED ONLY, NO MIRRORING (owner ruling): every output is one copper side plotted exactly as the file's coordinates say. No flip flag, no bottom-side handling, no two-side registration in v1. A bottom-layer file still opens and plots as-is.\n\nNOT: a CAM editor, a Gerber CRUD viewer, or a consumer of the pcb plugin's data model. It is a converter with a preview; input is files/notes, output is files.\n\nMINERVA SHAPE (parity rule: every GUI action has an MCP verb):\n- Panel scene: source picker (folder / zip / note / files), layer list with toggles + detected type, colour/process mapping editor, preview canvas with scale check, export button.\n- MCP from day one: `minerva_gerber_plot_open` {folder|zip|note_id|files}, `list_layers`, `set_layer_type`, `set_mapping` / `get_mapping`, `render` (preview PNG via get_image-style capture), `export` {preset, path, format}.\n- Presets: \"paper\" and \"laser\" ship built in; user presets saved via the plugin-preferences mechanism.\n- Worker: Go or Python; parses apertures/flashes/draws/regions (incl. macros, polarity) and Excellon (tools, drill/route); emits VECTOR output only — lasers want vectors; no raster-only path.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS to settle in design, not now: (a) whether \"ablate\" mode computes the copper NEGATIVE itself (polygon boolean of outline minus copper — non-trivial) or leaves that to the laser software; (b) kerf / ablation-width compensation; (c) DXF export; (d) whether Excellon slots/routes render as holes or cut paths.\n\nAcceptance: open the Smart Remote v2 gerber folder three ways (folder, zip of it, note attachment), print the paper preset and verify the ruler measures true on paper; export the laser preset and load it in LightBurn with the expected colour→operation mapping.","created_at":"2026-08-27T21:58:14","updated_at":"2026-08-27T21:59:30","priority":3,"tags":["deferred","gerber","hitl-2026-08-27","new-plugin","plugin"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a045642b56788abd8354824e19f8d0","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF — task-cycle 7 (2026-08-27 co-working HITL): bottom-side mirror, via credit, NPTH router refusal, add-by-ref, fab preview, dock (R), bench rev, refdes font, annotations skill","description":"Dispatch base: minerva-plugins main faf84a6a (after pcb 0.5.1). Owner brief: Opus implements; Fable batched cold review; fix loops; Codex serial review on the worker/wire tasks; fix loops; scoped execution incl. every real-worker suite; owner is away — decisions by the 7-axis rubric.\n\nORDER (dependency chain 1 → {2,3,4} → 7 → 8; 9 after 4; 5,6 float):\n1. 01a042fd1c83 bottom-side parts: B.* layers + mirrored offsets through resolve; _harvest_pads mirrors; 45° pad rotation sign.\n2. 01a04511e2dc mid-trace via credit (via↔segment through copper_contact; same in dangling; panel ratsnest via credit). Remoras: Codex note \"via loops centre-based\" (01a0430ff5f1), wrong_net_pad centreline-vs-pad-CENTRE + pour compiled twice per run_drc (01a042fdc4c8).\n3. 01a044a664fc router: exclude the unroutable pad/net with a warning instead of refusing the board; panel maps a worker refusal as a refusal, not route_worker_unavailable.\n4. 01a0452996e2 add_component by library ref (GUI + MCP), unresolved-geometry badge, named refusal instead of silent whole-board indeterminate. Remoras: rotation vocabulary (01a042fd8292), _refresh_zone_fill silent swallow (01a042fde882), free_pins side on wide 2-col parts (01a044a67de8), canonical_wire_board pad-drop heuristic only (01a042fda3df).\n5. 01a0447a288c fab preview: live-board palette, header out of the board rect, per-layer picker. Remora: STALE overlay keeps show_fab_preview raised (01a042fde882).\n6. 01a0451c3e7c dock (R): author_ui() → null, delete WaypointRouteHintAuthorTool, keep the Intents tool classes + apply_via_at_point + build_route_hint_envelope.\n7. 01a0448190a6 bench rev: R3 pour → B.Cu, drop R4, R9-B → inspect cell, new rows for 2/3/4; results sheet refreshed.\n8. 01a042fcfe21 refdes font → board_font.py; NOTICE section for source-embedded tables; goldens regenerated LAST.\n9. 01a0451de711 skill: core annotation kinds/anchors/verify-by-render + the add-by-ref verb from 4; 3-shot LLM eval with a no-skill control.\n\nTEST PLAN (authored during implementation, run at scoped execution): contact vectors for via-on-segment (touch / beside); Smart Remote v2 export as a worker golden (GND partial 3→2) + mutation (delete via (8,30) → 3); bench rows; GD real-worker add-by-ref → board_drc clean with real_worker_used=true; grep gate for the dead tool; refdes goldens regenerated from a measured run; skill eval 3/3 with control.\n\nCodex: on 1–4 as one pass (split if >500 KB); skipped for 5–9 (record why).","created_at":"2026-08-27T22:43:07","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:36:49","priority":2,"tags":["handoff","hitl-2026-08-27","task-cycle-7"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0457a53ea7f86bb59ceb79af03bee","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: bottom-side mirror follow-ups from TC7 task 1 — gerber._harvest and route_bridge.board_to_router still don't mirror; panel get_transform/_pads_from_list; dead OvalHole radians(+deg); NPTH pad present in raw census but absent from the IR projection","description":"Filed from the implementer's out-of-scope list while fixing 01a042fd1c83 (commit 5b9156b made drc._harvest_pads delegate to geometry.component_transform).\n\n1. `gerber._harvest` and `route_bridge.board_to_router` carry their own pad placement without the bottom-side mirror. Both are test-only today (production runs through the IR), so they were left; they should delegate to `geometry.component_transform` the same way drc does, or be deleted if truly dead.\n2. Panel side: `pcb_component.gd` `get_transform` / `_pads_from_list` do not mirror a bottom-side part's lands — the panel draws and hit-tests bottom parts un-mirrored. Needs the same single-rule treatment (one transform shared by draw, hit-test and to_board_dict) plus a contact vector on the panel test.\n3. Dead code: `drc_geometric.py` ~L795 `OvalHole` uses `radians(+deg)` — contradicts the pinned `geometry.rotation_radians` (radians(-deg)); unreachable today, delete or route through the shared helper.\n4. An NPTH pad appears in the raw census pad list but not in the IR projection — the two paths disagree on whether an NPTH is a \"pad\" at all. Decide once (NPTH = no copper per TC6) and make both read it.\n\nOracle: a bottom-side part in parity_corners renders/hit-tests mirrored on the panel (vector-pinned); gerber/route_bridge harvests equal the drc harvest for every fixture; grep finds no radians(+deg) in the worker.","created_at":"2026-08-27T23:07:19","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:40:02","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bottom-side","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-7"],"resolution":"TC8 T6 579f8ab: gerber/router harvests delegate to component_transform; panel get_transform mirrors + placed_pad_layers; OvalHole through rotate_local_offset; NPTH a non-pad in both censuses (bench R5_A 3→2). kicad loose path left as follow-up 01a0485d5200."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0458e039f713597ad27a24efe12e8","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Never put the owner's real board into pcb/worker/tests/testdata — POLICY.md bans it and test_corpus_policy.py catches it by CONTENT, any file type","created_at":"2026-08-27T23:28:50","updated_at":"2026-08-27T23:28:50","tags":["corpus","ip","pcb","tests"],"value":"A task that says \"copy the owner's saved board into the test corpus as a golden\" must be REFUSED, whatever the file extension. pcb/worker/tests/testdata/POLICY.md bans real product board designs from these public repos, and tests/test_corpus_policy.py enforces it independently of the CI workflow: it scans EVERY tracked text file of EVERY type (.json and .py fixtures included) in both minerva-plugins and Minerva, and recognises a board by DENSITY — >= 24 distinct identifying tokens (net names + refs + footprint ids, read at run time from ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote/EDA/minerva-fab/smart-remote-canonical.yaml) inside a 120-line sliding window. A 47-component board dict blows past that in one window.\n\nWHAT TO DO INSTEAD, and it loses nothing: measure the real board OUTSIDE the repo (a scratchpad probe against pcb_worker.methods._HANDLERS['drc']), read off the geometry that produces the defect, and author a SYNTHETIC fixture with the same numbers. Prove the fixture is a real oracle by running it against the base commit's module — load the base file under the live package name so relative imports still resolve:\n    import pcb_worker\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('pcb_worker.drc', BASE_COPY)\n    m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); sys.modules['pcb_worker.drc'] = m; spec.loader.exec_module(m)\nA fixture that fails on base and passes on HEAD is worth exactly as much as the product board would have been.","component":"pcb","key":"no-real-board-in-the-worker-corpus","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0458f560f76e48d6c0ea78813ef99","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb test corpus bans real product boards — goldens are synthetic","created_at":"2026-08-27T23:30:16","updated_at":"2026-08-27T23:30:31","tags":["corpus","pcb","policy","testing"],"value":"pcb/worker/tests/testdata/POLICY.md forbids real product designs in the corpus and test_corpus_policy.py enforces it by content density on ANY file type (yaml/json alike). Never copy an owner board (e.g. smart-remote-v2) into pcb/worker/tests — it is IP into public git. Pattern that works: measure the real board OUTSIDE the repo (worker handlers take {'params': {'board': <panel dict>}}; the saved board dict lives in the .minproj under Editors[n].plugin_state.payload.__panel_state), then rebuild the failing geometry as a small synthetic fixture with the same oracle + a mutation (e.g. TC7 test_via_copper_credit.py: pin_groups 2, via deleted → 3).","component":"pcb","key":"test-corpus-no-real-boards","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0458f70e07d13af585116477c72b1","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb live-board MCP editing gotchas (via snap order, placeholder parts, rotation)","created_at":"2026-08-27T23:30:23","updated_at":"2026-08-27T23:30:23","tags":["editing","gotcha","mcp","pcb"],"value":"1. update_via SNAPS onto any same-net trace its annulus reaches (reply: snapped_to_trace:true + landed coords). To shift a via+trace run: delete_traces FIRST, then update_via, then add_trace. Moving first pulls the via back onto the old copper.\n2. add_component with a generic footprint enum (HEADER, RESISTOR…) creates PANEL PLACEHOLDER geometry the worker refuses (footprint_unresolved, net_pin_unresolved) → geometric DRC AND pour fill go indeterminate for the WHOLE board. Until 01a0452996e2 lands: minerva_pcb_footprint_report <Lib:Part> for the pad facts, then import_footprint_geometry {components:{REF:{footprint_id:'Lib:Part', has_pad_geometry:true, bounding_box:{width,height,center_x,center_y}, pads:[{number,type:'thru_hole',shape,position:{x,y},size:{width,height},drill,layers}]}}}. Leaves component_graphics_absent (no silk/courtyard) — cosmetic.\n3. rotate_component: read the pads[] rows in the REPLY; do not predict. Observed on the panel: a pad at local +y (south of origin) lands EAST at 90 and WEST at 270 — opposite to the tool doc's example (01a042fd8292 tracks the vocabulary).","component":"pcb","key":"live-board-edit-gotchas","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a045c6a61570e69543b6720b7850b0","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: fab preview's \"INCOMPLETE — 1 emitted file(s) not shown\" banner fires on every board because the .gbrjob job manifest is counted as an unrendered layer","description":"Found by the TC7 task-5 implementer (fab preview readability, commit fe82d1d). Every Gerber emission includes a `.gbrjob` job file; it is not artwork and is always classified `unrendered`, so the orange incomplete banner appears on every preview — alarm fatigue that hides a real missing layer. Exclude non-artwork job/manifest files from the \"not shown\" count (and list them separately if useful). Oracle: a complete emission previews with no incomplete banner; deleting one copper layer from the artifact set raises it.","created_at":"2026-08-28T00:30:41","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:39:54","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["fab-preview","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-7"],"resolution":"TC8 T1 747dcb0: unrendered entries carry kind job|artwork; the banner counts missing artwork only."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a045e3b69279a1ac5019422baa5bff","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Scripts loaded by file path must not use @dataclass under `from __future__ import annotations`","created_at":"2026-08-28T01:02:26","updated_at":"2026-08-28T01:02:26","tags":["gotcha","notice","pcb","python"],"value":"pcb/scripts/gen_notice.py is imported by worker/tests/test_notice.py via importlib.util.module_from_spec WITHOUT registering the module in sys.modules. Under `from __future__ import annotations`, @dataclass resolves field types through sys.modules[cls.__module__].__dict__ — which is None for such a module, so the CLASS DEFINITION raises AttributeError at import time (not at use). `python3 scripts/gen_notice.py` works fine because __module__ is then \"__main__\". Use typing.NamedTuple instead: it keeps annotations as strings and never resolves them. Same trap applies to any pcb/scripts/*.py a test loads by path.","component":"pcb","key":"gen_notice_no_dataclass","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a045e4da8e7c39a275682d863cd7e7","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: LOGO_Owl_TestCoupon.kicad_mod bakes Newstroke (GPL-2) glyph strokes but is declared LicenseRef-TurnRock-Proprietary in footprints.lock.json — wrong licence label in the shipped NOTICE; payload_cap_fixture.json also carries Newstroke refdes strokes","description":"Found by the TC7 task-8 implementer while removing stroke_font.py (commit a4f9fe3 — no GPL TABLE remains; these are two Newstroke-DERIVED artifacts).\n\n1. `pcb/library/footprints/Minerva_Fixture.pretty/LOGO_Owl_TestCoupon.kicad_mod` bakes \"TEST COUPON\" as ~90 `fp_line`s generated from Newstroke glyphs, yet `footprints.lock.json` records it as `LicenseRef-TurnRock-Proprietary`, so the generated NOTICE.md carries a materially wrong licence label for shipped content.\n2. `pcb/tests/gd/testdata/payload_cap_fixture.json` carries U1..U30 refdes strokes at the old Newstroke coordinates (test-only, not shipped).\n\nBoth are sha/byte-pinned captures whose generators are not in-repo, so fixing either is a provenance change with its own bless. Options for (1): regenerate the legend from board_font (in-house) and re-bless the footprint; or keep the strokes and record the real licence (GPL-2.0-or-later, with the obligations that implies for a proprietary repo). (1) should be settled before the next tagged release. (2): regenerate the fixture from the current emitter when convenient. Oracle: NOTICE names the true licence of every shipped artifact; the source-grep test in test_notice.py could be widened to .kicad_mod/.json once the two are cleaned.","created_at":"2026-08-28T01:03:41","updated_at":"2026-08-28T07:34:56","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["fcib","licence","notice","pcb","release-blocker-candidate","task-cycle-7"],"resolution":"The proprietary label is now true. Remaining Newstroke-derived bytes: pcb/tests/gd/testdata/payload_cap_fixture.json (test-only) inlines this footprint as U23 with the OLD 105 fp_lines — stale but harmless; regenerate when that fixture is next touched."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0460126f27213a7c176d7878225eb","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: footprint_resolved round-trips through to_board_dict, so a saved board reopened on a machine whose library lacks the ref keeps a stale true and canonical_wire_board trims its pads","description":"Found by the TC7 fold re-review. `footprint_resolved` is the worker's resolve-success fact (resolve.py success path; host apply_geometry after a real pcb.footprint_geometry reply) and canonical_wire_board uses it to trim `pads` from the wire (keeps a by-ref board's wire ~9x smaller). But `pcb_component.gd` ~L1163 also persists it via to_board_dict, so a board saved on machine A and opened on machine B (library lacking the ref, best-effort resolve) still carries `footprint_resolved: true`, the wire trims the pads, and the worker — which has no library hit either — sees a PARTIAL part with no pads. Fix: treat the flag as session state — do not persist it (or re-derive it on load from the actual resolve result), so the trim decision always reflects THIS machine's library. Oracle: save a by-ref board, reopen with the footprint removed from the library layers → the part keeps its pads on the wire and compiles FULL (or the panel names the unresolved ref), never silently pad-less.","created_at":"2026-08-28T01:34:35","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:39:57","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["follow-up","library","pcb","task-cycle-7","wire"],"resolution":"TC8 T3 ce02aa5 (option c): footprint_resolved is session state — to_saved_board_dict strips it on every disk path, load restores it only from a live resolve, Go drops document flags at deserialize."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04603a25978d3b8cefb7e2c819b3a","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"codex exec from Claude Code: detach it with setsid nohup from a sandbox-off FOREGROUND call; disable its MCP servers per run","created_at":"2026-08-28T01:37:18","updated_at":"2026-08-28T01:41:53","tags":["codex","gotcha","review","task-cycle"],"value":"Driving `codex exec` from Claude Code Bash: (1) Claude Code's Bash sandbox blocks Codex's bubblewrap (user namespaces); run_in_background jobs are reported \"killed\" within ~10 s with an empty -o file EVEN with dangerouslyDisableSandbox=true (the flag only holds for foreground calls). Working pattern: a FOREGROUND call with dangerouslyDisableSandbox=true that launches `setsid nohup <script> > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null &` and returns; the script appends `exit=$?` to its err log; then a plain run_in_background `until grep -q '^exit=' ...; do sleep 10; done` waiter gives one notification. Keep Codex at `--sandbox read-only` (never danger-full-access; see codex/cross-provider-review). (2) With Minerva/Docket closed, silence its MCP servers: `-c 'mcp_servers.minerva.enabled=false' -c 'mcp_servers.docket.enabled=false'` (`-c 'mcp_servers={}'` does NOT override the table; the rmcp errors are harmless anyway). Form: `{ cat prompt; echo DIFF:; cat diff; } | codex exec --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -c ... -C <repo> -o <last_message.md> -` (stderr = transcript, -o = final answer only). Smoke-test with a PONG prompt first; ~250 KB per pass; tell Codex not to run commands.","component":"codex","key":"exec-with-minerva-down","confidence":"measured","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04628e9547b09a728fa5740812a35","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Real-worker GD preflight = rebuild pcb/pcb-plugin AND park the extracted runtime bundle; the probe alone lies","created_at":"2026-08-28T02:18:01","updated_at":"2026-08-28T02:19:02","tags":["gd","gotcha","pcb","preflight","runtime-bundle","testing"],"value":"Two ways a \"real_worker_used=true\" run tests OLD code: (1) pcb/pcb-plugin (dev Go binary) is stale — it owns the IPC channel list (internal/tools/worker_tools.go); rebuild with `cd pcb && go build -o pcb-plugin .`. (2) ~/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/<serverVersion>/ exists — the dev binary uses THAT extracted worker (pre-batch code) instead of pcb/worker/.venv; symptom: worker replies \"unknown method: <new method>\" while pcb/scripts/probe-worker-methods.py still says \"repo source and live worker agree\" (the probe resolves the venv itself, not what the binary spawns). Fix: `mv runtime/<ver> <scratch>/runtime-parked/` for the run (restore after if Minerva needs it; the marketplace install re-extracts anyway), confirm with `python3 pcb/scripts/e2e_route_stdio.py ./pcb-plugin req.json pcb.<new_channel>`. run-gd-tests.sh refuses real-worker suites on a stale bundle, but direct `godot --script` invocations do not — do both checks by hand for scoped runs. TC7 lost one full GD pass to this.","component":"pcb","key":"gd-real-worker-preflight-go-binary","confidence":"measured","retrieval_count":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04793a62078008cd3cd4673e6a00d","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"FnF: pcb canvas draws traces above pads and drill holes — a trace through a THT pad is rendered crossing the hole, which the fab never makes","description":"Observed 2026-08-28 on Smart Remote v2 by the owner: TP2.1 (1x02 header pad, 1.8 mm land, 1.0 mm drill) sits on the bottom +3V3_SW_OLED trace (x=24, y 45.6→24). The canvas paints the 0.5 mm trace stroke over the pad and straight across the drill circle, so the board appears to have copper in the hole. Physically and in CAM this is fine — the trace and the annular ring are one copper shape and the drill removes the middle — so this is presentation only.\n\nWanted (the convention every EDA canvas uses): draw order copper traces → pads/lands → drill holes as a void above everything (and vias the same way), per layer, so a trace visually ends under the land and the hole is always empty. Keep hit-testing/selection unchanged. Applies to the live canvas only; the fab preview (emitted artwork) already shows the true copper/drill.","created_at":"2026-08-28T08:54:13","updated_at":"2026-08-28T19:47:41","priority":3,"severity":4,"tags":["FnF","canvas","hitl-2026-08-28","pcb","rendering"],"resolution":"TC8 T5 739a8e7 (+ folds): copper draw order as data — traces, lands, drills last; paste-only apertures paint no copper."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a047952f267cbdb7cabb16a950449d","type":"bug","status":"closed","title":"pcb: remove the sidebar \"Add Part\" section — part and footprint work is LLM-only by design; humans do layout and traces","description":"Owner ruling 2026-08-28 on seeing the new sidebar \"Add Part\" section (task-cycle 7 task 4, commit a59f723 / 1aa79d4, pcb_add_part_section.gd + its mount in PCBPanel): \"This shouldn't be in UI — design of the system is that the LLM is the only way to add a part. Humans help with layout and trace, but not in part or footprint work.\"\n\nThis carves out the parity rule (every GUI affordance has an MCP verb and vice versa): part/footprint authoring is MCP/YAML-only, like net assignment. Remove the Add Part section and everything only it used (the ref field, button, result line/preview note, drop_point flow); keep the MCP verb minerva_pcb_add_component {footprint:\"Lib:Part\"}, the pcb.footprint_geometry channel, PcbLibraryPart (the badge/status-lead/is_fabricable rule still serves the canvas and replies), and the tests for those. Update test_add_by_library_ref (drop the GUI-section assertions, keep MCP), docs/tools.md and the design skill text if they mention the sidebar.","created_at":"2026-08-28T08:55:54","updated_at":"2026-08-28T19:47:40","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["hitl-2026-08-28","ownership","pcb","ux"],"resolution":"TC8 T2 ec0706c: sidebar Add Part section deleted (−118); MCP add-by-ref, footprint_geometry channel and badge kept."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04799b5bd709cbf9dfc8967363ae9","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb fab preview (live, 0.5.1+TC7): no pan/zoom, holes and edge cuts not visible, and the per-layer picker refuses every layer — view_state reads 12 emitted layers but the write path says the preview holds only [\"all\"]","description":"Owner HITL 2026-08-28 on Smart Remote v2 with View > Fab preview on (post task-cycle 7 rewrite, commits fe82d1d/1aa79d4/1ae5751): \"preview lacks pan/zoom capability, so I can't really see parts closely. Holes and cuts don't seem to be rendered in that view either.\" Unsure whether bug or design — it is a bug.\n\nAgent measurements on the same session:\n- `view_state` READ: show_fab_preview=true, fab_preview_layer=\"all\", fab_preview_layers = [all, f_mask, b_mask, f_paste, b_paste, f_cu, b_cu, pth, npth, f_silks, b_silks, edge_cuts] (12).\n- `view_state` WRITE fab_preview_layer=\"pth\": refused `layer_not_emitted`, `available: [\"all\"]`, note \"the fab preview holds [\"all\"] — it has no layer 'pth' to isolate\". So the read reply advertises rows the picker cannot select: the per-layer rasters were never adopted (or the read lists the emitted SET while the write consults the ADOPTED rows). Either way the picker — the whole point of the rewrite — is unusable on a real board.\n- `get_image` at the current camera with the flag raised shows the LIVE canvas (red/blue traces, refdes, the \"APPROXIMATE IN THIS VIEW\" caption) and no emitted artwork, no banner — consistent with nothing adopted, or with the known stale-overlay capture gap (01a042fde882).\n- Pan/zoom: the owner cannot zoom the preview; whether the overlay ignores the camera or is simply absent must be determined on a fresh fetch.\nLikely relevant: the by-ref fetch path for large boards (this board is over the broker cap; the fab preview rides pcb_overlay_fetch by ref) vs the small spike board the suite uses; PcbFabPreview.adopt / refit; test_fab_preview_legibility passes on the spike fixture, so the defect is in the live fetch/adopt seam, not the draw.","created_at":"2026-08-28T09:00:51","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:39:52","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["by-ref","fab-preview","hitl-2026-08-28","pcb","regression-candidate"],"resolution":"TC8 T1 747dcb0 (+ f4fb316 harness fix): preview placed through the board camera via bounds_board_mm; canvas input gated while the preview is up; drills above silk at full alpha; picker selectable. Live use needs the refreshed worker bundle.","repro_steps":"1. Open Smart Remote v2 (47 parts, by-ref transport). View > Fab preview.\n2. minerva_pcb_view_state {editor_name} → fab_preview_layers lists 12 keys.\n3. minerva_pcb_view_state {fab_preview_layer:\"pth\"} → layer_not_emitted, available [\"all\"].\n4. Zoom/pan on the canvas: the preview does not follow / cannot be inspected closely; PTH/NPTH holes and Edge.Cuts are not visible in the composite."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0479d23b177018de7848c045f0c18","type":"dcr","status":"reviewing","title":"DCR: Options menu with a trace-angle mode (Manhattan 0/90 · Octilinear 0/45/90 · Free) — the Trace tool snaps to it and DRC gc12 enforces it from design_rules.allowed_trace_angles_deg","description":"Owner idea, HITL 2026-08-28, while failing to draw a perfectly horizontal trace: \"Manhattan-style snap default that can be disabled … an Options menu next to View; Manhattan an option; traces always 0/45/90 snaps (can be full 360); Options → Manhattan to turn off.\"\n\nDesign (agent proposal, rubric-scored):\n1. ANGLE MODE IS A BOARD DESIGN RULE, not a view flag. Options → Trace angles: Manhattan [0,90] · Octilinear [0,45,90,135…] · Free (unset). Choosing one writes `design_rules.allowed_trace_angles_deg` on the board. Consequences: (a) the Trace tool quantises the rubber-band direction to the allowed set while drawing; (b) the worker's existing `gc12_trace_direction` check (today \"not evaluated: this board declares no design_rules.allowed_trace_angles_deg\") enforces the same set on every trace, agent-routed ones included. One source of truth; the rule travels with the YAML. Default for a new board: Octilinear. Manhattan for boards routed by mhroute.\n2. ESCAPE HATCH ON THE KEY: hold Shift while drawing for a free-angle segment (universal EDA gesture) — no need to flip the mode for one segment. The Options entry remains the deliberate change.\n3. SNAP PRIORITY near small lands (owner R9 note \"the snaps get in the way\"): angle snap first, then \"end on the target land\" (the land's copper, not its centre), grid last.\n4. OPTIONS MENU next to View: trace-angle mode, grid pitch, snap toggles (grid / pad / angle). Parity: read/write via existing `minerva_pcb_get_preference` / `set_preference` for the per-user snaps and via the board-rules verb (set_board_size/design_rules path) for the angle set; `view_state`-style read of the active mode.\n\nNot in scope: rerouting existing traces to the new rule (DRC just reports them); the mhroute router.","created_at":"2026-08-28T09:04:35","updated_at":"2026-08-28T11:23:19","priority":2,"tags":["design-rules","hitl-2026-08-28","options-menu","pcb","trace-tool"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a047a3ae107f2ca866d315583cbc5b","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb panel: the canvas draws a cached copy of the designator strokes (component.refdes_graphics) that is never refreshed on copy/rename — JP6 on Smart Remote v2 displays JP5's strokes; the Gerber is correct","description":"Owner HITL 2026-08-28: \"JP6's silk says JP5 — why?\" Measured: JP6.refdes_graphics in the saved board is byte-identical to JP5's (JP6 was evidently made by copying JP5). The panel draws these cached strokes on the canvas, so the screen shows the live ref label \"JP6\" and the stale strokes \"JP5\" stacked.\n\nCAM is NOT affected (corrected from the first filing): canonical_wire_board strips refdes_graphics (_WIRE_DROP_COMPONENT_FIELDS) and the emitter renders the designator from `ref` via silk_source.refdes_strokes — the Gerber says JP6. This is a panel display-cache defect plus one board with stale cache data.\n\nFix: treat refdes_graphics as a derived cache — regenerate it (or simply render the ref with the panel's own font) whenever ref changes or a component is duplicated, and refresh it on load/normalize; never persist it as authority. Also drop it from to_board_dict if the panel can render designators itself (the strokes are Newstroke-era leftovers in every pre-TC7 board). Oracle: rename/copy a part on the bench → canvas designator matches the ref immediately and after save/reload; Smart Remote v2 shows one JP5 and one JP6 after load.\n\nBoard remedy for the owner's file: re-resolve or clear JP6's refdes_graphics (agent-side, harmless to fab either way).","created_at":"2026-08-28T09:11:44","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:39:59","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["display-cache","hitl-2026-08-28","panel","pcb","refdes","silk"],"resolution":"TC8 T4 27ce0b1: designator strokes derived from the ref via PcbBoardFont; wire carries refdes_anchor; Go drops document refdes_graphics at deserialize, so Smart Remote v2 self-cleans on next load."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a047a9944f74af9408c7394c2024c7","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF: task-cycle 8 (2026-08-28) — seven pcb tasks from the post-TC7 HITL; Opus implements, Fable batched cold review, Codex batched final check, scoped execution","description":"Owner brief 2026-08-28: \"let's run a task-cycle. Opus implementer. batch fable reviewer. batch codex reviewer.\" Same discipline as TC7 (chore 01a045642b56): one base → one task → one commit; tests authored NOT run; static gates only; batch cold review by dimension; fix rounds folded; Codex serial last on the diff via stdin; scoped run with every real-worker suite + probe pre-flight; push only after; beep.\n\nDispatch base: minerva-plugins main 63927d22b21a2c00ee5f0646e9a6de05da512362 (pulled, clean). Go binary pcb/pcb-plugin rebuilt post-TC7 (2026-08-28 01:21); runtime bundle 0.5.1 PARKED (dev binary uses worker/.venv). Minerva is UP during implementation (no tests run); it must be DOWN before the scoped GD run.\n\nOrder: T1 fab preview (01a04799b5bd + 01a045c6a615) → T2 remove Add Part (01a047952f26) → T3 footprint_resolved session-state (01a0460126f2, option c — decided by rubric, owner did not pick; recorded here for veto) → T4 refdes cache (01a047a3ae10) → T5 draw order (01a04793a620) → T6 bottom-side remnants (01a0457a53ea) → T7 DCR Options menu + trace-angle mode (01a0479d23b1, sections 1+2).\n\nDeferred full suite: last full GD run 77/77 at pcb-v0.5.1 tag (2026-08-27); TC7 closed on a scoped run only → full suite OVERDUE for the TC7 range.","created_at":"2026-08-28T09:18:11","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:40:09","priority":2,"tags":["handoff","pcb","task-cycle-8"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04852d60776259da47e28e81d1d4c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Long test/review runs from Claude Code: setsid+nohup from a sandbox-off foreground call, then Monitor a log","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:23:03","updated_at":"2026-08-28T12:23:16","tags":["claude-code","task-cycle","testing","tooling"],"value":"Measured 3x in task-cycle 8 (2026-08-28): (1) Bash run_in_background jobs (a 5-min pytest, a 30-min GD suite loop, an until-loop waiter) get KILLED when the turn ends — status \"killed\", partial logs. Launch instead with `setsid nohup bash script.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 &` from a FOREGROUND Bash call with dangerouslyDisableSandbox=true (the sandbox reaps detached children), have the script `touch DONE` at the end, and arm a Monitor that tails the summary log and exits on the DONE marker. (2) `pkill -f <pattern>` inside the same Bash call matches the shell running it -> exit 144 and the rest of the command never runs; use pgrep first or a pattern that cannot match the invoking command line. (3) The GD summary's `fails=` grep counts any \"FAIL\" substring in suite output (status strings), so read the `Results:` line, not the count. (4) docket_hint_set: never let a closing tag or newline into the title argument — the title swallows the value and the value lands empty (happened TC7 twice, TC8 once). Same launch recipe as codex/exec-with-minerva-down.","component":"claude-code","key":"long-runs-detach-and-monitor","confidence":"measured","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485d2ae97d13b26d218b7380d40c","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb fab preview follow-ups: _invalidate_fab_preview fires on ANY data_changed (an MCP write blanks the preview and retracts the View flag); capture_to_image(fit) frames the board rect, not the artwork","description":"Found by task-cycle 8 T1 (747dcb0). (1) PCBPanel wires _invalidate_fab_preview to _data.data_changed outside the _restoring gate, so any incidental model touch from any source (MCP write, autosave, a future feature) silently blanks the preview and retracts show_fab_preview. The input gate removed the gesture that triggered it in practice, but the policy still fails closed on things a user would not consider an edit. Wanted: invalidate only on edits that change emitted artwork (copper/silk/mask/drill/outline), or re-fetch instead of retracting. (2) capture_to_image(fit=true) frames the board rect while the fab preview draws the artwork rect — a fitted capture of the preview is at a different scale from the screen (extents nearly coincide, WYSIWYG seam). Oracle: an MCP set_preference / annotation write while the preview is up leaves it up; a fitted capture of the preview equals the on-screen framing.","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:20","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:41","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["fab-preview","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-8"],"resolution":"TC9 T5 d71155f: preview invalidates only when the whole-board token of the rendered dict changes; fitted capture and screen share one framing call."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485d3e9e762585e1308a3f1f13a5","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: _run_draft_check hashes the v1 board fingerprint for board_token while the routing sidecar writes and compares v2 — the draft check and the sidecar disagree on what \"the same board\" is","description":"Found by task-cycle 8 T3 (ce02aa5). PCBPanel._run_draft_check (~L6382) uses compute_board_fingerprint (v1, hashes the whole GD dict) for board_token, while pcb_routing_sidecar.gd (~L183, ~L298) writes and compares the v2 fingerprint — v1 hashing the whole dict is precisely what v2's own doc comment says made sidecars quarantine on first promote. Also: legacy v1 sidecars on by-ref boards will read stale once after ce02aa5 (their stored hash covered a dict that carried footprint_resolved) — a degrade, not a loss. Fix: one fingerprint derivation (v2 over to_saved_board_dict) for both. Oracle: draft check token == sidecar token for the same saved board.","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:25","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:48","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["follow-up","pcb","sidecar","task-cycle-8"],"resolution":"TC9 T7 9c79fd1: check_draft and the routing sidecar both derive compute_board_fingerprint_v2(to_saved_board_dict()) — one token."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485d52007e77a6ac060155e4254b","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb worker: kicad._footprint loose-dict path does not flip pad layers for a bottom-side part — the third emitter still lacks the placement rule (gerber and route_bridge fixed in TC8)","description":"Found by task-cycle 8 T6 (579f8ab). kicad.py ~L709-714 comments that \"the resolved layer list has already been side-flipped by the placement transform\" — true only on generate_ir. On the loose-dict path iter_pads yields footprint-local layers, so a bottom component's paste-only pad emits \"F.Paste\" inside a (layer \"B.Cu\") footprint. Same defect class as gerber._harvest / route_bridge.board_to_router before 579f8ab. Fix: delegate to geometry.component_transform / drc.placed_pad_layers like the other two. Oracle: extend test_placement_parity with the kicad emitter as a fourth reader on parity_corners U2/SW10.","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:30","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:46","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bottom-side","follow-up","kicad","pcb","task-cycle-8"],"resolution":"TC9 T7 9c79fd1 (+ folds): kicad loose-dict path stores mirrored local y, mirrored reference text and negated pad local rotation for a bottom part. Silk-graphics analogue filed 01a04a93a8d9."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485d6aa57b3889e8eda953a0f096","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb canvas follow-ups from TC8: silk bucket filters literal F.SilkS (B.SilkS artwork on a flipped part unreachable); SMD lands not gated by _layer_visible while traces are; _draw_staged_placement bypasses _draw_pad; show_pads-off/show_pins-on draws nominal pins for a resolved part","description":"Four canvas oddities found while landing T5/T6 (739a8e7, 579f8ab, folds). (1) _draw_component_graphics_layer filters component graphics by the literal \"F.SilkS\"/\"F.CrtYd\" token; a bottom part's silk is now mirrored correctly, but a footprint authoring B.SilkS artwork never reaches the front bucket on a flipped part (the worker flips both ways in gerber._emit_silk). (2) In _draw_copper the trace passes are gated by _layer_visible but the SMD-land passes are not (pre-existing). (3) _draw_staged_placement renders ghost geometry on its own path, not through _draw_pad, so a staged ghost with real lands would not inherit the copper draw order. (4) With show_pads off and show_pins on, a RESOLVED component draws nominal fallback pins — reads like a latent eye-toggle bug. Oracles: recording_canvas.gd (pcb/tests/gd) can pin (1)-(3) as executed painter sequences.","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:36","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:44","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["canvas","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-8"],"resolution":"TC9 T6 4c6905c: flipped artwork via PcbComponent.placed_graphic_layer, SMD lands obey the layer eye, staged lands through _draw_pad (ghost mirrored on the back), no nominal pins over real lands; suite test_canvas_render_truth. Mount-side visibility follow-up 01a04a825a1d."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485d8c0c7c54b955ec5925ce1e32","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb tidy-ups from TC8: three hand-copied mirrors of _WIRE_DROP_COMPONENT_FIELDS; minpcb.go warns per non-canonical component key (refdes_anchor now, as refdes_graphics did); wire drop-list refdes_graphics entry is legacy-only; view_state has no tools.md section; Go DesignRules has no typed AllowedTraceAnglesDeg; set_board_size skips save_to_history; ruff not installed in worker/.venv","description":"Collected file-don't-fix items from task-cycle 8. (a) worker/tests/test_board_health_resolve_first.py:134 and tests/gd/test_canonical_wire_board.gd:62 each keep a hand-copied mirror of panel_tools._WIRE_DROP_COMPONENT_FIELDS — three copies of one list. (b) internal/board/minpcb.go warns for every non-canonical component key; refdes_anchor on a reply draws that warning as refdes_graphics did — nobody has suppressed it. (c) The wire drop-list entry for refdes_graphics is reachable only via legacy payloads (testdata/payload_cap_fixture.json); retire once no fixture carries the key. (d) minerva_pcb_view_state has no section or table row in pcb/docs/tools.md — its spec lives in a code comment. (e) internal/board DesignRules has no typed AllowedTraceAnglesDeg field; the key survives Go only via Extra (json:\"-\"), so it round-trips YAML but is invisible in the JSON board dict Go hands out. (f) minerva_pcb_set_board_size journals in the model and calls no save_to_history — diverges from the newer write idiom. (g) ruff is not on PATH nor in pcb/worker/.venv, so the Python lint gate is unavailable in this environment. (h) pcb_component.gd/gerber.py/resolve.py still carry pre-TC8 \"used to\"/\"no longer\" prose in older lines (reviewer note; out of any task's scope).","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:45","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:22","priority":4,"tags":["follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-8","tidy"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0485da3ac76a0863b6abb04797332","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Owner call (TC8 T7): should a Trace-tool finish on a pad land end on the land's COPPER (DCR snap-priority note) or stay on the pad CENTRE (today's add_trace byte-parity contract)?","description":"DCR 01a0479d23b1 asked for snap priority \"angle first, then end on the target land (the land's copper, not its centre), grid last\". T7 (8e10ee2) implemented angle-first for waypoints and the grid-along-run, but left the anchor FINISH on the pad centre: _finish_trace_on_anchor's documented contract is that a human click and minerva_pcb_add_trace author byte-identical copper from the same coordinates, and moving the endpoint off centre breaks that parity. Options: (a) keep centre (parity, deterministic); (b) end on the nearest copper point of the land and make add_trace do the same (one rule, both surfaces); (c) centre by default, Shift+click for copper edge. Also for the record: side_for_pin now answers in the board frame for bottom parts (north/south swap on a flipped part) — correct, but it changes minerva_pcb_free_pins {side} replies for bottom-side components.","created_at":"2026-08-28T12:34:51","updated_at":"2026-08-28T19:15:52","priority":3,"tags":["decision","pcb","task-cycle-8","trace-tool"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049e18477734a97a54c151e86895f","type":"dcr","status":"shipped","title":"pcb panel: hover card replaces the read-only Properties rows and the Pin Info section — a bordered multi-line box over a component, pin or trace","description":"PROBLEM. The Properties section and the Pin Info section in the pcb panel sidebar carry the selected entity's display-only facts (component ID/Position/Rotation/Layer/Footprint; pin ref/value/members; net connections). In the tools column these rows are routinely truncated or pushed out of view, so the information a human most wants while placing and routing (which net is this pad on, what is this pin's function) is the hardest to read.\n\nWANTED. A hover card: a 2-D bordered box (not a single line) drawn ON THE CANVAS when the pointer rests over a component, a pad/pin, or a trace, in any idle tool state (Select and the pin inspector at minimum). Content by entity: component → ref, value, footprint, layer, rotation, position; pad/pin → Component.Pin, pin name/function, net, pad layer; trace → net, width, layer, length. The card must be built from the SAME facts minerva_pcb_describe_component / pin_info / describe_region already return (one derivation, so GUI and verb cannot disagree), and must never capture input (it is paint only, positioned so it does not hide the entity under the cursor and stays inside the canvas rect). Precedent: the pin inspector's one-line _draw_inspect_hover_label (pcb_canvas.gd) — grow that idea, in its OWN file (the canvas is 12.4 KLOC and the panel 7.5 KLOC; nothing new lands inside them).\n\nREMOVE from the sidebar: the read-only component rows (ID/Position/Rotation/Layer/Footprint) and the Pin Info section. The status bar keeps its one-line selection summary.\n\nKEEP EXACTLY WHERE THEY ARE (owner ruling in the same conversation: \"those should stay — there's enough room for them\"): the fabrication-stage picker, the zone Net/Layer dropdowns, the trace Width field, the via Net/Size/Drill spin-boxes, the grouped-component Offset X/Y fields, and their commit handlers.\n\nORACLE. Hover a pad on a netted part: the card shows the pin's name/function and the net minerva_pcb_pin_info reports for the same pad, verbatim. Hover a trace: net + width match minerva_pcb_describe_region. Moving the pointer off the entity removes the card within one frame; the card never appears while a drag, marquee or authoring run is in progress. The sidebar no longer contains a PropertiesSection read-only row or a Pin Info section; every listed control still exists and still commits. Panel layout suites re-pinned for the shorter sidebar.","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:38:31","updated_at":"2026-08-29T06:40:48","priority":2,"tags":["hitl","hover-card","pcb","properties","task-cycle-9","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049e96b4f7b6aba21a6202bf373ff","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb fab preview FnF: drill and Edge.Cuts colours are hard to read — use black for holes and dark purple for edge cuts","description":"Owner HITL 2026-08-28 of task-cycle 8 T1 (fab preview as a view through the board camera): behaviour is \"very close — almost good enough for done\". Remaining fit-and-finish: the palette chosen for the drill layer and for Edge.Cuts in fab-preview mode is hard to read against the recoloured artwork. Owner's choice: holes/drills render BLACK; Edge.Cuts renders DARK PURPLE. Palette lives in pcb/ui/model/pcb_fab_preview.gd (the recolour table introduced in task-cycle 7 T5). Oracle: with the preview up on the HITL bench, the board outline is a dark-purple line and every drill is a black disc, distinguishable at a glance from copper, silk and mask; the per-layer picker colours agree with what the canvas paints.","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:47:09","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:42","priority":3,"severity":4,"tags":["Fnf","fab-preview","hitl","pcb","task-cycle-9"],"resolution":"TC9 T5 d71155f: drills black, Edge.Cuts dark purple (0.32,0.10,0.55), both on a 1 px white rim (21:1 / 11:1 measured); picker swatches from the same chip routine."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049ebe81c786eb2150e3ed4af3f37","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb ratsnest: rotating a part off its trace shows NO airwire — the disconnect is reported by the verb (dangling_copper) but not drawn on the board","description":"Suspects, not yet measured (file-and-enqueue during HITL): (1) quieting — GND is a high-fan-out net; a net needing more than QUIET_ABOVE_LINKS (8) joins draws only its QUIET_SHOWN_LINKS (6) shortest, and if GND already has more than 8 open joins on this board the new TP1 island may simply not be in the shortest six even though it is 1.4 mm from its own trace; if so, the rule needs a \"freshly broken\" exception or per-island markers for quieted nets. (2) The pad-less trace piece left behind: check whether a trace island with no pad is a join target at all, and whether the canvas's _ratsnest cache key (hash of extract) changes on a component rotation that moves pads — verify rather than assume. (3) GND may be a pour net; check the pour does not count as joining pads it does not actually reach at the current fill. Oracle: after the rotate above, PcbRatsnest.compute(data) for net GND reports TP1's island as unjoined and the drawn links (or a marker) include it; undo restores zero. Write the test at the model level (extract/solve on the SR2-shaped fixture), which is where the answer lives.","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:49:52","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:39","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","pcb","ratsnest","task-cycle-9"],"resolution":"TC9 T3 3a93268: cause was quieting; bare islands now float to the front of a quieted net's draw order (test_pcb_ratsnest §15). Related flicker/orphan-copper/marker legibility filed as 01a04a4c3c3b.","repro_steps":"Smart Remote v2 board (47 parts, all top), pcb 0.5.2 dev lane, 2026-08-28. minerva_pcb_rotate_component TP1 degrees:90. TP1 is a 2-pad GND test point at (8,30) with a 0.25 mm GND trace trace:3e767a801e90bdc9eeec5ab2e9169098 running pad-centre to pad-centre at (6.6,30)-(9.4,30). After the rotate the pads sit at (8,28.6)/(8,31.4); the verb reply lists BOTH trace endpoints under dangling_copper. On the canvas: the part and its designator turned, the trace stayed put — and NO ratsnest airwire appeared from TP1's pads (now an island of their own on GND) to any other GND copper. Owner: \"The trace got disconnected, but no ratsnest airwires showed up for indica of disconnect on the rotation.\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049ef25297aee844b43c7283c1586","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb refdes default anchor is a constant (local y −1.5 mm) — on any body taller than ~2.5 mm the designator lands INSIDE the part and is hidden after assembly (SW2 on Smart Remote v2)","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:53:24","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:37","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["hitl","pcb","refdes","silk","task-cycle-9"],"resolution":"TC9 T1 d56d513 (+ fold 2 709fa85: extent is ink incl. stroke and arc sweep): default designator anchor derived from the footprint's occupied extent in refdes_anchor.py; resolve/gerber/DRC read one derivation.","repro_steps":"Smart Remote v2, SW2 = EVP-ASAC1A:SW_EVP-ASAC1A at (5.5, 42), no authored refdes_anchor. Its silk body outline spans local y −3.05..+3.05 and its courtyard −3.3..+3.4. The synthesized designator is placed at REFDES_LOCAL_Y_MM = −1.5 (worker silk_source.py:181; panel pcb_component.gd REFDES_DEFAULT_Y_MM) at 1.0 mm text size — i.e. inside the switch body. Every part on the board uses this default; the owner sees \"a lot of silk labels colliding with components\"."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049ef5460745e9aa32bd3439909fb","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb silk collisions: GC9 rules for silk under a part and silk over silk, each finding carrying a suggested free anchor (or hidden), plus a minerva_pcb_set_refdes verb so an agent can apply it","description":"Owner ask 2026-08-28: \"on placed boards those silks will be invisible / useless — I'd like the LLM to be able to detect silk collisions.\"\n\nWHAT EXISTS. drc_geometric GC9 already projects every silk primitive as the emitter draws it — authored footprint graphics, board graphics AND the synthesized designator strokes (SilkPrimitive.origin == \"refdes\") — and checks stroke width and silk-to-pad. It does not check silk that ends up under a part or on top of other silk.\n\nADD, as GC9 advisory rows (never move the DRC verdict):\n1. silk_under_part — a silk stroke inside a FOREIGN component's courtyard, or a DESIGNATOR stroke inside its own body/pad extent. A footprint's own body-outline silk inside its own courtyard is the footprint convention (SW2's EVP-ASAC1A outline) and is NOT a finding.\n2. silk_over_silk — designator strokes crossing another part's designator/outline or board-level silk text.\nEach finding: ref, origin (refdes|graphic|board_graphic), offender ref, side, overlap length mm, and a SUGGESTION: the first of the compass slots around the part (N, S, E, W, then diagonals) at the derived default offset that clears rules 1–2 and silk-to-pad; when no slot clears, suggest hidden:true — owner: \"hidden is probably ok\" — and say so in the finding.\n\nVERB. refdes_anchor travels the wire (task-cycle 8 T4) but nothing sets it. Add minerva_pcb_set_refdes {editor_name, component_id, x_mm?, y_mm?, rotation_deg?, size_mm?, hidden?} — footprint-local, undoable, one history step, reply echoes the resolved anchor and the board-frame strokes bbox; manifest + docs/tools.md; GUI parity later via a drag-the-label gesture on the same anchor (not in this item).\n\nDepends on the default-anchor bug filed alongside (a derived default removes most of the noise before this check runs). Oracle: a synthetic spec board with one designator deliberately inside a neighbour's courtyard and one in the clear yields exactly one silk_under_part row whose suggestion, applied through set_refdes, clears the re-run; a boxed-in part yields hidden. Worker-side test at the model level; Go handler test for the verb.","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:53:36","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:02","priority":2,"tags":["drc","hitl","mcp","pcb","refdes","silk","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049f360077597baca1942844a3d0f","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb GDScript load warnings + one runtime error — FIX, don't suppress: shadowed names (snapped/name/visible), confusable lo_j/hi_j, incompatible ternary in panel_tools, and PCBPanel._zoom_to_fit_deferred connects `resized` twice (ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER on project load)","description":"Owner HITL 2026-08-28, Godot editor output on Minerva start + project load. Rule: fix each at the root (rename, restructure, guard) — NO @warning_ignore annotations, no project-setting downgrades.\n\nERROR (a bug, not a lint):\n- PCBPanel.gd:7160 @ _zoom_to_fit_deferred(): \"Signal 'resized' is already connected to given callable 'Control::zoom_to_fit'\" → ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER. Path: vboxEditor.deserialize → ProjectMenuActions.deserialize_project → vboxEditor.gd:771 lambda → PCBPanel._on_panel_load_request (7361) → _zoom_to_fit_deferred. A second load request re-connects without checking is_connected (or without CONNECT_ONE_SHOT). Fix so the connect happens once per canvas lifetime, and find out whether the duplicate load request itself is expected.\n\nWARNINGS:\n- pcb/ui/model/pcb_trace_angles.gd:237 — parameter \"snapped\" shadows the built-in snapped() (SHADOWED_GLOBAL_IDENTIFIER).\n- pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:8141 — variable \"snapped\" shadows the built-in.\n- pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd:8612 — local \"visible\" shadows CanvasItem.visible; 9708 — local \"name\" shadows Node.name (SHADOWED_VARIABLE_BASE_CLASS). Both fire twice (two loads).\n- pcb/ui/model/pcb_bus_geometry.gd:1421/1422 — \"lo_j\"/\"hi_j\" declared below in the parent block (CONFUSABLE_LOCAL_DECLARATION): hoist or rename so the inner and outer names differ.\n- pcb/ui/panel_tools.gd:10626 — INCOMPATIBLE_TERNARY: the two arms have different types; give both arms one declared type (or split the ternary).\n\nOracle: `godot --headless --path Minerva/src --script` load of each file (the gd-syntax-check.sh gate) prints none of the above; the LSP on port 6005 reports zero warnings for the five files; loading a project with a pcb tab twice logs no ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER. Good remora for whichever TC9 task already has the file open (canvas: T4/T6; panel_tools/PCBPanel: T4); the rest can ride as one small commit.","created_at":"2026-08-28T19:58:01","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:17","priority":3,"tags":["hitl","pcb","remora","task-cycle-9","warnings"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a049f86c68751bb1c392294c69bc8d","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF: task-cycle 9 (2026-08-28) — eight pcb tasks from the post-TC8 HITL; Opus implements, Fable batched cold review + fix iterations, Codex batched final check, scoped execution","description":"Owner brief 2026-08-28: \"approve. Run autonomously using my rubric to make decisions. Iterate fixes with fable after batched review, then run codex batched review. Beep me after codex batched review lands.\" Roles: Opus implementer, Fable batched reviewer, Codex batched reviewer. Same discipline as TC8 (chore 01a047a9944f): one base → one task → one commit; tests authored NOT run; static gates only; batch cold review by dimension; fix rounds folded; Codex serial last on the diff via stdin; scoped run with every real-worker suite + probe pre-flight; push only after; beep.\n\nDispatch base: minerva-plugins main 864a99c (b563ba4 + docket chore), == origin/main. Minerva DOWN (Godot editor open, app closed). Go binary + worker/.venv fresh; no runtime bundle shadowing.\n\nOrder: T1 refdes default anchor from courtyard (01a049ef2529, remora minpcb warning from 01a0485d8c0c) → T2 silk GC9 + set_refdes (01a049ef5460, remora tools.md view_state) → T3 ratsnest airwire after rotate-off (01a049ebe81c) → T4 hover card (01a049e18477) → T5 fab preview follow-ups + FnF colours (01a0485d2ae9 + 01a049e96b4f) → T6 canvas follow-ups (01a0485d6aa5) → T7 worker smalls (01a0485d5200 + 01a0485d3e9e, remora typed AllowedTraceAnglesDeg + set_board_size history) → T8 warnings fixed at root (01a049f36007 pcb + 01a049f37c37 Minerva; two commits, one per repo).\n\nDeferred full suite: OVERDUE — last full run at pcb-v0.5.1; the 2026-08-28 run on b563ba4 was cancelled by the owner in favour of this cycle. Run after the cycle closes.","created_at":"2026-08-28T20:03:32","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:47:35","priority":2,"tags":["handoff","pcb","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a0ffc087b96a0b579c93e5bc697","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: inline_footprint.footprint_from_component drops the library's authored reference fp_text, so a FULL-geometry board (pads key) compiles its designator from the derived rule while the panel resolves the library's authored anchor — same part, two places (SW2: −4.445 vs −3.625)","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T1. pcb/worker/pcb_worker/inline_footprint.py:80-99 footprint_from_component never sets reference_text, so a board carrying FULL inline geometry (a `pads` key — every panel-saved board) compiles to a FootprintDefinition with no authored anchor and the emitter uses the derived rule. The panel meanwhile re-resolves the same part from the LIBRARY, whose .kicad_mod authors its reference (SW2's SW_EVP-ASAC1A: (−0.635, −4.445)). Both are clear of the body after T1, but the screen and the fab disagree on where the designator is. This is also why the owner saw \"SW2\" at −1.5 on Smart Remote v2 before T1 — the inline path, not the library path. Wanted: one source. Options: (a) feed the board dict's refdes_anchor into footprint_from_component when present (cheap; trusts a derived key a stale YAML could carry — T1 made Go drop it at deserialize, so the panel-sent board is fresh, but a hand-edited YAML is not); (b) have footprint_from_component look the ref up in the library for reference_text only (the pads stay the board's authority per the FULL rule). Oracle: for SW2 on a FULL board, compile_board's designator strokes == the panel's refdes_anchor strokes byte-for-byte; the kicad export's reference placement agrees too (see the F.Fab constant in kicad.py:690, which still hard-codes (0, −1.5) and should read the same anchor).","created_at":"2026-08-28T20:29:16","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:17:49","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["follow-up","pcb","refdes","silk","task-cycle-9"],"resolution":"Subsumed by TC9 T2b 1b04bed: an authored refdes_placement outranks both paths, so screen and fab agree wherever a placement is set; a FULL-geometry part still cannot inherit its library's fp_text (that arm never reads the library, by design — documented in inline_footprint.py and board-yaml.md)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a100ef177f3a31c5ee6ed3ac300","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb gerber goldens re-blessed by task-cycle 9 T1 (4 × F_SilkS: board, coupon_jlc1, quadlayer, oracle/golden_emitter board) — the README's second-witness (gerbv/KiCad viewer) check and re-bless note are OWED","description":"T1 (derived designator anchor) necessarily moved silk; fold 2 (Codex: occupied extent is INK — stroke half-width + arc sweep) moved it again by 0.025 mm (half a 0.05 mm courtyard stroke). Both re-blesses were hand-diffed: every changed line a bare X…Y…D01/D02 coordinate inside designator hunks; no aperture, copper, mask, paste, drill, edge or gbrjob byte moved. Files: gerber_golden/{board,coupon_jlc1,quadlayer}-F_SilkS.gbr (both times) and oracle/golden_emitter/board-F_SilkS.gbr (T1 only; check whether fold 2 leaves the oracle golden stale — the scoped run's tests/oracle will say). pcb/worker/tests/testdata/gerber_golden/README.md requires a gerbv + KiCad-viewer second witness for quadlayer and a re-bless note for coupon_jlc1; neither could be done headless. To do with a display: open each F_SilkS in gerbv and the KiCad gerber viewer, confirm every designator reads correctly and sits clear of its part, then add the re-bless note to the README.","created_at":"2026-08-28T20:29:21","updated_at":"2026-08-28T23:57:26","priority":3,"tags":["follow-up","goldens","pcb","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a2af8b67c469d53a35e14934d6a","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb test fixture coupon_jlc1.yaml: J1's authored designator dips 0.098 mm into FID1's courtyard — the new gc9_silk_under_part row flags a real defect in the shipped coupon (suggestion = the derived N slot)","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T2 while authoring test_silk_placement.py: on pcb/worker/tests/testdata/coupon_jlc1.yaml the new placement check reports J1's designator 0.098 mm inside FID1's courtyard envelope; the suggestion is the derived N slot, i.e. J1's footprint authors a reference position worse than the default. The fixture was left alone (a fixture edit would change goldens). Owner glance wanted: either accept the row as the coupon's known advisory (pin it in test_hitl/coupon baselines) or move J1's designator in the coupon source and re-bless the F_SilkS golden together with 01a04a100ef1.","created_at":"2026-08-28T20:58:45","updated_at":"2026-08-28T20:58:45","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["fixture","follow-up","pcb","silk","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a4c3c3b719d9fdc06cdf9f55ef7","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb panel ratsnest: every board edit drops the adopted pour fill until the debounced worker round-trip lands, so a plane net flickers from 1 open join to ~28 (quieted) on SR2 GND; orphaned copper carrying no pad (the trace and via a part left behind) is invisible on the canvas; quieted-net markers are a 4 px dark-grey arc","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T3 while measuring bug 01a049ebe81c on the real Smart Remote v2 board. Three related, none fixed: (1) PCBPanel.gd:~6822 _refresh_zone_fill drops the adopted fill on every mutation and re-fetches debounced; pcb_ratsnest.gd:~239 credits a pour only through an adopted fill. Measured: SR2 GND = 2 islands with the fill, 29 without — so after any edit the net briefly (permanently, if the fetch fails) reads \"28 joins left, quieted\". Deliberate per the \"an unproven join stays an airwire\" doctrine, but the flicker is large and user-visible; keep the previous fill until the new one lands, or mark it stale rather than dropping it. (2) pcb_ratsnest.gd:~423 _islands drops copper that reaches no pad, so the trace and GND via a rotated part leaves behind — exactly what the verb reported under dangling_copper — never show on the canvas; T3 made the pad island visible, not the orphan copper. (3) pcb_canvas.gd:~3212 draws a quieted-net marker as a 4 px hollow arc, 1.5 px, net colour at 60% alpha; GND's colour lifts to dark grey on the dark green board. Oracle: after a part edit on a board with an adopted GND fill the drawn GND join count does not spike; a left-behind trace end is drawn/marked as orphan copper; the marker is legible at 1x on the default palette (contrast measured like the fab-preview inks).","created_at":"2026-08-28T21:35:05","updated_at":"2026-08-28T21:35:05","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["follow-up","pcb","ratsnest","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a7268627bb5bd10e4f2b02193f1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb canvas (normal view): drill holes are painted the exact canvas background colour (drill_hole_color 0.08 grey \"match background\"), so a hole is indistinguishable from the ground — the same legibility complaint the fab preview FnF fixed, one view over","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T5 while recolouring the fab preview: pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd ~line 1110 `var drill_hole_color := Color(0.08, 0.08, 0.08, 1.0) # Drill holes (match background)`. In the normal board view a drill is deliberately the ground colour, i.e. invisible except as a gap in copper. Possibly what the owner read as \"drills render black\". Decide with the owner: keep (a hole IS the absence of board) or give drills a rimmed ink as the preview now does (PcbFabPreview.RIM / RIMMED_KEYS is reusable). Oracle if changed: a bare drill on empty laminate is visible at 1x in the default palette, contrast measured as in test_fab_preview_legibility.gd.","created_at":"2026-08-28T22:16:46","updated_at":"2026-08-28T22:16:46","priority":3,"severity":4,"tags":["Fnf","canvas","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a825a1d77e3b6cf8f0ecef8fbed","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb canvas: _component_visibility keys on the mount side only — closing the TOP eye hides a top-mounted part entirely, including a land it declares on B.Cu whose layer is still visible (the mirror of the SMD_LANDS gate fixed in TC9 T6)","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T6 (commit 4c6905c). pcb/ui/pcb_canvas.gd ~line 3390 _component_visibility decides a whole part's visibility from its mount side, so a top-mounted part with a B.Cu land (a mixed-layer footprint, or a THT part) disappears entirely when the top eye closes, even though its B.Cu land's layer is visible. T6 fixed the same class one level down (SMD_LANDS pass now honours _layer_visible) and dropped the test arm that would have asserted this. Also noted, untouched: pcb_component.gd _graphics_layer_bounds compares the AUTHORED layer, so a footprint authoring only B.CrtYd derives no bounds; a stray doc line at pcb_canvas.gd ~3789 above set_mask_view describes _draw_component_graphics_layer. Oracle: with the top eye closed, a top-mounted part's B.Cu land is still drawn while its F.Cu land and body are not (recording canvas, test_canvas_render_truth.gd).","created_at":"2026-08-28T22:34:11","updated_at":"2026-08-28T22:34:11","priority":3,"severity":4,"tags":["canvas","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04a93a8d975358ca3195455aec048","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb kicad export, loose-dict path: footprint SILK graphics are emitted footprint-local with no bottom-side mirror (the analogue of the pad-position bug fixed in TC9 T7), and a bottom pad with a non-zero local rotation keeps its unmirrored orientation","description":"Found by task-cycle 9 T7. (1) pcb/worker/pcb_worker/kicad.py ~line 844 _footprint_graphics emits a footprint's silk/courtyard graphics local with no mirror on the loose-dict path, while _localize_graphic_points bakes the mirror in on the IR path — so a bottom part's outline in the .kicad_pcb sits where its top-side twin's would, exactly the pad bug T7 fixed one class over. (2) kicad.py ~199-224 _pad_at emits PadGeom.rotation (footprint-LOCAL) as KiCad's ABSOLUTE pad angle on the legacy resolve-dict path (already a filed follow-up); T7's mirror deliberately does not negate it, so a bottom pad with a non-zero local rotation still carries the unmirrored orientation. (3) pcb_data.gd set_board_size journals resize_board unconditionally with no no-op guard, unlike set_board_layers. Oracle: extend test_placement_parity's kicad arm to graphics (emitted .kicad_pcb silk points re-read through the footprint's (at) equal gerber._harvest_component_graphics for a bottom part) and to a rotated-pad bottom part.","created_at":"2026-08-28T22:53:06","updated_at":"2026-08-28T22:53:06","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["bottom-side","follow-up","kicad","pcb","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04aa4720c71b88c0de8f19a8e5ad0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb god-file census after task-cycle 9: pcb_canvas.gd ~12.8k (+444 this cycle: hover-card wiring, _draw_staged_lands, _draw_silk_faces ≈180 lines of logic), PCBPanel.gd ~7.6k (+225), panel_tools.gd ~11.7k (+68) — move the three new canvas blocks into modules and widen the split item","description":"Review B measured the batch deltas: pcb_canvas.gd +444 lines, PCBPanel.gd +225, panel_tools.gd +68 (864a99c..01f1fd0). The hover card's content/layout/paint and the refdes verb went into modules as required, but ~180 lines of new canvas LOGIC landed in pcb_canvas.gd: the _hover_card_* state machine (target/content/suppression), _draw_staged_lands, _draw_silk_faces. Wanted: a pcb_canvas_hover.gd (or fold into pcb_hover_card.gd as a controller) and a pcb_canvas_artwork.gd for the two draw helpers, leaving the canvas with call sites only; same treatment for PCBPanel's fab-preview token bookkeeping (_fab_preview_board_token/_drop_fab_preview). Relates to the standing split item 01a03c07a23a and the census 01a042fe0703. Oracle: recording-canvas suites (test_canvas_render_truth, test_pcb_hover_card) unchanged and green after the move; pcb_canvas.gd line count below its pre-cycle 12372.","created_at":"2026-08-28T23:11:26","updated_at":"2026-08-28T23:11:26","priority":3,"tags":["follow-up","god-file","pcb","refactor","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ae012dc7a91acdb002cbc6ce4b0","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb panel: selecting a pad narrows the canvas by ~52 px mid-gesture (a sidebar section appears), shifting the board under the cursor on the very click that selected it; and check_libraries vs footprint_report disagree on whether ref R_0805 resolves","description":"Found by task-cycle 9's scoped-run fix loop. (1) PCBPanel.gd sidebar: the first pad selection shows the pad-selection section, the canvas goes 738 → 686 px while zoom/pan stay put, so world→screen mapping shifts under the pointer on that same click (two suites had cached the empty-canvas point across it and clicked the sidebar). Wanted: the sidebar's width must not change on selection (reserve the section's space, or keep the canvas rect stable and let the sidebar scroll), so a click never moves the board it targets. Oracle: canvas.size before and after the first pad selection is equal (GD, test_pcb_pin_select). (2) pcb/main_test.go:~719 minerva_pcb_footprint_report resolves ref \"R_0805\" from the shipped seed while minerva_pcb_check_libraries reports it missing and suggests R_0805_2012Metric — two library-resolution surfaces disagree about one ref; make both answer from one resolver.","created_at":"2026-08-29T00:16:34","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:16:34","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["follow-up","panel","pcb","task-cycle-9","ux"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04af027eb725ea447f5e759f3e74c","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Scoped test set: when a batch adds a value to a declared set (DRC row type, advisory family, counts key, reply key), grep every test tree for the SET's existing members — those suites are direct consumers and belong in the scope","created_at":"2026-08-29T00:34:07","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:34:07","tags":["pcb","scoped-run","task-cycle","testing"],"value":"Task-cycle 9 (2026-08-29): the scoped run was green (pytest 786, GD 31/31) but the deferred full suite went red in three places the scope missed — tests/test_gc9_silk_dfm.py (hand-listed GC9_TYPES), tests/test_candidate_overlay.py (per-key counts equality) and two GD suites (test_trace_pour_terminator, test_export_yaml_verb) — all direct consumers of a set the batch grew (gc9_* advisory types / counts keys; ratsnest island rule; status-bar text). Rule: before naming the scoped set, take every NEW enum value / row type / counts key / reply key the batch adds, grep for an EXISTING sibling value across pcb/worker/tests and pcb/tests/gd (e.g. grep -rl gc9_silk_width tests/), and add every hit. Also add any suite that asserts on status-bar text or ratsnest links when the panel's status composition or pcb_ratsnest.gd changed. Cheaper than a full-suite round trip.","component":"pcb","key":"scoped-run-grep-new-enum-values-across-tests","confidence":"high","retrieval_count":1,"research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04afcd68d7bc297e6c0aa984fb1b0","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Run a pcb GD suite against an arbitrary git tree (in-batch vs pre-existing bisect) without touching the working tree: git archive the base + a symlink farm of the Minerva project","created_at":"2026-08-29T00:47:59","updated_at":"2026-08-29T00:47:59","tags":["bisect","gd-tests","pcb","task-cycle"],"value":"TC9 (2026-08-29) pattern: `git -C ~/github/minerva-plugins archive <sha> | tar -x -C <scratch>/base` gives the base pcb tree; for MODEL-only suites a minimal Godot project whose res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb symlinks into that extraction is enough; for PANEL suites make a symlink farm of ~/github/Minerva/src (every top-level entry symlinked) beside a `minerva-plugins` symlink pointing at the extraction so the suite's `res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/...` preloads resolve, then `godot --headless --path <farm> --script res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/tests/gd/<suite>.gd`. Minerva must be DOWN. The batch's worker/.venv serves pytest at base too (git archive the worker sources, keep the venv). Proved two \"in-batch\" attributions false in one cycle (test_trace_pour_terminator since 579f8ab; test_export_yaml_verb since 754dda9). Harnesses left at scratchpad tc9baserun / tc9basefull (session-scoped; recreate from this recipe).","component":"pcb","key":"gd-suite-at-base-tree-harness","confidence":"high","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04b85e62079198f6366db6b0d1acd","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb hover card: Options toggle (per-user pref, default on), +5 px pointer clearance, and a pad click in the pin inspector raises the card even when the toggle is off","description":"Owner review of DCR 01a049e18477 (2026-08-28 evening): \"partial pass, incomplete\". Three concrete gaps:\n\n1. OPTION. The card must be switchable from the Options menu — a per-user preference (like the snap toggles), default ON, readable and writable through minerva_pcb_board_rules / the preference verbs like every other Options row. Off means no hover card anywhere.\n\n2. OFFSET. The card sits too close to the pointer: raise PcbHoverCard.GAP_PX from 14 to 19 (~5 px more clearance). The \"never covers the hovered point\" rule is unchanged.\n\n3. CLICK IN THE PIN INSPECTOR. With the option OFF, clicking a pad with the pin inspector armed still raises that pad's card, anchored at the click, and it stays until the next inspector click, mode exit, a clear (board change, gesture) or the option being turned on. Hover alone raises nothing while off. With the option ON, behaviour is unchanged (hover already shows the pad).\n\nOracle: test_pcb_hover_card — a motion over a part with hover_card=false raises no card; an inspector click on a pad with hover_card=false raises the pad card at the click; rect_for's anchor clearance ≥ 19 px; Options menu read_state carries the key and apply flips it.","created_at":"2026-08-29T03:17:41","updated_at":"2026-08-29T03:21:34","priority":2,"tags":["follow-up","hitl","hover-card","pcb","task-cycle-9","ux"],"resolution":"d999969 — toggle, 19 px clearance, inspector click-to-show; suite authored, unrun (editor open); owner re-HITL pending."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04b860e7c74b3b05e0bab8675dfd7","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"pcb sidebar: the \"Properties\" area is still there after the hover card — dissolve it, rename it, or keep it?","description":"Owner review of DCR 01a049e18477: \"The 'Properties' area still in the pane tools\" listed as an incompleteness. The DCR's own ruling kept the editing controls (fabrication stage, zone Net/Layer, trace Width, via Net/Size/Drill, group Offset X/Y) \"exactly where they are\", so what survives today is a collapsible section titled \"Properties\" holding ONLY those editors plus three small read-outs (group \"N parts, anchor\", zone kind, via position). The collapse state is also load-bearing for the medium layout tier and the \"Set trace width…\" flow (test_pcb_panel_ui, test_pcb_canvas_input_probe, test_pcb_panel_layout §7, test_via_edit_and_fab_stage §7).\n\nOptions:\n (a) DISSOLVE — no header; the fabrication row becomes a board-level control near the top, the selection editors sit in a headerless block shown only when something editable is selected; the three read-outs move onto the hover card (zone and via get cards). Biggest change, four suites re-pinned.\n (b) RENAME — keep the section and its collapse, title it for what it now is (e.g. \"Edit\"), drop the three read-outs onto the card.\n (c) KEEP — the section is fine as ruled in the DCR; close as no-change.\n\nNot done in the same pass as 01a04b85e620 because the readings lead to materially different work. Owner to pick.","created_at":"2026-08-29T03:17:51","updated_at":"2026-08-29T03:42:33","priority":2,"tags":["follow-up","hitl","hover-card","pcb","properties","task-cycle-9","ux"],"resolution":"Owner: shrink, not remove — header goes, fabrication stage stays implicit, selection editors + Pin Selection section become MCP-only, read-outs to hover cards. Work item 01a04b9c9064."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04b9c90647c8fbe505e37115a5187","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb sidebar shrink: Properties header gone (fabrication stage stays, implicit), selection editors + Pin Selection section removed (MCP-only), group/zone/via read-outs move to hover cards, Pin Select click keeps the pad card up","description":"Owner ruling 2026-08-28 (re-HITL of DCR 01a049e18477, supersedes question 01a04b860e7c): a SHRINK, not a remove.\n\nKEEP, implicit: the fabrication-stage picker stays in the sidebar where the Properties section was, with NO \"Properties\" header and no collapse — it is just a row.\n\nREMOVE from the sidebar (these become MCP-only; the verbs are unchanged): trace Width (minerva_pcb_set_trace_width), via Net/Size/Drill (minerva_pcb_update_via), zone Net/Layer (set_zone_net / set_zone_layer), group Offset X/Y (set_group_member_offset), and the whole Pin Selection section — pad rows, free-pins side chooser, Move net to… and Swap nets (minerva_pcb_move_net / swap_nets). Owner: \"I don't expect humans to do this activity, only LLMs.\"\n\nMOVE to the hover card (same derivations the read verbs use, no new rule): component card gains a Group line when grouped; NEW zone card (kind, net, layer) and via card (position, net, size, drill) — _hover_card_target already resolves KIND_ZONE / KIND_VIA and today gives them no card.\n\nPIN SELECT: while armed, the card follows the pointer when the hover-card toggle is on; a click keeps the CLICKED pad's card up regardless of the toggle until the next click or the tool exits. No sidebar section grows on a pad selection.\n\nOracle: PCBPanel has no PropertiesSection/PropertiesHeader/PropertiesBody and no PinSelectionSection; FabricationRow still present and still commits; hovering a zone/via/grouped part gives the card lines above, equal to describe_zone / list_vias / describe_component; Pin Select click leaves the pad card up after the pointer moves off. Suites re-pinned: test_pcb_panel_layout §7, test_pcb_panel_ui (Set trace width… flow), test_pcb_canvas_input_probe (collapsed-Properties case), test_via_edit_and_fab_stage §7, test_pcb_pin_select, test_pcb_pin_inspector, test_pcb_hover_card.","created_at":"2026-08-29T03:42:26","updated_at":"2026-08-29T04:42:43","priority":2,"tags":["follow-up","hitl","hover-card","pcb","properties","task-cycle-9","ux"],"resolution":"647717d — shrink landed; suites authored, unrun; owner re-HITL pending."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04c1a3ff17edbb2a145a05f512be0","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF 2026-08-28 (Mac evening HITL): run the authored-only suites before DCR 01a049e18477 ships — plugins main 6f752d2..eebd655 + Minerva development d499240f..8465ec25","description":"Everything below is PUSHED and owner-HITL'd on the Mac; NONE of the re-pinned/new GD suites were run (Godot editor + Minerva were open all evening). This chore is the resume point on any machine.\n\nSTATE\n- minerva-plugins main @ eebd655 (== origin). Since TC9 close (40d9d02): hover-card toggle/offset/click (d999969), sidebar shrink (647717d), fabrication read-out (4be5ba6), sidebar width/dividers (1de9073, a5a95fc, fd4e3e6), dock-pane height (e58aec3), unused local (eebd655). Owner HITL: all pass.\n- Minerva development @ 8465ec25 (== origin). Since 81af9d5f: dialog guards (86181deb, be88f0b3, a4d6509e), broker unregister on tab close, drive deserialize warning (d499240f), Project→Save clears plugin tabs (d499240f, option a), dock rows show words only (7a9ccd4f), adopt_editor for restored tabs (561e4e80), UiScaleSync (2ab9a9b7). Owner HITL: pass.\n- DCR 01a049e18477 stays REVIEWING until the suites are green; then ship.\n\nRESUME STEPS (Minerva DOWN, Godot editor closed):\n1. plugins: pcb/scripts/run-gd-tests.sh <minerva-checkout> — at minimum test_pcb_panel_layout (156), test_pcb_panel_ui (230), test_pcb_canvas_input_probe (121), test_via_edit_and_fab_stage (81), test_pcb_hover_card (110), test_pcb_pin_inspector (80), test_pcb_pin_select; EXPECTED_SUITES counts were hand-counted — fix the manifest if a count is off, fix the code if a check is red. Known risk: hover_card §7 zone hover fixture (moved to top layer), §6 pinned-card rule.\n2. Minerva: scripts/run-functional-tests.sh (test_ui_scale_sync added to HERMETIC_TESTS) + test/annotations_v2/* + plugin-host/broker suites.\n3. Deferred full suite is OVERDUE again for the plugins range 864a99c..eebd655 (last green 01777cc per 40d9d02).\n4. Then transition DCR 01a049e18477 → shipped; close this chore.\n\nOPEN/DEFERRED (owner calls): Minerva 01a04bf11489 tab restore ⇧⌘T + re-mount (backlog); 01a04c04dc5a project-load busy indicator (deferred); \"Activity tab reads dirty\" not filed by choice; plugins 01a04a100ef1 gerber second-witness (needs display), 01a04aa4720c god-file census. Smart Remote v2 pane declares `routed` (in the project, not yet exported to YAML — export is a deliberate step).","created_at":"2026-08-29T05:59:43","updated_at":"2026-08-29T06:40:49","priority":1,"tags":["handoff","hitl-2026-08-28","task-cycle-9"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04c6a8405743a8de432a33e0e8add","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb release: a main push with a NEW manifest version releases itself — a manual tag is only for re-firing, and then it must be annotated","created_at":"2026-08-29T07:27:24","updated_at":"2026-08-29T19:21:50","tags":["ci","pcb","release"],"value":"pcb.yml's `release` job (needs: package) runs on every main push and publishes release pcb-v<manifest version> with all four tarballs when that version has no release yet — 0.5.4 (2026-08-29) was cut by the bump commit's own run, no manual tag needed. Recipe: bump pcb/manifest.json + pcb/main.go serverVersion in lockstep → push main → wait for the pcb workflow (registry-check is red on the bump commit by design) → `git fetch --tags` → `python3 scripts/regen_registry.py` → commit registry.json → push. Only when you must (re)fire the workflow by hand: `git tag -a pcb-vX.Y.Z -m ... && git push origin pcb-vX.Y.Z` — a LIGHTWEIGHT tag does not trigger pcb.yml (0.5.3 lesson). The package job's real-bundle step (pcb.fab_preview through the packed tarball) is the gate that proves the marketplace binary's worker actually runs on each OS.","component":"release","key":"tag-must-be-annotated","confidence":"high","research_cost":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ec47edc7a29b15b67c90958fb34","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: board-level bottom-silk art parked in TP1's graphics (ccsandbox 65426d6) drags TP1's DERIVED designator 11.7 mm off the part — the fab prints \"TP1\" inside J2's courtyard","description":"RCA (HITL 2026-08-29, pcb 0.5.3). Why is TP1's designator reported inside J2's courtyard? → compile_board derives TP1's refdes anchor at footprint-local (12.85, -1.6) (worker refdes_anchor.default_anchor: centre_x of the occupied extent, above its top edge). → Why is the extent centre 12.85 mm east of the pads? → TP1's `graphics` hold 65 B.SilkS polys spanning local x -3..28.7, y 38..65 (plus its 1 F.CrtYd square) — OUTLINE_LAYERS includes B.SilkS, so they are unioned into the \"body\". → Why does a 1206 test point carry 65 polys? → ccsandbox smart-remote-v2 commit 65426d6 deliberately attached the board's bottom-silk stroke-font text (© line, board name, mirrored) and the peace-sign icon to TP1's graphics because the board has no board-level silk carrier the fab path emits. → Why isn't it visible on the canvas? → the panel draws EVERY designator at the constant default (0, -1.5) — sibling bug 01a04ec8481b — so the canvas shows \"TP1\" beside the pads while Gerber/DRC print it at x+12.85. The DRC row is CORRECT about what the fab would print; the canvas is the liar. Proof: worker `.venv/bin/python` compile_board on the live export → ResolvedComponent TP1 refdes.position = (12.85, -1.6); minerva_pcb_set_refdes read TP1 → anchor (0,-1.5), bounds beside the part. Fix options (owner call): (a) move the parked art to board-level silk (minerva_pcb_add_silk_text / add_graphic) and strip it from TP1; (b) author refdes_placement for TP1 via set_refdes {x_mm:0, y_mm:-1.6} so authored beats derived; (c) both. The derivation itself is per spec — footprint graphics are supposed to describe the part. Expected: TP1's designator prints beside TP1 on the Gerber and the canvas agrees; board-level art has a board-level home.","created_at":"2026-08-29T18:24:55","updated_at":"2026-08-30T05:17:09","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["gc9","hitl","pcb","rca","refdes","silk","smart-remote-v2"],"resolution":"Owner chose option (a) by rubric. Via MCP on the live board: the 65 parked B.SilkS polygons were re-homed as BOARD-owned graphics (minerva_pcb_add_graphic, one call/one undo step, same strokes in board frame = local + (8,30), bounds x 5–37 / y 68–95, width 0.15); TP1 deleted and re-added by library ref Fuse:Fuse_1206_3216Metric at (8,30) rot 0 value 'GND test point'; TP1.1/TP1.2 reconnected to GND (no traces touched TP1). Result: set_refdes TP1 anchor (0,-1.48) above its own pads; geometric DRC verdict clean, the TP1 gc9_silk_under_part row is gone (8→7 remaining rows are the C16/R4/R5, JP2/C3, C13 and C4/C12 items). Kept as raw strokes rather than `text` entries deliberately: the strings were never recorded (only their glyph polygons) and the © glyph is outside the board font — converting to text is a follow-up if the owner supplies the strings. Board is dirty in the editor pending the owner's save + ccsandbox commit.","repro_steps":"1. Load smart-remote-v2 in the PCB editor. 2. minerva_pcb_board_drc {editor_name, geometric:true}. 3. Find the gc9_silk_under_part row with ref TP1 → witness (19.68,27.40), suggestion y_mm 66.32. 4. Compare with minerva_pcb_set_refdes {component_id:\"TP1\"} read (bounds in board frame) and the canvas."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ec8481b792bad76906cb6a681d1","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: canvas designators disagree with the fab — (A) a board opened through the host document path never gets a derived refdes_anchor (constant default for every part); (B) resolve derives a FULL-geometry part's anchor from the LIBRARY footprint while compile_board derives it from the board's own pads+graphics (TP1: −1.48 vs 12.85)","description":"Owner-confirmed 2026-08-29 with pcb 0.5.4: fab preview prints TP1's label ~12 mm east of the part (inside J2's courtyard); the main view draws it beside the pads. The fab is what the Gerber says; the canvas is wrong. TWO mechanisms, both measured. (A) WHOLE-BOARD: ui/PCBPanel.gd:6184 _on_panel_load_request (the host's document open / project restore path — how the owner's tab got its board) calls _data.from_board_dict(doc) DIRECTLY: no pcb.deserialize, no worker resolve, no enrichment. The saved board dict carries no refdes_anchor (it is a derived key; to_saved_board_dict strips session keys; the YAML has 0 of them), so pcb_component.gd from_board_dict → _adopt_derived_anchor({}) → every designator renders at REFDES_DEFAULT (0,−1.5) (pcb_component.gd:672-679). Only load_board_from_yaml (PCBPanel.gd:5062, the YAML import verb) goes through pcb.deserialize → attachFootprintGraphics → resolve_best_effort → adoptResolvedGeometry (internal/tools/project.go:470) and would attach anchors. Measured: set_refdes read on J2/U3/C16/TP1 all return (0,−1.5); resolve_best_effort on the same board returns J2 −5.45, C16 −1.33, TP1 −1.48 — the panel never received them. (Earlier note blaming a 'HEADER' refusal was wrong: plain `resolve` refuses on TP2's HEADER ref, but the load path uses resolve_best_effort, which succeeds.) (B) TP1-SPECIFIC, survives fixing A: worker/pcb_worker/resolve.py:286-334 resolves a component by LIBRARY ref even when the board carries a `pads` key, and computes comp[\"refdes_anchor\"] = _refdes_anchor(comp, parsed) from the LIBRARY footprint's parse (Fuse_1206 outline → y −1.48), whereas compile_board's FULL arm never reads the library and derives the anchor from the board's OWN pads+graphics (TP1's 65 parked B.SilkS polys → centre_x 12.85, bug 01a04ec47edc). Same designator, two derivations, two answers — the geometry-authority rule ('pads' key = board is the authority) is honoured by compile_board and not by resolve's anchor. FIX SHAPE (implementer's call): one derivation, owned by the board's own geometry when it carries `pads` (resolve's anchor arm must go through the same FULL/PARTIAL split as its pad arm), and the panel must obtain it on EVERY load path — either the host document path also runs the enrichment, or the panel derives the anchor itself from the component's pads+graphics through the shared rule (pcb_refdes_anchor.gd mirrors refdes_anchor.py). Oracle: open smart-remote-v2 through the host (project restore), set_refdes read J2 → −5.45 and TP1 → (12.85, −1.6) exactly as compile_board resolves them; canvas label positions equal fab preview positions for all 47 parts.","created_at":"2026-08-29T18:29:03","updated_at":"2026-08-29T22:47:26","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["canvas-vs-fab","hitl","load-path","pcb","rca","refdes","resolve","silk"],"resolution":"One derivation, delivered on every load path. Worker resolve stamps a FULL-geometry part's refdes_anchor from its own inline definition (refdes_anchor.anchor_dict_from_definition — compile_board's route) before the library lookup; Go pcb.deserialize accepts the live board dict (inline + verified JSON snapshot); panel _on_panel_load_request → _adopt_worker_anchors (whole board, whole-board token guard with bounded retry, restore gate, PCBData.adopt_derived_anchors). Measured on smart-remote-v2: resolve vs compile anchors 0/47 mismatches (TP1 = (12.85,-1.6) on both). Reviews: Fable ×2 (must_fix folded → approve_with_notes), Codex ×3 (must_fix folded → approve → approve). Tests executed: pytest test_refdes_anchor 11/11 + scoped 105/105; GD test_refdes_anchor_document_load 12/12 real_worker_used=true (first execution found the wire-trim gap → bug 01a04fb36897); 5 deserialize-consumer GD suites at pinned counts; go test ./internal/tools. Commit on minerva-plugins main.","repro_steps":"1. Load smart-remote-v2 board file in the PCB editor. 2. minerva_pcb_set_refdes {editor_name, component_id:\"J2\"} → anchor y_mm -1.5. 3. minerva_pcb_export_yaml, then worker: compile_board(yaml) → J2 refdes.position y = -5.45. 4. minerva_pcb_board_drc geometric: witnesses match step 3, not step 2."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ed115c372b7b483ed5729c7ad58","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: fab preview has NEVER worked from a marketplace install — \"worker error (python) — No module named 'gerbonara'\"; the runtime bundle bans the library fab_preview renders with","description":"HITL 2026-08-29, pcb 0.5.3 marketplace install on the Minerva CI build: the Fab Preview button toasts \"PCB plugin [pcb.fab_preview]: worker error (python) — No module named 'gerbonara'\" (godot.log host.notify). RCA: (1) worker methods.py:848 `_fab_preview` lazily imports `gerbonara` (ExcellonFile, GerberFile) — by design the preview \"renders the emitted bytes with a different library than the one that wrote them\". (2) gerbonara is declared ONLY in pyproject `[dev]` extras, and scripts/runtime-bundle.lock PIP_PKGS = pyyaml, gerber-writer, pyclipper with an explicit rule that \"dev extras (pytest, pygerber, gerbonara) must NEVER enter the bundle: … shipping the oracle beside the emitter would let a packaging mistake grade its own homework\". So the feature's dependency and the packaging policy contradict each other, and the bundle wins. (3) It was never caught because the dev-lane pcb-plugin binary runs the worker out of pcb/worker/.venv (dev extras installed) — every GD suite, pytest and prior HITL exercised fab preview there — and scripts/smoke/mcp_smoke.py (the only thing that runs the REAL bundle) never calls fab_preview. Verified: runtime/0.5.3/bin/python → ModuleNotFoundError gerbonara; site-packages = agent_router gerber_writer pcb_worker pip pyclipper yaml; the local dev worker renders all 11 layers fine on the same board. Since: b2bde31 (2026-08-13) — every release pcb-v0.3.1..0.5.3 shipped it broken. Same trap family as the TC7 stale-runtime-bundle note. Fix (implementer's call, but recommendation): gerbonara is a pure-Python reader; promote it to a runtime dependency for the PREVIEW (the bundle-lock rule was about the CAM oracle TESTS, which stay dev-only — a reader used to draw pixels is not the oracle grading the emitter), pin it in runtime-bundle.lock, and add a fab_preview call to mcp_smoke.py so the bundle smoke fails when a preview import is missing. Alternative: render the preview from the IR instead of re-parsing the bytes — but that discards the whole \"what the fab receives\" guarantee.","created_at":"2026-08-29T18:38:40","updated_at":"2026-08-29T19:21:08","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["fab-preview","hitl","packaging","pcb","rca","runtime-bundle"],"resolution":"gerbonara==1.6.3 is a runtime dependency; runtime-bundle.lock ships it via the new PIP_NO_DEPS_PKGS tier (builder scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh), LAYER1_IMPORTS probes it, and pcb.yml's package job drives pcb.fab_preview through the PACKED tarball layout on all four targets (scripts/smoke/fab-preview-board.yaml, 9 layers). Commits d6e7633, eaf6211, 269f0cc; released as pcb-v0.5.4. Verified: CI package jobs green on linux-x86_64, linux-arm64, macos-universal, windows-x86_64; locally the packed linux binary + fresh data dir renders 9 layers.","repro_steps":"1. Install pcb from the marketplace (any 0.3.1+). 2. Load any board. 3. Press Fab Preview (or minerva_pcb_pcb_fab_preview). 4. Toast \"worker error (python) — No module named 'gerbonara'\". Dev-lane binary (pcb/worker/.venv): works."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ed817687a8cb41feb91ed73a8af","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A worker method that imports something lazily can pass every dev-lane test and fail on every marketplace install","created_at":"2026-08-29T18:46:19","updated_at":"2026-08-29T18:46:19","tags":["gotcha","packaging","pcb","release","runtime-bundle"],"value":"The dev-lane pcb-plugin binary runs the worker out of pcb/worker/.venv (dev extras installed); the marketplace binary runs the embedded PBS bundle built from pcb/scripts/runtime-bundle.lock (PIP_PKGS + PIP_NO_DEPS_PKGS ONLY). A lazy `from X import ...` inside a method body (fab_preview's gerbonara, 2026-08-13..08-29, five releases) is invisible to pytest, GD suites, HITL on the dev lane AND to scripts/smoke/mcp_smoke.py (initialize + tools/list never starts the worker). Rules: (1) any new third-party import in pcb_worker must be in [project.dependencies] AND in the lock's PIP_PKGS / PIP_NO_DEPS_PKGS AND in LAYER1_IMPORTS; (2) the pcb.yml package job now drives pcb.fab_preview through the packed binary (scripts/smoke/fab-preview-board.yaml, FULL-geometry so no library read) — add the method there if a new one takes a new import; (3) to test a bundle candidate locally: ~/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/<ver>/bin/python with PYTHONPATH=<pip --target dir>:pcb/worker and env -i, call pcb_worker.methods._<method> directly. PIP_NO_DEPS_PKGS exists for a wheel whose metadata drags in deps its shipped code path never imports (gerbonara → click/rtree/quart).","component":"pcb","key":"runtime-bundle-deps-are-not-the-dev-venv","confidence":"high","research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ef2bd4e707387b58626dae5fc2d","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb (and any bundle-embedding plugin): Windows marketplace install never runs its embedded runtime — manifest.sha256 written as \"<sha> *./path\" by Git-for-Windows sha256sum, verifier accepts only two-space form, silently falls back to PATH python then dies on chdir <root>\\worker","description":"Found 2026-08-29 by the new real-bundle CI check (pcb.yml package job → pcb.fab_preview through the packed tarball). Windows job: 'bridge.Worker.Start: exec: chdir D:\\a\\...\\pcb\\dist\\pcb-0.5.4-windows-x86_64\\worker: The system cannot find the file specified' — i.e. workerCwd() got a NON-runtime python: PythonPath tier 1 (EnsureRuntime) had failed and fell through to PATH python silently. Proof of cause: extracted the embedded zstd bundle from the RELEASED pcb-0.5.3-windows-x86_64 pcb-plugin.exe (magic at offset 7626208) → its manifest.sha256 has 2913 lines like `54e9d3…5ce *./DLLs/_asyncio.pdb` — Git-for-Windows sha256sum hashes in binary mode and marks the path with \" *\". scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh's `sed 's| \\./| |'` never matched, and shared/runtime/extract.go manifestValid splits on two spaces → \"malformed manifest line\" → EnsureRuntime post-extract verify error → tier 2/3. Consequence: on every Windows marketplace install since the bundle epoch the worker either used whatever python was on PATH with cwd=<install>\\worker (does not exist → exec error) — so NO minerva_pcb_* worker tool has ever worked on a Windows marketplace install; the toast would read 'tool error: bridge.Worker.Start: exec: chdir …'. Linux/macOS use `shasum`/coreutils text mode (two spaces) and are fine. cad shares the builder → same on Windows (verify). Never caught: go test ./internal/pcbruntime only checks the embed/sidecar pair; shared/runtime extract tests build a synthetic manifest in Go; mcp_smoke never starts the worker. Fix: builder canonicalises the line with `sed -E 's|^([0-9a-fA-F]{64}) [ *]\\./|\\1  |'`; manifestValid also accepts the \" *\" form; the real-bundle CI check now covers it on all four targets.","created_at":"2026-08-29T19:15:26","updated_at":"2026-08-29T19:21:28","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["cad","packaging","pcb","rca","release","runtime-bundle","windows"],"resolution":"Builder canonicalises manifest lines (sed -E 's|^([0-9a-fA-F]{64}) [ *]\\./|\\1  |') and shared/runtime/extract.go manifestValid accepts the binary-mode \"<sha> *<path>\" form (unit test TestManifestValid_AcceptsBinaryModeLines). Commit 269f0cc; released as pcb-v0.5.4. Verified by the real-bundle CI check: package (windows-x86_64) green — the packed Windows binary extracted its embedded runtime and rendered the fab preview. cad's Windows bundle shares the builder and is fixed at its next release; not re-released here.","repro_steps":"1. Install pcb from the marketplace on Windows. 2. Call any minerva_pcb_* worker tool (or press Fab Preview). 3. Error 'bridge.Worker.Start: exec: chdir <install>\\worker'. Or: extract the embedded bundle from the shipped .exe and read manifest.sha256 — lines carry \" *./\"."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04ef8761c727e80e9fe3dd68e047e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Git-for-Windows sha256sum writes \"<sha> *path\" — anything that parses hasher output must accept the \"*\" form","created_at":"2026-08-29T19:21:41","updated_at":"2026-08-29T19:21:41","tags":["cad","gotcha","packaging","pcb","runtime-bundle","windows"],"value":"On Windows runners, coreutils sha256sum hashes in binary mode and prints `<sha> *./path` (one space + asterisk), not the POSIX `<sha>  ./path`. This silently broke the runtime bundle's manifest.sha256 for every Windows build until 2026-08-29 (bug 01a04ef2bd4e): the verifier rejected it, PythonPath fell back to a PATH python, and the worker died on chdir <install>\\worker. Now: scripts/build-python-runtime-bundle.sh canonicalises with `sed -E 's|^([0-9a-fA-F]{64}) [ *]\\./|\\1  |'` and shared/runtime/extract.go manifestValid accepts both. To inspect a shipped bundle without Windows: find the zstd magic 28 B5 2F FD in the .exe (python: data.find), decode from that offset with `zstd -d -c` (ignore the trailing-data error), `tar -xf - ./manifest.sha256`. SHA256SUMS in the tarball is produced the same way — check any consumer of it too.","component":"runtime-bundle","key":"windows-sha256sum-binary-mode","confidence":"high","research_cost":6}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a04fb368977958b12a2f9e677ff827","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: canonical_wire_board drops `graphics` for a FULL-geometry part — the worker sees a body the board owns without its silk/courtyard on every wire-trimmed channel","description":"Found 2026-08-29 by the real-worker GD suite test_refdes_anchor_document_load.gd on first execution: sending the panel's board through PanelTools.canonical_wire_board (ui/panel_tools.gd:3908 _WIRE_DROP_COMPONENT_FIELDS includes \"graphics\") strips every component's graphics. That list predates the FULL-vs-PARTIAL contract (a `pads` KEY = the board is the sole geometry authority, the library is never consulted): for a FULL part the worker now measures a body with lands but no courtyard/outline — the derived designator anchor came back (0,-1.025) (pads-only) instead of the fab's (11.185,-1.3), and any worker consumer of silk/courtyard on these channels (pcb.assembly_check, pcb.board_health, drc_geometric over the wire form as tests/gd/test_add_by_library_ref.gd sends it) is judging a part the board never described. PARTIAL parts are unaffected (the worker re-derives them from the library). The designator fix (bug 01a04ec8481b) sidesteps it by sending the whole board like _refresh_zone_fill does. Fix shape: keep `graphics` on the wire when the component carries a `pads` key (mirror the existing `pads` rule: drop only when footprint_resolved says the worker re-derives), and re-prove the wire-weight claim in worker/tests/test_board_health_resolve_first.py against a FULL part. Oracle: a FULL part with a wide silk line sent through canonical_wire_board → worker DRC/resolve reports the silk; today it cannot.","created_at":"2026-08-29T22:45:52","updated_at":"2026-08-29T22:45:52","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["full-geometry","pcb","rca","silk","wire"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05168d6de77d5812f5062d04a6d43","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: promote writes `version: 1` for a board whose entities carry v2 ids — every load re-migrates v1→v2 and mints an id, so the promoted file is not the fixed point of load→promote","description":"Round-trip test 2026-08-30 on smart-remote-v2: minerva_pcb_promote (gate clean) wrote /home/imran/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml with `version: 1` at line 1 and no top-level board id, although the live board is a v2 board (board:fc22594aca79…, trace/via/zone ids, board_graphics, refdes_placement). minerva_pcb_load_board {path} then reports warnings [\"migrated board source v1→v2: minted 1 persistent entity id(s)\"] on EVERY load, and the geometric DRC keeps carrying the `ordinal_ids` static warning (\"trace/via/hole/zone ids are ordinal-derived… NOT stable under reorder/insert\"). The committed HEAD of the file is also version 1, so this is pre-existing, not a regression from today's silk/route work — but promote is the serialize-back verb and should emit the schema the board actually is (v2 with its ids), so that load(promote(board)) is a fixed point and no id is minted on read. Content otherwise round-trips exactly (47 components, 35 nets, 117 traces, 43 vias with ids preserved incl. via:b304c0c1/559e818c, refdes_placement, 65 board_graphics, stage routed, connectivity complete, geometric clean).","created_at":"2026-08-30T06:43:40","updated_at":"2026-08-30T06:43:40","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["pcb","promote","round-trip","schema"],"repro_steps":"1. Load smart-remote-v2 (live board is v2). 2. minerva_pcb_promote {path}. 3. head -1 of the file → `version: 1`. 4. minerva_pcb_load_board {path} → warning \"migrated board source v1→v2: minted 1 persistent entity id(s)\"."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05168fac57f7986c9b242cff790c6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: load_board's board_health preflight reports GND indeterminate — \"canonical board schema requires an integer version 1 or 2 (present)\" — on a board that checks clean one call later","description":"2026-08-30, pcb 0.5.5: minerva_pcb_load_board {path} on smart-remote-v2 returned board_health.complete:null with indeterminate [{net:GND, reason:zone_copper, detail:\"the pour fill could not be computed: canonical board schema requires an integer version 1 or 2 (present)\"}], board_revision 0, preflight.rendered_this_revision false. The very next minerva_pcb_board_drc (connectivity) on the same tab reports complete:true and the geometric DRC is clean with the pour filled (5395.71 mm²). So the load-time health ledger is judging a board dict whose `version` is not an integer — almost certainly the Godot JSON round trip turning 2 into 2.0 (JSON.parse returns floats; see memory project_godot_json_int_to_float) on the load reply before the ledger is computed, while every later channel serialises it as an int. User-visible as a false \"GND pour could not be computed\" in the load reply. Fix: coerce `version` to int on the panel's load path before the health call (or have the worker's schema check accept an integral float), plus a test that load_board's board_health on a clean fixture is complete:true.","created_at":"2026-08-30T06:43:49","updated_at":"2026-09-02T15:06:26","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["board-health","json-float","load","pcb"],"repro_steps":"1. New PCB tab. 2. minerva_pcb_load_board {path: smart-remote-v2 yaml}. 3. Reply board_health.indeterminate names GND with the schema-version detail. 4. minerva_pcb_board_drc {geometric:false} → complete:true."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05189fb8479bfb7143a6a5aa90d69","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb panel adopts the plugin_data note hooks — Save to Note / inject toggle carry a fit-to-board render + caption and restore the board from the note","description":"Goal: a pcb editor tab's Save to Note and chat-inject toggle produce a plugin_data note (Minerva DCR minerva:019df4dc365f, host side done; presentation is the only adopter so far) instead of the host's raw viewport-screenshot fallback, and Edit-from-note reopens the board. The note carries: payload = the board dict (what from_board_dict restores), preview_image = the canvas rendered fit-to-board at a resolution where designators survive, preview_alt_text = a short caption (board name, size, layers, fabrication stage, component/net/trace/via counts, canonical YAML path when adopted). Nothing is computed for the caption — no DRC, no worker call: chat-only mode injects what the user is looking at. No _on_panel_render_for_llm: the host fallback (image + alt text) is the LLM-facing form, so every provider that carries images sees the board and a text-only path still gets the caption. Restore: _on_panel_restore_from_note(payload) -> from_board_dict, true. Regression test (authored, run after review): GD suite — create the note from a loaded board, restore it into a fresh panel model, assert the whole-board token matches; assert the preview is a non-empty image with the board's aspect ratio and the caption names the board. Owner decisions recorded 2026-08-30: image-first ('LLMs always get screenshots'), caption is alt text not a report card, full board dict as payload (path+digest variant is a follow-up if project files bloat).","created_at":"2026-08-30T07:19:52","updated_at":"2026-08-30T08:12:45","priority":2,"tags":["notes","pcb","plugin-host","task-cycle-10"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0518a0c6c733db1907cc48910f460","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"HANDOFF — task-cycle 10 (2026-08-30): pcb notes for chat (T1 Minerva ChatGPT image parts, T2 pcb plugin_data note hooks)","description":"Record of task-cycle 10. Dispatch bases: Minerva development a1cb6455; minerva-plugins main (after docket chore). Tasks: T1 minerva:019df4f5426076 ChatGPT provider emits image parts for Image entries in InjectedNotes (hermetic Format() test); T2 plugins work item (pcb panel plugin_data note hooks; GD create→restore token test). Flow: Opus implementer per task, one commit each, tests authored not run, static gates; batch cold Fable review + folds; Codex serial last; scoped run; push; beep. Comments below are the running record.","created_at":"2026-08-30T07:19:56","updated_at":"2026-08-30T17:59:47","priority":2,"tags":["handoff","minerva","pcb","task-cycle"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","type":"dcr","status":"implementing","title":"DCR: Fab order-package exporter — house-profile BOM/CPL/gerber packages from canonical YAML (JLC first)","description":"# Goal (r2 — reshaped per Codex review, comment 199)\n\nAn **order-release pipeline**: strict-compile the canonical YAML once into a single resolved physical board, then derive every submitted artifact and every preflight claim from that exact object — gerber zip, BOM, CPL, manifest, preflight report, local assembly preview, order checklist — for a selectable house service profile (JLC Economic first), refusing by name when the board is not orderable.\n\nDriving use case: smart-remote-v2 ordered from JLCPCB as Economic PCBA (SMD reflow + THT hand-solder for the DevKit socket rows).\n\n# Owner decisions (settled — do not re-litigate)\n\n1. **UX**: extend the existing \"Export YAML\" button into an exporter dropdown + export button. MCP verb parity required (owner is GUI-only; agents are MCP-only).\n2. **Extensible house profiles**: JLC first; mechanism admits later houses. Surveying other houses is OUT of scope.\n3. **YAML is source of truth, forever.** Part identity lives in the YAML. Inventory drift handled at order time by updating the design (LLM + human); no stock-watch subsystem.\n4. **House-rules advisory checker** validating published house guidelines; advisory-grade — EXCEPT selected-service incompatibilities (e.g. bottom-side population under Economic), which are blockers (r2).\n5. **Variants are new YAML** — just another board.\n6. **IP**: exporter path surfaces any known IP/licence question to the owner; never silently ships it.\n7. Implementation runs as **task-cycle** (not work-cycle). Code work NOT yet approved — planning only.\n\n# Rubric verdicts (7-axis, 2026-08-30; provenance amended r2)\n\n- **BOM spreadsheet**: frozen as human ledger once identity moves to YAML. Not generated, not sync-checked. (Durability.)\n- **Export gates**: hard refusal on facts that make files WRONG; guideline findings advisory; no promote-grade gate (mid-layout quote exports legitimate, labeled honestly). Refusals named per component, visible in export UI. (User-visible.)\n- **Provenance (r2)**: silk carries an immutable design revision `<name> <rev> <digest-8>` — NOT the export month. Digest uses an explicit projection (provenance text normalized to a sentinel before hashing — no fixed-point). Export timestamp lives in the manifest. Packages regenerated from git YAML, not committed. JLC's order-number-on-silk mechanism was DISCONTINUED May 2026 — no order-number spot; their barcode/serial service is a separate opt-in. (Debuggability.)\n\n# Codex review disposition (comment 199, 2026-08-30)\n\nAccepted must-fix: §1 single compilation; §2 schema shape + `assembly: exclude` scalar migration + authored stable expansion refs; §3 DNP paste policy (refuse SMD DNP with paste:auto); §4 digest projection + rev + order-number removal; §6 readiness states; §7 BOM/CPL hard gates; §8 template pinning + semantic (not byte) goldens; §9 assembly anchors + transform fixtures + local preview. Trimmed: §5 minimal service profile (references existing fab-rule profile + BOM/CPL dialect + template pin; exhaustive capability tables grow only as checks need them); §10 no `order_intent` snapshot — goal narrowed to \"all upload artifacts + ORDER-CHECKLIST.md\"; manifest holds the auditable core, not the full wish list. §11 tooling-holes = advisory + checklist line. §12 sequence adopted as the plan's two cycles.\n\n# Design shape (r2)\n\n## Schema (canonical YAML)\nStructured `assembly` block per component (migrating the existing scalar `assembly: exclude`):\n```yaml\nassembly:\n  populate: true            # false = DNP: stays in gerbers, exits both CSVs, logged\n  manufacturer: …\n  mpn: …\n  package: …                # BOM Footprint column\n  comment: …                # BOM Comment column\n  house_parts: {jlcpcb: C41376161}   # portable core; no bare `lcsc`\n  paste: auto | include | exclude    # SMD DNP with auto REFUSES\n  placements:               # synthetic expansion; omitted = one placement, component ref\n    - {ref: U1S_A, offset_mm: {x: 0, y: 0}, rotation_deg: 0}\n    - {ref: U1S_B, offset_mm: {x: 22.86, y: 0}, rotation_deg: 0}\n```\nExpanded refs are authored, stable, globally unique — never exporter-invented. Footprint-lock assembly fields are suggestion/provenance only; board YAML wins (state the precedence rule in docs). Boards without the block still load; assembly EXPORT refuses by name. Promote/deserialize round-trip preserves all fields.\n\n## Worker: one compilation\nExtend the resolved IR with assembly data; BOM, CPL, gerbers, paste decisions, expansion, preview, manifest all derive from the SAME strict compilation. Kill the raw-dict BOM/CPL path (assembly_outputs.py reads raw YAML today while gerbers read ResolvedBoard — two boards, one order). Every physical placement gets a resolved explicit assembly anchor (centroid — KiCad THT origins are pin-1; the board-yaml doc's \"origins are pin 1\" claim is too broad and needs correcting); transform composition (expansion offset × parent rotation × side) is measured with fixtures.\n\n## Hard gates (refuse, named)\nBOM refs == CPL refs after expansion; each designator exactly once, case-fold safe; ≤200 designators per grouped row (group by complete emitted identity, not part number; consider ref-prefix-family splits); metric coordinates; ≥0.2 mm between placement designators; populated part missing identity/package/comment; synthetic without placements; invalid/non-finite transforms; SMD DNP with paste:auto; assembly fields lost in round-trip; export-service vs board fab-rule profile mismatch.\n\n## Service profile (minimal)\n`jlcpcb-economic`: references the existing fab-rule profile (jlcpcb-2layer); assembly tier + sides; BOM/CPL dialect; pinned official template artifacts (URL + fetch date + SHA-256; parsed into semantic fixtures — no byte-equality claims against XLSX); checked / advisory / uncheckable rule lists. Current facts to encode from the capability page, not this DCR: Economic = single-sided, 2/4/6 layers, 0.8–1.6 mm, 2–50 boards. Pricing is informational, never contract.\n\n## Readiness states (honest outcomes)\n1. `package_generated` — files internally consistent. 2. `preflight: pass | advisories | blocked` — selected-service compatibility + known checks. 3. `order_page_verified` — human uploaded and recorded the quote-page result. Never report \"orderable\" because CSV serialization succeeded.\n\n## Package layout (deterministic)\n```\n<board>-<profile>/\n  gerbers.zip        # ONLY gerber/drill/job files (JLC ignores/objects to extras)\n  bom.csv  cpl.csv\n  order-manifest.json  preflight.json\n  assembly-preview.svg  ORDER-CHECKLIST.md\n```\nManifest core: source digest projection + git rev/dirty; SHA-256 per output; profile + template pins + tool versions; logical→physical placement map; DNP list + paste policy; transforms used; check results incl. explicitly-unchecked; timestamp; surfaced IP questions. Checklist carries what gerbers don't encode (qty, finish, color, tier, \"confirm production file\", tooling-hole consent — JLC may ADD tooling holes on Economic).\n\n## Host/GD\nExport dropdown; gates and advisories shown named in-panel; MCP verbs with identical behavior. Local assembly preview shows body outline, emitted ref, claimed centroid, rotation, pin-1/polarity — the pre-payment orientation catch, alongside mandatory JLC placement-preview review (per-part rotation zero follows their tape orientation; no rotation DB — preview/HITL is the boundary, JLC's own guidance).\n\n# Guiding questions still open for the implementer (measure, don't trust)\n\n1. Prove the emitted rotation/Y-sign composition against JLC CCW-positive with an asymmetric-part fixture (Q1 worked example); top AND bottom (JLC stopped mirroring bottom coords Nov 2023).\n2. What anchor does each of OUR footprint sources actually resolve to (library vs FULL-authority YAML parts)? Correct the docs claim.\n3. What do the pinned current JLC templates actually contain (header drift is real: `LCSC Part #` vs `JLCPCB Part #（optional）`)? Semantic goldens from the download, not help-page prose.\n4. Gerber zip recognition: if the only true oracle is a live quote-page upload, say so — that is the owner's HITL step; do not fake a pass.\n5. Which house rules are checkable from board data (component-to-edge — SW2 sits ~2.5 mm from west edge; hole-to-edge; spacing)? Implement the checkable subset, NAME the remainder as unchecked.\n6. Confirm THT emits no paste today and seal with a regression test.\n\n# Acceptance-oracle ladder (r2)\n\n- Schema round-trip: promote/deserialize/load preserves all assembly fields + stable physical refs (real-worker GD test).\n- Unit: normalized BOM/CPL semantic goldens from pinned JLC samples.\n- Consistency: BOM refs == CPL refs; unique/case-safe; ≤200/row.\n- Transform: hand-derived fixtures — top, bottom, asymmetric, rotated, expanded.\n- Paste: populated SMD / DNP+include / DNP+exclude / THT-no-paste.\n- CAM: every gerber/drill layer independently parses; zip content allowlist exact.\n- End-to-end: smart-remote-v2 package from one resolved board, checksums + preview.\n- HITL: JLC quote page recognizes all artifacts; intended parts selected; preview orientation/polarity accepted; tooling-hole/production-file modifications reviewed. Quote-page acceptance proves uploader compatibility, NOT assembly yield — first physical boards are follow-up bench evidence.\n\n# Phasing\n\nTwo task-cycles (plan + tasks are children of this DCR):\n- **Cycle A — resolved board + schema + gates**: assembly schema/migration/round-trip; single-compilation IR; anchors + transforms; BOM/CPL hard gates + paste policy.\n- **Cycle B — profile + package + surfaces**: pinned service profile + semantic goldens; package assembly + manifest + preflight states + digest projection; assembly preview; host dropdown + MCP verbs; smart-remote-v2 end-to-end + owner HITL upload.\n\n# Not in scope\n\nRotation-correction database; panelization; multi-house survey; stock/price watch; variant machinery; fiducial addition (JLC Economic needs none supplied — but tooling-hole advisory stays); spreadsheet codegen; `order_intent` order-form modeling; exhaustive house capability tables beyond what checks consume.\n\n# References\n\nJLC: pick-place-file-for-pcb-assembly; bill-of-materials-for-pcb-assembly; advice-for-bom-and-cpl-files-preparation; pcb-assembly-capabilities; how-to-mark-on-pcb; instructions-for-ordering; common-pcba-after-sales-issues-and-faq; pcb-assembly-faqs-part-2. Rotation ecosystem: Bouni/kicad-jlcpcb-tools#636; KiBot notes_position. Codex review: comment 199 (this item). Frozen ledger: spreadsheet \"Smart Remote v2b DevKit Socketed\". Session nudge: smart-remote/jlc-order-specs.","created_at":"2026-08-30T19:37:47","updated_at":"2026-08-31T15:50:13"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545c5f1c75b7911da020b07549f4","type":"work_item","status":"in_progress","title":"PLAN: Fab order-package exporter — two task-cycles (A: resolved board + schema + gates; B: profile + package + surfaces)","description":"Plan of record for DCR 01a0542d902f (r2). APPROVED BY OWNER 2026-08-30 — the approval spans BOTH task-cycles; execution rolls from cycle 11 into cycle 12 without an approval stop, ending at the B5 owner HITL.\n\nExecution model: task-cycle skill (serial tasks, one commit each, batch cold review by Fable, Codex serial final check, scoped test execution, push at cycle close). Implementer: Opus.\n\n## Cycle A — one resolved board, schema, gates (task-cycle 11; A1, A2a, A2b, A3, serial)\n\n- A1 assembly schema + scalar-exclude migration + round-trip preservation.\n- A2a single strict compilation feeds BOM/CPL/gerbers; raw-dict path retired; uncompilable board refuses BY NAME.\n- A2b resolved assembly anchors (centroid, not footprint origin) + measured transform composition + JLC rotation/Y-sign proof.\n- A3 BOM/CPL hard gates + DNP paste policy, refusals named per component and field.\n\n## Cycle B — profile, package, surfaces (task-cycle 12; B1..B5, serial)\n\n- B1 pinned jlcpcb-economic service profile + semantic template goldens + checkable house-rules subset.\n- B2 deterministic package + manifest + preflight readiness states + digest projection.\n- B3 local assembly preview from the same resolved compilation.\n- B4 export dropdown (both surfaces) + MCP parity with identical refusal names.\n- B5 smart-remote-v2 end-to-end + owner quote-page HITL — the end-HITL, sequenced after cycle 12's scoped run and push.\n\n## Review amendments folded 2026-08-30 (cycle review, owner-approved)\n\n1. A2 split into A2a/A2b — A2b changes already-emitted coordinates and must be bisectable alone.\n2. A2a states the capability regression as a named refusal (BOM/CPL used to work on an uncompilable board).\n3. B1 must pick among THREE existing profile id spaces; the rule-profile loader rejects unknown fields, so assembly keys cannot ride jlcpcb-2layer.json.\n4. A3 reads the max-refs-per-row limit from the profile B1 pins, not a hard-coded 200.\n5. A3 drops \"assembly fields lost in round-trip\" from its runtime gates — it is A1's test property.\n6. B2 treats ABSENT provenance silk as advisory; only a mismatch refuses (the rev is authored in B5).\n7. B4 converts BOTH export surfaces — the inline button and the View-menu item.\n\n## Standing constraints\n\nSerial code-change tasks; tests authored during tasks but executed only at each cycle's scoped run; house-rules advisory checker scope per DCR guiding questions; no rotation DB, no panelization, no order_intent. Judgement points resolve against the owner's 7-axis rubric with the deciding axis recorded; the escalation list lives on the cycle items.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:28:54","updated_at":"2026-08-30T20:56:29","parent":"plugins.dct:01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545c83a876c79b339e16c72f0056","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"A1: Structured assembly schema + scalar-exclude migration + round-trip preservation","description":"Goal: canonical YAML gains the structured per-component `assembly` block (populate, manufacturer, mpn, package, comment, house_parts, paste, placements with authored stable globally-unique refs); existing scalar `assembly: exclude` migrates to the structured non-populated state; footprint-lock assembly fields become provenance-only with a stated precedence rule; docs/board-yaml.md updated, including correcting the too-broad \"footprint origins are pin 1\" claim to what resolution actually does.\n\nOracle: promote → deserialize → load round-trip preserves every assembly field and placement ref (real-worker GD test); a legacy board with scalar exclude and a board with no assembly data both load unchanged.\n\nConstraints: task-cycle rules (tests authored not run; file-don't-fix); parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2 are the spec. Owner ruling: variants stay separate YAML boards — no variant fields.\n\nREVIEW AMENDMENT (2026-08-30, finding 7 + notes): `assembly` is UNDOCUMENTED today — docs/board-yaml.md never mentions it, and on the panel side it survives only as an unmodelled \"canonical extra\" passthrough (ui/model/pcb_component.gd:1787, :1609, :1476). First act of this task is to ENUMERATE the actual readers of the key across worker and panel before changing its shape; a reader missed here silently drops assembly data. Decide explicitly whether the panel models the block or keeps passing it through — passthrough is the simpler option and already has round-trip evidence; record which axis of the rubric decided it. The pin-1 claim to correct appears TWICE: docs/board-yaml.md:43 and :823.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:04","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:33:24","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460d37f7b96b119784f5e6b6c18"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545c99b67f22a5be5898380b030a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"A2a: Single strict compilation feeds BOM, CPL and gerbers — raw-dict path retired","description":"Goal: every order artifact derives from ONE strict compilation of the board — the resolved IR is extended to carry assembly data, and the raw-dict BOM/CPL path is retired so BOM, CPL and gerbers can no longer describe two different boards in one order.\n\nToday's defect, measured: worker/pcb_worker/assembly_outputs.py builds rows straight off the loose board dict (_cpl_rows reads comp[\"x_mm\"]/[\"y_mm\"] at :339), and methods.py:1371 states the choice outright — \"Operate on the RAW canonical board dict ... NOT the compiled ResolvedBoard IR\" — while gerbers compile.\n\nNAMED REFUSAL (review amendment, finding 2): retiring the raw-dict path is a deliberate capability regression — a board that cannot strictly compile can produce BOM/CPL today and will not afterwards. That outcome MUST surface as a named, structured refusal identifying the component/footprint that blocked compilation, never a traceback and never a partial CSV.\n\nOracle: a board that compiles emits BOM/CPL rows identical to the pre-change raw-dict emission for parts whose footprint origin already equals the anchor (proving the plumbing swap changed nothing it should not); a board with an unresolvable footprint refuses by name instead of emitting.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2. Anchor and transform work is A2b — this task moves the data path only. Measure the current emitter's actual conventions before changing them; if a tool-doc claim is wrong, saying so is success.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:09","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:33:25","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460d37f7b96b119784f5e6b6c18"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545cafb27f998b69765338a89d1d","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"A3: BOM/CPL hard gates + DNP paste policy (named refusals)","description":"Goal: assembly export refuses by name on: BOM/CPL reference-set inequality after expansion; duplicate or case-fold-colliding designators; grouped BOM row exceeding the profile's max-refs-per-row; non-metric coordinates; placement designators closer than 0.2 mm; populated part missing profile-required identity/package/comment; synthetic component without placements; invalid/non-finite transforms; SMD DNP with paste:auto (must author paste include|exclude). DNP parts stay in gerbers, exit both CSVs, exclusion logged. THT-emits-no-paste sealed by regression test.\n\nOracle: each refusal test names the component and field; paste matrix fixtures (populated SMD / DNP+include / DNP+exclude / THT-no-paste) inspect the actual paste apertures.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2; refusal-by-name, never best-effort.\n\nREVIEW AMENDMENTS (2026-08-30):\n- Finding 4: the max-refs-per-row figure (200 for JLC) is a DIALECT fact that task B1 pins a cycle later. This gate reads the limit as a profile-supplied parameter (default 200, grouped by complete emitted identity, consider ref-prefix-family splits) so B1 fills it without rewriting the gate.\n- Finding 5: \"assembly fields lost in round-trip\" is REMOVED from the runtime gate list — it is A1's test property, and at export time there is nothing to compare against.\n\nCARRIED FORWARD FROM A2b: the resolved IR now records HOW each placement's anchor was determined, as PhysicalPlacement.anchor_basis, over a three-step ladder — fab_outline, then lands, then footprint_origin. A populated part landing on `footprint_origin` means the pipeline could not measure that part's body and is emitting its drawn origin as if it were a centroid. That is precisely an advisory this task should surface (\"we could not measure this part's body — verify it on the placement preview\"), NOT a refusal: silk-only furniture legitimately lands there. The data is on the IR and nothing consumes it yet.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:15","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:33:28","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460d37f7b96b119784f5e6b6c18"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545cc94c7709aa2eb39386a0c0f7","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B1: Pinned jlcpcb-economic service profile + semantic template goldens + house-rules check","description":"Goal: a minimal versioned service profile `jlcpcb-economic` that references the existing fab-rule profile (jlcpcb-2layer), carries the BOM/CPL dialect, and pins JLC's official template artifacts (URL, fetch date, SHA-256) parsed into semantic fixtures — no byte-equality claims against XLSX; header drift is real (`LCSC Part #` vs `JLCPCB Part #（optional）`). Encode service constraints from the live capability page (Economic: single-sided, 2/4/6 layers, 0.8–1.6 mm, 2–50 boards — verify, don't copy). Export-service vs board fab-rule profile mismatch = refusal. House-rules advisory check implements the subset checkable from board data (component-to-edge, hole-to-edge, spacing) and NAMES the unchecked remainder; selected-service incompatibility (e.g. bottom-side population) is a BLOCKER, not advisory. Tooling-hole possibility surfaces as advisory + checklist line. Pricing informational only.\n\nOracle: goldens derived from the downloaded templates; profile mismatch and blocker tests refuse by name.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2; no third profile island — one selected profile pins everything the export consumes.\n\nREVIEW AMENDMENT (2026-08-30, finding 3 — measured blocker): the existing rule-profile loader REJECTS unknown top-level fields (worker/pcb_worker/manufacturer_profile.py:278) AND unknown floor fields (:314-317), so assembly keys CANNOT simply be added to library/profiles/jlcpcb-2layer.json. There are already THREE id spaces in play: the fab-rule files (`jlcpcb-2layer`), assembly_outputs.PROFILES[\"jlc\"], and this new `jlcpcb-economic`. This task must state which namespace it extends and collapse the redundancy. Recommended shape (rubric: debuggability + durability): a new artifact kind, e.g. library/service-profiles/jlcpcb-economic.json, carrying `fab_profile: \"jlcpcb-2layer\"` and the BOM/CPL dialect, with PROFILES[\"jlc\"] reduced to an alias so ONE selected id pins everything.\n\nNETWORK FALLBACK: pinning templates needs a live fetch. If the fetch fails, pin what is reachable and record the rest as `template_pin: unavailable` in the explicitly-unchecked list — never synthesize a golden from help-page prose and never claim a pin that did not happen.\n\nCARRIED FORWARD FROM CYCLE A (measured, will bite this task immediately):\n- The CSV renderers branch on `profile.id` — `build_bom`/`build_cpl` do `if emission.profile.id == \"jlc\"`, so a new id refuses with \"no BOM renderer wired\" until a branch exists. A `renderer` field on the profile is the smaller fix than a second branch.\n- A3 gave HouseProfile three dialect parameters with defaults — `max_refs_per_row` (200), `min_designator_separation_mm` (0.2), `coordinate_unit` (\"mm\") — read by name via `assembly_gates._param(profile, name, default)`. This task fills them from the pinned dialect; adding further parameters needs no gate-code change.\n- `assembly_gates` deliberately does NOT implement ref-prefix-family splitting of an over-long BOM row; it names the remedy in the refusal. Automatically re-splitting a row changes what the house is sent, and that is this task's dialect decision to make.\n- The \"non-metric coordinates\" gate has no board-side expression (the canonical schema is millimetre-by-construction, every coordinate key is `_mm`-suffixed, no units field), so it is implemented as a profile/dialect guard on `coordinate_unit`. If a board-data check was ever intended, the schema must carry a unit first.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:22","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:45","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545cded775bd8f456fb7ee250976","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B2: Deterministic package assembly + order manifest + preflight readiness states + digest projection","description":"Goal: one action emits the deterministic package layout — gerbers.zip (STRICTLY gerber/drill/job files), bom.csv, cpl.csv, order-manifest.json, preflight.json, ORDER-CHECKLIST.md — with per-output SHA-256. Digest uses the explicit projection (provenance silk text normalized to sentinel before hashing; silk carries immutable design rev, manifest carries the export timestamp). Readiness states reported honestly: package_generated; preflight pass|advisories|blocked; order_page_verified is human-recorded only. Manifest core per DCR r2 (source digest+git state, output hashes, profile+template pins+tool versions, logical→physical map, DNP+paste, transforms, check results incl. explicitly-unchecked, IP questions). Checklist carries order-form choices gerbers don't encode.\n\nOracle: zip content allowlist exact; every gerber/drill layer independently parses; manifest replays to the same digests.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2; no order_intent modeling.\n\nREVIEW AMENDMENT (2026-08-30, finding 6): the silk rev/digest check is NOT refusal-grade on absence. The provenance silk text is authored in B5, so a refuse-on-absent gate would block every package this cycle produces — including the smart-remote-v2 one B5 needs. ABSENT provenance silk = advisory (\"this package carries no design revision on the board\"); only a MISMATCH between the silk rev/digest and the compiled board refuses.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:27","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:47","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545cef3f7fdf9c6d5786736742b6","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B3: Local assembly preview (body, ref, centroid, rotation, pin-1/polarity)","description":"Goal: each package includes assembly-preview.svg rendered FROM the same resolved compilation: body outline, emitted physical ref, claimed centroid, rotation, and pin-1/polarity marks for orientation-sensitive parts — the local pre-payment orientation catch that pairs with the mandatory JLC placement-preview HITL (per-part rotation zero follows their tape orientation; no rotation-correction DB by design).\n\nOracle: preview coordinates/rotations equal the CPL emission for the A2 fixture set (same numbers, one derivation).\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:31","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:49","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545d027a75048a057e4a7ba56eb2","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B4: Export dropdown UI + MCP verb parity with named refusals","description":"Goal: the Export YAML affordance becomes an exporter dropdown (YAML / JLC package / future profiles) + export button; running a package export shows gates and advisories IN-PANEL, named per component; MCP verb(s) expose identical behavior and identical refusal names (owner is GUI-only, agents are MCP-only — parity is the contract). God-file rule: PCBPanel.gd (6487 lines) and panel_tools.gd (11646 lines) take WIRING ONLY; logic lives in a dedicated module.\n\nOracle: GD test drives the panel path and the MCP path over the same refusing board and asserts the same named refusals; a passing board produces the B2 package from both paths.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2.\n\nREVIEW AMENDMENT (2026-08-30, finding 7): Export YAML exists on TWO surfaces, not one — the inline toolbar button (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1267, pressed → _on_export_yaml_pressed) AND a View-menu item \"Export YAML…\" (ui/PCBPanel.gd:1231, _VIEW_MENU_EXPORT_ID) that exists because the inline button hides at narrow widths (:1223). Both must reach the exporter selection, or the menu becomes a divergent second path that can only ever emit YAML.\n\nCARRIED FORWARD FROM A2a: the assembly replies now carry _diagnostic_to_payload warnings, at parity with the gerbers reply (measured: 6 warnings on the A2a twin fixture, mostly captured_geometry_not_emitted). Go's assemblyCallReply ignores unknown keys so nothing breaks today, but this UI surface should decide whether to show them — a warning nobody renders is a warning nobody acts on.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:36","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:50","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0545d16e870f796b80cd7fb313125","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"B5: smart-remote-v2 end-to-end package + owner quote-page HITL (order_page_verified)","description":"Goal: stamp part identities from the frozen ledger (spreadsheet \"Smart Remote v2b DevKit Socketed\") into the board YAML via the LLM lane (U1S placements U1S_A/U1S_B → jlcpcb C41376161; TP2–TP4 populate:false; DNP paste decisions authored); author the silk revision text; generate the full package; owner uploads gerbers.zip + bom.csv + cpl.csv to the JLC quote page and records: recognized layers/dimensions/drills, BOM/CPL recognition, selected parts, placement-preview orientation/polarity verdict, tooling-hole/production-file behavior. That record IS order_page_verified.\n\nOracle: the quote page itself — explicitly not automatable; quote-page acceptance proves uploader compatibility, NOT assembly yield (first physical boards are follow-up bench evidence).\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2; board edits are MCP/YAML lane; freezing the spreadsheet (handover header row) happens here.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:29:41","updated_at":"2026-08-30T20:34:08","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05460d37f7b96b119784f5e6b6c18","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"TASK-CYCLE 11 (Cycle A): resolved board + schema + gates — A1, A2a, A2b, A3","description":"CLOSED GREEN 2026-08-30. First task-cycle of DCR 01a0542d902f r2 / plan 01a0545c5f1c. Pushed as e547d16..0181c5d (dispatch base 6062b64).\n\nTASKS, all done, serial: A1 92d1db4 (schema + scalar migration + round-trip) -> A2a abf85ac (single strict compilation, raw-dict path retired) -> A2b 3a6036d (resolved anchors + measured transforms) -> A3 84ae861 (hard gates + DNP paste policy). Batch-review fix pass 9a7fc18; cross-provider fold 19d867f; fold corrections 2a56233 + 3efa97f; scoped-run fixes b5421f7 + 0181c5d.\n\nREVIEW RECORD. Fable batch cold review: APPROVE with four notes (three applied, one filed as 01a054cc00dd). Codex batch cold review: SIX must_fix, all confirmed by measurement, none refuted — on a batch the first reviewer had approved. Codex re-check 1: two of the six partially resolved, two NEW defects introduced by the fold. Codex re-check 2: those resolved, no new defects, approval withheld solely on the end-to-end authored-blank contract (escalated to the owner as question 01a054f343c5 and 01a054ed6fb5; both non-blocking, B1 owns the identity/dialect contract).\n\nSCOPED RUN (the first execution of any of this code). Worker pytest, 12 suites: 368 passed, 0 failed. Go `go test ./...`: green except the PRE-EXISTING TestSmartRemoteV2RoundTripsUnchanged (proven at the dispatch base in a detached worktree; filed 01a054ff5e47). Real-worker GD test_assembly_schema_roundtrip: 23/23. test_export_yaml_verb: 34/34. Two defects were found by execution that three reviews missed: the structured blocked_by list was silently discarded by shared/bridge.WorkerError (fixed b5421f7 — the same defect was dropping code/component/field on every refusal), and a GD check tested for the substring \"assembly\" against a document whose board is named \"assembly-roundtrip\" (fixed 0181c5d).\n\nENVIRONMENT NOTE, cost a red herring: the stdio smoke initially ran against the marketplace runtime bundle 0.5.6 rather than the dev venv, so it reported \"unknown method assembly_package\". Bundles must be parked out of ~/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime for any dev-lane run. They were parked for the run and RESTORED afterwards, so that smoke test passes on the dev lane only until pcb is next released.\n\nDEFERRED: full suite last green 2026-08-29; not re-run for this cycle.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:33:46","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:33:48","parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545c5f1c75b7911da020b07549f4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"TASK-CYCLE 12 (Cycle B): profile + package + surfaces — B1..B5","description":"CODE COMPLETE AND PUSHED 2026-08-31; only B5 (the owner HITL) remains. minerva-plugins main 0181c5d..dd2708a, 22 commits. CI GREEN on dd2708a — every job including windows-latest and the hermetic clean-checkout fab gate.\n\nTASKS: B0a 01a0553f6140 (faabb19), B0b 01a0553fba08 (3460eef), B0c 01a055401187 (20669b8), B0d 01a0555135 (09aa431), B1 01a0545cc94c (b05f950), B2 01a0545cded7 (9b08ff4), B3 01a0545cef3f (83fc8c7), B4 01a0545d027a (09d635e) — all done. B5 01a0545d16e8 OPEN.\n\nThe four B0 tasks were prepended mid-plan: the owner delegated five open decisions (\"I don't have the knowledge needed — investigate, present options, grade on my rubric, pick the winner, prepend\"), and B0d was added during the cycle when B0a measured a worse defect than the one it was sent to fix.\n\nREVIEW RECORD. Two Fable batch cold reviews (the second found two must_fixes the first had missed, both in fold code); four Codex passes; five fold commits; three fix-loop rounds under the owner's Fable-fixes/Codex-reviews protocol, then Opus after Fable's budget ran out.\n\nFINAL VALIDATION: full worker suite 3153 passed, 4 skipped, 3 xfailed, 0 failed. go test ./... green except internal/board::TestSmartRemoteV2RoundTripsUnchanged, verified failing identically at the dispatch base and filed as 01a054ff5e47. Five GD suites green (346 assertions). A real seven-file package built end to end and its drawing RENDERED AND INSPECTED.\n\nWHAT EXECUTION AND CI CAUGHT THAT REVIEWS DID NOT — the cycle's most useful evidence:\n- a one-character bug (a missing trailing comma made a dict into a bare tuple of its keys) that silently replaced a real advisory in the package's own findings channel;\n- two visible defects in the preview — a clipped header sentence and labels drawn across their own pads — invisible to three cold reviews and 534 passing tests because nothing had ever rendered the page;\n- a regression red on main for hours (the coupon board handed the retired raw-dict emitter path a dict), missed because the scoped set never named that suite and nobody checked CI after the cycle-A push;\n- a Windows-only failure no local run could produce (os.geteuid does not exist there, so the guard meant to skip the test for root crashed before the test could run).\n\nSTOPPED DELIBERATELY, NOT CONVERGED (owner ruling): three defects remain, filed for a REVERSE ROUND — Codex as fixer, Opus as reviewer — under the DCR as 01a056ae9b6d.\n\nB5 REMAINS, and per the owner's ruling it runs off a CI BUILD, not a local binary: confirm CI green (done), get a CI-produced pcb build installed, bring Minerva up, stamp smart-remote-v2 identities through the MCP lane, generate the package, upload to the JLC quote page. The owner asked to be BEEPED when a build is in their hands — not when code merely pushed. Expect the licence advisory on the checklist (01a05593ea5e, lockfile hygiene, not an IP problem) and expect preflight to read `advisories` rather than `pass` because of it. The U1S expansion now has anchor_mm; the DCR's own worked example was wrong and is corrected in comment 202.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:33:51","updated_at":"2026-08-31T16:12:03","parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545c5f1c75b7911da020b07549f4"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05474433678d0a785bf15875e183b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"A2b: Resolved assembly anchors + measured transform composition","description":"Split out of A2 by the 2026-08-30 cycle review (finding 1) because this task CHANGES NUMBERS ALREADY EMITTED and must be bisectable on its own commit.\n\nGoal: every physical placement (synthetic expansion included) carries a resolved explicit assembly anchor — the body centroid, not the footprint origin. Today CPL emits the component's authored x_mm/y_mm verbatim (assembly_outputs.py:339, Y negated), which is the footprint origin; for KiCad THT parts that origin is pin 1, so the emitted centroid is wrong for exactly the parts this board's socket rows are made of. Transform composition (expansion offset × parent rotation × side) is proven by fixtures, and the emitted rotation/Y-sign is proven against JLC's CCW-positive, unmirrored-bottom convention with an asymmetric part.\n\nBlast radius to state honestly in the commit: the emitted coordinate of every part whose footprint origin differs from its centroid changes with this task.\n\nOracle: hand-derived placement fixtures — top, bottom, asymmetric, rotated, expanded (U1S-style two-row socket) — match emission exactly; one worked example documented (Q1-class SOT-23). A fixture whose footprint origin already IS its centroid must emit the same numbers as before this task.\n\nConstraints: parent plan 01a0545c5f1c + DCR 01a0542d902f r2; base is A2a. Measure the current conventions before changing them — the tool-doc rotation claim may be wrong, and saying so is success.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:55:00","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:33:26","parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460d37f7b96b119784f5e6b6c18"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05487bd307ca9b071a1ad63b06bba","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: legacy .minpcb import warns on `assembly` and stores it twice (Extra + typed field)","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A1 (file-don't-fix).\n\n`assembly` is absent from `knownComponentFields` (pcb/internal/board/minpcb.go:43), so a legacy `.minpcb` carrying it lands in `Component.Extra` AND emits the warning \"non-canonical field preserved as passthrough\", while the typed `Assembly` field stays nil. It round-trips correctly only by accident: Extra's yaml inline emits the key, and the next YAML load types it properly.\n\nTwo defects in one: the warning is wrong (assembly IS canonical), and the value has two homes that are one edit away from colliding.\n\nPre-existing before A1; A1 deliberately did not touch it.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:17","updated_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:17","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05487cd8e75ac846e95fea4bd7ad7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: decide whether assembly-block refusals need a Python mirror + spec vectors","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A1 (file-don't-fix).\n\nboard_validate.py has never validated the `assembly` key — the Go check has always been a superset. A1's new shape refusals (`invalid_component_assembly`, `duplicate_assembly_designator`) were deliberately kept OUT of pcb/spec/vectors/, so no cross-language parity obligation was created and nothing is currently lying about coverage.\n\nOpen question for a later cycle: should these become shared codes? That is a Python mirror plus vectors task, not a bug — the current split is honest, just asymmetric.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:21","updated_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:21"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05487e328799b91ca52c5d303670b","type":"question","status":"asked","title":"Owner call: assembly placement offset spelled `offset_mm: {x, y}` — yaml.v3 quotes the bare `y` key","description":"Surfaced during task-cycle 11 / A1.\n\nThe DCR spells the expansion offset `offset_mm: {x: 0, y: 0}`. Go's yaml.v3 emits the second key QUOTED (`\"y\": 0`) because YAML 1.1 consumers resolve a bare `y` as boolean true. A1 kept the DCR's literal spelling rather than deviating on its own.\n\nMeasured as safe today: PyYAML 6.0.3 (the worker's pin) resolves bare `y` as the string \"y\", not True, so both directions agree. The wart is cosmetic now and only bites against a YAML-1.1 parser.\n\nQuestion: live with `\"y\": 0` appearing in emitted YAML, or rename the keys to `x_mm`/`y_mm` (consistent with the rest of the schema, costs a schema change while only one task has landed)? Cheap to change now, expensive after boards carry the block.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:26","updated_at":"2026-08-30T21:16:26","directed_to":"owner"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0549afc5c7dc9acde86fc311e9dba","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: a \"PARKED — NOT COLLECTED\" test file was in fact gating CI (renamed without updating its header)","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A2a (file-don't-fix; A2a fixed only the one header it had to touch).\n\npcb/worker/tests/test_assembly_outputs_matrix.py opened with \"PARKED — NOT COLLECTED. The filename has no `test_` prefix\" while sitting at a path that default pytest patterns DO collect. Someone renamed the file and left the header, so a suite documented as held only to py_compile was actually gating CI.\n\nTwo consequences: a reader trusting the header would not know its assertions run, and a file believed inert was enforcing characterization behaviour (including two real defects recorded as expected output — see the A2a report).\n\nACTION: sweep the rest of pcb/worker/tests/ for other files whose headers claim parked/not-collected status while matching a collection pattern. The claim and the filename must agree.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:37:18","updated_at":"2026-08-30T21:37:18","severity":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0549b15467dc986c1c596ce1e8b7c","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: parity corpus has no bottom-side part at an off-axis rotation","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A2a (file-don't-fix).\n\ntest_placement_parity.py's own header records that no corpus board carries a bottom-side part at an off-axis rotation, so it synthesises a 45-degree case in-test instead. A2a's new fixture assembly_resolved.yaml has exactly that shape (D1 bottom @ 45 degrees) but was deliberately NOT added to the corpus: it now owns the hand-derived BOM/CPL numbers, and a second owner on those coordinates would be a trap.\n\nThe gap is real and outlives A2a: either author a dedicated corpus board with a bottom-side off-axis part, or accept the in-test synthesis permanently and say so in one place instead of two.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:37:24","updated_at":"2026-08-30T21:37:24"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054b04fa772a99b5adf21afcdf0c1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: the real U1S socket-set footprint cannot express its expansion cleanly — decide before smart-remote-v2 ordering","description":"Found during task-cycle 11 / A2b (file-don't-fix). BLOCKS the B5 end-to-end unless answered.\n\n`Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1_SocketSet_2x22_THT` draws BOTH socket rows in one footprint, and its own `descr` says it \"expands at assembly into 2x C41376161 (1x22)\". But `assembly.placements` translates copies of the WHOLE drawn footprint, so each expanded part inherits the 2x22 body centre (0, 30.8485) rather than its own row's centre (-11.43, 26.67) / (+11.43, 26.67).\n\nIt IS authorable today by compensating: offsets (-11.43, -4.1785) and (+11.43, -4.1785) put both anchors in the right place. That is a trap — the numbers are derived from a body centre nobody can see in the YAML, and any library edit silently invalidates them.\n\nThree options, to be graded on the 7-axis rubric when B5 is reached (or earlier if the owner rules):\n(a) compensating offsets — zero code, worst durability and debuggability;\n(b) per-placement footprint override in the schema — a real schema change, honest data;\n(c) redraw the library part as a single 1x22 strip placed twice — no schema change, moves the fix into the library where the geometry lives.\n\nCurrent lean is (c) on durability and debuggability, but this is a library/schema call and the owner may want it made explicitly.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:36","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:36","priority":2,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545d16e870f796b80cd7fb313125"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054b060a37734872fe97c5f142ce5","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: two near-identically-named body-extent functions answer different questions","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A2b (file-don't-fix).\n\n`refdes_anchor.body_extent_from_parsed` is courtyard-first, single-basis. `refdes_anchor.body_extent_from_definition` is outline-union-lands. The names differ only in their suffix and give different answers on real parts: D_SMA (0,0) vs (-0.205, 0); the ESP32 socket set (0, 27.767) vs (-0.655, 28).\n\nA2b did not touch them — it added `fab_extent_from_definition` / `land_extent_from_definition` alongside — but a caller picking the wrong one by name gets a plausible wrong number with no error. Rename to say which basis each uses.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:40","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:40","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054b074ab7270b1e3c6008ba45e61","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: library rule — bless should require an F.Fab body outline","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A2b (file-don't-fix).\n\nNine shipped footprints carry no `F.Fab` outline and therefore fall through the new anchor ladder to the `lands` basis: Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC, R_0805_SeedSynthetic, C_0805_SeedSynthetic, TH_TestPoint, DAM_MinWeb_2P, FID_Circle_1mm, MountingHole_3.2mm_M3, TP_MinAnnular_0p6, SMD_WeirdPads_2P.\n\nHarmless where the part is symmetric. Not harmless for `Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC`, which anchors on its pad array (11.428, 26.67) rather than its module body.\n\nA bless-time rule requiring an F.Fab body outline would close this at the point geometry enters the library, instead of leaving each consumer to guess.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:45","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:00:45"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054c5f6ec7a10bfde56ca400cf2a6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: an authored-empty `assembly.placements: []` is erased by the Go codec (omitempty)","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A3 (file-don't-fix).\n\n`ComponentAssembly.Placements` is tagged `omitempty`, so an authored-empty list marshals away. The worker parses a YAML file directly and sees it, so the `assembly_empty_expansion` refusal fires on the authored file — but the same board after a Go codec round-trip has had the fault silently erased.\n\nThe gate's reachability is therefore codec-path-dependent, which is the worst kind of gate: it fires in one lane and not the other for the same board. A3 carried `expansion_authored` on ResolvedAssembly so the distinction survives compilation, which is what makes the gate work at all; the codec asymmetry itself is untouched.\n\nFix is one of: drop omitempty for this field, or have the codec refuse an empty list outright.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:15","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:15","severity":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054c61767745db5175e492a0b5b0b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: two components sharing a ref refuse as an unnamed `board_invariant`","description":"Found out-of-scope during task-cycle 11 / A3 (file-don't-fix).\n\nTwo components sharing a designator are caught by ResolvedBoard._unique_ids on derived ids, and the refusal reads `board_invariant on <board>` — it never names the duplicated ref. For one of the most common authoring mistakes there is, that is a refusal the author cannot act on.\n\nVisible consequence: A3's case-fold collision test had to use R1/r1 rather than R1/R1, because the exact-duplicate path produces the unnamed refusal instead of a named one.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:23","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:23","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054c6332873ec8b7b9a33f2d36f6f","type":"question","status":"asked","title":"Owner call: should legacy `assembly: exclude` furniture have to author a paste policy?","description":"Surfaced during task-cycle 11 / A3.\n\nThe DCR mandates that an SMD do-not-populate part with `paste: auto` is a refusal — you must author include or exclude, because a stencil that pastes an unpopulated pad is a real assembly defect. A3 implemented that.\n\nConsequence: a board carrying the LEGACY scalar `assembly: exclude` on a paste-bearing SMD footprint must now author a paste policy before an order package can be built. Measured over the whole test corpus that is exactly ONE component — coupon_jlc1's DAM1, a solder-dam coupon structure. Fiducials and test points declare no paste and are untouched; gerbers are unaffected either way.\n\nThe deeper point: A1's migration collapsed \"furniture\" (a bare-copper structure the assembly line never touches) and \"DNP\" (a real part deliberately not fitted) into one non-populated state, so the distinction that would let furniture skip the question does not exist today.\n\nDecision taken for now, on RELIABILITY over cost: keep the refusal as the DCR specifies. Reversing it is a one-line change in assembly_gates._component_faults. Reinstating the furniture/DNP distinction is a schema change and a bigger conversation.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:30","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:30","directed_to":"owner"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054c64384715e9e210749046544f3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: refactor the long-standing God files methods.py (4236) and gerber.py (2089)","description":"Filed per the standing God-file rule during task-cycle 11 / A3.\n\nBoth files were far over the 1-2 KLOC preference BEFORE this cycle, so the rule says file rather than fix in-task. A3 added 61 lines to methods.py and 41 to gerber.py — both pure wiring — and put its 358 lines of new logic in a separate module (assembly_gates.py) precisely to avoid growing them further.\n\nmethods.py is a dispatch table that has accumulated per-method logic; gerber.py mixes IR harvesting with emission. Neither is urgent; both make every future task in this area slower to read.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:34","updated_at":"2026-08-30T22:24:34"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054cc00dd7713adddf367900411d9","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: an order export strict-compiles the board twice (assembly_bom + assembly_cpl are separate RPCs)","description":"Raised by the task-cycle 11 batch cold review as a `note` (cost only, no correctness consequence) and FILED rather than fixed, because the fix belongs to B2.\n\nAfter cycle A, BOM and CPL each go through `_compile_for_assembly`, so a caller wanting both artifacts compiles the same board twice — despite `AssemblyEmission` being designed to compile once and feed both. Nothing is wrong with the output; the two compilations are of the same board with the same profile.\n\nB2 introduces the single package action that emits gerbers, BOM, CPL, manifest and preflight together. That action is the natural place for one compilation to feed everything, which is what the DCR's \"one strict compilation\" goal actually asks for at the package level. Closing this inside B2 is cheaper than adding a caching layer now.","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:30:50","updated_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:36","parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545cded775bd8f456fb7ee250976"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054d5d3cc73a4a1c7cbf7b127af68","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Owner call: the \"no ticket IDs in comments\" rule contradicts a pervasive existing idiom in pcb","description":"Surfaced during task-cycle 11's batch-review fix pass.\n\nThe standing rule is that a comment describes mechanism or a non-obvious code constraint, and that dates, ticket IDs, review history and project rationale in comments are defects. The fix pass removed every such comment ADDED BY THIS BATCH.\n\nBut the existing pcb codebase carries docket/bug IDs in comments at scale — geometry.py:61, tests/gerber_fab.py:10/55/64, test_gerbers.py:267, and many more. Two pre-existing comments carrying review history and epoch names were also found and left: main_test.go:427, tests/gd/test_export_yaml_verb.gd:58.\n\nSo the rule and the codebase disagree, and a file-by-file cleanup during unrelated work just creates inconsistency.\n\nQuestion: (a) sweep the pcb tree once and enforce the rule everywhere, (b) keep the rule for NEW comments only and let existing ones age out, or (c) relax the rule to permit a bare tracking ID where it points at a measurement that cannot fit in the comment?\n\nNo action taken beyond the batch's own comments. Current practice is effectively (b).","created_at":"2026-08-30T22:41:34","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:05:32","directed_to":"owner","answer":"Answered by the owner ruling recorded on 01a0556354ac (2026-08-31). None of the three options offered here was taken.\n\nThe rule of salience STANDS as written — SKILL.md unedited, and dates/ticket IDs/review history/project rationale remain defects. The sweep of the existing tree is DEFERRED to work_item 01a05634ffe8, to be scheduled only after functionality is where the owner wants it. Until then the working test is: does the comment still read correctly with the tag removed? Evidence (a tag dating or sourcing a number, threshold, spec or acceptance criterion) stays; narrative (who noticed, and when) goes.\n\nThe measurement that made this necessary: 1,189 comment lines across 179 files carry docket-style IDs, and a later pass measured ~900-1000 more carrying epoch names, review history, HITL mentions or dates. Many anchor the measurement the code rests on, so a sweep is rewriting, not deleting."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054ea5d4272cab53c32731a240b41","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: Go Validate accepts duplicate component refs that can never compile","description":"Measured during task-cycle 11's cross-provider review fold (file-don't-fix).\n\nGo `board.Validate` accepts a board carrying two components with the same ref — `validatePlacementRefUniqueness` is deliberately scoped to placement refs and its comment says widening was declined. Such a board loads and saves in the panel but can never compile or export.\n\nThe fold made the Python compiler refuse it BY NAME (`duplicate_component_ref`, naming the designator and both positions; previously an unnamed `board_invariant`). So the two surfaces now disagree about whether such a board is loadable at all: Go says yes, the compiler says no.\n\nSupersedes the narrower bug 01a054c61767 (unnamed refusal), which the fold fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:04:00","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:04:00","severity":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054ea6c0e7ebbb4ee916d4b067e08","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: the worker's assembly out_dir write is not all-or-nothing","description":"Found during task-cycle 11's cross-provider review fold (file-don't-fix).\n\n`_write_assembly_files` under the new `assembly_package` method can leave the BOM on disk if the CPL write raises, so a caller can be left with half an order package and no signal.\n\nThe EXPOSED path is safe: the Go tool strips `out_dir` and does its own all-or-nothing write. The direct worker path is not. B2 assembles the full package to disk and should own the atomic-write contract for every artifact, not just these two.\n\nAlso noted: `CplRow.mpn` is carried on the row and never rendered by the CPL renderer — dead column data.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:04:04","updated_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:43","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054ed6fb57febb22846c2db74dc85","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Owner call: duplicate component refs refuse as `duplicate_component_ref`, not the DCR's `assembly_duplicate_designator`","description":"ESCALATED per the task-cycle 11 autonomy contract: this is the second time the cross-provider reviewer has raised the same finding, so it goes to the owner rather than being decided silently a second time. It is NOT blocking — the cycle is proceeding, and reversing this later is a small change.\n\nTHE DISAGREEMENT. The DCR's hard-gate list promises that duplicate or case-fold-colliding designators refuse via the assembly gate, with the code `assembly_duplicate_designator`. What the code now does:\n- an EXACT duplicate component ref refuses upstream at compile, as `duplicate_component_ref`, naming the designator and both positions (it previously refused as an unnamed `board_invariant` that named nothing);\n- a CASE-FOLD collision — the only kind that survives exact comparison — refuses at the assembly gate as `assembly_duplicate_designator`, as documented;\n- the fold corrected the gate table to describe that split rather than forcing both through one code.\n\nCodex's position: the documented contract was changed to accommodate a different error code instead of being fulfilled.\n\nMy position, decided on DEBUGGABILITY and SIMPLICITY: a user gets a named, actionable refusal naming the offending designator either way, which is the thing that was actually broken. Routing exact duplicates back through the assembly gate would mean the same condition carries two codes depending on which surface caught it, and would require the compiler to tolerate a board it cannot build so a later gate can re-refuse it. One refusal, at the earliest point that can see the fault, is simpler.\n\nThe counter-argument I acknowledge: a published gate table is a contract, and quietly editing the contract to match the implementation is exactly the move that makes contracts worthless. This one was edited deliberately and is recorded here rather than buried.\n\nOwner: accept the split as implemented, or require both to surface as `assembly_duplicate_designator`?","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:07:21","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:12:44","directed_to":"owner","answer":"OWNER APPROVED 2026-08-31: \"the ratification is fine, approved.\"\n\nSo the split stands as implemented and as ratified in DCR comment 202 (amendment 3), now with the owner's own confirmation rather than only the orchestrator's delegated authority — which was the substance of the cross-provider reviewer's objection: that a party had edited a published contract constraining itself.\n\nBehaviour, unchanged and now confirmed: exact component-vs-component ref repeats refuse upstream at compile as `duplicate_component_ref`, naming the designator and both positions; case-fold collisions and exact expansion-vs-component collisions refuse at the assembly gate as `assembly_duplicate_designator`; a component repeating a designator inside its own placements refuses as `invalid_component_assembly`; and Go's `validatePlacementRefUniqueness` carries a same-named check that is not in front of the worker's board-path lane. Four codes, two surfaces, documented in the gate table by B0c.\n\nThe DCR's hard-gate list continues to name BEHAVIOUR (\"each designator exactly once, case-fold safe\") rather than codes, deliberately."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054f31cf47796bf6e33cb5c1865e3","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: three BOM tests in test_assembly_outputs.py contradict the fixture after the review fold (R1 authors package: 0805)","description":"The review fold 19d867f made the BOM Footprint column read `assembly.package` (falling back to the authored footprint ref). The shipped fixture pcb/worker/tests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_resolved.yaml authors `package: 0805` on R1 only, so R1's Footprint cell is now \"0805\" while R2's stays \"R_0805\". R1 and R2 therefore no longer group, and three tests written against the pre-fold grouping now contradict the emitter. The fold ran static gates only, so nothing caught it.\n\nDiscovered during a narrow correction pass on two other fold defects; FILED rather than fixed, per that pass's scope.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:13:33","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:13:33","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","bom","pcb","review-fold","task-cycle-11"],"repro_steps":"From pcb/worker, without pytest:\n\n    import yaml, pathlib\n    from pcb_worker import assembly_outputs as ao\n    from pcb_worker.compile_board import compile_board\n    b = yaml.safe_load(pathlib.Path(\"tests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_resolved.yaml\").read_text())\n    r = compile_board(b)\n    print(ao.build_bom(r.board, \"jlc\", name=\"afix\")[\"afix-bom-jlc.csv\"])\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-30, HEAD 05b6530):\n  rows = [('R1',) 10k/0805/C25804, ('D1',) 1N4148/Diode_SMD:D_SMA/C2128, ('R2',) 10k/R_0805/C25804]\n  csv  = 'Comment,Designator,Footprint,LCSC Part #\\r\n10k,R1,0805,C25804\\r\n1N4148,D1,Diode_SMD:D_SMA,C2128\\r\n10k,R2,R_0805,C25804\\r\n'"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054f343c5729a863bc44a3dab135f","type":"question","status":"asked","title":"pcb: should an authored BLANK assembly.comment / assembly.package mean a blank BOM cell? Today the identity fold erases it","description":"assembly_outputs._walk now falls back per BOM column only when the resolved field is None (fixed 2026-08-30 — it used `or`, which fell back on any falsy value). That makes the EMITTER honour an authored blank. Nothing upstream preserves one, so end to end an author still cannot blank a column that has a fallback.\n\nMEASURED (pcb/worker, 2026-08-30): a component with value \"10k\", footprint \"R_0805\" and `assembly: {comment: \"\", package: \"\", mpn: \"M1\"}` compiles to ResolvedAssembly(comment=None, package=None) and renders the BOM line `10k,R1,R_0805,M1`. The blank is erased in assembly_spec._identity, whose documented rule is \"the first NON-BLANK string wins\" across the block, the top-level scalar and the properties mapping.\n\nThat rule is deliberate and load-bearing for IDENTITY: test_whitespace_only_mpn_is_treated_as_missing_not_a_blank_cell seals that a present-but-empty mpn must refuse exactly like an absent one (a CRLF-mangled edit would otherwise ship an LCSC cell holding a lone carriage return). So the two readings collide inside one shared fold.\n\nQUESTION: is \"authored blank\" meaningful for the DESCRIPTIVE fields (comment, package) while blank stays equivalent to missing for the IDENTITY fields (mpn, manufacturer)? If yes, _identity needs a per-field distinction, _check_identity has to treat blank as missing rather than testing `is None`, and docs/board-yaml.md plus the Go codec's precedence prose need to say which fields carry the asymmetry. If no, the current behaviour is correct and the emitter's `is None` tests are simply defence in depth.\n\nFILED, not decided: this changes a documented compile-time contract and was outside a narrow two-defect correction pass.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:13:43","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:13:43","priority":3,"tags":["assembly","bom","pcb","schema","task-cycle-11"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054fbf583772f97f852f4a56bab83","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: an unquoted `package: 0603` in YAML becomes an int and is silently dropped from the BOM","description":"Measured during task-cycle 11's seal re-baseline. This is a SILENT wrong-data path in the order package itself, which is why it is filed at severity 1 despite being cheap to fix.\n\n`assembly_spec._identity()` accepts a value only when `isinstance(value, str)`. PyYAML resolves unquoted `0603` to the integer 387, `0402` to 258, `0201` to 129, `1206` to 1206. So an unquoted package designator is dropped by the identity fold, reaches the IR as None, and the BOM's Footprint column silently falls back to the footprint ref — with NO diagnostic.\n\n`0805` survives unquoted only by accident: 8 is not an octal digit and the leading zero blocks the decimal regex, so it stays a string. Every other common package size fails.\n\n`docs/board-yaml.md:484` currently TEACHES the unquoted form (`package: 0805`), so an author following the documentation hits this the moment they use any size other than 0805.\n\nDirect consequence for B5: smart-remote-v2 carries 0603 and 0402 parts. Stamping their packages from the ledger as the docs describe would produce a BOM whose Footprint column silently disagrees with what was authored.\n\nFix is one of: coerce int-like identity values to strings at the fold, or refuse a non-string identity field by name. Either way the doc example must be quoted.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:23:13","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:23:13","severity":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a054ff5e47716a9cdc2c79ee09dec6","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: TestSmartRemoteV2RoundTripsUnchanged is red — the fixture board now carries 65 board_graphics","description":"Found by task-cycle 11's scoped run, and PROVEN PRE-EXISTING: the same test fails identically at the dispatch base 6062b64, verified in a detached worktree.\n\n`internal/board/board_graphics_test.go:265` asserts \"the board declares no board_graphics\" and gets 65. Those are the smart-remote-v2 board-art strokes added by earlier board work; the test's premise aged out from under it.\n\nNot attributable to this cycle. Filed so the deferred full-suite run does not re-discover it as a fresh regression, and so B5 (which builds the real order package from this very board) knows the board carries board art that at least one test does not expect.\n\nFix is to update the test's premise to the board's current shape, or to pin the count deliberately.","created_at":"2026-08-30T23:26:57","updated_at":"2026-08-30T23:26:57","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0553f61407ae2acaa803c601ccfff","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B0a: identity fold — refuse a non-string identity value by name, and document blank as absent","description":"PREPENDED to task-cycle 12, ahead of B1. Resolves owner decisions 01a054fbf583 (severity 1) and 01a054f343c5 together, because both live in the same ten-line function (assembly_spec._identity, :138-147) and share its two seal tests — implementing them separately would have each fix perturbing the other's seals.\n\nDECISION 1 (from 01a054fbf583) — option 1b, REFUSE A NON-STRING IDENTITY VALUE BY NAME. Deciding axis: DURABILITY. The same file already refuses a non-string in `_house_parts` (:214-222); making `_identity` agree removes an asymmetry inside one module rather than adding a second YAML parse semantics to own forever. Reliability co-signs: the refusal replaces a silent wrong-data path.\nRejected: 1c (coerce) is FALSIFIED by measurement — PyYAML resolves 0603 to 387 as YAML-1.1 octal, so coercion emits \"387\" in the BOM Footprint column, a fabricated value that is strictly worse than today's fallback, and 0603/0402 are exactly the parts the real board carries. 1a (status quo) leaves a severity-1 silent corruption in the order package. 1d (custom SafeLoader dropping the octal resolver) genuinely fixes the Python/Go divergence and makes the documented unquoted form work, but changes parse semantics for every scalar in every board file and obliges every future Python reader to use the custom loader — a durability cost paid forever to save authors a pair of quotes.\n\nSCOPE: raise a named refusal (AssemblySpecError -> the existing invalid_component_assembly code) when an identity value is present but not a string, naming the component, the field and the offending value, and saying what to do (\"quote it: '0603'\"). Applies to every field in IDENTITY_FIELDS (manufacturer, mpn, package, comment) across all three authoring homes. Flip the seal test test_non_string_identity_is_treated_as_absent_not_coerced, whose stated rationale (\"a coerced identity is a plausible-looking wrong answer\") is the argument FOR refusing rather than for dropping. Quote every package example in docs/board-yaml.md, including :484 which currently teaches the one size that survives unquoted by accident.\nAlso fix the shadowing consequence Fable found: a dropped int does not merely vanish, it falls through to the next home, so `assembly: {package: 0603}` over `properties: {package: \"0402\"}` silently emits 0402.\n\nDECISION 2 (from 01a054f343c5) — option 3a, BLANK MEANS ABSENT, FOR ALL FIELDS, AND IT GETS DOCUMENTED. Deciding axis: COST. No board has ever needed a blank cell while its fallback source is populated, and both alternatives require a TWO-LANGUAGE change: Go tags all four identity fields omitempty (internal/board/assembly.go:79-82), so an authored blank is dropped from the file on any Go serialize — a \"blank is meaningful\" rule in Python alone would produce a value that dies on the first promote, which the DCR makes a hard gate. Durability co-signs: one rule for four sibling keys beats two classes of field.\nRejected: 3b (per-field asymmetry) and 3c (blank meaningful everywhere) both pay 50-80 lines plus Go pointer surgery for a need nobody has demonstrated; 3b additionally sets a trap where a future identity_required entry for `package` would pass an authored blank straight into a blank identity cell.\n\nSCOPE: no behaviour change — document the rule where an author will find it. docs/board-yaml.md carries no blank-vs-absent prose today; the rule lives only in a module docstring. Say plainly that a blank identity value is treated as absent and falls through to the next authoring home, and that there is deliberately no way to force an empty cell.\n\nThis also closes the objection the cross-provider reviewer withheld approval on in cycle 11: the answer is \"blank means absent, and it is now written down\", not \"unresolved\".\n\nOracle: a board authoring an unquoted numeric package refuses by name and names the field; a board authoring a quoted one emits it; a blank falls through to the next home exactly as the existing seal requires.","created_at":"2026-08-31T00:36:52","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:34","priority":1,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0553fba08758996d1970fe1c2bf9a","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B0b: per-placement anchor override — the minimal honest datum for a multi-part footprint","description":"PREPENDED to task-cycle 12, ahead of B1. Resolves owner decision 01a054b04fa7, which BLOCKS the B5 order of smart-remote-v2.\n\nDECISION — option 2d, A PER-PLACEMENT `anchor_mm` OVERRIDE. This option was NOT in the original item; the investigation was asked to challenge the three-option framing and found a fourth. Deciding axis: DEBUGGABILITY. Each expanded child needs exactly one thing the parent cannot supply — its own anchor point — and under this option that number is derivable from the part actually purchased (22 pins x 2.54 pitch / 2 - 1.27 = 26.67), so a reader can check it against the datasheet without opening a .kicad_mod. Durability co-signs: it forecloses nothing, and option 2b could later supersede it by having an override footprint POPULATE the default anchor.\n\nTHE DEFECT, measured: assembly_anchor.physical_placements (:105-132) computes ONE anchor_local from the PARENT footprint and applies it to every expanded child. The socket-set footprint's F.Fab box spans y -1.1..62.797, giving a fab-basis centre of (0, 30.8485), while each row's true centre is (-11.43, 26.67) and (+11.43, 26.67).\nWORSE, AND THE REASON THIS IS PRIORITY 1: the DCR's own worked example is the trap sprung. Its placements — U1S_A at offset (0,0) and U1S_B at (22.86, 0) — put both anchors at y 30.85, both wrong, 22.86 mm apart, PASSING EVERY GATE, because the spacing gate only catches proximity. Nothing in the pipeline catches it before the B3 preview or JLC's own placement preview.\n\nREJECTED, with the measurements that decided it:\n- 2a (compensating offsets, 0 code): the x half is honest (half the row spacing, stated in the footprint's own descr) but the y half (-4.1785) is the difference between the row centre and an INVISIBLE fab-box centre, derivable only by reading the .kicad_mod. Debuggability fails. The footprint is sha256-pinned in footprints.lock.json so an edit is detectable, but nothing ties the authored offsets to the lock entry, so a deliberate re-lock orphans them silently.\n- 2b (per-placement footprint override, 80-150 lines): expansion children carry NO copper, so the field would be consumed only for the anchor and body extent and never etched — a schema key naming a footprint that contributes no geometry is a permanent invitation to misread, and schema fields are the hardest thing to remove.\n- 2c (redraw as two 1x22 strips) was sketched in the item as \"no schema change\" and is MUCH more expensive than that: expansion children have no geometry, so this means two ordinary components — and the board is already routed with 44 explicit pin overrides, 40+ nets bound to DevKit signal names that differ between the two rows, and a module-body courtyard, antenna keepout and centreline silk spanning both rows that no 1x22 strip can hold. The library has no 1x22 socket to use. Right answer for a FUTURE board before routing; wrong one for this board now.\n\nSCOPE: one optional `anchor_mm` key on a placement, in _PLACEMENT_KEYS and AssemblyPlacementSpec, honoured in assembly_anchor as `child.point(anchor_override or anchor_local)`; the matching Go field with round-trip preservation (the DCR makes round-trip loss a hard gate, and note the omitempty class of bug already found twice this DCR); docs; tests. Board authoring stays in B5.\n\nOracle: a U1S-shaped fixture with per-placement anchors emits each row's own centre, and the same fixture WITHOUT overrides still emits the parent anchor for both children — so the override is proven to be what changed the number.","created_at":"2026-08-31T00:37:14","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:36","priority":1,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05540118775f291cbf118d56f1ce9","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B0c: ratify the duplicate-ref refusal split, and sweep review-history comments","description":"PREPENDED to task-cycle 12. Resolves owner decision 01a054ed6fb5 (the doc half) and the code half of 01a054d5d3cc.\n\nSCOPE NARROWED mid-cycle: the DCR amendments are the orchestrator's to make and are being done separately, because the DCR is the spec of record and an implementer should not edit the contract that constrains it — which is the very objection this task exists to answer. This task owns the repo-side documentation and the comment sweep ONLY.\n\nDECISION 1 (from 01a054ed6fb5) — KEEP THE SPLIT, and describe it accurately. Deciding axis: DEBUGGABILITY. One condition, one code, refused at the earliest surface that can see it. The alternative would teach the compiler to tolerate a board it provably cannot build so a later gate could re-refuse it, and would regress the diagnostic for every non-export lane.\n\nThe investigation corrected the framing twice, and the corrections are what this task must get right in the docs:\n- THE DCR NEVER PROMISED A CODE. Its hard-gate list says \"each designator exactly once, case-fold safe\" — behavioural.\n- THERE ARE THREE CODES, NOT TWO. Go's Validate carries `duplicate_assembly_designator` for exact placement-ref collisions; Go validation is NOT in front of the worker's board_path lane, so an exact collision between an expansion ref and another component's ref still reaches the assembly gate's exact branch. Only exact component-vs-component refs go upstream as `duplicate_component_ref`. The current gate-table wording says case-folding is the only kind that survives exact comparison, which is wrong.\n\nSCOPE: correct the gate table in docs/board-yaml.md so it describes all three codes and which surface raises each. Verify the three-code claim yourself before writing it down — if the investigation was wrong, say so.\n\nDECISION 2 (from 01a054d5d3cc) — option 5d: a bare tracking ID that anchors a measurement STAYS; project-management narrative, dates and review history GO. Deciding axis: DISCOVERABILITY — this is the line the owner's own CLAUDE.md draws, and the tree splits cleanly along it.\nMeasured scale that killed the alternatives: 1,189 comment lines across 179 files carry docket-style IDs, and many anchor the measurement or acceptance criterion the code rests on, so a sweep would be REWRITING each one to stay self-contained, not deleting — days of judgement for zero behaviour change, with real risk of destroying provenance.\n\nSCOPE: sweep only the review-history, date and epoch-name class. Known instances left as pre-existing by cycle 11: main_test.go:427 and tests/gd/test_export_yaml_verb.gd:58. Find the rest of that class with a targeted grep. LEAVE every ID that anchors a measurement — if a comment cites a docket ID next to a number or an acceptance criterion, it stays.\nTHE SKILL-FILE WORDING IS OUT OF SCOPE: the rule lives in Minerva/.claude/skills/task-cycle/SKILL.md and governs future cycles beyond this work, so it is proposed to the owner rather than edited here.\n\nOracle: no comment in the pcb tree carries review history, a date, or an epoch name; every comment citing a docket ID alongside a measurement is untouched; the gate table names three codes and the surface that raises each.","created_at":"2026-08-31T00:37:37","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:37","priority":2,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055510fc076a2b7cc5066d58a1ff4","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: a Go save REWRITES unquoted identity values on disk — `package: 0603` becomes `package: 387`","description":"Measured during task-cycle 12 / B0a, and strictly worse than the bug B0a was dispatched to fix (01a054fbf583).\n\n`Component` has NO typed fields for the pre-block identity homes — the top-level `package:` / `mpn:` scalars and the `properties:` map ride `Extra map[string]interface{}` with `yaml:\",inline\"` (internal/board/board.go:397). Decoding into `any` applies YAML-1.1 octal resolution, so a Go codec round-trip REWRITES THE FILE ON DISK: `package: 0603` becomes `package: 387`, `properties: {package: 0402}` becomes `258`. Verified against the real structs with yaml.v3.\n\nThe authored text is destroyed by a SAVE, before any compile ever runs. B0a's Python refusal catches the consequence and refuses to emit a BOM from it, but by then the original string is gone and cannot be recovered.\n\nThe structured `assembly:` block is SAFE — `ComponentAssembly.Package` is a typed string, and yaml.v3 keeps `0603` intact and re-marshals it quoted. So the corruption is confined to the two pre-block authoring homes, which are exactly the homes the documentation described until B0a.\n\nThis partially invalidates the grading of 01a054fbf583, which weighed \"Go reads it correctly, Python drops it\" and concluded that making Python loud was enough. Go does not read it correctly in these two homes; it silently renumbers the file.\n\nB5 IS NOT BLOCKED: identities will be stamped into the structured block, which is typed and safe.\n\nFix is one of: typed string fields on Component for the pre-block identity homes, or a load-time refusal mirroring the Python one.","created_at":"2026-08-31T00:56:10","updated_at":"2026-08-31T00:56:10","severity":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05551353672d4bab452a431dc485e","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"B0d: stop the Go codec renumbering unquoted identity values on save","description":"ADDED to task-cycle 12 during the cycle, after B0a measured the defect. Resolves bug 01a054... (the Go Extra renumbering). Runs after B0c, before B1.\n\nWHY THIS WAS ADDED MID-CYCLE rather than filed for later: it is a silent data-destruction path inside the exact feature this DCR is shipping, and B0a's refusal makes it MORE visible without making it safe — an author now gets a refusal on a file whose authored text has already been overwritten, which is a worse experience than the silent drop it replaced unless the write side is fixed too. Deciding axis: RELIABILITY.\n\nTHE DEFECT: Component carries no typed fields for the top-level `package:`/`mpn:` scalars or the `properties:` map; they ride Extra map[string]interface{} with yaml:\",inline\" (internal/board/board.go:397). Decoding into `any` applies YAML-1.1 octal resolution, so a save rewrites `package: 0603` to `package: 387` and `properties: {package: 0402}` to `258`. The structured `assembly:` block is unaffected — its fields are typed strings and round-trip intact.\n\nSCOPE: make a Go load/save preserve the authored text of the pre-block identity homes, by typed string fields on Component or by a load-time refusal that mirrors B0a's Python one. Prefer whichever keeps ONE rule for the author: if Python refuses an unquoted value, Go refusing it at load is more coherent than Go quietly accepting and normalising it. Whatever is chosen, a round-trip of a board carrying quoted identity values in all three homes must be byte-stable.\n\nOracle: a board authored with `package: 0603` in each of the three homes, round-tripped through the Go codec, either comes back byte-identical or refuses by name — and never comes back as 387.","created_at":"2026-08-31T00:56:20","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:48:39","priority":1,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0555e05547d4db9c974d5d7a9ce36","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb-worker/ad-hoc-python-must-not-put-tests-on-syspath","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:10:20","updated_at":"2026-08-31T01:10:20","value":"When writing a throwaway probe script against pcb_worker, do NOT put pcb/worker/tests on sys.path. The test package shadows the vendored `agent_router` package of the same name, and `pcb_worker.compile_board` then fails to import with an error that points nowhere near the real cause. Import pcb_worker from pcb/worker only, and reach test helpers by file path if you need them. Measured during task-cycle 12 / B0b.","component":"pcb-worker","key":"ad-hoc-python-must-not-put-tests-on-syspath"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05562df92780bb36ed8e95dcff8ec","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"Four surfaces refuse a duplicate designator; Go's Validate is NOT one of them on the export lane","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:15:38","updated_at":"2026-08-31T01:15:38","tags":["assembly","pcb","validation"],"value":"A duplicate reference designator is refused by FOUR codes on four surfaces, and they do NOT split into \"exact upstream / case-fold at the gate\":\n\n1. duplicate_component_ref -- compile_board.py:2930 -- two components authoring the same ref, exact.\n2. invalid_component_assembly -- assembly_spec.py:247, recorded by compile_board.py:3120 -- one component repeating a designator inside its OWN assembly.placements. Shares the generic malformed-block code.\n3. duplicate_assembly_designator -- internal/board/assembly.go:450 validatePlacementRefUniqueness, reached only through board.Validate -- a placement ref colliding exactly with another placement's ref or with another component's ref. Deliberately scoped to placement refs; component-vs-component is never checked there.\n4. assembly_duplicate_designator -- assembly_gates.py:215 check_designators -- board-wide, CASE-FOLDED, over every physical placement. Has BOTH an exact branch (\"is already placed by\") and a case-fold branch (\"case-folds onto\").\n\nKEY GOTCHA: board.Validate is called ONLY from internal/tools/project.go:175 (HandleSerialize) and :327 (HandleDeserialize) -- the Go codec boundary. HandleExportAssembly forwards params to the worker unchanged, and the worker reads the board straight off yaml/board/board_path via board_model.load_board. So Go validation is NOT in front of the assembly export lane, and an EXACT expansion-ref collision (a placements ref that is already another component's designator) reaches assembly_duplicate_designator and refuses on its exact branch. Never write \"an exact repeat is already refused upstream\".\n\nDocumented in pcb/docs/board-yaml.md, section \"Which surface refuses a duplicate designator\".","component":"pcb-assembly","key":"duplicate-designator-surfaces","confidence":"high","research_cost":12}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0556308387499b2de365451272cc4","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"check_designators' EXACT branch is untested: the expansion-collision test uses a case-folded ref","description":"Found while measuring the duplicate-designator surfaces for B0c (01a055401187).\n\nassembly_gates.check_designators (worker/pcb_worker/assembly_gates.py:215) has two branches: an EXACT one (\"is already placed by\") and a CASE-FOLD one (\"case-folds onto\"). Only the case-fold branch is exercised.\n\nworker/tests/test_assembly_gates.py:174 test_expansion_designator_colliding_with_another_component_refuses is the test that should cover the exact branch -- its docstring says \"an authored expansion ref that is already some other drawing's designator\" -- but the board it builds uses component \"R1\" and placement ref \"r1\", so it takes the CASE-FOLD branch. Nothing in the suite reaches the exact branch of this gate.\n\nThat branch is reachable in production: Go's validatePlacementRefUniqueness (the only other check covering placement-vs-component-ref collisions) runs through board.Validate, which is called only from HandleSerialize/HandleDeserialize (internal/tools/project.go:175,327) and is not in front of the assembly export lane.\n\nFIX: change the placement ref in that test to exact \"R1\" (or add a sibling case), and assert the message says \"is already placed by\" rather than \"case-folds onto\".\n\nNo behaviour change needed -- test-only gap.","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:15:48","updated_at":"2026-08-31T01:15:48","priority":3,"tags":["assembly","pcb","test-gap"],"expected_result":"A board whose expansion placement ref exactly equals another component's ref refuses with assembly_duplicate_designator on the exact branch, and a test asserts that message."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0556354ac73c797ea5fca8ba41ad8","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"Comment-hygiene rule needs re-scoping: narrative and evidence do not separate in the pcb tree","description":"Raised from B0c (01a055401187), whose Part 2 asked for a sweep of the \"project-management narrative, dates, review history and epoch names\" class and told the implementer to stop and report if the two classes did not separate cleanly. They do not. Only the two instances the work item named were changed.\n\nMEASURED, over git-tracked pcb sources only (405 .go/.py/.gd/.sh files; runtime-build/runtime-stage and worker/.venv are untracked and excluded):\n  epoch names            ~446 lines\n  review history         ~289 lines (cold review / Codex finding / review round / blocking finding)\n  HITL mentions          ~229 lines\n  dates (20xx-mm-dd)      ~84 lines\nOverlapping, so on the order of 900-1000 comment lines across the tree -- the same order of magnitude as the 1,189-line docket-ID measurement that already killed the alternative option in 01a054d5d3cc.\n\nWHY IT DOES NOT SEPARATE. The dominant shape is not a narrative comment; it is a mechanism sentence carrying a parenthetical provenance tag. Examples:\n  ui/PCBPanel.gd:546   \"order is load-bearing (station-2 cold review F1): attach the pre-accept ...\"\n  ui/PCBPanel.gd:4725  \"ATOMIC tmp-to-rename (cold review F6): the design of record must never be ...\"\nStripping the tag means rewriting each comment, not deleting it -- exactly the days-of-judgement work the decision rejected.\n\nTHE DATE RULE IS ACTIVELY HARMFUL AS WRITTEN. Dates split both ways:\n  KEEP  worker/pcb_worker/silk_source.py:144  \"0.15mm (capabilities page, re-fetched 2026-08-09)\" -- the date is the provenance of a manufacturer figure the code enforces.\n  KEEP  worker/tests/oracle/zone_fill_oracle.py:15 \"measured against KiCad 9.0.9, 2026-08-02\" -- dates the measurement.\n  KEEP  worker/tests/test_manufacturer_profile.py:773 \"JLCPCB multilayer service, quoted 2026-08-13\".\n  GO    ui/PCBPanel.gd:6 \"HITL-caught 2026-07-16\" -- pure narrative.\nA blanket \"dates go\" strips measurement provenance, the very destruction the docket-ID decision was made to avoid.\n\nThe B0c oracle (\"no comment in the pcb tree carries review history, a date, or an epoch name\") is therefore not satisfiable without losing provenance.\n\nPROPOSED REFINEMENT, for the owner to rule on before any sweep is scheduled:\nThe test is not WHICH tokens a comment contains but WHETHER the comment still reads correctly with the tag removed. A tag is EVIDENCE (stays) when it dates or sources a number, a threshold, an external spec, or an acceptance criterion. It is NARRATIVE (goes) when it only says who noticed the problem and when. Under that rule a sweep is still large but at least mechanical, and it can be scoped file-by-file rather than tree-wide.\n\nAlso note: an owner ruling with a date (\"owner boundary ruling 2026-07-30, docket 019fb5ad6d20\") is a third case -- a non-obvious constraint attribution that tells a future reader not to change the behaviour. It reads as evidence, not story, and the current rule would delete the date half.","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:16:08","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:05:31","priority":2,"tags":["comment-hygiene","pcb","process"],"answer":"OWNER RULING 2026-08-31, verbatim: \"my rule of salience stands, but we can defer it by filing a bug and use your suggestion for now. We'll come back to a salience pass much later -- after we have all functionality where I want it.\"\n\nThree things follow.\n\n1. THE RULE IS UNCHANGED. Minerva/.claude/skills/task-cycle/SKILL.md was NOT edited and must not be — comments describe mechanism or a non-obvious code constraint, and dates, ticket IDs, review history and project rationale remain defects by the standing rule. Nothing here relaxes it.\n\n2. ENFORCEMENT OVER THE EXISTING TREE IS DEFERRED to work_item 01a05634ffe8, which will not be scheduled until the owner says functionality is where they want it. Rationale accepted: ~900-1000 comment lines across 405 tracked files, the dominant shape is a mechanism sentence carrying a provenance tag so the work is rewriting rather than deleting, and it produces zero behaviour change — running it inside a feature cycle would bury real defects in blame churn.\n\n3. THE PROPOSED REFINEMENT IS ADOPTED AS INTERIM GUIDANCE. The test is not which tokens a comment contains but whether it still reads correctly with the tag removed. Evidence stays (a tag dating or sourcing a number, threshold, external spec or acceptance criterion); narrative goes (a tag saying only who noticed and when); an owner ruling attributed with its date counts as evidence, being a constraint attribution that tells a future reader not to change the behaviour.\n\nThis also answers the earlier question 01a054d5d3cc, which asked the same thing in the narrower form of sweep-versus-relax-versus-new-only. The answer is: none of those — the rule stands, the sweep is deferred and filed, and the evidence/narrative test governs in the meantime."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0556ac87175b9817eaaa00356c9e7","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: a Go save still renumbers unquoted NON-identity passthrough keys (`lot_code: 0777` -> 511)","description":"Out-of-scope discovery from task-cycle 12 / B0d, filed not fixed.\n\nB0d fixed the four IDENTITY keys (manufacturer, mpn, package, comment) in the two pre-block homes by re-reading their authored text from the source nodes (preserveIdentityText, pcb/internal/board/assembly.go). Every OTHER key riding the same inline `Extra map[string]interface{}` passthrough is unchanged and still resolves by YAML tag: measured, `lot_code: 0777` decodes to the int 511 and a save writes 511 back over the author's text. The same holds for any unmodelled key on Component, Net, Trace, Via, Hole, DesignRules and the Board root, all of which carry the same untyped inline map.\n\nDeliberately not fixed in B0d: the scope was the identity homes, and the untyped passthrough is what makes forward-compat keys work at all. A general fix would have to carry passthrough scalars as raw yaml.Nodes, which the JSON boundary (mergeExtra, internal/board/json.go) and the writers in internal/tools/project.go both consume as interface{} — a much larger change with no known author hitting it.\n\nOracle if this is ever picked up: a component carrying `lot_code: 0777` comes back from a Go round trip carrying `0777`, and every other passthrough shape (nested mapping, sequence, float, bool, null) is unchanged. The regression seal for the current scope is TestIdentityTextRepairLeavesEveryOtherPassthroughKeyAlone, which asserts lot_code == 511 today; flip that assertion if the behaviour is ever widened.","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:24:16","updated_at":"2026-08-31T01:24:16","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05593a8c57feda21e037ce3062346","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: worker/pcb_worker/methods.py is a god file at 4381 lines — split the dispatch handlers by surface","description":"FILED, not fixed (out of scope for B2, which added ~100 lines to an already-oversized file).\n\nMEASURED at B2: worker/pcb_worker/methods.py is 4381 lines. It was 4281 before B2 added the `order_package` handler, so it did not cross the boundary during this work — it has been a god file for a while, which is the case the repo rule says to FILE rather than fix in place.\n\nIt is absent from the existing god-file census 01a042fe07037150946566a45f0c7d93, which lists pcb_canvas 12.2k, panel_tools 11.4k, PCBPanel 7.4k, pcb_data 4.1k, pcb_routing_workspace 4.1k, compile_board.py 3.5k and drc.py 1.2k. methods.py at 4.3k belongs on that list.\n\nSHAPE THE SPLIT ALREADY HAS. The file is organized into commented surface blocks that map almost one-to-one onto modules: fab (validate/generate/gerbers/drc/drc_geometric/resolve), assembly (assembly_check/assembly_bom/assembly_cpl/assembly_package/order_package), routing (route/draft_check, which is the largest single block at roughly 1500 lines), footprint bless/acquire/import, and library/health. `_HANDLERS` stays here as the one dispatch table; the handlers move.\n\nThe routing block alone is over a third of the file and has no dependency on the assembly or footprint blocks.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:55","updated_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:55","priority":3,"tags":["god-file","pcb","refactor"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05593ea5e702c8bae9324e308e47a","type":"insight","status":"confirmed","title":"CLOSED AS NOT A DEFECT: the licence advisory does not fire — every footprint declares a licence","description":"CORRECTED AND CLOSED 2026-08-31, after measuring instead of repeating a report.\n\nTHE ORIGINAL CLAIM WAS FALSE. This item said `libraries.lock.json`'s 19 entries carry no `license` key, so `ip_licence_undeclared` fires on essentially every board and pins preflight at `advisories`. Measured:\n- there is no `libraries.lock.json`; the file is `library/footprints.lock.json`\n- it holds 39 entries and ALL 39 declare a licence\n- a real order_package build reports `ip_questions: 0`\n\nSo the advisory does not fire, has never fired on a board in this repo, and is not what keeps preflight off `pass`. The owner said as much when this was first raised — \"there's not an IP issue now, AFAIK, just poor docket hygiene\" — and the correct response was to verify, not to re-frame the same unverified premise as a lockfile-hygiene problem. The hygiene problem does not exist either.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY HOLDS PREFLIGHT AT `advisories` on the jlcpcb-economic profile, measured on a clean fixture:\n- `assembly_house_tooling_holes` — JLC adds tooling holes on this tier. Unconditional, fires on every board, a statement about the house rather than the design.\n- `assembly_provenance_absent` — no design revision stamped in board_graphics yet. B5 authors this; it should clear once smart-remote-v2 is stamped.\n- `gc9_silk_width` x N — silk at 0.12 mm against the profile's 0.15 mm floor.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, recorded so it is not re-litigated: `ip_questions()` raises ONE finding naming every footprint whose artwork ships without a declared licence, and is silent when there are none. Separately, `ip_licence_compatibility` sits permanently in the UNCHECKED list — declared licences are recorded in the manifest and never judged, because whether a licence permits this use is an owner decision. That entry appearing on every package is by design and is not a finding.\n\nLESSON: this is the second time in this DCR that an agent's report was carried forward as fact without measurement (see also 01a0565f591d, where \"pre-existing\" was asserted against the wrong baseline). A report is evidence about what an agent saw, not a measurement.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:09:12","updated_at":"2026-08-31T16:12:12","priority":3,"tags":["ip","order-package","pcb"],"assumed":"That footprint provenance carries a declared licence for library-sourced parts, so the exporter's IP surface would fire only on genuinely unattributed artwork.","corrected":"Nothing declares one. `compile_board` fills `Provenance.license` from the board's own `library_lock` entry (compile_board.py:3090, `license=entry.get(\"license\")`); a board authoring no `library_lock` gets `entry = {}` and every footprint compiles with `license=None`. The shipped libraries.lock.json carries no `license` key on any of its 19 entries either — only name/kind/dest/url/sha256/size_bytes — so even a board that locks against the seed gets nothing.","surprise":"The exporter's honest IP question therefore fires on essentially every board, which means preflight_status is `advisories` rather than `pass` on every package until a licence is recorded somewhere."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055a7e40f7b3997494c76c2502544","type":"question","status":"asked","title":"Should an expansion that lets every placement inherit ONE measured anchor raise an advisory, not just a preview note?","description":"FILED FROM TASK B3 (01a0545cef3f), not fixed — it revisits a B0b decision.\n\nMEASURED, in B3, against the override fixture with its authored anchors stripped (the exact board B0b's `anchor_mm` key repairs):\n\n`assembly_gates.ADVISORY_ANCHOR_UNMEASURED` (assembly_gates.py:90, fired at :340) covers exactly ONE shape: a placement whose anchor fell through the whole basis ladder to `footprint_origin`. It does NOT cover the shape the DCR's own worked example fell into — a component with SEVERAL physical placements where every one of them inherited one anchor measured off the WHOLE parent drawing. Those anchors are `fab_outline`, i.e. genuinely measured, so no advisory fires; they are the correct distance apart, so the spacing gate passes; and the BOM/CPL reference sets agree, so nothing else looks.\n\nWHAT B3 DID ABOUT IT: the assembly preview names every such placement group in a sentence on the page (\"one drawing, N parts, and every anchor was measured off the WHOLE drawing rather than stated per placement\"), and draws the lands so a crosshair sitting between two pin rows rather than on one is visible. It raises NO code and NO advisory, because DCR 01a0542d902f r3 amendment 2 and comment 202 put this boundary at a human on purpose.\n\nTHE QUESTION: is the preview note enough, or should `assembly_gates.advisories()` gain a second anchor advisory for `len(physical_placements) > 1 and no placement authored an anchor_mm`? Arguments each way:\n\nFOR an advisory — it would reach the MCP/agent lane and the preflight report, not just a page somebody has to open; an agent building a package never sees the SVG. It is cheap and precise, and the false-positive shape (a multi-placement component whose parts really do share the drawing's centre) is rare and answerable by authoring `anchor_mm` explicitly.\n\nAGAINST — it fires on a legal, sometimes correct authoring, and the DCR already ruled that a rule is the wrong instrument here; an advisory nobody can clear without editing a board they believe is right is an advisory people learn to ignore.\n\nMEASURED LIMIT OF THE PREVIEW'S OWN RING, which is why this is worth asking: of the six wrong anchors in the stripped fixture, the preview's \"claim landed off its own drawing\" ring catches exactly ONE (U5S_B, which claims x 52.86 against a drawing whose ink stops at 42.93). The other five land INSIDE the drawn body and are visible only to a person reading the crosshair against the pin rows. That is the honest result, not a weak one — an authored anchor may legitimately be anywhere on its part — but it means the eye is doing five sixths of the work.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:31:01","updated_at":"2026-08-31T02:31:01","directed_to":"owner"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055bfebb07127ab2077dfca06d340","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"A blocked order_package refusal drops the compile warnings it already computed","description":"Found in task-cycle 12 B4 while wiring the export surfaces; NOT fixed there because it is B2's reply shape and methods.py is already a filed god file (01a05593a8c5).\n\nmethods.py `_order_package` computes `warnings = [_diagnostic_to_payload(d) for d in compiled.diagnostics]` BEFORE calling order_package.build, and returns them on the success path only. Every refusal path returns without them:\n- the not-compilable branch returns assembly_outputs.not_compilable_error(...) + preflight;\n- the gate branch returns _order_package_refusal(exc), which builds {kind, code, component, field, refs, message, preflight} and nothing else.\n\nSo a board that compiles WITH warnings and is then stopped by an assembly gate tells the caller nothing the compiler said about it — exactly the class of loss B4 fixed one level up by rendering warnings in the panel report and forwarding them through minerva_pcb_export_assembly. The blocked board is the case where a person most wants them: they are about to go edit the board.\n\nFix is one line in _order_package_refusal's two callers (attach the already-computed list), plus the same in the Go reply if a decoder field is needed. Oracle: a fixture that compiles with a WARNING diagnostic and then trips a hard gate — the refusal must carry the warning.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:57:16","updated_at":"2026-08-31T02:57:16","priority":3,"severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055ec08d9734b979c05c054b13a75","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: service-profile unchecked rules use {id, reason} while every other finding channel uses {code, message}","description":"Found by the cross-provider review of task-cycle 12 and folded around, not resolved.\n\nThe service profile's `unchecked_rules` entries are shaped `{id, reason}`. Every other finding channel in the system — advisories, warnings, blockers, IP questions — uses `{code, message}`. The GUI's finding renderer read only the latter, so the fifteen rules the profile carefully names as UNCHECKED rendered as blank bullets: the list telling a reader what nobody looked at was the one list they could not read.\n\nThree things made it survive review: the renderer failed silently rather than erroring, the report predicate omitted `unchecked_rules` so a clean-looking export suppressed the section entirely, and the parity test's canned fixture used the WRONG shape too — so a test whose whole purpose is proving two surfaces agree passed while they disagreed.\n\nThe fold taught the renderer BOTH vocabularies and made the parity fixture read the shipped profile file directly, so it can no longer drift. That is the safe fix, not the right one: the profile file is the authority for its own content, but two vocabularies for one concept means the next consumer hits the same trap, and the next fixture author has to know which shape to fabricate.\n\nPick one vocabulary and migrate. If `{id, reason}` is right for a rule that is not a finding about a specific board, then say so explicitly and name the distinction, so the renderer's dual support is a documented contract rather than a workaround.","created_at":"2026-08-31T03:45:27","updated_at":"2026-08-31T03:45:27","severity":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055fa567d72bb920e6edfe1c2cf02","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: test_a_one_terminal_part_says_why_it_carries_no_mark fails — footprint ref 'TestPoint:TH_TestPoint' is not a lockfile key","description":"Found while correcting the cross-provider re-check findings (task-cycle 12). Out of scope for that commit, so filed rather than fixed.\n\npcb/worker/tests/test_assembly_preview.py::test_a_one_terminal_part_says_why_it_carries_no_mark has never passed since it was added in 83fc8c7 (B3). Its in-test board names the footprint \"TestPoint:TH_TestPoint\", but pcb/library/footprints.lock.json keys that entry as the BARE name \"TH_TestPoint\" (every other seed entry is keyed \"Lib:Name\" — e.g. \"Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric\"). compile_board therefore refuses with footprint_unresolved: \"footprint ref 'TestPoint:TH_TestPoint' is not in the seed library lockfile\".\n\nThe other suites that use this footprint (e.g. test_assembly_advisory.py:291) pass the bare \"TH_TestPoint\" and resolve fine.\n\nTwo candidate fixes and they are not the same decision: (a) change the test to the bare ref, which leaves the lockfile keyed inconsistently; (b) key the generated seed entry \"TestPoint:TH_TestPoint\" like every official_kicad entry and re-lock, which touches the lockfile digest surface. Pick one deliberately.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:01:04","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:38:55","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["library-lockfile","pcb","task-cycle-12","test"],"repro_steps":"cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_assembly_preview.py::test_a_one_terminal_part_says_why_it_carries_no_mark -q\n=> AssertionError: board did not compile: footprint_unresolved"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055fa7beb7057a441f84ff8d177a5","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: test_a_dialect_only_export_says_it_claims_no_tier fails on letter case — asserts \"no manufacturing tier\", note says \"NO manufacturing tier\"","description":"Found while correcting the cross-provider re-check findings (task-cycle 12). Out of scope for that commit, so filed rather than fixed.\n\npcb/worker/tests/test_order_package.py::test_a_dialect_only_export_says_it_claims_no_tier asserts `\"no manufacturing tier\" in package.manifest[\"profile\"][\"service_note\"]`, but _profile_record writes \"this export claims NO manufacturing tier: ...\" (order_package.py ~line 311). The substring check is case-sensitive, so the test fails on HEAD (5c3c272) and on every commit since the two strings diverged.\n\nThe checklist renderer has a THIRD casing of the same sentence — \"No manufacturing tier is claimed by this export: ...\" (order_package.py ~line 671). Three transcriptions of one claim is the drift shape; consider having the test (and the checklist) read the note off the one string the record carries.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:01:14","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:39:12","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["pcb","task-cycle-12","test"],"resolution":"Test was wrong on letter case; two of the three transcriptions folded into one constant (NO_SERVICE_NOTE). The preview header's compact label is deliberately left as its own phrase.","repro_steps":"cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_order_package.py::test_a_dialect_only_export_says_it_claims_no_tier -q\n=> assert 'no manufacturing tier' in \"this export claims NO manufacturing tier: ...\""}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a055fa9c6872f4a842401c2672a8c1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb docs: order-package.md still says compile warnings do NOT move preflight_status — they do since 5c3c272","description":"Found while correcting the cross-provider re-check findings (task-cycle 12). Out of scope for that commit, so filed rather than fixed.\n\npcb/docs/order-package.md, section \"What the panel shows, and the warnings nobody used to render\", states of compile warnings: \"They do NOT move `preflight_status`: that is the service talking about the order, and these are the compiler talking about the board.\"\n\nFinding 6 of the cross-provider review (commit 5c3c272) reversed that: order_package.build now computes `status = PREFLIGHT_ADVISORIES if (advisories or questions or warnings)`, and the module docstring plus the tool description say the WARNING channel of the one compilation and the one emission moves the status. The doc paragraph is now false for the order package and should be brought in line (a warning means the files are not a complete rendering of the board, so `pass` is refused).","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:01:22","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:01:22","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["docs","pcb","task-cycle-12"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0561d85057962a9a775563433d4ef","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: Go stdio sweep fails on minerva_pcb_order_package — the new tool needs the same identity carve-out export_assembly has","description":"Found while fixing the task-cycle 12 scoped run's six pytest failures. Out of scope for that commit, so filed rather than fixed.\n\n`go test ./...` from pcb/ has TWO failures, not the one known pre-existing one:\n\n1. internal/board — TestSmartRemoteV2RoundTripsUnchanged (\"the board declares no board_graphics, got 65\"). Known, pre-existing, left alone.\n2. root package — TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly. This one is NEW with task-cycle 12 and is not caused by the Python fixes in the scoped-run commit.\n\nMechanism: the sweep dispatches every agent-facing broker tool against pcb/spikes/gerber/board.yaml using only that tool's declared-schema keys. That fixture board carries no `mpn` on any component, and the default \"jlc\" profile REQUIRES mpn — so a tool that emits an assembly package is a NAMED refusal every time, correctly. main_test.go already carves out `minerva_pcb_export_assembly` for exactly this reason (see the comment block at main_test.go ~line 794, D0-5 / docket 019fc2f8b903).\n\n`minerva_pcb_order_package` was added in 9b08ff4 (B2) and is the same shape — it calls the same emitter — but was never added to that carve-out list. The sweep now flags it as a dispatch failure it is not:\n\n    assembly_missing_identity / component R1 / field assembly.mpn\n    \"component 'R1' is missing required identity field(s) mpn for assembly profile 'jlc'\"\n\nNote the refusal is well-formed all the way out (preflight readiness=blocked, blockers list carries the code/component/field) — the tool is behaving correctly; the generic sweep is asserting the wrong thing about it.\n\nThe decision this needs (which is why it is filed, not fixed): the export_assembly precedent adds a carve-out AND points at a dedicated happy-path test (TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_ExportAssembly). Doing the same for order_package means writing its dedicated stdio test too, otherwise the tool leaves the sweep with nothing replacing it. The cheaper alternative — giving the spike board an `mpn` so both tools dispatch cleanly — would change a fixture ~20 other suites read, so it is not obviously cheaper.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:39:30","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:56:54","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["go","pcb","task-cycle-12","test"],"resolution":"Sweep carve-out (test-wrong) plus dedicated stdio coverage, per the export_assembly precedent.","repro_steps":"cd pcb && go test -run TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly ./\n=> minerva_pcb_order_package: dispatch using ONLY its declared-schema keys ... failed\n   (worker error (assembly) — component 'R1' is missing required identity field(s) mpn)"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05620926771aa84c4a6aa5b95efc6","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"pcb/render-an-svg-to-look-at-it","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:42:50","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:42:50","value":"To actually LOOK at an SVG this repo generates (assembly preview, fab preview, any drawing), rasterise it with the headless Chrome that is already on this machine — there is no rsvg-convert, inkscape, ImageMagick or cairosvg here, which is why drawings kept shipping unseen:\n\ngoogle-chrome --headless --disable-gpu --no-sandbox --screenshot=OUT.png --window-size=1600,2000 --default-background-color=FFFFFFFF \"file:///abs/path/to/drawing.svg\"\n\nThen read OUT.png as an image. Set --window-size WIDER than the SVG's declared width, or you cannot tell the page's own clipping from your capture's. Read the width off the root element first.\n\nMeasured 2026-08-31 on the task-cycle 12 assembly preview: the SVG declared width=1284 and carried a 207-character header line that ran off the page — a defect three cold reviews and 534 passing tests could not see, because nothing had ever rendered it. A drawing whose purpose is being looked at needs someone to look at it.","component":"pcb","key":"render-an-svg-to-look-at-it","retrieval_count":1}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0562dd41a7ed7ab89681a9de3bb86","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: assembly-preview.svg clips its own header and draws placement labels across the lands they label","description":"Found by actually rendering the drawing (hint pcb/render-an-svg-to-look-at-it) — nobody had rendered it before. Two visible defects in a page whose entire purpose is being looked at:\n\n1) HEADER OVERFLOW. The page declared width=1284 but header prose was emitted as single unwrapped <text> lines; two sentences exceed 200 characters (the \"Outlines and lands come from the board's own geometry; …\" line is 207 chars, ~1650px at 12px DejaVu Sans) and their tails rendered past the page's own right edge. Page width is computed from the drawing/table extents only — header text never participated.\n\n2) LABEL COLLISIONS. Each placement label (\"U4S_B  90.0000°  Bottom\", font-size 1.15 board-mm) was drawn at a fixed offset from its crosshair, so on a dense footprint it lands on the pad row it labels; on assembly_anchor_override.yaml both bottom-side labels sat directly across their own lands. Confirmed by render before the fix.\n\nFIX (same commit): (1) a deliberately-wide per-glyph width estimator + greedy word wrap; header and notes prose wrap to PROSE_BUDGET_PX = the page's guaranteed minimum content width, so no page can clip its own words; meaning kept verbatim. (2) per-label placement search — candidates ring the crosshair (E/W/above/below/diagonals) at growing gaps, dodging all lands, body-outline perimeter bands, every crosshair's ink and previously placed labels; a detached label gets a thin leader. Regression tests: test_no_page_text_runs_past_the_pages_own_edge (old header estimates 1672px > 1284px page — red on the old code) and test_placement_labels_cover_no_lands_no_marks_and_not_each_other. Verified by re-rendering and looking: all six labels readable, header fully on-page.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:57:19","updated_at":"2026-08-31T04:57:24","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly-preview","pcb","python","task-cycle-12"],"resolution":"Prose wrap to the page's minimum content width + collision-searched label placement with leaders; two regression tests pin both."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05634ffe875049d4e8eb6a96ee6c2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"DEFERRED: comment-salience pass over the pcb tree — schedule only after functionality is where the owner wants it","description":"OWNER RULING 2026-08-31: \"my rule of salience stands, but we can defer it by filing a bug and use your suggestion for now. We'll come back to a salience pass much later — after we have all functionality where I want it.\"\n\nSo: the rule in Minerva/.claude/skills/task-cycle/SKILL.md is UNCHANGED and still governs. Nothing about it is relaxed. What is deferred is the SWEEP of the existing tree, and this item is that sweep.\n\nDO NOT schedule this inside a feature cycle. It is days of judgement work, it touches ~900-1000 comment lines across 405 tracked source files, and it produces zero behaviour change — running it alongside functional work would bury real defects in blame churn. It waits until the owner says functionality is where they want it.\n\nSCOPE WHEN IT RUNS. Apply the interim test (below) file by file, not tree-wide. Rewrite rather than delete: a comment carrying both mechanism and a provenance tag keeps the mechanism and the evidence, and loses the story. Expect to touch: epoch names ~446 lines, review history ~289, HITL mentions ~229, dates ~84 (overlapping), plus the 1,189 lines carrying docket-style IDs measured in 01a054d5d3cc.\n\nTHE INTERIM TEST, in force from now until this pass runs (owner-approved 2026-08-31): the question is not WHICH tokens a comment contains, but WHETHER it still reads correctly with the tag removed.\n- EVIDENCE, stays: a tag that dates or sources a number, a threshold, an external spec, or an acceptance criterion. \"0.15mm (capabilities page, re-fetched 2026-08-09)\"; \"measured against KiCad 9.0.9, 2026-08-02\".\n- NARRATIVE, goes: a tag that only says who noticed a problem and when. \"HITL-caught 2026-07-16\".\n- THIRD CASE, treat as evidence: an owner ruling attributed with its date, e.g. \"FIRST-CLASS, not Extra (owner boundary ruling 2026-07-30, docket 019fb5ad6d20)\" — a constraint attribution telling a future reader not to change the behaviour.\n\nNew comments written from now on should need no sweep: keep them to mechanism, and where a number needs provenance, cite the source rather than the story.\n\nSupersedes the sweep options in questions 01a054d5d3cc and 01a0556354ac, both now answered.","created_at":"2026-08-31T05:05:09","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:05:09","priority":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0565f591d7dcda33805fa7a2b7f2d","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: three suites red — introduced by THIS DCR's work, not pre-existing (CI caught them; the scoped set did not)","description":"ATTRIBUTION CORRECTED 2026-08-31. These were filed as \"pre-existing\" because each fix agent compared against its OWN HEAD rather than against the cycle's dispatch base. Measured properly: `tests/test_coupon_board.py` gives 20 passed at the task-cycle 11 dispatch base 6062b64, and is red now. It is OUR regression.\n\nTHE FAILURE: `TestSeedCoupon::test_bom_cpl_carry_exactly_the_two_real_parts` fails with `'dict' object has no attribute 'components'`. That is the A2a signature — BOM/CPL were moved onto a compiled ResolvedBoard and the raw-dict path retired, and this suite still hands them a raw dict. Same root cause reaches `assembly_gates.py:195`.\n\nAlso in this item, and to be re-attributed the same way: two doc-example tests failing on the `placements` fragment at docs/board-yaml.md:815 having no splicer. That fragment was ADDED by this DCR's schema work, so those are almost certainly ours too — verify against 6062b64 rather than assuming either way.\n\nHOW IT ESCAPED: my scoped execution set for cycle A named the assembly, order, gerber, compile and profile suites but never `test_coupon_board.py`, and the cycle-A push went out without checking CI. CI caught it immediately — run 33342117022 on 0181c5d, five jobs red (linux, macos, windows, oracle-with-kicad-cli, and the hermetic clean-checkout fab gate), all on this one test. It has been red on main since the cycle-A push.\n\nLESSON, worth a durable hint: \"pre-existing\" is only meaningful against the CYCLE'S DISPATCH BASE. An agent that measures against its own HEAD will call every earlier task in the same cycle \"pre-existing\", and a chain of such judgements launders a regression into the baseline. Verify in a detached worktree at the dispatch base, which costs one command.","created_at":"2026-08-31T05:51:24","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:11:27","priority":3,"severity":3,"repro_steps":"cd pcb/worker && .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_compile_board.py::test_every_yaml_example_in_board_yaml_md_compiles \"tests/test_coupon_board.py::TestSeedCoupon::test_bom_cpl_carry_exactly_the_two_real_parts\" -q"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0565f79c57c21b272c3e41d4ec478","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Give minerva_pcb_export_assembly the same expected-refusal sweep entry order_package now has","description":"TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly now keeps minerva_pcb_order_package in the sweep via expectedRefusalSubstrings (ok=false + message naming R1/mpn), so a declared-schema rename of \"yaml\" is caught. minerva_pcb_export_assembly still has the plain `continue` carve-out, so the same hidden regression (schema renames yaml -> source while the worker keeps accepting yaml) would pass its dedicated test. Move it into the expectedRefusalSubstrings map with {\"R1\", \"mpn\"} and drop the carve-out block. Out of scope for the 2026-08-30 fix round (the carve-out predates it), filed instead of fixed.","created_at":"2026-08-31T05:51:32","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:51:32","priority":3}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a056602ecc7ec093b0978718bc5df2","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"assembly_preview text widths come from embedded DejaVu advance tables","created_at":"2026-08-31T05:52:19","updated_at":"2026-08-31T05:52:19","tags":["assembly-preview","fonts","testing"],"value":"assembly_preview.py bounds text width with per-glyph DejaVu Sans 2.37 hmtx advances (padded +2%, ceil 3dp; bold table separate; mono = 0.6021 em uniform both weights; off-table glyphs = 2.05 em, wider than DejaVu's max 2.016). tests/test_assembly_preview.py embeds the EXACT (unpadded) tables as independent ground truth — never point the test at the module's tables, that recreates the circular-test defect (bug round 2026-08-30). To extend either char set, regenerate from /usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/*.ttf with fontTools (in pcb/worker/.venv): advance = hmtx[glyph][0]/unitsPerEm. Also: pcb/worker tests are a package — import as `from tests import test_assembly_preview`.","component":"pcb-worker","key":"assembly-preview-font-tables","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a056a8bdf67199b49b76351bbd54b9","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"task-cycle/pre-existing-is-measured-against-the-dispatch-base","created_at":"2026-08-31T07:11:34","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:11:34","value":"A failing test is \"pre-existing\" ONLY if it fails at the CYCLE'S DISPATCH BASE — not at the agent's own HEAD.\n\nEvery fix agent in a cycle sits on top of earlier tasks from the same cycle. If it verifies \"pre-existing\" by stashing or by testing at its own HEAD, it will correctly observe the test was already failing when it arrived — and wrongly conclude the cycle did not cause it. A chain of such judgements launders a real regression into the baseline, and the cycle closes reporting green.\n\nMeasured 2026-08-31, task-cycle 12: tests/test_coupon_board.py was called pre-existing by two separate agents. At the task-cycle 11 dispatch base 6062b64 it gives 20 passed. It was broken by A2a (BOM/CPL moved onto a compiled ResolvedBoard; that suite still passes a raw dict) and had been red on main since the cycle-A push, failing five CI jobs.\n\nRULE: verify in a detached worktree at the dispatch base — `git worktree add -q --detach <dir> <base>` then run the suite there, and `git worktree remove --force <dir>` after. Never stash to do this (the docket .dct may be dirty). Put the dispatch base SHA in every fix-round brief so the agent can check it without asking.\n\nAND: check CI after every push. A scoped set is chosen from the modules you touched; CI runs what you did not think of. Here CI had the answer within minutes and nobody looked for hours.","component":"task-cycle","key":"pre-existing-is-measured-against-the-dispatch-base"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a056ae9b6d748986710edb13d3ad91","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"REVERSE ROUND: Codex fixes, Opus reviews — the three defects task-cycle 12 stopped on","description":"OWNER RULING 2026-08-31: \"this will be our last iteration -- even if we haven't converged, let's stop. Record issues in a docket work-item. I want to do a reverse round where codex is the fixer and opus the reviewer. So, we'll file and stop when you hit a good stopping point.\"\n\nSo task-cycle 12's fix loop STOPPED here deliberately, not because it converged. The loop was narrowing — 5 findings, then 5, then 3, each more marginal — and the three below are what was open when we stopped. Nothing here is red; all three are gaps in what tests PROVE or in what the drawing SHOWS, not failures.\n\nTHE NEXT ROUND RUNS IN REVERSE: Codex is the FIXER, an Opus agent is the REVIEWER. That inversion is the point of the exercise — every round so far has had Opus (or Fable) fixing and Codex reviewing, and the two have very different failure modes worth measuring against each other.\n\n## The three defects\n\n1. DUPLICATE ZIP MEMBER NAMES PASS THE ARCHIVE TEST. `pcb/main_test.go`'s order-package test builds `memberBytes[f.Name] = raw`, so a second `AssemblyResolved-F_Cu.gbr` entry silently overwrites the first and the map still holds ten keys. An archive carrying the ten expected names PLUS a duplicate passes. Count members as a list and assert uniqueness.\n\n2. A LONG DESIGNATOR ERASES ROTATION AND SIDE FROM ITS PLACEMENT LABEL. `assembly_preview._label_body` truncates the whole combined string `<ref>  <rotation>°  <side>`, so a long enough reference ends in an ellipsis before the rotation and side are ever reached — concrete trigger: the 4001-character designator already in the test corpus. Two references sharing a retained prefix also become visually indistinguishable. The hidden data-ref and the CSV do not help someone reading the SVG as a drawing, which is the whole purpose of the page. FIX SHAPE: cap only the reference portion, then append rotation and side unconditionally.\n\n3. THE ARCHIVE VALIDITY CHECKS ARE SYNTACTIC, NOT SEMANTIC. Ten files each containing only `%FSLAX46Y46*%` and `M02*` under the expected names, plus a job file naming the right project, would pass — as would an archive with front/back or copper/mask layers swapped. The test proves the files are gerber-SHAPED, not that they are THIS BOARD's fabrication geometry. Deepening it to catch a layer swap is the useful increment; full semantic validation is not.\n\n## Not a defect, deliberately: the fallback-font limit\n\nThe width guarantee covers the glyphs we table (DejaVu Sans/Bold/Mono). A viewer whose fallback font paints an untabled glyph wider than the estimate can still overflow its line or collision box. This was an explicit instruction during the cycle — bound the PAGE, not every font on earth — and the code now states the limit plainly rather than implying a guarantee it cannot make. Page dimensions ARE bounded (measured: a 4001-character designator gives 808px, against 29,967px uncapped). Do not \"fix\" this in the reverse round; if it is ever revisited, the honest options are shipping a font or refusing untabled glyphs, both larger decisions.\n\n## For whoever runs the reverse round\n\nCycle dispatch base for attribution questions: task-cycle 11's base is 6062b64; task-cycle 12's is 0181c5d. Verify \"pre-existing\" in a detached worktree at the base, never by stashing — see hint task-cycle/pre-existing-is-measured-against-the-dispatch-base. Scoped suites for this area: test_assembly_preview, test_order_package, test_order_provenance, test_service_profile, test_assembly_anchor, test_assembly_outputs, test_assembly_gates, test_assembly_spec, test_methods, test_coupon_board, plus `go test ./...`. To LOOK at the drawing, see hint pcb/render-an-svg-to-look-at-it — headless Chrome, since this box has no other rasteriser.","created_at":"2026-08-31T07:17:58","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:24:09","priority":2,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a056b4265f70f3992d144b602ab8e2","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: sweep the corpus for legacy `assembly: exclude` boards that will refuse on their first BOM","description":"Found while repairing task-cycle 12's CI reds, and it is the kind of thing a test should have found rather than CI.\n\nA3 made an SMD do-not-populate part with `paste: auto` a named refusal — a part that is not fitted but whose footprint puts paste on its lands has no defensible default. Correct gate. But the coupon fixture `coupon_jlc1.yaml` was still carrying the pre-block scalar `assembly: exclude` on DAM1, a solder-dam structure with paste-bearing lands, so the first time anything asked it for a BOM it refused with `assembly_paste_undecided`. It took a CI failure to surface that, not a test.\n\nThe fixture is migrated now (`assembly: {populate: false, paste: include}` — `include` keeps the apertures, since a mask dam is only witnessed with solder present, and per gerber.py:579 include is the ABSENCE of exclusion so the blessed paste golden is byte-identical).\n\nWHAT REMAINS: nothing in the order path is gate-tested against a MIGRATED fixture, and the coupon was the only board in this repo still carrying the legacy scalar with paste-bearing lands. Any board outside this repo — the owner's own, anything in ccsandbox, anything a user authored before the schema change — hits the same refusal the first time someone asks for a BOM, with no warning until then.\n\nTWO PIECES OF WORK:\n1. Sweep the corpus (and ccsandbox) for components still carrying the scalar `assembly: exclude`, and report which of them sit on paste-bearing footprints. Those are the ones that will refuse.\n2. Add a gate test that runs the order path over a board authored in the LEGACY form, so the migration case is proven by a test rather than discovered by a user.\n\nRelated: 01a054c63328 (the owner question on whether legacy furniture should have to author a paste policy at all — answered \"keep the refusal, on reliability over cost\").","created_at":"2026-08-31T07:24:02","updated_at":"2026-08-31T07:24:02","priority":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0585ff81e7f45a4884021a5d9f49e","type":"question","status":"answered","title":"OPUS CODE REVIEW — PCB order-release repairs after reverse-round gap analysis","description":"Repository: /home/imran/github/minerva-plugins\nBranch: main\nBase before repair: 888e4cf\nReview target: the current UNCOMMITTED working-tree diff (`git diff -- pcb/`), excluding docs/plugins.dct and excluding the separate dirty smart-remote-v2 repository. Do not edit source during review; report findings as comments on this question.\n\nWHY THIS REVIEW EXISTS\nA gap analysis against parent DCR 01a0542d902f7683 and reverse item 01a056ae9b6d found that the order package machinery was mostly present but the release path still had one major fail-open gap and three confirmed reverse-round defects. The current diff repairs those findings and directly related release-readiness issues.\n\nREPAIRS TO REVIEW\n1. FAIL-CLOSED GEOMETRIC RELEASE GATE\n- order_package.build runs drc_geometric.run_geometric_drc over the SAME already-compiled ResolvedBoard before Gerber emission; there is no second compilation.\n- verdict=violations refuses as order_package_geometric_violations; anything other than clean/violations refuses as order_package_geometric_indeterminate.\n- the full union rides the refusal/preflight or successful preflight+manifest.\n- GC9 advisories are projected into the existing {code, component, message} report shape while raw rows remain in the geometric union.\n- counterexample pinned: a JLC-profile board with a 0.05 mm trace (0.10 mm floor) compiles but now writes no package and names gc1_trace_width.\n\n2. ARCHIVE SEMANTIC IDENTITY\n- build_archive still allowlists members, then requires exactly one .gbrjob.\n- each Gerber's embedded X2 FileFunction/FilePolarity is normalized only across the emitter's measured X2-vs-job vocabulary differences and compared with the job row assigned to that exact path.\n- PTH/NPTH drill filenames are bound to the generator's explicit role comments.\n- swapped F_Cu/B_Cu bytes and a minimal syntactic `%FSLAX...M02*` impostor now refuse as order_package_archive_semantics.\n- the Go stdio smoke independently asserts X2 functions.\nPlease look especially for valid Gerber/X2 variants this normalization would falsely reject, multi-layer behavior, malformed job-table edge cases, and whether any identity can still be laundered by coordinated renames.\n\n3. REVERSE DEFECTS\n- Go smoke detects duplicate ZIP names before map insertion and also counts raw zip entries, so 11 entries cannot collapse to 10 keys.\n- assembly-preview labels reserve the suffix budget first and truncate only the unbounded reference, preserving rotation and Top/Bottom.\n- semantic layer swaps are now refused as described above; independent parsing remains a syntax oracle, not claimed as semantic proof.\n\n4. REPORT SALIENCE\n- bounded GUI report sections stable-sort fabrication-affecting copper/drill/mask warnings above repetitive documentation-only/fab notes, while preflight.json preserves the complete original-order list.\n- test pins 13 documentation warnings followed by a copper omission and requires the copper finding to remain visible.\n\n5. RELEASE/BASELINE HYGIENE\n- plugin/runtime cache key bumped in lockstep 0.5.6 -> 0.5.7.\n- manifest and broker descriptions remain byte-identical and document the new gates.\n- the smart-remote round-trip test now accepts either legacy TP1-hung graphics or migrated board_graphics, but still requires byte-identical round trip and non-empty artwork. This was necessary because the sibling checkout changed underneath the task; its dirty board was not edited.\n\nVERIFICATION\n- Python worker suite, correct cwd: 3157 passed, 4 skipped, 3 xfailed.\n- Focused order/preview suite: 83 passed.\n- Candidate-overlay path-sensitive suite: 21 passed.\n- Go: `go test ./pcb/... ./shared/... -count=1` passed.\n- `go vet ./...` from pcb/ passed.\n- Godot GUI/MCP export parity: 89 passed, 0 failed.\n- git diff --check passed.\n\nQUESTIONS FOR OPUS\n- Does the new release gate preserve the one-compilation/one-board invariant without overstating what geometric clean proves?\n- Are refusal payloads and successful preflight/manifest records complete, JSON-safe, and consistent with existing consumers?\n- Is the X2/job semantic binding fail-closed without rejecting valid output from every supported layer stack and drill split?\n- Do the tests fail on the old behavior for the intended reason, including duplicate ZIP entries, long labels, swapped layers, and geometric violations?\n- Did report salience ordering or the external-board baseline repair weaken any prior contract?\n- Identify any blocker, should-fix issue, or missing adversarial test. If none, say APPROVE explicitly.","created_at":"2026-08-31T15:11:19","updated_at":"2026-08-31T15:50:18","directed_to":"opus","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["code-review","opus","order-package","pcb","reverse-round","task-cycle-12"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a0542d902f7683","answer":"Owner approved Opus comment 210. All blocker, should-fix, and note follow-ups were implemented and verified: profile-sensitive geometric handling, structured stable salience, frozen artwork fixtures plus optional live smoke, four-layer X2/job coverage, and strict finite JSON."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05892f3847a569570c12aeaa41011","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"registry-check certifies a registry whose download URLs 404 — it never resolves one","description":"Measured 2026-08-31. `cf6073b` shipped a registry whose pcb download URLs paired the `pcb-v0.5.6` TAG with `pcb-0.5.7` FILENAMES. Every platform returned HTTP 404; the marketplace could not install pcb at all. Fixed in abf0dcc.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENED: the `pcb-v0.5.7` release was created at 15:51:51 and the commit landed ~15:53, so `regen_registry.py` ran before that tag was visible locally. It took the version from `pcb/manifest.json` (0.5.7) and the release tag from the newest tag it could see (0.5.6), producing a URL that is internally plausible and externally dead.\n\nWHY CI DID NOT CATCH IT — this is the part worth fixing. `registry-check` PASSED on the broken commit. It verifies that `regen_registry.py` output equals the committed `registry.json`. Author and checker were blind to the same missing tag, so the check confirmed the file matched a generator that was itself wrong. It is a self-consistency check wearing the clothes of a correctness check.\n\nFIX: have `registry-check` (or the release job) issue a HEAD request per declared download URL and fail on anything that is not 200. That is a handful of requests on a file that changes rarely, and it is the only check that would have caught this class at all. Consider also asserting that `release_tag` matches the version embedded in the download filenames — a pure-string check needing no network, which would have caught this exact mismatch offline.\n\nRELATED, same shape, all found in task-cycle 12: a width test that measured with the estimator it was validating; a GUI parity fixture hand-written in the wrong reply shape so it passed while the two surfaces disagreed; an archive test that asserted member names without reading member bytes. A check that shares its subject's blind spot proves nothing, and every one of these was green.\n\nOPERATIONAL NOTE: run `git fetch --tags` before `regen_registry.py`. The tag is the input the generator cannot infer.","created_at":"2026-08-31T16:07:00","updated_at":"2026-08-31T16:07:00","severity":2}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a058c1e8f67458b9eb7b18bf05ce30","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"smart-remote-v2 sourcing: U3 and L1 both RESOLVED 2026-08-31","description":"Measured 2026-08-31 against JLC's LOGGED-IN parts library — the inventory a PCBA order actually draws from, not LCSC's. Both blockers are now closed.\n\n=== U3 — RESOLVED, via rev B ===\nC2840615 (MSM261S4030H0R) was 0 In Stock, Extended, pre-order min 5 / $9.17, \"Assembly Difficulty: High\". Octopart finds it at no distributor; it is LCSC-only. Digi-Key carries ZERO active top-port I2S mics — that category has effectively ended. Two TDK I2S candidates (ICS-43432, ICS-43434) are both manufacturer-marked EOL while JLC's listing showed no lifecycle marker at all.\n\nDECISION: rev to SPK0641HT4H-1 (JLC C5159510, in stock, $1.6478-2.8058). JLC brands it Knowles; it is SYNTIANT since the MEMS-mic line was sold. Same part — every JLC spec matches the Syntiant datasheet Rev D-1.\nAn UPGRADE, not a compromise: SNR 64.5 vs 61 dB(A), THD 0.1% vs 1%, PSR -90 vs -72 dBFS(A) (18 dB better, and this rail comes off a switching buck), RF SHIELDED (the incumbent claims nothing) beside an ESP32 antenna, low-power 270 vs 400 uA, plus a 26 uA sleep the incumbent lacks.\nCosts a board rev: different pinout AND different land pattern. Tracked as smart-remote:01a05902e07b. Requires JLC STANDARD assembly (the part is \"Standard Only\", X-ray required), which the exporter will need a jlcpcb-standard profile for. SW7 dropped by the owner in the same decision.\n\nSUPPORTING PART: the datasheet forbids Class 2 dielectrics near the mic (piezoelectric). Both caps on +3V3_SW_MIC are Class 2 — C13 (100nF X7R) and C14 (10uF X5R). 10uF does not exist in C0G at a sane size, so: C0G for the local decoupler, distance for the bulk.\n100nF C0G/NP0 exists at JLC but ONLY IN 1206 — no 0805 option. CHOSEN: C170182 (Walsin 1206N104J500CT, 100nF 50V NP0 ±5%, 222,302 in stock, $0.2334 at 1+). The forced 1206 is convenient: the board already uses 1206 for JP1-JP6 and F1, and 1206 sits ABOVE the owner's \"minimum passive package = 0805\" hand-rework policy.\n\n=== L1 — RESOLVED 2026-08-31, and it is a DROP-IN ===\nC2838247 (Chilisin BWVF004040182R2M00) is 0 In Stock, Extended, pre-order. Without it there is no 3.3V rail.\n\nDECISION: substitute C2846183 (Sunlord AMWPH4018S2R2MT), IN STOCK, $0.1134-0.1775.\n\nTHE LAND-PATTERN CHECK PASSES — this was the gating item and it is now measured, not assumed. From the Sunlord AMWPH4018S datasheet Table 3-1: body 4.0 x 4.0 x 1.8 mm (identical to the Chilisin original), terminals 3.3 x 1.1 mm, recommended land a=1.2 / b=1.8 / c=3.7 mm. Either reading of a and b puts the PAD CENTRES AT +/-1.5 mm. The board's footprint (Inductor_SMD:L_Vishay_IFSC-1515AH_4x4x1.8mm) has them at +/-1.475 mm. A 25 um difference against ~1.8 mm pads — 1.4% of pad width, inside fab tolerance, invisible to the joint. NO BOARD CHANGE NEEDED.\n\nElectrically better than the original too: 4A Isat vs 3A, 2.55A rms vs 2.7A (irrelevant — real load is ~1.2A out), DCR 50 vs 47 mOhm. And it is an AEC-Q200D G1 AUTOMOTIVE series, -40 to +125 C, MSL 1.\nRunner-up if more margin is ever wanted: C98361 (Sunlord WPN4020H2R2MT, 4.3A rms / 7.6A Isat, 48 mOhm, $0.1004-0.1428) — but 4.0 x 4.0 x 2.0 mm, 0.2 mm taller.\n\n=== STILL WATCHING ===\nC12 (C6120014) at 183 In Stock on JLC, matching LCSC exactly — genuine depletion from the sheet's 2,391 on 2026-08-20. One per board so a small run survives, but it is the most expensive passive; order sooner rather than later.\nThe sheet's C17407 warning is CORRECT and live: LCSC marks it verbatim \"Not recommended for new designs\". Replacement C149504 is clean. No other BOM part carries any lifecycle marker.","created_at":"2026-08-31T16:58:18","updated_at":"2026-08-31T19:13:13","priority":1,"parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545d16e870f796b80cd7fb313125"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0597b889872cba4cbae40fd4f0c74","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"B5 BLOCKER: stamp U1S assembly.placements — a missing key silently ships BOM qty 1 and one CPL row between the socket rows","description":"Found while checking rev B footprint coverage, 2026-08-31. The plugin side is READY (B0b landed anchor_mm); the BOARD DATA is what is missing.\n\nWHAT U1S IS\nOne drawn component, footprint Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1_SocketSet_2x22_THT (44 pads), standing for TWO physical 1x22 socket strips: 2x Hong Cheng HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P, LCSC C41376161, THT/wave soldered. The BOM sheet already says \"Qty 2 is correct for ordering\".\n\nMEASURED STATE OF THE BOARD\n~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml — NO component carries an `assembly` block at all, U1S included. U1S has only ref/footprint/value/x_mm/y_mm/rotation_deg/layer/pins/properties.\n\nTHE SILENT-FAILURE TRAP (assembly_spec.py, \"AUTHORED-EMPTY IS NOT ABSENT\")\n- `placements: []` -> expansion_authored set -> the ORDER GATE REFUSES. Loud and safe.\n- NO `placements` key -> not authored -> resolves to ONE implicit part under the component's own ref -> NO REFUSAL.\n\nSo stamping mpn/dist_part_numbers onto U1S for B5 while omitting placements produces, with no warning:\n- BOM: qty 1 (one socket strip where the board needs two)\n- CPL: a single row\n\nWHERE THAT ROW LANDS (measured from the footprint, not assumed)\n  row x = -11.430   22 pads   centre y = 26.670\n  row x = +11.430   22 pads   centre y = 26.670\n  pad-field centre an implicit part inherits: (0.0000, 26.6700)\nDead between the rows, 11.43mm from each, on neither. One socket strip, placed where no socket goes. (The fab-box centre is different again — y=30.8485 — because the courtyard is deliberately asymmetric in Y to cover the module body overhang past the pin field.)\n\nACTION FOR B5\nWhen stamping identities through the MCP lane, give U1S an assembly block with TWO placements at x offsets -11.43 and +11.43, each with its own anchor_mm at that row's centre, mpn HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P, dist_part_numbers [C41376161].\n\nACCEPTANCE — verify BEFORE uploading to JLC, do not infer from a clean gate:\n1. The BOM row for the socket reads qty 2.\n2. The CPL contains TWO rows for U1S, at the two row centres, not one at x=0.\n\nRELATED OBSERVATION (not filed, offered): the order gate could plausibly ADVISE when a footprint's pads form disjoint clusters but no expansion is authored — the signal is detectable and would have caught this without anyone looking. Deliberately not proposed as a refusal: a board mid-layout must still load, compile and gerber.","created_at":"2026-08-31T20:21:03","updated_at":"2026-08-31T20:21:03","tags":["assembly","b5","pcb","silent-failure","smart-remote-v2"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a0545d16e8"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05a0b1f857115a7b93e925ab6d929","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"PCB editor drops F.Fab on load — the marker vanishes, and a promoted export would silently change the CPL anchor basis","description":"Found 2026-08-31 while adding a pin-1 marker to the Sunlord inductor for smart-remote-v2 rev B: the marker was correct in the library and in KiCad's own render, but simply not present in the editor.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A MISSING DOT\n\nassembly_anchor resolves the CPL placement point by trying FAB extent first, then LANDS, then ORIGIN. The canonical board YAML on disk carries NO `graphics` key on any component, so the compiler resolves geometry from the LIBRARY and the basis is `fab_outline` — verified for L1 and U3 on the current board. That is correct today.\n\nBut the compiler PREFERS board-carried graphics when they are present. That is the `footprint_from_board` static warning: \"geometry came from the board's own pads/graphics and is taken as complete; the library was not consulted ... so no footprint feature outside this list is fabricated\".\n\nSo if an editor export were ever promoted into the canonical YAML, every component would then carry graphics WITHOUT F.Fab, and the anchor basis would fall back fab_outline -> lands with no warning. For a part whose pads are symmetric about the footprint origin both bases give the same point, so nothing looks wrong. For an ASYMMETRIC part the placement moves, and it moves ONLY in the CPL — invisible on the canvas, invisible to DRC, visible for the first time at the assembly house.\n\nThat is the same failure shape as the -0.0925mm mic anchor bug fixed in minerva-plugins d7ffc54/4976d50, where an F.Fab pin-1 marker poking past the body outline shifted every placed part.\n\nSCOPE AS REQUESTED: make the editor carry F.Fab through load and export, so library fab geometry survives the round trip and fab-layer markers are visible.\n\nWORTH CONSIDERING FIRST, though — it may be the better fix: the root cause is that board-carried graphics SHADOW the library at all. An alternative is for the compiler to always resolve footprint-derived geometry from the library whenever a component has a footprint ref, and treat board-carried graphics as a fallback for inline/refless parts only. That fixes the anchor hazard for every layer at once rather than for F.Fab specifically, and would make the editor's omission cosmetic. Owner/implementer should pick between the two rather than assume the narrower one.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n1. Load a board whose components resolve library footprints carrying F.Fab; export it; F.Fab graphics are present in the exported components.\n2. A footprint with an ASYMMETRIC pad field resolves the same assembly_anchor basis and the same CPL x/y whether the board carries graphics or not — this is the test that actually guards the defect, and it needs a deliberately asymmetric fixture, not a symmetric one like L1 or U3 which cannot fail.\n\nRelated: hint pcb/editor-drops-f-fab-and-that-can-move-the-anchor; hint pcb/land-pattern-letters-read-the-figure.","created_at":"2026-08-31T22:57:53","updated_at":"2026-08-31T22:57:53","tags":["anchor","cpl","editor","f-fab","pcb","silent-failure"],"repro_steps":"1. Load ~/gitlab/ccsandbox/smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml with minerva_pcb_load_board.\n2. minerva_pcb_export_yaml on the same editor.\n3. Inspect component L1's `graphics` in the exported document.\n\nOBSERVED: {'F.SilkS': 2, 'F.CrtYd': 4}. No F.Fab.\nEXPECTED: the library footprint Sunlord:L_Sunlord_AMWPH4018_4x4x1.8mm carries 5 F.Fab items — 4 body-outline lines plus a pin-1 dot at (-0.35, 1.45).\n\nThe parser is not at fault: parse_kicad_mod on the same file returns F.Fab: 5, F.SilkS: 2, F.CrtYd: 4, with the circle intact. The loss is in the editor's component model."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05dd4a5f978eab4c396b7eb59d05c","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Part-orientation oracle: compute connector opening direction from the CPL and gate the order against declared intent","description":"PROBLEM\n\nPart orientation is a blind spot. Geometric DRC, connectivity DRC, the assembly gates and the\nacceptance checks all pass on a board whose connectors open the wrong way. assembly_outputs.py\nsays so itself, in its own words: one of its two declared non-claims is \"this is not a promise a\npart mounts right-side-up\".\n\nEvery orientation error found on smart-remote-v2 rev B was found by a human looking at JLCPCB's\n3D preview, or by the owner physically handling parts. That is the only detector we have, it is\nmanual, it happens after the package is built, and it is unreliable in both directions: the owner\nread J2 as wrong when it was right, and I read J2/J4/J5 wrong from the same render before\narithmetic corrected me.\n\nTHE INSIGHT: THE ANSWER IS ALREADY IN THE CPL, EXACTLY\n\nThe CPL's \"Mid X\"/\"Mid Y\" differ from the component's board x,y by exactly the footprint's F.Fab\nbbox centre rotated by the placement (the assembly_anchor). That offset vector is computable, and\nfor every side-entry JST connector in our seed library it lies along footprint-local +y, which is\nALSO the direction the housing opens. So:\n\n    THE CPL CENTROID OFFSET VECTOR POINTS WHERE THE CONNECTOR OPENS.\n\nMeasured on smart-remote-v2 rev B, exact to the emitted precision, four independent parts:\n\n    ref  F.Fab centre   rot   CPL offset (board frame)   opening\n    J5   (0, +1.800)     0     (0, +1.8)                 south\n    J4   (0, +0.600)    90     (+0.6, 0)                 east\n    J3   (0, +0.600)   180     (0, -0.6)                 north\n    J2   (0, +0.600)   270     (-0.6, 0)                 west\n\nSolving local (0,m) -> board (m*sin@, m*cos@) in board coords (x right, y down) fits all four,\nwhich independently re-derives the documented rotation contract in assembly_outputs.py:91\n(\"Rotation is the authored rotation_deg verbatim (modulo 360) - which reads as JLC's\ncounter-clockwise-positive convention precisely because Y is negated\"). The exporter is CORRECT;\nthis DCR is not a bug fix.\n\nApplied to rev B it immediately found a real defect no gate caught: J3 opens NORTH, into the\nboard, where the sheet says \"Opens DOWN\".\n\nWHY NOT JUST TRUST F.Fab GEOMETRY\n\nBecause we tried and it was INVERTED. An \"which body edge is drawn open\" heuristic got J2\nbackwards and J4 wrong. The direction must come from the pads (a relative, rotation-invariant\nfact), never from an absolute axis claim.\n\nPROPOSED CHANGE\n\n1. ORIENTATION FACT, per footprint, derived from pads not axes. Establish and record, for each\n   footprint that has a meaningful facing, the opening/facing direction as a LOCAL vector\n   expressed relative to identifiable pads (e.g. \"away from the signal-pad row\"). For our four\n   JST connectors this is uniformly local +y (signal pads at -y, MP tabs at +y across\n   PH2/PH4/PH5/XH4 - verified per-part, NOT assumed as a family rule, because EasyEDA's own\n   library draws S2B/XH Y-flipped relative to S4B/S5B).\n\n2. DECLARED INTENT IN THE BOARD YAML. Today the only record of \"J2 opens left\" is a BOM\n   spreadsheet, and J4's entry there is already stale (\"Opens DOWN\" vs an owner-confirmed east).\n   Intent belongs next to the component, in the source of truth.\n\n3. THE CHECK. Compute each facing part's opening direction from the composed placement and\n   compare against declared intent. Mismatch is a hard finding.\n\n4. THE GATE. Fail the order package on mismatch, the same way the assembly gates already refuse\n   on a missing mpn. A board that opens a connector into its own interior should not reach a\n   board house.\n\n5. MCP SURFACE, per the standing rule that new features are reachable from MCP on day one.\n   Report computed vs declared facing for every part, so the owner can see it without a render.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- Given rev B as-is, the check reports J3 as a mismatch (computed north, declared south) and\n  passes J2 (computed west, declared left/west).\n- The oracle is derived from cpl.csv + footprint geometry only. No 3D model, no render, no\n  network, no human in the loop.\n- Rotating a component 90/180/270 in the board moves the computed facing correspondingly.\n- A footprint with no declared facing is skipped, not guessed at.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE\n\n- Deciding whether a given opening direction is a good idea. This checks intent-vs-reality, not\n  taste.\n- Non-connector orientation (polarised passives, IC pin 1). Those need a different fact; see the\n  board-3D-export DCR for the visual complement.\n\nRELATED\n\n- The board-3D-export DCR is the visual complement: this one is exact but narrow, that one is\n  broad but requires a human to look.\n- assembly_outputs.py:84-98 carries the frame/rotation contract and must not be touched by this\n  work; the oracle consumes it, it does not redefine it.\n- orientation_convention in footprints.lock.json is NOT the hook for this. It is a reserved empty\n  slot: bless.py _blank_assembly() hardcodes it to None and nothing in the tree ever reads it.\n  Either give it a real meaning here or leave it alone deliberately.\n\nTEST-COVERAGE GAP FOUND WHILE INVESTIGATING\n\ntests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_anchor_override.yaml sets rotation_deg: 0 on all six\nplacements, including U4S whose PARENT is at rotation 90. So no test covers a placement whose\nrotation differs from its parent's - which is exactly the U1S_A/U1S_B case that shipped wrong.\nWorth a fixture that varies child rotation independently of parent rotation.","created_at":"2026-09-01T16:36:52","updated_at":"2026-09-01T16:36:52","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","drc","order-gate","orientation","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05dd52d11735888097f14c5c3aa06","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Build and export a 3D model of the board, assembling vendor part models fetched by LCSC number","description":"PROBLEM\n\nThe only 3D view of our boards belongs to JLCPCB, and it only exists after a package has been\nbuilt and uploaded. It cannot be scripted, cannot be diffed, cannot run in CI, and cannot be\nreached before we commit to a vendor. Yet every orientation defect on smart-remote-v2 rev B was\ncaught there and nowhere else.\n\nWe also cannot inspect a part's real shape while designing. That gap produced two separate errors\nin one session: a land pattern authored from a table when the figure said otherwise (the Sunlord\na/b swap), and a connector opening direction inferred from a drawn body outline that turned out\nto be INVERTED.\n\nWHAT WAS PROVEN ON THE FLY\n\nDuring the rev B audit I built a throwaway version of this and it worked, with a property worth\nkeeping: IT REQUIRED NO 3D BINARIES IN THE REPOSITORY. LCSC/EasyEDA serve the same vendor models\nJLCPCB's own viewer renders, publicly, keyed by LCSC part number - the number we ALREADY carry in\nassembly.dist_part_numbers.\n\n    https://easyeda.com/api/products/<LCSC>/components?version=6.4.19.5\n        -> .result.packageDetail.dataStr.shape[] : PAD~ / TRACK~ / SOLIDREGION~ / SVGNODE~ records.\n           Coordinates are in units of 10 mil = 0.254 mm (verify against a known pitch).\n           The SVGNODE record (strip \"SVGNODE~\", the remainder is JSON) carries .attrs.uuid,\n           plus c_origin and c_rotation for placing the model against the footprint.\n    https://modules.easyeda.com/3dmodel/<uuid>\n        -> Wavefront OBJ, in millimetres. ~270 KB for a connector. No auth, no cookies.\n\nFetched, placed and cross-sectioned this way, the models answered questions nothing else could:\nthe true opening direction of four JST connectors, and - decisively - that C41376161's 22 pads\nspan 53.34 mm along X and 0 along Y, so the ESP32 socket strip's native long axis is HORIZONTAL.\nThat single fact explained why U1S_A/U1S_B shipped crosswise to their own holes.\n\nPROPOSED CHANGE\n\nAdd \"build and export a 3D model of the board\" as a first-class capability.\n\n1. GEOMETRY WE ALREADY OWN. Board outline, cutouts, copper and drills come from the board YAML /\n   compiled IR. This part needs no network at all and should work standalone when part models are\n   unavailable.\n\n2. PART MODELS FETCHED ON DEMAND, BY LCSC NUMBER, CACHED OUTSIDE GIT. Never vendored, never\n   committed - this respects the no-FCIB rule by construction, since the models are obtainable\n   from a well-known channel keyed by data the board already carries. Cache in the worker's\n   scratch area with the fetch keyed by part number so a rebuild is reproducible.\n\n3. PLACEMENT FROM THE COMPOSED TRANSFORM, reusing the SAME arithmetic the CPL uses, so the render\n   and the position file cannot disagree. A part that renders wrong here renders wrong at the\n   board house.\n\n4. GRACEFUL ABSENCE. A part with no LCSC number, or no upstream model, renders as its courtyard\n   prism rather than failing the export. Missing models are reported, not fatal.\n\n5. EXPORT FORMAT. A widely-readable mesh (glTF/GLB or OBJ) so it opens in any viewer, plus\n   whatever the in-app CAD surface wants. STEP is desirable but should not gate the first cut.\n\n6. MCP SURFACE from day one, per the standing rule, and a GUI affordance - the owner is GUI-only\n   and cannot call MCP, so parity means both.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH BUILDING RATHER THAN LEANING ON JLCPCB\n\n- It runs BEFORE a vendor is chosen, and before an order exists.\n- It is scriptable, so a cross-section or a measurement is a command, not a squint. Reading\n  JLCPCB's raster render directly caused me to misidentify contact beams seen through an open\n  mouth as solder tails, and to reverse three connectors before arithmetic corrected me.\n- It gives the mechanical side of the design something to check enclosure fit against.\n- It is vendor-neutral.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- Exporting rev B produces a mesh containing the board and the placed parts, openable in a\n  standard viewer.\n- A component rotated in the board is rotated in the export, consistently with its CPL row.\n- With the network unavailable, the export still succeeds with courtyard prisms and reports which\n  parts lacked models.\n- No 3D binary is added to any repository.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE\n\n- Accurate materials, finishes or photorealism.\n- Enclosure/mechanical CAD import.\n- Replacing the orientation oracle DCR: this one is broad but needs a human to look, that one is\n  exact and machine-checkable. They are complements.\n\nRELATED\n\n- The part-orientation oracle DCR (exact, narrow, gateable).\n- The Sunlord land-pattern and connector-opening errors that motivated this both came from\n  reading documents instead of geometry.","created_at":"2026-09-01T16:37:26","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:41:39","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["3d","assembly","export","fcib-free","pcb","visual-review"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05dd7a6757a5d93e9fd45b00e4879","type":"test","status":"draft","title":"Synthetic-part expansion: cover placement rotation composed against a rotated parent (U1S to U1S_A/U1S_B)","description":"THE GAP, MEASURED\n\ntests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_anchor_override.yaml sets rotation_deg: 0 on ALL SIX\nplacements it declares (lines 64, 65, 79, 80, 93, 94) - including U4S_A/U4S_B, whose PARENT is at\nrotation_deg: 90 (line 74).\n\nSo every existing placement case has child rotation 0. Nothing in the suite exercises a placement\nwhose rotation differs from its parent's, and nothing pins the COMPOSITION of the two. The one\nfixture that could have caught it instead models the exact shape that shipped wrong, so it reads\nas an endorsement of the pattern.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED WRONG BECAUSE OF IT (smart-remote-v2 rev B)\n\nU1S is one drawn component (Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1_SocketSet_2x22_THT) that expands to two\nphysical 1x22 socket strips, U1S_A and U1S_B. Its two assembly.placements carry NO rotation_deg.\nPer assembly_spec.py:255 a missing rotation_deg becomes 0.0, so each child composes to the\nparent's own rotation - 180 - and both CPL rows emitted Rotation 180.0000.\n\nThat is wrong, and the reason is only visible from part data: the physical strip is LCSC\nC41376161 (HC-PM254-8.5H-1X22PZ), whose 22 pads span 53.34 mm along X and 0.00 mm along Y, so its\nNATIVE LONG AXIS IS HORIZONTAL at rotation 0. Our board's socket rows run along board Y. At the\nemitted 180 the strips render crosswise to their own holes. Correct composed rotation is 90 or\n270 (the strip is mechanically symmetric, so either).\n\nNothing caught it. It was found by the owner looking at JLCPCB's 3D preview and hand-rotating the\nparts in the web editor - a fix that does not propagate back, so a regenerated package silently\nreverts to the defect.\n\nTESTS TO ADD\n\n1. COMPOSITION, the core case. Parent at rotation R, placement with explicit rotation_deg D ->\n   the child's CPL row carries (R + D) mod 360. Parameterise over R and D from {0, 90, 180, 270}\n   with at least one case where D is not 0 and R is not 0, and one wrap case (180 + 270 = 90).\n   This is the assertion that would have failed on rev B.\n\n2. DEFAULT IS INHERIT, pinned deliberately rather than by accident. A placement with NO\n   rotation_deg composes to the parent's rotation exactly. This behaviour is fine, but it is the\n   silent half of the defect, so it should be an explicit, named expectation rather than an\n   emergent one.\n\n3. OFFSET AND ROTATION COMPOSE TOGETHER. A rotated child at a non-zero offset_mm lands where the\n   parent's rotation puts it AND carries its own composed rotation - the two must not be applied\n   in the wrong order or one of them dropped. Use distinguishable offsets (not symmetric about\n   the parent origin) so an order error cannot cancel out.\n\n4. FIXTURE FIX. Give the existing assembly_anchor_override.yaml, or a sibling, at least one\n   placement whose rotation_deg is non-zero under a rotated parent, so the worked example stops\n   teaching rotation_deg: 0 as the norm.\n\nORACLE\n\nThe composed rotation is independently checkable and must not be asserted against the\nimplementation's own arithmetic. Anchor it to the documented contract: CPL Rotation is the\nauthored rotation verbatim modulo 360, reading as JLC's counter-clockwise-positive convention\nprecisely because Y is negated (assembly_outputs.py:91). An independent confirmation of that\nconvention was obtained on rev B by solving the anchor offsets: local (0,m) -> board\n(m*sin@, m*cos@) fit all four board connectors exactly, at rotations 0/90/180/270.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE\n\n- Whether a part's native axis matches the parent footprint's pad-row axis. That is a real and\n  separate gap - nothing in the tree knows a part's native orientation, and\n  orientation_convention in footprints.lock.json is an inert placeholder (bless.py\n  _blank_assembly() hardcodes it to None; nothing reads it). Tracked under the part-orientation\n  oracle DCR.\n- Side/flip composition for bottom-side placements, unless it falls out cheaply.","created_at":"2026-09-01T16:40:09","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:45","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","coverage-gap","pcb","placements","regression","rotation"],"component":"pcb","test_setup":"pcb worker test suite, pytest. Fixture lives alongside\ntests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_anchor_override.yaml. Oracle is the CPL row's Rotation\ncolumn produced by assembly_outputs, whose frame/rotation contract is documented at\nassembly_outputs.py:88-98 and docs/assembly-outputs.md.","expected_result":"A placement with rotation_deg D under a parent at rotation R emits a CPL row with\nRotation (R + D) mod 360, offsets composed in the correct order. Re-running the rev B board with\nrotation_deg: 270 on both U1S placements yields U1S_A and U1S_B at Rotation 90, with the strips\nalong board Y. The pre-fix board (no rotation_deg authored) yields 180 and must be distinguishable\nfrom the fixed one by assertion, not by eye."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05de4bddd78f9be7271064c764452","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Seed footprints drawn 180 deg from the LCSC canonical orientation place parts rotated; CPL carries no per-footprint orientation offset","description":"WHAT IS WRONG\n\nThe CPL's Rotation column is not self-contained. A pick-and-place machine composes it with the\npart's CANONICAL orientation as the vendor's own library defines it. Our seed footprints are\nauthored independently - from datasheets, SnapEDA exports, KiCad libraries - and nothing requires\nor checks that they are drawn in the same rotation as the LCSC part. Where they differ, every CPL\nrow for that footprint is off by the difference, and the part is assembled rotated.\n\nNEITHER DRAWING IS \"WRONG\", WHICH IS WHY NOTHING CATCHES IT\n\nFor U3 the datasheet (SPK0641HT4H-1 Rev D-1, page 7, Example Land Pattern) numbers pin 1 at the\nlower-left inner pad, and OUR footprint matches it pin for pin, all eight. EasyEDA's C5159510\ndraws the same part rotated 180 - their pin 1 is upper-right, their acoustic port marker moves\nwith it, so their pin-1-to-port relationship is preserved. Both are self-consistent, correct\ndrawings of the same part. The defect is the RELATIONSHIP between our drawing and theirs, and no\nsingle-footprint check can see it.\n\nSEVERITY: THIS SHIPS BOARDS THAT DO NOT WORK\n\n- On J2/J3 (side-entry JST) a 180 error puts the signal leads on the mounting-tab lands. Visible\n  in the 3D preview, catchable by a human, and the owner did catch it.\n- ON U3 IT IS INVISIBLE. The SPK0641HT4H-1's pad field is 180-rotationally symmetric: pads 1<->3\n  and 2<->4 swap onto each other and the four ground bars map onto each other, so EVERY LEAD\n  STILL LANDS ON A PAD. The acoustic port is also symmetric under the swap (opposite pin 1 in\n  both drawings), so the render looks right too. What actually changes is function:\n      pad 1 DATA   <-> pad 3 SELECT\n      pad 2 CLOCK  <-> pad 4 Vdd\n  Vdd is driven onto the CLOCK pin. Geometric DRC, connectivity DRC, the assembly gates, the\n  acceptance checks and the 3D render ALL PASS.\n\nWHY THE EXISTING FIELD DOES NOT HELP\n\nfootprints.lock.json carries orientation_convention on every entry, but it is inert: bless.py\n_blank_assembly() hardcodes it to None so the census \"reserved slots exist\" predicate holds, and\nNOTHING IN THE TREE EVER READS IT. All 42 entries are null. It is the right place for this fact\nand it currently holds nothing.\n\nNOT A DEFECT IN THE ROTATION MATH\n\nassembly_outputs.py's frame/rotation contract is correct and was independently re-derived during\nthis investigation: solving the anchor offsets against F.Fab centres fits all four board\nconnectors exactly at 0/90/180/270, confirming \"Rotation is the authored rotation_deg verbatim\n(modulo 360) - which reads as JLC's counter-clockwise-positive convention precisely because Y is\nnegated\" (assembly_outputs.py:91). Do not go looking for a sign error in _walk; there isn't one.\n\nPROPOSED FIX\n\n1. Give orientation_convention a real meaning: the rotation, in degrees, from OUR footprint's\n   drawing to the vendor canonical orientation for the part it is blessed against. Populate it by\n   measuring, not by asserting.\n2. Have the CPL export add it to the emitted rotation.\n3. Make the order gate REFUSE, not warn, when a component carries an LCSC part number and its\n   footprint's orientation_convention is null - unknown must be loud, per the same principle as\n   assembly_spec's \"AUTHORED-EMPTY IS NOT ABSENT\". Today unknown is silently treated as zero.\n4. Measure the offset automatically at bless time where the part has an LCSC number, using the\n   pad-number matching in the repro steps, and record it.\n\nWORKAROUND UNTIL THEN\n\nRotating the part in JLCPCB's web editor fixes that one order (the copper never moves, so the\nleads simply meet their pads) but DOES NOT PROPAGATE BACK - regenerating the package silently\nreverts. Do not treat a JLC-side rotation as a fix.\n\nDO NOT \"FIX\" THIS BY ROTATING THE COMPONENT IN THE BOARD. Rotating the component rotates our\ncopper and carries the nets with it, which moves the problem rather than solving it and forces a\nre-route. The compensation belongs in the export.\n\nBOARD ACTION FOR rev B: J2, J3 and U3 each need +180 applied at export (or by hand at JLC, per\norder). J4 and J5 need nothing. U3 MUST BE RESOLVED BEFORE ORDERING - it is the one that cannot\nbe seen.\n\nRELATED\n- DCR 01a05dd4a5f9 (part-orientation oracle) - complementary: that one checks opening direction\n  against declared intent, this one is about the part's own canonical frame.\n- DCR 01a05dd52d11 (build/export a 3D model of the board).\n- Test 01a05dd7a675 (synthetic-part rotation coverage).","created_at":"2026-09-01T16:54:27","updated_at":"2026-09-01T16:59:51","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["assembly","cpl","misassembly","orientation","pcb","rev-b","seed-library"],"environment":"smart-remote-v2 rev B, fab/smart-remote-v2-jlcpcb-economic, JLCPCB PCBA Economic.\npcb seed library at pcb/library/footprints, lock at pcb/library/footprints.lock.json.","repro_steps":"1. Export the smart-remote-v2 rev B order package (fab/smart-remote-v2-jlcpcb-economic).\n2. Upload gerbers + bom.csv + cpl.csv to JLCPCB PCBA and open the 3D preview.\n3. Look at J2 (CPL 270) and J3 (CPL 180): the rendered part's leads do NOT sit on their copper\n   pads - the signal leads land on the mounting-tab pads and vice versa. Rotating each by 180 in\n   JLC's web editor makes the leads meet their pads and match the silk.\n4. J4 (CPL 90) and J5 (CPL 0) render correctly and need no rotation.\n\nMEASURING THE CAUSE (reproducible, no render needed):\nCompare our footprint's numbered pad positions against the LCSC part's own footprint, matched by\npad number, after removing each set's pad centroid, over rotations 0/90/180/270:\n\n  curl -A '<browser UA>' \"https://easyeda.com/api/products/<LCSC>/components?version=6.4.19.5\"\n  -> .result.packageDetail.dataStr : head.x/head.y is the origin, shape[] PAD~ records are\n     PAD~shape~x~y~w~h~layer~net~NUMBER~... in units of 10 mil = 0.254 mm.\n\nResult (script kept at ~/.claude/jobs/a9a929fe/tmp/libcmp.py):\n\n  ref  LCSC        our footprint vs LCSC canonical   max err\n  J2   C265102     180 deg                           0.0002 mm\n  J3   C265104     180 deg                           0.0002 mm\n  J4   C295747       0 deg                           0.0000 mm\n  J5   C161861       0 deg                           0.0000 mm\n  U3   C5159510    180 deg                           0.0626 mm\n\nThe offset column predicts the observed misplacement EXACTLY: the two parts measured at 180 are\nthe two the owner had to rotate, and the two measured at 0 are the two that rendered correctly."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e0f0c817b83a85fd4a33f933b29","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Differential footprint verification: measure our footprint against the vendor part, record the rotation offset, apply it at export, and demote bless to the no-reference path","description":"THE DESIGN CHANGE IN ONE LINE\n\nBless asks \"does a human vouch for this artifact?\". It should ask \"does an independent source\nagree with this artifact?\". Change the question, and the same computation that answers it also\nproduces the per-part rotation offset that bug 01a05de4bddd needs. Two problems, one mechanism.\n\nWHY ATTESTATION FAILED - MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED\n\nbless.py tiers on PROVENANCE: official_kicad is auto, hand_authored is a MANDATORY human check\nbecause \"WE typed the numbers\". Both halves misfired on smart-remote-v2 rev B.\n\n  entry                                   source_kind      tier    measured offset\n  Connector_JST:JST_PH_S4B-PH-SM4-TB      official_kicad   auto    180   (J2)\n  Connector_JST:JST_PH_S5B-PH-SM4-TB      official_kicad   auto    180   (J3)\n  Package_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23               official_kicad   auto    180   (Q1,Q2)\n  Package_TO_SOT_SMD:TSOT-23-6            official_kicad   auto    270   (U4)\n  Package_DFN_QFN:VQFN-16-1EP_3x3mm       official_kicad   auto    270   (U2)\n  Syntiant:SPK0641HT4H-1_TopPort_LGA-8    hand_authored    human   180   (U3)\n\n- WHERE BLESS SKIPPED THE HUMAN is where five of six defects live. The auto tier's rationale\n  (\"upstream KiCad libraries are reviewed and mass-verified\") is true about LAND PATTERNS and\n  silent about ORIENTATION, which is what broke.\n- WHERE BLESS DEMANDED A HUMAN it caught nothing that mattered. The Sunlord AMWPH4018 land was\n  hand-authored, mandatory tier, and WRONG - pads authored 1.2mm wide when the datasheet figure\n  says 1.8 - and it passed. It was found later by re-reading the figure.\n- The one thing the system did catch, U3's F.Fab anchor error, was caught by a NUMBER in the fact\n  table (body_bbox reading 3.22 instead of 3.00), not by anyone looking at the rendered picture.\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL REASON: every one of these defects lives in a RELATIONSHIP - our drawing vs the\ndatasheet, our drawing vs the vendor's drawing - and bless examines a single artifact. A gate that\nlooks at one artifact cannot see a relationship no matter who is looking or how carefully.\n\nSecond-order problem: the standing guideline is now to author footprints from datasheets rather\nthan adopt third-party libraries. Under the current tiering that makes every new footprint\nhand_authored, i.e. MANDATORY human bless. The better authoring rule is taxed by the weaker gate.\n\nTHE OFFSET IS NOT INFERABLE - MEASURING IS THE ONLY SOUND METHOD\n\nWithin one manufacturer, one series, one mounting style, differing only in circuit count:\n  S2B-PH-SM4-TB (J4, 2 ckt)   offset 0\n  S4B-PH-SM4-TB (J2, 4 ckt)   offset 180\n  S5B-PH-SM4-TB (J3, 5 ckt)   offset 180\n  S4B-XH-SM4-TB (J5, 4 ckt)   offset 0\nOUR library is uniform across all four (signal pads at -y, MP tabs at +y). The VENDOR's is not:\nS4B/S5B-PH place tabs at smaller y, S2B-PH and S4B-XH at larger y. So the offset is a property of\none specific vendor drawing of one specific part number. No attribute - manufacturer, series,\npitch, circuit count, package family, footprint name - predicts it. Community rotation databases\nkey on FOOTPRINT NAME and are self-described as \"just a list of common cases\"; one KiCad footprint\nsuch as SOT-23 is reused across many LCSC parts whose drawings disagree, which is the known\n\"two components, same footprint, different rotations in one project\". Import such a list only as\na cross-check, never as the source of truth.\n\nTHE MECHANISM\n\nA reference exists for nearly every part we use, free, keyed by the LCSC number we ALREADY carry\nin assembly.dist_part_numbers:\n    https://easyeda.com/api/products/<LCSC>/components?version=6.4.19.5\n    -> .result.packageDetail.dataStr : head.x/head.y is the origin; shape[] PAD~ records are\n       PAD~shape~x~y~w~h~layer~net~NUMBER~..., units of 10 mil = 0.254 mm.\nMatch pads BY NUMBER, subtract each set's own pad centroid, and solve for the rotation in\n{0,90,180,270} that maps ours onto theirs. Working implementation:\n~/.claude/jobs/a9a929fe/tmp/audit.py (all 47 rev B components) and libcmp.py.\n\nThree outcomes, and the third is the point:\n  AGREE within tolerance          -> admit automatically; the recorded evidence is the DIFF, not\n                                     a person's name. Offset 0 recorded explicitly, not implied.\n  AGREE UP TO A ROTATION          -> admit, and RECORD THE ROTATION. This is not a failure. Our\n                                     U3 is datasheet-correct AND the vendor draws it 180 round;\n                                     both are true, and the relationship is exactly the fact that\n                                     was missing. The diff does not adjudicate who is right.\n  DISAGREE ON GEOMETRY            -> REFUSE, naming which pad differs and by how much. A human is\n                                     handed a concrete discrepancy, not an invitation to stare.\n                                     This is where human attention is actually worth spending -\n                                     and it is where the Sunlord 1.2-vs-1.8 error would have been\n                                     caught instantly.\n  NO REFERENCE AVAILABLE          -> the ONLY case that still needs attestation. Rare and loud.\n\nPROPOSED CHANGE\n\n1. A measurement primitive: given our footprint and an LCSC part number, return\n   {offset_deg, max_residual_mm, matched_pads, verdict}. Pure, deterministic, offline-testable\n   against cached vendor fixtures - NO NETWORK IN TESTS.\n2. Record the result per (footprint, LCSC part) - NOT per footprint alone, per the evidence above.\n   orientation_convention is the natural home but is currently inert: bless.py _blank_assembly()\n   hardcodes it to None and nothing in the tree reads it. Give it meaning or replace it\n   deliberately; do not leave a second empty slot.\n3. Apply the offset in the CPL export: emitted rotation = (component rotation - offset) mod 360.\n   Validated on three parts already: J2 270-180=90, J3 180-180=0, U4 0-270=90, each confirmed\n   against the owner's independent reading of the JLCPCB 3D preview.\n4. THE GATE REFUSES ON UNKNOWN. A component carrying an LCSC part number whose offset has never\n   been measured must fail the order package. Silently treating \"unknown\" as \"zero\" is what let\n   this ship. Same principle as assembly_spec's AUTHORED-EMPTY IS NOT ABSENT.\n5. Demote bless: keep the digest, the licence/provenance capture, and the rendered SVG + fact\n   table as ARTIFACTS; drop the mandatory human verdict except on the no-reference path. Re-tier\n   on CORROBORATION AVAILABLE rather than on WHO TYPED IT. Turn the fact table's numbers into\n   assertions - body_bbox already earned that.\n6. MCP surface from day one, and a GUI affordance, per the standing parity rule.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- The primitive reproduces the measured rev B table exactly: 0 for all passives + J4/J5/SW1-4,\n  180 for J2/J3/Q1/Q2/U3, 270 for U2/U4.\n- Regenerating the rev B package emits J2=90, J3=0, Q1=90, Q2=180, U2=90, U3=180, U4=90 and\n  leaves every other part unchanged.\n- A component with an LCSC number and no recorded offset REFUSES at the order gate.\n- A footprint whose pad geometry disagrees with the vendor beyond tolerance REFUSES, and the\n  message names the offending pad and the delta.\n- Tests run with no network access, against cached vendor fixtures.\n- Feeding the pre-fix Sunlord land (1.2mm pads) to the primitive produces a geometry refusal.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE\n\n- Deciding which drawing is \"correct\". The diff records the relationship; the datasheet remains\n  the authority for our own geometry.\n- Connector opening direction vs declared intent - DCR 01a05dd4a5f9, which DEPENDS on this one\n  because it computes in a frame this change makes trustworthy.\n- Synthetic-expansion child rotation - test 01a05dd7a675, an independent axis.\n\nLIMITS, STATED HONESTLY\n\nNot every part has a vendor entry (mechanical parts, test points, the ESP32 socket composite);\nthose keep the attestation path. The vendor library is not the datasheet and can itself be wrong,\nso agreement is CORROBORATION, not proof - but two independent sources agreeing is far stronger\nthan one person looking. For high-stakes parts, rendering the datasheet's mechanical page beside\nthe diff is cheap and was decisive for U3.\n\nRELATED\n  bug  01a05de4bddd  the defect this fixes (P1) - see its comment for the full 47-part audit\n  DCR  01a05dd4a5f9  opening-direction oracle, depends on this\n  DCR  01a05dd52d11  3D board export - a secondary visual check, NOT a substitute (it cannot see\n                     U2 or U3, whose pad fields are rotationally symmetric)\n  test 01a05dd7a675  synthetic-expansion rotation coverage","created_at":"2026-09-01T17:40:39","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:37","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["bless","cpl","footprint","order-gate","orientation","pcb","verification"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e0f5a31775cac13d4409ad384cc","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Close the 21 footprints with no measured orientation pair (was: backfill 42 lock entries)","description":"RETITLED AND RESCOPED 2026-09-01 — the original framing was wrong in two ways, both established\nby the work that followed it.\n\nWHAT THE ORIGINAL SAID: populate orientation_convention on all 42 footprints.lock.json entries.\n\nWHY THAT UNIT DOES NOT EXIST:\n1. orientation_convention was RETIRED, not filled (commit 6883757). It was keyed on a footprint,\n   named for a family convention that does not exist, and lived in a file that is provenance about\n   OUR bytes when the fact is a relation to a third party's drawing. It also had no room for the\n   three states.\n2. The offset belongs to a (footprint, house, catalogue part) PAIR, so \"42 entries\" is not the\n   denominator. A land pattern like SOT-23 is bought as many different parts, and a measured pair\n   on one of them says nothing about the others.\n\nWHERE IT ACTUALLY LIVES NOW: pcb/library/part_orientation.json, generated by\npcb/scripts/gen_part_orientation.py from pcb/library/part_orientation_declared.json plus the\nvendor payloads, read through pcb_worker/orientation_ledger.py, with coverage rendered to\npcb/library/part_orientation_coverage.md.\n\nTHE REAL REMAINING WORK, as of commit b89ce63:\n  11 footprints have at least one measured pair\n  10 carry a declared no-reference (nothing purchasable is drawn like this)\n  21 have neither — every one a genuine purchasable package with no vendor payload on hand\nThose 21 are the unit. Each needs a vendor payload fetched and committed under the provenance and\ntrim discipline in tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/README.md, index.json extended, and the\ngenerated artifacts regenerated with both diffs READ rather than blessed.\n\nDO NOT CLOSE THE GAP BY DECLARING. A footprint-wide declaration makes the emitter pass the placed\nrotation through unchecked for every part on that land — the coverage report says so in its own\nwords, and one such declaration (the ESP32 socket set) was already removed for exactly this reason\nin c709782. Absent refuses loudly; wrong ships a rotated part silently.\n\nNOTE ON WHAT \"MEASURED\" MEANS HERE: coverage is FOOTPRINT-LEVEL SAMPLING, not exhaustive pair\ncoverage, and the report now says so. Exhaustive pair coverage is not computable — nothing in the\ntree knows which catalogue parts a future board will buy. So closing all 21 does NOT mean no order\ncan ever stop; it means every drawing we ship has been measured at least once. A measured SOT-23\nstill refuses for every SOT-23 part nobody has measured.\n\nONE CANDIDATE IS ALREADY IDENTIFIED: Sunlord:L_Sunlord_AMWPH4018_4x4x1.8mm, whose catalogue number\nC2846183 the lock already names. The other twenty need a human decision about what they are bought\nas before any payload is fetched — that decision is the expensive part, not the fetching.\n\nOVERLAPS, deliberately not merged because they are different questions:\n  01a05e664c5d  the same 21, filed from the coverage side with the per-footprint list\n  01a05e570ff8  ledger coverage as a constraint on what a board can be ordered on\n  01a05e56ba6f  the mpn-only gate hole — names ~5 specific parts that must be measured FIRST,\n                because the refusal there cannot land until they are. START WITH THAT SUBSET:\n                PinSocket_1x07_P2.54mm_Vertical (C7501749 / C41376161), SOT-23 (C20526),\n                Diode_SMD:D_SMA (C2128), D_SOD-123 (C2128).","created_at":"2026-09-01T17:40:59","updated_at":"2026-09-01T20:37:15","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["backfill","lock","orientation","pcb","seed-library"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e1adb637a4e8bd1ab9e03895e28","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"TASK-CYCLE 13 TRACKER — part orientation: differential footprint verification + CPL rotation correction","description":"PURPOSE\n\nThis is the TRACKER for the task-cycle that fixes the part-orientation defect. It exists to\nsurvive compaction: if context is lost, read this item first, then the linked items. It carries\nthe dispatch base, the task list, per-task status, and what must be true before dispatch.\n\nWHY THIS CYCLE EXISTS (one paragraph, so the tracker stands alone)\n\nThe CPL's Rotation column is interpreted by the placement machine against the VENDOR's canonical\npart orientation, not against our footprint drawing. Our seed footprints are authored\nindependently and nothing requires or checks that they match. Where they differ, the part is\nassembled rotated. Measured on smart-remote-v2 rev B: six parts affected - J2, J3, Q1, Q2, U3 by\n180 degrees and U2, U4 by 270. Confirmed against the JLCPCB 3D preview for J2, J3, Q1, Q2 and U4.\nU2 and U3 are UNDETECTABLE by any render because their pad fields are rotationally symmetric -\nevery lead still lands on a pad while the pin functions rotate. Failure mode on U3 is Vdd driven\nonto the CLOCK pin.\n\nITEMS IN THIS CYCLE\n\n  01a05e0f0c81  DCR   Differential footprint verification (the design; T1-T3 implement it)\n  01a05e0f5a31  chore Backfill 42 lock entries with measured offsets (T4)\n  01a05dd7a675  test  Synthetic-expansion rotation coverage, U1S -> U1S_A/B (T5)\n  01a05de4bddd  bug   The defect being fixed (P1). Its comment carries the full 47-part audit.\n\nRELATED, DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS CYCLE\n\n  01a05dd4a5f9  DCR   Connector opening-direction oracle. BLOCKED BY 01a05e0f0c81 - it computes in\n                      a frame this cycle makes trustworthy, so it must come after.\n  01a05dd52d11  DCR   Build/export a 3D board model. A secondary visual check, NOT the detector -\n                      see its comment 215.\n  BLESS DEMOTION      Deliberately held for a SECOND cycle. It moves a trust boundary and is much\n                      safer once T1 has proven the measurement primitive against real parts.\n                      T1-T4 already fix the P1 and deliver the detector without touching bless.\n\nPRE-FLIGHT (task-cycle skill) -- *** BOTH GATES CLOSED 2026-09-01. CLEARED TO DISPATCH. ***\n  repo      ~/github/minerva-plugins\n  branch    main  (the repo's working branch; the five preceding session commits are also on main)\n  [x] GATE 1: working tree clean. docs/plugins.dct committed as de906bb\n      \"chore(docket): task-cycle 13 tracker + part-orientation fix items\".\n  [x] GATE 2: deferred full suite - OWNER-ATTESTED 2026-08-31, run repeatedly that day against\n      the current code, all of it validated. The only commits since are DOCKET DATA ONLY\n      (93f6ec4 and de906bb touch docs/plugins.dct and no code), so the attestation still covers\n      HEAD. Re-establish before the batch is pushed if any code lands outside this cycle.\n\n  *** DISPATCH BASE: de906bba91c4fd0c1388d2073c46cac57897a237 ***\n\n  unpushed at dispatch time: 6 commits d7ffc54..de906bb (rev B seed footprints + docket writes).\n  Nothing in this cycle is pushed until review and scoped execution finish.\n\nTASKS - dispatch in order, one commit each, nothing pushed until the batch closes\n\n  T1  measurement primitive                                            status: [ ] not started\n      Goal   Given our footprint and an LCSC part number, return the rotation offset between our\n             drawing and the vendor's, with a residual and a verdict.\n      Oracle Reproduces the independently measured offsets for the seed footprints (0/180/270).\n             Three of those - J2, J3, U4 - were confirmed by the owner reading the JLCPCB 3D\n             preview BEFORE seeing the computed numbers, so the oracle is genuinely independent.\n      Constraints  Pure and deterministic. NO NETWORK IN TESTS - cached vendor fixtures only.\n             Match pads BY NUMBER, never by position (symmetric pad sets make position matching\n             ambiguous, which is exactly how a 180 error hides). Vendor units are 10 mil = 0.254mm.\n      Not in scope  Writing to the lock; changing export; touching bless.\n\n  T2  record the offset                                                status: [ ] not started\n      Goal   Persist the measured offset per (footprint, LCSC part), and make \"never measured\" a\n             DISTINCT state from \"measured as 0\".\n      Oracle An entry round-trips; deleting a value is distinguishable from a recorded zero.\n      Note   Keyed per PART, not per footprint - no attribute predicts the offset. Within JST PH\n             side-entry alone the 2-circuit part measures 0 while the 4- and 5-circuit parts\n             measure 180. One KiCad footprint is reused across many LCSC parts that disagree.\n      Not in scope  Applying it; the backfill.\n\n  T3  apply at export + order gate                                     status: [ ] not started\n      Goal   Emitted CPL rotation = (component rotation - offset) mod 360, and the order package\n             REFUSES when a component carrying an LCSC part has no recorded offset.\n      Oracle Regenerating rev B emits J2=90 J3=0 Q1=90 Q2=180 U2=90 U3=180 U4=90 with every other\n             part unchanged; removing one offset causes a refusal. NOTE: J3's expected value moves\n             once the board-side J3 south-entry rework lands - recompute, do not hardcode 0.\n      Constraints  DO NOT touch the arithmetic in assembly_outputs._walk. Its frame/rotation\n             contract is CORRECT and was independently re-derived by solving the anchor offsets\n             against F.Fab centres at 0/90/180/270. There is no sign error there; do not go\n             looking for one.\n      Not in scope  bless re-tiering.\n\n  T4  backfill 42 lock entries (01a05e0f5a31)                          status: [ ] not started\n      Goal   No entry left null; each measured, or explicitly marked as having no vendor reference.\n      Oracle Values produced by the tooling, not pasted by hand. A board using only backfilled\n             entries passes the gate; removing one value makes it refuse.\n      Note   \"No reference\" (MountingHole, TestPoint, Minerva_Fixture, the ESP32 socket composite)\n             is a different state from \"measured as 0\" and the gate must distinguish them.\n\n  T5  synthetic-expansion rotation coverage (01a05dd7a675)             status: [ ] not started\n      Goal   Pin composed child rotation to (parent R + placement D) mod 360.\n      Oracle The pre-fix U1S board emits 180 and the fixed one emits 90, distinguishable by\n             assertion rather than by eye.\n      Note   Also fix tests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_anchor_override.yaml, which sets\n             rotation_deg: 0 on all six placements including U4S whose parent is at 90 - so the\n             one worked example currently teaches the exact shape that shipped wrong.\n\nCYCLE-WIDE CONSTRAINTS\n  Implementer Opus / Adversary Fable per task; Codex cross-provider check serially at batch end,\n  only after every Fable round closes.\n  Real product boards are BANNED from pcb/worker/tests/testdata (test_corpus_policy.py catches by\n  content). Fixtures are seed footprints + cached vendor JSON - never our board.\n  Run pytest with pcb/worker/.venv/bin/python, not the ambient interpreter.\n  Out-of-scope discoveries are FILED, not fixed.\n\nBOARD WORK IS NOT IN THIS CYCLE and has no docket items by the owner's rule. It is tracked in\nnudge smart-remote/rev-b-board-worklist. Decided 2026-09-01: J3 gets a SOUTH-ENTRY REWORK in the\nboard (rotate 180 -> 0 and re-route its 5 nets + 2 MP); the top-entry part swap was considered and\nREJECTED, so no new seed footprint is needed and the library-content question is moot.","created_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:33","updated_at":"2026-09-01T20:37:44","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["cpl","orientation","pcb","task-cycle","tracker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e56ba6f769a859fa7093a8971e1","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: a part identified only by assembly.mpn bypasses the part-orientation gate","description":"The part-orientation ledger is keyed on (footprint ref, house, catalogue number), so assembly_orientation.apply joins on assembly.house_parts[profile.house_part_id]. That is the only join the ledger supports.\n\nBut a board can be ORDERED without house_parts: the BOM's part-number column falls back to assembly.mpn, and the jlc/jlcpcb-economic dialect only requires `mpn` as identity. A populated part carrying mpn alone therefore reaches a position file with its placed rotation emitted verbatim and no refusal — the exact silent-zero behaviour the gate exists to stop, reached by a different door.\n\nDeliberately not closed in the implementing task: joining a MANUFACTURER part number to a HOUSE catalogue number would invent the loose key the ledger explicitly refuses to have (one land pattern is bought as many parts, drawn differently by their vendors).","created_at":"2026-09-01T18:58:57","updated_at":"2026-09-01T21:03:42","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","cpl","orientation","pcb"],"repro_steps":"Author a board component with `assembly: {mpn: C780769}` and no `house_parts`, on footprint Package_TO_SOT_SMD:TSOT-23-6 at rotation 30. Emit a CPL under profile \"jlc\". The row emits Rotation 30.0000; the same part authored as `house_parts: {jlcpcb: C780769}` emits 300.0000 (offset 270). Asserted as a stated gap by tests/test_assembly_orientation.py::test_a_part_identified_only_by_mpn_is_not_gated."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e56e1987bd49de65d8fea58a749","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: MCP tool descriptions do not name the two orientation refusals","description":"Two new named refusals now run on every assembly emission (assembly_orientation_unknown, assembly_orientation_undecided — see pcb/docs/assembly-outputs.md). The service profile's checked_rules, docs/board-yaml.md and docs/assembly-outputs.md were updated with them; the MCP surface was explicitly out of scope for the implementing task and was not.\n\nBoth tool descriptions carry an exhaustive-looking list of the assembly hard gates, and a list that reads as exhaustive while omitting a live refusal is worse than no list: an agent that hits one gets a code the tool never mentioned. Also worth stating there that the emitted CPL Rotation is now the placed angle PLUS the measured part-orientation offset — the descriptions currently say the rotation is the composed placement angle.\n\nTouch points: pcb/manifest.json (minerva_pcb_export_assembly + minerva_pcb_order_package descriptions), pcb/internal/tools/worker_tools.go (~lines 688-695 and ~987-989).","created_at":"2026-09-01T18:59:07","updated_at":"2026-09-01T18:59:07","priority":3,"tags":["docs","mcp","orientation","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e570ff87b52802e6aa9c2eecded","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: seed footprints with no measured orientation pair are order-blocking","description":"assembly_orientation refuses an emission for a populated part whose (footprint, house, catalogue number) pair has no ledger row. That is intended. The consequence is that the ledger's coverage is now the list of footprints a board can be ORDERED on.\n\nCoverage today: 11 measured pairs (from tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/index.json) and 9 declared no_reference footprints. The seed lock carries 42 entries. Every uncovered footprint blocks an order the moment a board buys a catalogue part against it.\n\nTwo consequences to work through, neither fixed by the implementing task (out of scope):\n\n1. BACKFILL. Every seed footprint a real board buys on needs either a vendor payload in the corpus and a measured row, or a declared no_reference row with a reason.\n\n2. THE TEST CORPUS IS COVERED FROM THE TESTS, NOT THE LEDGER. tests/orientation_corpus.py declares R_0805, C_0805 and Diode_SMD:D_SMA as no_reference for the suites that emit from corpus boards — they are synthesized or hand-authored in-repo, so the statement is true, but it lives in the test tree on purpose (a shipped declaration would let a REAL 0805 part be bought against the synthetic land pattern with no gate). Decide whether that is where it should stay.","created_at":"2026-09-01T18:59:19","updated_at":"2026-09-01T18:59:19","priority":2,"tags":["ledger","library","orientation","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e664c5d7944b0909387c5724c59","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Close the 20 unmeasured footprints in the part-orientation coverage report","description":"The part-orientation coverage report (`pcb/library/part_orientation_coverage.md`, added\ntask-cycle 13) says 11 measured / 11 declared / 20 UNKNOWN of 42 shipped footprints.\n\nThe 20 unknown are all GENUINE purchasable packages. They stay unknown on purpose: a measured\nrow needs a real vendor drawing of a real catalogue part, and declaring a purchasable package\n`no_reference` to clear the count would disarm the orientation gate for every part placed on that\ndrawing (`OrientationLedger.lookup` falls back to a footprint-wide declaration for any part).\nAbsent refuses loudly; wrong ships a rotated part silently.\n\nClosing one means: fetch the LCSC/EasyEDA package payload for the part it is bought as, trim it\nper `pcb/worker/tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/README.md`, pair it in that directory's\n`index.json`, run `python3 pcb/scripts/gen_part_orientation.py`, and READ the diff.\n\nBEST FIRST CANDIDATE — the acquisition lock already names the catalogue number, so no guessing is\nneeded about what it is bought as:\n\n  * `Sunlord:L_Sunlord_AMWPH4018_4x4x1.8mm` — lock states mpn `AMWPH4018S2R2MT`, dist `C2846183`.\n\nThe other 19 have no catalogue number stated anywhere in the repo, so each needs a human decision\nabout what it is actually bought as BEFORE any payload is fetched. Do not infer a part number from\na footprint name.\n\nBlocked on network fetch, which is why this was filed rather than fixed.","created_at":"2026-09-01T19:15:57","updated_at":"2026-09-01T19:15:57","priority":3,"tags":["assembly","coverage","orientation","pcb"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05e8146857f3ab07cb7c7511771f3","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Shipped part-orientation ledger derives production rows from a TEST fixture tree","description":"FOUND BY: cold adversarial review of the part-orientation batch (minerva-plugins 8dcb621..27d97a9). Reported, not fixed, on instruction.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF IT\npcb/scripts/gen_part_orientation.py derives two SHIPPED artifacts —\n  pcb/library/part_orientation.json (the ledger a production order refuses on)\n  pcb/library/part_orientation_coverage.md\n— and the MEASURED half of the ledger comes from\n  pcb/worker/tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/*.json + index.json\ni.e. the release path's data is generated out of the test corpus. `DEFAULT_PAYLOAD_DIR` in the generator points straight at `pcb/worker/tests/testdata/vendor_footprints`.\n\nWHY IT WAS DONE THAT WAY (recorded so a fix does not re-break it)\nThe vendor payloads are third-party LCSC/EasyEDA content. `pcb/library/` is the shipped tree whose third-party obligations `gen_notice.py` certifies from the acquisition lock, so moving the payloads there would put unattributed third-party data on a release path the NOTICE gate does not walk. That reasoning is written down in both the generator's docstring and the corpus README, and it is sound as far as it goes.\n\nWHY IT IS STILL WRONG\n- A test fixture is edited under test-fixture rules; a production input is not. Nothing marks the difference.\n- A prune of the test corpus silently deletes measured rows from a shipped artifact, turning measured pairs back into UNKNOWN and refusing orders that used to work.\n- The direction of dependency is inverted: `pcb/library` (shipped) depends on `pcb/worker/tests` (not shipped).\n- A shipped install cannot re-measure, which is why every measured row has to pin `footprint_sha256` as a workaround.\n\nOPTIONS, unranked and unvalidated:\n1. Move the payloads to a third-party source tree of their own (e.g. pcb/vendor-payloads/) and extend the NOTICE/attribution gate to walk it.\n2. Keep them where they are, but make the coupling explicit and gated — a manifest under pcb/library naming the payloads it derives from, with a check that fails when one disappears.\n3. Accept it and record it as a knowing constraint, with the sha256 pins as the stated mitigation.\n\nNOT URGENT: the current state is correct today and gated by `gen_part_orientation.py --check`.","created_at":"2026-09-01T19:45:25","updated_at":"2026-09-01T19:45:25","priority":3,"tags":["library","orientation","pcb","review-finding","task-cycle-13"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05eaf4ed671e49b5762bc78a6f57b","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Three release gates went red when the rev B seed footprints landed, and nothing surfaced it for two days","description":"WHAT HAPPENED\n\nCommit d7ffc54 (\"feat(pcb/library): rev B seed footprints — Syntiant PDM mic, 1206 C0G cap\") and\nits siblings added three entries to pcb/library/footprints.lock.json:\n\n  Syntiant:SPK0641HT4H-1_TopPort_LGA-8_4x3mm\n  Sunlord:L_Sunlord_AMWPH4018_4x4x1.8mm\n  Capacitor_SMD:C_1206_3216Metric\n\nThree gates depend on that lock's contents and none of them was updated:\n\n1. pcb/NOTICE.md — the GENERATED license/attribution inventory, and by its own description the\n   RELEASE gate. It records 39 entries against the lock's 42, so gen_notice.py --check reports\n   drift and test_notice.py fails.\n2. test_footprints.py::test_lockfile_covers_every_seed_footprint — an expected-set census.\n3. test_footprint_def.py::test_lockfile_wide_adapter_census_is_explicit_and_nonblocking — ditto.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH A P1 RATHER THAN A QUICK REGENERATE\n\nThe regenerate is trivial. The problem is that THREE INDEPENDENT GATES FAILED SILENTLY FOR TWO\nDAYS, including the one that certifies our third-party licence obligations before a release. The\nwork that added the footprints ran static checks and a scoped test set; none of those touched the\ncensus or the NOTICE gate, so nothing said a word. It surfaced only because a later task-cycle\nhappened to run the full suite.\n\nThat is the same shape as the defect this whole area is about: a fact is checked somewhere, the\ncheck is not reached by the path people actually take, and the failure waits.\n\nALSO NOTE THE ATTRIBUTION TRAP. Because these predate the dispatch base of task-cycle 13\n(8dcb621), every task in that cycle correctly classified them as pre-existing and left them alone\n— which is the right rule (see hint task-cycle/pre-existing-is-measured-against-the-dispatch-base).\nBut the effect is that a red gate can ride along untouched through an arbitrary number of clean\ncycles. Pre-existing must not mean invisible.\n\nWHAT TO DO\n\n1. Regenerate NOTICE.md and update the two census expectations. Regenerating NOTICE is a\n   release-attribution act, so it wants a human eye on the diff rather than a reflex — the three\n   new entries carry a proprietary licence and two third-party ones.\n2. Then decide the real fix: adding a lock entry should fail loudly AT THAT MOMENT. Options worth\n   weighing — make the acquisition/bless path regenerate the dependent artifacts as part of\n   landing an entry; or have the scoped-test guidance name the census/NOTICE suites as direct\n   consumers of any lock change (compare hint 01a04af027eb, which already says a batch touching a\n   declared SET must scope every suite that reads that set — the lock is exactly such a set and\n   the rule was not applied to it).\n\nRELATED\n  01a05e1adb63  task-cycle 13 tracker — this is why its push gate is blocked\n  01a04af027eb  the scoped-set hint whose rule would have caught this","created_at":"2026-09-01T20:35:42","updated_at":"2026-09-01T21:07:23","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["ci","notice","pcb","pre-existing","release-gate","seed-library"],"repro_steps":"From pcb/worker:  .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests -q -p no:randomly\n\n  tests/test_notice.py::test_shipped_lock_generates_notice_byte_identical_to_committed_file\n  tests/test_footprints.py::test_lockfile_covers_every_seed_footprint\n  tests/test_footprint_def.py::test_lockfile_wide_adapter_census_is_explicit_and_nonblocking\n\nConfirm NOTICE staleness directly:\n  pcb/worker/.venv/bin/python pcb/scripts/gen_notice.py --check\n  -> \"pcb/NOTICE.md is stale (drift detected); regenerate ... and commit the result\"\n\nMeasured on the task-cycle 13 batch: 3 failed / 3261 passed BEFORE any of that cycle's work, and\n3 failed / 3270 passed after — the same three failures throughout. They are not that cycle's."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05f5eb0fa76b7bca5d6af252f97c4","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"The pcb release job depends on no test, so a release publishes even when CI is red","description":"TWO SEPARATE HOLES THAT COMPOUND\n\n1. NO TEST IS UPSTREAM OF `release`. Jobs run in parallel and `release` waits only for\n   `package`. A red suite does not stop a publish. This is not specific to tags — it is true of\n   every push, and has been true of every pcb release cut so far.\n\n2. A TAG SKIPS THE CROSS-PLATFORM MATRIX. `test-crossplatform` and `oracle` are gated to\n   `refs/heads/main` or manual dispatch. The reasoning in the workflow's own comment is sound as\n   far as it goes — GitHub bills macOS runners at 10x and Windows at 2x, and paying that on every\n   dcr/** push buys little because every round lands on main first. But the conclusion does not\n   extend to TAGS. A release is rare and is exactly the artifact where a portability break is most\n   expensive, because it ships.\n\nTogether: a tagged release runs neither the platform matrix NOR any gate on the tests that did\nrun. The commit that produced pcb-v0.5.8's artifacts fails on Windows, and the release published\nwithout complaint.\n\nWHY THIS KEEPS HAPPENING. Compare the pcb 0.5.4 incident, where the Windows worker turned out\nnever to have run and the marketplace fab preview had never worked. The same shape: a check exists,\nthe release path does not traverse it, and the failure is discovered by a human on a laptop some\ntime later. A real-bundle CI gate was added after that one; this hole was not closed, because the\nmissing piece is not a check — it is a DEPENDENCY EDGE.\n\nPROPOSED FIX\n  a. Make `release` need the jobs that establish the build is good — at minimum `test`,\n     `fab-gate` and `panel`, plus `test-crossplatform` where it runs.\n  b. Extend `test-crossplatform` (and `oracle`) to run on version tags, not just main:\n     `startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/')`. The cost argument holds for branches and inverts for\n     releases.\n  c. Consider whether `package` should also depend on the tests, or whether building an artifact\n     that is then not released is acceptable. Building is cheap; publishing is what needs the gate.\n\nWATCH OUT WHEN FIXING: a job that is SKIPPED is not a job that PASSED. If `release` gains\n`needs: [test-crossplatform]` while that job keeps a branch-only `if:`, GitHub will skip `release`\ntoo on any ref where the matrix does not run — which would silently stop releasing rather than\nrelease unsafely. Either make the matrix run everywhere a release can happen, or gate on\n`always() && !failure() && !cancelled()` deliberately and say why in a comment.\n\nNOT A REGRESSION FROM TASK-CYCLE 13. That cycle's work exposed it — its vendor payload digests are\nwhat finally made a Windows job fail — but the missing dependency edge predates it entirely.","created_at":"2026-09-01T23:47:16","updated_at":"2026-09-01T23:47:16","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["ci","gate","pcb","release","windows"],"repro_steps":"Read the job graph in .github/workflows/pcb.yml:\n\n  job                 needs        if\n  push                -            -\n  test                -            -\n  fab-gate            -            -\n  test-crossplatform  -            github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' || workflow_dispatch\n  oracle              -            github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' || workflow_dispatch\n  panel               -            -\n  package             -            -\n  release             package      github.event_name == 'push'\n\n`release` depends on `package`; `package` depends on nothing. No test job is\nupstream of a release.\n\nOBSERVED 2026-09-01, both on commit 46f1442:\n  run 33571607977 (main)          pcb test (windows-latest)  FAILURE\n                                  release                    SUCCESS  <-- published\n  run 33571703055 (pcb-v0.5.8)    pcb test (matrix)          SKIPPED\n                                  pcb oracle                 SKIPPED\n                                  release                    SUCCESS  <-- published\n\n`gh release view pcb-v0.5.8` shows four live assets, built from a commit whose\nWindows tests were failing at the time."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05fd602f07c69ae0ea7a5e7fad5fa","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb MCP: get_components / describe_component report footprint \"CUSTOM\" for every component","description":"On smart_remote_v2_board.yaml (47 components, every one with a real library footprint such as `Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1_SocketSet_2x22_THT`, `Package_TO_SOT_SMD:TSOT-23-6`, `EVP-ASAC1A:SW_EVP-ASAC1A`), both `minerva_pcb_get_components` and `minerva_pcb_describe_component` return `\"footprint\": \"CUSTOM\"` for all 47. The field is a constant and carries no information; an agent cannot tell which land pattern a component uses without opening the YAML, which the MCP-only working rule forbids mid-session.","created_at":"2026-09-02T01:57:36","updated_at":"2026-09-02T01:58:48","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["ergonomics","llm","mcp","pcb"],"repro_steps":"1. Import smart-remote-v2/eda/smart_remote_v2_board.yaml into a fresh pcb editor.\n2. Call minerva_pcb_get_components editor_name=&lt;that editor&gt;.\n3. Observe `footprint: \"CUSTOM\"` on every entry; compare with the `footprint:` key of the same component in the YAML."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05fd64b9a7ae9ab9348107e92ac04","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"pcb MCP: expose assembly identity and resolved physical placements on components (LLM ergonomics)","description":"PROBLEM. The board YAML carries, per component, an `assembly:` block — manufacturer, mpn, package, house_parts (e.g. jlcpcb: C41376161) and, for a synthetic expansion, `placements[]` naming the real purchased parts (ref, offset_mm, anchor_mm, rotation_deg). The compiler resolves that into `ResolvedComponent.physical_placements` (assembly_anchor.physical_placements: one entry under the component's own ref for an ordinary part, one per authored placement for a synthetic), and every CAM output — order_package, assembly_outputs (CPL/BOM), assembly_preview, service_profile — reads that spine. So CAM knows exactly what a synthetic part is and what backs it.\n\nNone of it reaches the LLM-facing MCP surface. get_components / describe_component return id, pins, x/y/rotation/layer, a constant footprint \"CUSTOM\", and `value` — a free-text human caption. The ONLY signal that U1S is two purchased 1x22 sockets is the prose a person typed into value: \"(expands to 2x C41376161)\". An agent working MCP-only re-derives \"is this synthetic, what is really bought, where will the nozzle go\" on every compaction by reading the YAML, which the working rule forbids. `value` is also unreliable as identity: U3's value names MSM261S4030H0R while footprint and assembly.mpn are Syntiant SPK0641HT4H-1.\n\nREQUEST. get_components and describe_component carry, per component:\n- `footprint`: the real library ref (see bug 01a05fd602f0).\n- `assembly`: { manufacturer, mpn, package, house_parts:{house: id}, populate/paste as authored }.\n- `physical_placements`: the RESOLVED list the CPL will emit — [{ref, origin_mm, anchor_mm (board frame), rotation_deg (composed), side, anchor_basis}]. One entry for an ordinary part (ref == component ref); n entries for a synthetic. Present on every component so a reader never has to branch on \"is this synthetic\" — len(physical_placements) &gt; 1 or ref != component ref answers it.\nThis is existing compile_board output surfaced, not new logic. Do NOT surface the authored offsets alone: the composed/resolved values are what a reader needs to check against the copper and the preview.\n\nORACLE. For U1S on smart_remote_v2 the surfaced physical_placements must equal what order_package emits for U1S_A/U1S_B (same anchor_mm, rotation_deg, side) — diff the two; any drift means MCP is computing its own answer instead of reporting the compiler's.","created_at":"2026-09-02T01:57:54","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:47","priority":2,"tags":["assembly","ergonomics","llm","mcp","pcb","synthetic"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05fd69e5f79c5b8851a94c11e9cc7","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Synthetic expansion children carry their own land pattern: placement-keyed orientation ledger + measured child anchor","description":"PROBLEM (the incomplete feature). A synthetic expansion (`assembly.placements[]`) resolves to one CPL row per child, and assembly_orientation.py:361 gates each row with `ledger.lookup(row.footprint_ref, house, part)`. But a child carries NO drawing of its own — `row.footprint_ref` is the PARENT's footprint. For U1S on smart_remote_v2 the pair the gate asks for is (Espressif:ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1_SocketSet_2x22_THT [44 pads], jlcpcb, C41376161 [a 1x22 strip, 22 pads]). That pair is not a valid measurement — part_orientation refuses on overlap, and it should, because the 44-pad drawing is not that part. So the ledger can never learn it, the order package refuses forever, and the only escape is to smuggle a vendor-convention correction into the child's `rotation_deg` (a DESIGN field) — which (a) double-applies the moment the ledger does learn the pair, and (b) rotates the hand-authored `anchor_mm` with it (see bug: no gate catches an anchor off the child's copper; this happened 2026-09-01 — nozzle anchors moved 26.67 mm sideways off the strips with DRC clean).\n\nROOT CAUSE. The child is treated as a rotation+offset with no identity. It IS a real purchased part with a real land pattern; the parent just happens to draw the copper for all of them.\n\nREQUEST. `assembly.placements[].footprint` — each child names the library footprint it IS (for U1S: a datasheet-authored 1x22 2.54 mm socket, e.g. Connector_PinSocket_2.54mm:PinSocket_1x22_P2.54mm_Vertical, matching HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P). Then:\n1. The orientation pair for the CPL row is (child footprint, house, part) — measurable by the existing part_orientation primitive, ledgered by the existing generator, gated by the existing exporter. No new ledger concept.\n2. The child's anchor is MEASURED off the child footprint through the basis ladder (fab -&gt; lands -&gt; origin), exactly as an ordinary component's is. `anchor_mm` stays as an authored override only; the U1S hand-written anchors go away.\n3. The child's rotation_deg becomes purely design (\"this strip runs along parent +y\"), and the vendor offset is applied by the ledger on export — one fact in one place, no double-apply.\n4. A consistency check that the child footprint's pads, placed at the child's composed transform, coincide with a subset of the parent's pads (within land tolerance). That is the oracle that the child footprint is the right drawing AND that offset/rotation are right — it is what would have caught the sideways anchor.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: changing how the parent draws copper; the MCP surface (separate DCR 01a05fd64b9a); the SW1–SW4 EVP-ASAC1A pad-name gap (a plain pair, different fix).\n\nRUBRIC. Determinism high: anchor and offset both become measured, re-run and converge; the alternatives (per-pair pad alias, human-asserted offset) are hand transcription the ledger validator was deliberately built to refuse. Cost M.","created_at":"2026-09-02T01:58:15","updated_at":"2026-09-02T01:58:15","priority":1,"tags":["assembly","cam","determinism","orientation-ledger","pcb","synthetic"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05fd6cf487b0b860e5f24fca96589","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Assembly gates accept an expansion child anchor that resolves off the child's own copper","description":"`assembly.placements[].anchor_mm` is stated in the child's local frame and rides the child transform (assembly_anchor.physical_placements: `anchor=child.point(anchor_local)`). Rotating the child therefore rotates the anchor. Nothing checks the result lands on the child's part: assembly_gates has too-close, empty-expansion, duplicate-designator and an anchor-UNMEASURED advisory, but no anchor-OFF-COPPER check. Copper is unaffected (the parent draws it), so DRC, board_check and connectivity all stay clean; the defect is visible only in the CPL / assembly preview — i.e. at the assembly house.","created_at":"2026-09-02T01:58:28","updated_at":"2026-09-02T01:58:46","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["anchor","assembly","gate","pcb","synthetic"],"repro_steps":"1. smart_remote_v2_board.yaml at ccsandbox 52a3c5e: U1S parent (45, 62.797) rot 180 top; children offset (∓11.43, 0), anchor (0, 26.67), rotation 0. Resolved anchors (56.43, 36.13) / (33.57, 36.13) = strip centres. Correct.\n2. Set both children `rotation_deg: 270` (composed 90). Change nothing else.\n3. Resolve with PlacementTransform (or run the order package): anchors become (83.1, 62.797) / (60.24, 62.797) — 26.67 mm east of each strip's y=0 end pin, on empty board.\n4. board_drc / board_check / pcb_assembly_check: all pass."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"Task-cycle 14 — pcb 0.6.0: synthetic child footprint, placement-keyed orientation, MCP assembly surface","description":"Tracking record for task-cycle 14. Dispatch base ee72f5a3 (main). Owner approved 2026-09-01; run autonomously on the rubric. Beep owner after Codex rounds close, before scoped test execution.\n\nSerial order, one commit per task, implementer does not commit:\n T1  bug 01a05fd6cf48 — anchor-off-copper gate (Opus)\n T2a chore under 01a05fd69e5f — 1x22 socket land pattern from the HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P datasheet, vendor pad numbering (Opus)\n T2  DCR 01a05fd69e5f — child footprint, placement-keyed orientation pair, measured child anchor, pad-subset check (Fable; cold Opus per-task review before commit)\n T3  bug 01a05fd602f0 — footprint \"CUSTOM\" (Opus)\n T4  DCR 01a05fd64b9a — MCP assembly identity + resolved physical placements (Opus)\nThen: batched Fable review; Fable/Fable fix loops; batched Codex; Fable/Codex fix loops; BEEP; scoped test-ex; push; 0.5.9 -&gt; 0.6.0, annotated tag, CI green on every job; marketplace install; owner HITL (fab view, assembly preview for U1S anchors); Phase B order package.\n\nOutside the cycle (board, ccsandbox): after T2, U1S gains placements[].footprint, loses hand anchors, children back to rotation 0; U3 value label (smart-remote 01a05fd70312). SW1–SW4 / C4365033 pair is a separate decision.\n\nDeferred full suite: CI green on 2ac6ecd (0.5.9) 2026-09-01 — not overdue.","created_at":"2026-09-02T02:21:39","updated_at":"2026-09-02T06:57:36","priority":1,"tags":["0.6.0","pcb","task-cycle","tc14"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a060439f5d7910a7234b2137e4040c","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: MPN spelling drift HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P vs -1x22PZ across the lock and the rev B board","description":"footprints.lock.json records the DevKitC-1 socket set's MPN as HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22P; the 1x22 strip entry added in task-cycle 14 (65ad307) records HC-PM254-8.5H-1x22PZ, which is what LCSC lists for C41376161. The rev B board YAML (smart-remote) also says 1x22P. Pick one (LCSC's PZ) and align the lock entry; the board edit belongs to the board owner at the next export boundary. Provenance note: the drawing's title block names ShenZhenYongFengYing (CST) CS-1165SDIG while LCSC lists Hong Cheng — same drawing, two vendor names; recorded in the lock's provenance_note, no action.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:19","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:19","priority":3,"tags":["library","pcb","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06043b9c6765ea8a84af0cf67ef44","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: stale counts and latent prose surfaced by task-cycle 14","description":"Docstring-only or dead-code items found by implementers and reviewers during TC14, none load-bearing:\n- worker/tests/test_assembly_anchor.py:6 says \"18 of the 39 seed footprints\" — library is at 43.\n- docs/assembly-outputs.md says \"nine of the eleven pairs measured so far\" — ledger has 20 measured rows (17 flat, 3 quarter-turns).\n- tests/gd/test_pcb_hover_card.gd:149 fixture doc claims U1 carries a library footprint; _board() sets none.\n- worker/pcb_worker/assembly_gates.py:324 (from aea43af) an f-string with no placeholders (pyflakes; harmless).\n- ui/model/pcb_component.gd get_description() still formats the FootprintType render bucket rather than the canonical footprint name; no callers in the plugin today (latent, same class as bug 01a05fd602f0).\n- library/part_orientation_coverage.md: the UNKNOWN lead for the SocketSet_2x22 parent still says \"the lock names C41376161\" — the parent is not a purchasable part, so the lead points at a pair that should never be measured; the generator's lead text should recognise a footprint whose children carry the parts.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:26","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:26","priority":4,"tags":["docs","pcb","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06043c870779380111e64c1498df5","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb: manifest.json and worker_tools.go enumerate assembly gate codes in prose and lack the two new ones","description":"The order_package and export_assembly tool descriptions in pcb/manifest.json, and comments in internal/tools/worker_tools.go, list the hard-gate refusal codes by name. Task-cycle 14 added assembly_anchor_off_lands (aea43af) and assembly_child_lands_mismatch (216605c); neither appears in that prose. Either add them or, better, stop enumerating codes in prose and point at the one place they are registered (service_profile.IMPLEMENTED_CHECKS) so the list cannot drift again.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:30","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:30","priority":3,"tags":["docs","mcp","pcb","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06043d72078f0883323a320c89761","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: worker/tests/testdata/assembly_boards/assembly_fixture.yaml does not compile","description":"Noticed during TC14 T1 (pre-existing, unrelated to the change): compile_board on this fixture reports invalid_design_rule ×2 and pin_pad_desync ×3+. Either the fixture is deliberately broken for a test that expects those refusals (then say so in the file header) or it has rotted. Determine which; if rotted, repair or delete.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:33","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:33","priority":4,"severity":4,"tags":["pcb","tc14-followup","tests"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06043f15d76488d80d23e7d50d64b","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"pcb: a parent pad owned by no expansion child (shield / mechanical land) cannot be declared, so the child-lands cover rule refuses","description":"assembly_child_lands_mismatch (216605c) has a cover half: when EVERY child names a drawing, every parent pad must be claimed by some child — that is what refuses a strip shorter than the row. A parent drawing that also carries a pad belonging to no purchased child (a shield tab, a mechanical land, a fiducial inside the outline) therefore cannot have all its children named; the only escape is to leave one child un-named, which silently drops that child back to the parent-drawing pair and the parent-measured anchor. board-yaml.md states the limitation. Design a way to declare such pads (e.g. a parent-level list of pads owned by no child, or a child-level `pads:` claim) so the cover rule stays exact without forcing an un-named child. Raised by the cold review of T2; not needed for the rev B board, whose parent draws only the two strips' pads.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:40","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:40","priority":3,"tags":["assembly","pcb","synthetic","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0604405ef7eb8a89c3d451c2b7ae1","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb: ui/panel_tools.gd is 11,781 lines — split the MCP tool handlers by domain","description":"panel_tools.gd holds every minerva_pcb_* handler in one static class: 11,781 lines at 354f750 (task-cycle 14 added ~100). It has been a God file for many cycles, so this is a tracked refactor rather than a fix-now. Split by domain along the dispatcher's existing groupings (components/placement, traces/routing, zones/cutouts, workspace/staged, export/fab, assembly), keeping one dispatcher and the awaited-coroutine contract documented at the top of the file. Oracle: the golden-parity suites (test_pcb_panel_tools*.gd, test_export_surfaces_parity.gd) pass unchanged with their pinned assertion counts.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:45","updated_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:45","priority":3,"tags":["god-file","pcb","refactor","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06055d6ef7fa39ee5a11959a39b99","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb panel: two more revision-keyed caches go stale across a board load (assembly gate state, last-rendered revision); yaml load passes revision 0","description":"board_revision deliberately does NOT bump on a load (pcb_data.gd \"LOAD-FAMILY EXCLUSIONS\": it answers \"did the current board change since this candidate was generated\", and a load resets the baseline). Any panel cache keyed on board_revision alone is therefore stale after load A → read → load B. Task-cycle 14 fixed this for the placements memo by adding a `_board_generation` counter in PCBPanel.gd, incremented in the data_changed relay, and keying the memo on (revision, generation). The same hole exists, pre-existing, in:\n- `_assembly_state` — `_assembly_gate` (panel_tools.gd ~8355) compares board_revision only; `_load_board` never refreshes it despite its \"a load … refreshes it\" wording.\n- `_last_rendered_board_revision` — board_health's `rendered` flag reads true for a render of the PREVIOUS board after a load.\nFix: key both on (board_revision, _board_generation) like the placements memo, with a load-A/read/load-B test on the real path (see tests/gd/test_assembly_placements_memo.gd for the pattern).\nAlso noticed: `load_board_from_yaml` passes a literal 0 as the revision to `load_into_workspace` (PCBPanel.gd ~5286) while the project-file path passes the live revision — determine which is right and align them.","created_at":"2026-09-02T04:17:13","updated_at":"2026-09-02T04:17:13","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["cache","panel","pcb","tc14-followup"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062752f6f75bfb7fcf0c2254e9be0","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"pcb: a NEW board can never fab-preview — the panel seeds no design_rules, draws with silent defaults, and the compiler refuses to invent them","description":"REPRO (0.6.0 marketplace install, 2026-09-02): File → New PCB, press \"Fab preview (exact)\". Toast: `PCB plugin [pcb.fab_preview]: worker error (compile) — design_rules.trace_width_mm must be a positive number; got None`. NOT a 0.6.0 regression: `git diff pcb-v0.5.9 pcb-v0.6.0` touches no design_rules handling; the refusal dates to 2026-07-19 (7ce1152, the K2 compiler).\n\nWHY-CHAIN\n1. Why does fab preview fail? compile_board._build_design_rules (worker/pcb_worker/compile_board.py:1061-1073) refuses a board whose design_rules block lacks any of trace_width_mm / via_diameter_mm / via_drill_mm / clearance_mm (\"must be a positive number; got None\"), and refuses outright when the block is absent (\"board has no design_rules block; v1 refuses to invent trace/via/clearance\"). Fab preview compiles first, so it inherits the refusal.\n2. Why does the blank board lack them? The panel model initialises `design_rules: Dictionary = {}` (ui/model/pcb_data.gd:108) and the new-board path seeds name/width/height/grid but NO rules. minerva_pcb_export_yaml on a fresh editor returns a block holding only `allowed_trace_angles_deg` (written by set_trace_angles, pcb_data.gd:2431) — width/clearance/via never appear.\n3. Why does nothing look wrong until fab preview? The panel draws copper with its own fallback: authored_trace_width returns DEFAULT_TRACE_WIDTH_MM when the board declares none (pcb_data.gd:2447-2455), and the Options menu reports 0.0 for an undeclared rule (pcb_options_menu.gd:115-130). Routing, DRC-lite and the canvas all proceed on values the board does not carry; only the strict compiler (fab preview, gerbers, order package) asks the board itself.\n4. Why is the compiler strict? By design — \"v1 refuses to invent\": the YAML is the source of truth and a fab output built on a default the file never stated would silently differ from the file. That policy is right; the gap is upstream of it.\n5. Why is there no prompt? The GUI affordance EXISTS (Options menu: Trace width / Via diameter / Via drill / Clearance value dialogs, pcb_options_menu.gd:51-54; MCP twin minerva_pcb_board_rules) but nothing requires or suggests it on New, and the toast says \"worker error (compile)\" rather than \"this board declares no design rules — set them under Options\".\n\nUSER-VISIBLE: the message is technically exact but names a compile internal, not the action. Fails the user-visible axis.\n\nOPTIONS (not implemented; owner decides): (a) New board seeds the same defaults the panel already draws with (DEFAULT_TRACE_WIDTH_MM etc.) INTO design_rules so file and canvas agree — most deterministic, one source of truth; (b) fab preview / gerbers / order package pre-check design_rules in the panel and toast \"set design rules under Options\" with a button, leaving the file untouched; (c) both. Option (a) removes the silent divergence the panel currently has between what it draws and what it exports, which is the actual defect.","created_at":"2026-09-02T14:10:42","updated_at":"2026-09-02T14:10:42","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["design-rules","fab-preview","new-board","pcb","rca","user-visible"],"repro_steps":"1. Install pcb 0.6.0 from marketplace; File → New PCB (do not set any rule). 2. minerva_pcb_export_yaml editor_name=\"PCB Editor Panel\" → design_rules block has no trace_width_mm/clearance_mm/via_*. 3. Click \"Fab preview (exact)\" → toast \"worker error (compile) — design_rules.trace_width_mm must be a positive number; got None\". 4. Options → Trace width / Via diameter / Via drill / Clearance, set all four → fab preview succeeds."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062a75bab7e9ea35b1a201cfa1505","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"describe_component connected_to repeats every member of a multi-pin net once per pin of the described part","description":"HITL 2026-09-02, pcb 0.6.0, rev B board: minerva_pcb_describe_component U1S returns connected_to with the whole GND membership list (41 entries) repeated 5 times — once per U1S pin on GND (GND_22/23/24/44 + one more). Same for +3V3 (2 pins). ~200 of the ~250 entries are duplicates. Token waste on every describe call for any part with several pins on one net (socket rows, ICs with multiple GND pads), and it hides the real fan-out count.","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:05:30","updated_at":"2026-09-02T15:05:30","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["llm-ergonomics","mcp","pcb","tc14-hitl"],"repro_steps":"Load smart_remote_v2_board.yaml; minerva_pcb_describe_component editor_name=\"Smart Remote v2 rev B\" component_id=\"U1S\"; count occurrences of \"C1.2 via GND\" in connected_to (5)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062a785467d84a840bfc82e720f1f","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"board_check reports assembly \"pass\" on a board the order package refuses with assembly_anchor_off_lands","description":"HITL 2026-09-02, pcb 0.6.0, rev B board with U1S children rotation_deg 270 + hand anchors. minerva_pcb_board_check → promotable:true, assembly:{status:\"pass\", findings:[]}. minerva_pcb_order_package (jlcpcb-economic) on the same bytes → refused assembly_anchor_off_lands (\"U1S_A anchors at (83.1, 62.797), 25.82 mm outside the lands box (32.72, 8.607)-(57.28, 63.647)\"). The TC14 gates (assembly_anchor_off_lands, assembly_child_lands_mismatch) run in assembly_outputs.emit()/service_profile, not in the assembly_check tri-state the census, promote gate and workspace_commit consult. So the census says the board is promotable and the design of record gets written with a placement no nozzle can use; the refusal only appears at export, after promote. Same class as the filed \"census must match promote\" work (OFC-4): the census promised to report every refusal a promote would raise — it does, but the export raises more than promote does.","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:05:40","updated_at":"2026-09-02T15:05:40","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["assembly","gates","llm-ergonomics","pcb","tc14-hitl"],"repro_steps":"board_check on smart_remote_v2_board.yaml @ 5ea8b1e5 (uncommitted Phase A state) vs order_package on the same file with board_path+board_digest."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062ba77137c2d8a0d3c9813c6f2a2","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"pcb: electrical rules check (ERC) over the board YAML — pin types per part, net-level rules, one verb, folded into the census","description":"WHY: DRC (connectivity + geometric) takes the netlist as given. Nothing checks whether the netlist itself makes electrical sense before copper is bought. On rev B (HITL 2026-09-02) the whole chain passed — DRC clean, census promotable, fab preview rendering — while the board carried a one-pin net (I2S1_WS on U1S.GPIO5), an I2C bus with no pull-up source on the board, and a switched rail whose only source is a PFET drain. None of that is visible to any existing verb. ERC is the schematic-side sibling of DRC and the board YAML IS the schematic here (YAML source of truth), so ERC runs on the YAML.\n\nWHAT:\n1. Pin types. Add an optional per-pin `type` to the footprint/part layer (power_in, power_out, output, input, bidirectional, open_drain, passive, no_connect, unspecified), sourced the same way footprints are: datasheet-authored, in the library `.kicad_mod` (KiCad already carries pin type on symbols, not footprints — so this is a small sidecar table per footprint, `<lib>.pretty/<name>.pins.json`, or a `pins:` block on the board component). Missing type = unspecified = participates only in the weak rules. Passive is the default for 2-pin R/C/L/fuse/jumper footprints.\n2. Rules, each with a code (erc_*), severity, and the pins named:\n   - erc_power_input_unpowered: a net with power_in pins and no power_out / declared source (a `power_source: true` annotation on a component pin, e.g. Q2.3 for +3V3_SW, or a net-level `source:` key).\n   - erc_output_conflict: two push-pull outputs, or an output on a power net.\n   - erc_input_undriven: an input/bidirectional pin whose net has no output, power_out, passive path or connector pin.\n   - erc_single_pin_net: a net with one member (advisory unless the pin is an input).\n   - erc_unconnected_pin: a pin not in any net and not marked no_connect (advisory; connectors and MCU sockets will dominate — allow a per-component `unused_pins_ok: true`).\n   - erc_duplicate_ref, erc_net_without_pins.\n   - erc_bus_no_pullup (advisory): a net named/typed as open-drain (I2C) with no passive to a power net on the board — the module may carry them, so advisory with a per-net `pullups: external` acknowledgment.\n3. Surface: worker method `erc` over a canonical board → {ok, findings[], advisories[], counts, not_evaluated[]}; MCP verb minerva_pcb_erc (yaml|board|board_path+digest, and the live-board form via editor_name like board_drc); folded into minerva_pcb_board_check as a fourth block beside connectivity/geometric/assembly, and into order_package preflight as advisory rows. GUI: Check menu entry + findings in the same panel as DRC (parity rule: GUI affordance AND MCP verb).\n4. Pin-map export rides on the same pin table: a `pins` block in the order-package manifest and a generated `board_pins.h` (net name per MCU GPIO) so firmware cannot drift from the board — this is what would have caught the v1-firmware vs rev B pin mismatch (OLED 8/9 vs 39/38, I2S mic vs PDM on 4/6, amp WS/DIN swapped).\n\nORACLE: rev B as of digest 0899fd60 must yield exactly: erc_single_pin_net I2S1_WS (advisory), erc_bus_no_pullup I2C_SCL/I2C_SDA (advisory), and zero errors once Q2.3 is declared the +3V3_SW source; a mutated fixture with U4.3 (VIN) removed from VIN must fail erc_power_input_unpowered naming U4.3; a fixture tying U1S.GPIO15 and U2.1 both typed output must fail erc_output_conflict.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: simulation of any kind; voltage-domain checks (a later rule once pins carry a voltage); rewriting existing library footprints — the pin table is additive.","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:26:22","updated_at":"2026-09-02T15:26:22","priority":2,"tags":["erc","llm-ergonomics","pcb","smart-remote-v2","validation"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062c387487f05a24933712adcfadd","type":"discussion","status":"active","title":"Board-level simulation for pcb: three approaches (crawl/walk/run, boil the ocean, LLM substitution) and the full stack each needs","description":"CONTEXT (2026-09-02, rev B HITL). DRC, census, fab preview and assembly gates all pass on smart-remote-v2 rev B, yet nothing in the chain can say whether firmware will run on it. Reading the netlist by hand found: v1 firmware pin map disagrees with rev B (OLED SDA/SCL 8/9 vs 39/38; mic is I2S on 16/17/18 in firmware vs PDM on CLK 4 / DATA 6 on the board; amp WS/DIN swapped), a one-pin net (I2S1_WS on GPIO5), no on-board I2C pull-ups, all peripherals behind a load switch firmware must enable early. Question: what stack answers \"will it work\" and what is the cheapest way there. ERC DCR 01a062ba7713 filed separately; its per-pin type table is the keystone every approach below depends on.\n\nCOMMON FOUNDATION (needed by all three)\n- Board YAML = the schematic (source of truth). Components, nets, footprints, assembly.\n- Pin-type table (ERC DCR): per footprint/part, pin → power_in | power_out | output | input | bidirectional | open_drain | passive | no_connect. Datasheet-authored, library sidecar.\n- Pin-map export: `board_pins.h` (net name per MCU GPIO) generated from the YAML so firmware cannot drift from the board.\n- ERC verb over the YAML, folded into board_check.\n\nAPPROACH A — CRAWL / WALK / RUN (layered, each layer is the oracle for the next)\nCrawl (no engine):\n- Pins header generator: YAML nets → board_pins.h. Job: kill firmware/board drift by construction.\n- Power-tree derivation: YAML → which rail feeds which part, which GPIO gates which rail, per-rail total decoupling, divider ratios and ADC full-scale. Job: answers \"who powers what\" without simulation. Arithmetic on the YAML.\nWalk (firmware against a virtual board, no analog):\n- Translator: YAML + pin table → Wokwi diagram.json (or a QEMU machine description). Job: wire virtual parts to the exact GPIOs on the board.\n- Footprint→virtual-part mapping table (pushbutton, SSD1306, analog joystick, I2S sink stub, PDM source stub). Job: give each board part a behavioral twin.\n- MCU emulator: Wokwi ESP32-S3 (hosted, free tier, CLI + VS Code) or Espressif's QEMU fork (OSS, no I2S/PDM/ADC/WiFi peripherals). Job: run the real firmware binary.\n- Test driver: scripted button presses, OLED framebuffer assertions, serial-log assertions. Job: turn a run into a pass/fail.\nRun (analog on demand, targeted, not general):\n- Power-path SPICE netlist generator: YAML → ngspice netlist for battery, F1, Q1, U4 (vendor model), L1, output caps, Q2 + gate network, switched-rail lumped capacitance, R4/R5/C16 only. Job: four numbers — rail settle time, Q2 inrush peak, Q2 gate margin at 3.3 V logic, ADC pin voltage at battery full/empty.\n- ngspice (BSD, headless) + vendor/authored device models. Job: solve the analog. Missing model = not_evaluated, never pass.\n- Calibration pass against the real rev B with a scope, once. Job: make the numbers trustworthy.\nSizing: crawl 1 cycle; walk 2–3; run 1–2 plus calibration; QEMU open-ended and only if the host path hides a bug class.\n\nAPPROACH B — BOIL THE OCEAN (one unified sim; re-priced with LLM labor ≈ 0)\nSplit into engine (never rewrite), model (LLM writes from datasheets, fixture-pinned), glue (LLM writes):\n- Analog engine: ngspice via subprocess/PySpice (OSS). Job: continuous-time solve of the power path. Do not write a solver.\n- Analog device models: AP63203 (vendor, possibly LTspice-encrypted → LLM-authored table model from datasheet curves), AO3401A PFET (LLM-authored from datasheet Vth/Rds/Ciss), passives. Job: fidelity. This is the hard part — plausible ≠ verified; needs a real-board calibration oracle.\n- Peripheral behavioral models (LLM-written, one per part, datasheet-pinned tests): pushbutton, pot/joystick, SSD1306 register state machine + framebuffer, PDM mic as bitstream-from-WAV, MAX98357A as I2S sink-to-WAV, load switch as rail on/off, PTC, reverse-polarity FET. Easy; compounds into a library like footprints do.\n- MCU: either (b1) ESP-IDF host/Linux build of the real firmware against a HAL shim (I2C/I2S/GPIO/ADC calls routed to the models) — moderate, loses cycle accuracy and WiFi, keeps all application logic; or (b2) Espressif QEMU with peripheral models injected as QEMU device code in C — hard integration, cycle-accurate core, still no WiFi.\n- Co-simulation scheduler: lockstep fixed timestep with analog solved between digital events, or decoupled (power-up vs steady state as separate questions). Moderate; semantics, not code, is the work.\n- YAML→everything translators: YAML → SPICE netlist (power section), YAML → peripheral wiring, YAML → HAL pin config. Easy once the pin table exists.\n- Test driver as in A-walk.\nDifficulty ranking: peripherals easy; translators easy; host-HAL MCU moderate; co-sim moderate; analog fidelity hard (oracle-limited, not code-limited); QEMU peripherals hard. ~80% of value in a handful of cycles; last 20% limited by measurements not yet taken. Still build in A's order because each layer is the oracle for the next; that is engineering order, not YAGNI.\n\nAPPROACH C — LLM SUBSTITUTION (be the box instead of building it; reality instead of a model)\nWhat the LLM substitutes outright (judgment boxes):\n- The reviewer: LLM reads the YAML and does ERC-plus (catches things rules can't, e.g. module-borne pull-ups, dual 3V3 sources over USB). Not repeatable → keep the ERC tool for the census; LLM for the one-off review.\n- The pin-map checker: LLM diffs firmware headers vs YAML nets (done today). Generator still wins by construction.\n- Back-of-envelope analog: divider ratio, RC corner, gate margin, inrush order of magnitude, inductor in-range. Most of what SPICE is used for on a 3.3 V board.\n- The vendor SPICE model: LLM reads datasheet curves → table-driven behavioral model, inspectable.\n- The virtual user: in a host-HAL harness the LLM presses buttons, reads the OLED framebuffer as data, judges the menu — exploratory testing instead of scripted assertions.\n- The simulator GUI: waveform/diagram viewers exist for eyes; the LLM reads arrays.\nThe big lateral: substitute simulation with REALITY (hardware-in-the-loop)\n- Real ESP32-S3 devkit (it is socketed on the carrier — same part) + the real v1 peripheral modules on a breadboard, wired to rev B's GPIO map.\n- Terminal MCP: flash, read serial, ask the owner to press a button, read the log. Job: the \"sim\" is the board minus the copper.\n- What remains: (1) copper matches the breadboard pin map = the pins-header diff; (2) power path behaves = the only place SPICE or a scope earns its keep.\n- Peripheral models and QEMU only return if the bench is unavailable, which for a socketed-devkit design it never is.\nCannot substitute: physics with numbers attached (switcher waveforms, ADC accuracy, timing margins); the real oracle (scope/serial); determinism (rubric pivot — anything gating promote/order stays a tool).\nResulting stack for C: LLM review now → pins header (tool) → breadboard HIL via terminal MCP → small SPICE power-path run calibrated once on rev B.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS FOR THE OWNER\n1. Is the devkit+modules breadboard available now? If yes, C's HIL path is the cheapest \"as if real\" and can start before rev B ships.\n2. Does the power-path SPICE run (A-run / B analog) get built before or after a scope calibration on rev B? Recommendation: after — author the generator, gate its numbers on calibration.\n3. Wokwi (hosted, closed core) vs QEMU (OSS, thin peripherals) vs host-HAL (OSS, no cycle accuracy) for the firmware layer if the bench is ever not enough.\n4. Where does the peripheral-model library live if built: plugins repo beside footprints (same datasheet-oracle discipline) or the smart-remote repo?","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:36:16","updated_at":"2026-09-02T15:36:16","tags":["architecture","erc","firmware","pcb","simulation","smart-remote-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062cd06997293886314b493f06269","type":"chore","status":"done","title":"pcb ledger: measure (C_1210_3225Metric, jlcpcb, C394395 Taiyo Yuden EMK325ABJ107MM-T) — smart-remote-v2 C12 substitution for out-of-stock C6120014","description":"Owner chose C394395 (55,505 in stock) over C6120014 (9 in stock) for the 100uF 16V X5R 1210 bulk cap C12 on smart-remote-v2 rev B (2026-09-02). Same land pattern (Capacitor_SMD:C_1210_3225Metric). The orientation ledger is keyed on (footprint, house, part), so the new part needs its own vendor payload in worker/tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/ + index entry and a regenerated pcb/library/part_orientation.json (+ coverage.md). Expected verdict: aligned, like every other passive on that drawing. Board YAML edit (house_parts.jlcpcb + mpn on C12) rides in the ccsandbox repo.","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:46:38","updated_at":"2026-09-02T16:06:20","priority":2,"tags":["bom","orientation-ledger","pcb","smart-remote-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a062cd90c176e2920bd1e3cd2c5b2b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb library: datasheet-authored footprint for Panasonic EVP-ASAC1A (LCSC C4365033) with vendor pad numbers, corpus payload + measured ledger row — unblocks smart-remote-v2 SW1–SW4","description":"The only order-package blocker left on smart-remote-v2 rev B (2026-09-02): (EVP-ASAC1A:SW_EVP-ASAC1A, jlcpcb, C4365033) has no ledger row — assembly_orientation_unknown for SW1..SW4. The existing footprint names its pads A/B, the vendor drawing numbers them, so the pair cannot be measured by pad name. Owner chose the datasheet route: (1) fetch the Panasonic datasheet for EVP-ASAC1A (via the LCSC product page's datasheet link); (2) author the land pattern from the datasheet figure with the VENDOR's pad numbers, including the two mechanical/ground tabs if the drawing has them, into a new .kicad_mod (record the rotation to the vendor canonical orientation per the footprint discipline); (3) add the trimmed EasyEDA payload C4365033.json + index entry to worker/tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/; (4) regenerate the ledger + coverage, read the diff; (5) footprint test row (pad count/pitch/span oracle from the datasheet); (6) board side (ccsandbox): SW1..SW4 footprint ref → new drawing, nets BTNx/GND re-pointed from pins A/B to the vendor pad numbers, placement re-checked on the canvas (owner) — silk/courtyard may differ from the old drawing. Oracle: order_package on rev B emits with zero blockers.","created_at":"2026-09-02T15:47:14","updated_at":"2026-09-02T16:06:21","priority":1,"tags":["footprint","order-blocker","orientation-ledger","pcb","smart-remote-v2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0638f9ccb7752a6e5454651ac98d1","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: component value is stored TWICE in the canonical YAML (`value` + `properties.value`) against the DRY requirement — properties copy silently wins on load, top-level edit lost on promote","description":"2026-09-02, pcb 0.6.1. smart_remote_v2_board.yaml carries U3 `value: SPK0641HT4H-1` (edited by hand) AND `properties: {value: MSM261S4030H0R}` (the older string). load_board then minerva_pcb_promote wrote the file back with `value: MSM261S4030H0R` — the panel takes the properties copy as the live value and re-emits both keys from it, so the top-level edit vanished with census_delta all zeros and no warning. The canonical schema (docs/board-yaml.md) exposes `value` as THE component value; `properties.value` is the panel's legacy shape leaking into the file. Every component on rev B carries both keys; only U3 disagreed, so only U3 lost data. Same class as the earlier canonical-extra losses (assembly: exclude, mpn) fixed in pcb_component.gd — this one is a key the model DOES have a field for, so the passthrough guard did not protect it.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:19:11","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:00","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["data-loss","dry","pcb","smart-remote-v2","task-cycle-15","tc14-hitl","yaml"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T1 (7ca6573): value has one home; properties.value refused by name at every boundary (Go walk, Python parse + validator vector 460, panel loader with a held status lead). Scoped run green.","repro_steps":"1. In a board YAML set a component's `value: X` and `properties: {value: Y}` (Y != X). 2. minerva_pcb_load_board path=... 3. describe_component → value Y. 4. minerva_pcb_promote → the file now says `value: Y`. No warning at load or promote."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"Board YAML DRY cleanup — one fact, one key: positive codec schema, no session state in the design file, one serializer, promote field delta (ships as pcb 0.7.0)","description":"OWNER RULING 2026-09-02: the design required DRY and never specified compatibility with the v1 experiment's minpcb shape; the duplicates in the canonical YAML are implementation bugs, fixed by DELETION with no migration path. Not a DCR.\n\nTHE DISEASE (survey of rev B, 47 components): the canonical file is defined negatively — the panel's dictionary minus a blocklist, with a passthrough that parks whatever it does not recognise — across THREE independent codecs (Go typed struct + inline Extra bag; GD known-key list + canonical_extra; Python get()-with-fallbacks). Nothing refuses a duplicate because no schema knows one.\n  - `properties.value` restates `value` (47/47) — bug 01a0638f9ccb, and the U3 BOM mislabel that exposed it.\n  - `pins[].x_mm/y_mm` (+drill/annulus) restate the library footprint (170 pins); compile_board._check_coincidence exists to police the duplicate.\n  - `assembly.package` free text restates the footprint (43).\n  - session/render state in the design file: net `color`/`is_power_net`/empty `properties`, `grid_mm`, component render keys.\n  - two serializers of one board: export_yaml (saved shape, 306 KB) vs promote (canonical, 112 KB).\n\nDECISION (owner, rubric-graded 2026-09-02): the BOM Footprint column reads a package label authored ONCE on the FOOTPRINT (footprints.lock.json entry `assembly.package`, already present for the strip and the switch); when a footprint carries no label the column prints the footprint ref verbatim (honest fallback, not a guess). No per-component override — two parts needing different labels are on different footprints. Backfilling the ~40 unlabeled lock entries is a chore, not a blocker.\n\nCHILDREN (one task each, in order): T1 bug 01a0638f9ccb value; T2 Go positive schema; T3 pins emitted only as real overrides; T4 session state out; T5 one serializer; T6 promote field-level delta; chore: footprint package labels; chore (smart-remote): rev B cleaned + package regenerated byte-compared.\n\nCAM IMPACT: none by design — compile_board consumes footprint + canonical facts; gerbers/CPL byte-identical (determinism gate + kicad-cli oracle + IR/placement parity are the proof); BOM Footprint column changes per the decision above.\n\nCORPUS IMPACT (measured): 23 board fixtures — properties.value 1, pins-with-coords 6, assembly.package 2, render keys 3, grid_mm 3; ~12 test files invert from \"survives the passthrough\" to \"refused by name\" (minpcb_test, assembly_test x4, test_assembly_spec fallback chain, test_assembly_outputs package tests, the \"provably unread\" key lists, 5 GD properties[\"value\"] sites). resolve_corners' deliberately misplaced pin and THT drill overrides are REAL overrides and must survive the pin cleanup.\n\nRELEASE: format-breaking for files carrying the old keys → pcb 0.7.0, not a patch.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:41:38","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:32","priority":1,"tags":["codec","dry","pcb","task-cycle-15","umbrella","yaml"],"resolution":"All six children landed; pcb 0.7.0 tagged at b8ad1d7; CI watch in progress; owner HITL on the release pending."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a4a910720e9621d04527fb3214","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb Go codec parks unknown component keys in an inline Extra bag instead of refusing them — the canonical schema is defined negatively","description":"internal/board Component has 9 typed fields + `Extra map[string]interface{} yaml:\",inline\"`; refuseUnknownKeys runs only inside the assembly sub-structs. A mistyped or stale key on a component (or net/trace/via/zone) is parked and round-tripped rather than refused, which is how `properties`, `color`, `footprint_id`, render bounds and the rest reached the design of record. The GD panel and the Python worker each carry their own key list / fallbacks, so the format is defined three times and enforced nowhere. GOAL: the file boundary (Go) owns a POSITIVE schema — every key on every entity typed; an unknown key refuses with the entity ref and the key named, as assembly does today; no Extra bag on components/nets/traces/vias/zones (board-level annotations stay whatever they are today). The GD model consumes the typed shape (its known-key list and canonical_extra passthrough shrink accordingly); the Python readers drop get()-with-fallback chains (assembly_spec.py:202 block→comp→properties). ORACLE: a fixture with one mistyped component key is refused naming the ref+key; rev B round-trips byte-stable through deserialize→serialize; the \"provably unread\" key lists in test_board_health_resolve_first and test_canonical_wire_board shrink and stay green; the gerber goldens do not move.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:10","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:02","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["codec","dry","pcb","task-cycle-15","yaml"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T2 (0865652 + review/scoped-run fixes): positive schema via one reflective walk on YAML and JSON; Extra bags, json.go pairs and the .minpcb importer deleted; identity only in the assembly block; deserialize reply carries `resolved` beside the board; panel dict = design keys only; BOM Footprint = lock label else ref (option C). Scoped run green."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a4c71674ffbe412b03f8c9a7d6","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: component pin coordinates restate the library footprint; the coincidence check exists to police the duplicate — emit pins only as real overrides","description":"rev B carries 170 pins with x_mm/y_mm (44 with drill/annulus) that restate the library pads the `footprint` key already names, because the seed export wrote every pin out. compile_board._check_coincidence (line ~1696) then re-derives what a pin \"overrides\" by comparing the two copies — a check whose only job is catching a duplicate that disagrees. GOAL: a pin entry in the file is an OVERRIDE and nothing else — emitted only when it differs from the resolved footprint's pad (position, drill, annulus, plated); a pin equal to the library is not written. The coincidence check becomes the override check it was meant to be (a pin that names no library pad still refuses). A board with a `pads` key (full authority) is unchanged. ORACLE: gerber goldens + IR parity + placement parity byte-identical; resolve_corners' deliberately misplaced pin still fails coincidence; a THT fixture with an authored drill override keeps it through a round trip; rev B serializes with zero restated pins and its gerbers do not move. Fixture cleanup is a per-pin comparison against the library by a one-off script whose output is reviewed — NOT a blanket strip.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:18","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:03","priority":1,"severity":2,"tags":["dry","footprints","pcb","task-cycle-15","yaml"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T3 (8d37945 + 9af793f): a pins entry is an override only; both writers drop restating pins; every pin correlated by number; a differing bare position still refuses (typed `override` is the only channel); prune script checked in; fixture pruning deferred to chore 01a0640bc4d9; inline Go Pin fields deletion filed 01a0640b999e."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a4e4c17f6882a6cab11f8bd3d0","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: session and render state is written into the design file — net color / is_power_net / empty properties, grid_mm, component render keys","description":"rev B: every net carries `color` (white, all 35), `is_power_net` (false on all, including +3V3), and `properties: {}`; the board carries `grid_mm`; fixtures carry component `color`, `local_bounds`, `bbox_center_offset`, `has_pad_geometry`, `footprint_id`, `graphics`. None is a design fact, none is read by CAM (the \"provably unread\" tests prove it), all ride through promote because the passthrough keeps them. GOAL: the design file carries design facts only. Net color, grid, label visibility, lock state and render bounds live in the panel's own session state (the .minproj __panel_state already exists for that); `is_power_net` goes unless a consumer needs it, in which case it is DERIVED. Once the Go schema is positive (sibling bug) these keys refuse; this task removes the WRITERS and relocates the state. ORACLE: rev B promote emits none of the listed keys; reload keeps the panel's net colors and grid from session state, not the file; the \"provably unread\" key lists shrink to the annotations that remain.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:25","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:05","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["dry","pcb","task-cycle-15","yaml"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T4 (8449591): grid pitch and component locks ride a `__panel_session` block beside the board in the host payload; grid_mm deleted from the Go schema and fixtures; is_power_net derived from the name; host envelope keys stripped before the loader's positive root check."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a4ff2f7ecdb69751d7b9d93e16","type":"bug","status":"resolved","title":"pcb: two serializers for one board — export_yaml emits the panel's saved shape (306 KB) while promote emits the canonical document (112 KB)","description":"minerva_pcb_export_yaml runs the Export YAML button's implementation, which serializes `_data.to_saved_board_dict()` (render extras included), while promote serializes the canonical `to_board_dict()`. Same live board, two documents that differ in dozens of keys; the export is documented as \"the canonical YAML text\" and is what an agent diffs against disk. During the 2026-09-02 HITL the semantic diff between export and disk was dominated by render keys before the real difference surfaced. GOAL: one canonical serializer; export_yaml and promote emit byte-identical text for the same board. If the saved shape is still needed for the .minproj panel state it is named as panel state and never offered as YAML. ORACLE: export text == promote text for rev B; the by-ref path still works over the cap.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:32","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:06","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["dry","pcb","task-cycle-15","yaml"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T5 (c2e99d4): one serializer — export_yaml and promote share one pcb.serialize helper; to_saved_board_dict / strip_session_state deleted; structural test oracle (one request site)."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a5165271b4a505f2b7ffc5744b","type":"work_item","status":"done","title":"pcb: minerva_pcb_promote reports a field-level delta against the prior file (per component: value/footprint/x/y/rotation/layer/assembly; nets; copper counts)","description":"The promote reply's census_delta is counts only; on 2026-09-02 it read all zeros while U3's value silently flipped. The prior file is already parsed for the copper-regression guard, so a field-level diff rides on data promote already holds. GOAL: the reply names every authored field that changed per component (value, footprint, x_mm, y_mm, rotation_deg, layer, assembly identity), plus nets added/removed and per-net trace/via counts — so a promote that changes something the session did not edit is visible in the reply and the status line. Advisory, never gating. ORACLE: promote after a one-field YAML edit reports exactly that ref+field with old→new; an unchanged board reports an empty delta; the reply shape is documented in docs/tools.md.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:38","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:08","priority":2,"tags":["guard","pcb","promote","task-cycle-15"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","resolution":"Task-cycle 15 T6 (9d6d654): promote reply carries an advisory design_delta (per-component changed fields with old/new, components/nets added/removed, per-net trace/via counts), on the status line, documented in docs/tools.md; limits filed 01a0642eebfc."}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063a536dd7dabbf8efe99fb803c6f","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb library: author the package label (footprints.lock.json entry assembly.package) on the ~40 unlabeled footprints — the BOM Footprint column reads it, falling back to the footprint ref","description":"Owner decision 2026-09-02 (rubric option C): the BOM Footprint column prints a short package label authored ONCE on the footprint (lock entry `assembly.package`, present today for the 1x22 strip and the EVP-ASAC1A switch), and the footprint ref verbatim when no label exists — an honest fallback, never a derived guess. No per-component `assembly.package`. Two halves: (1) the emitter change — assembly_outputs reads the label through the resolved footprint's lock entry, else the ref (part of the value/schema tasks, since the component key goes away there); (2) THIS CHORE — backfill labels for the unlabeled lock entries (0805/0603/1206/1210 chips, SOT-23, TSOT-23-6, VQFN-16, LGA-8 mic, JST PH/XH horizontals, Sunlord 4018, PinHeader 1x02, DIP-6, DevKitC socket set, …), short and human (\"0805\", \"SOT-23\", \"JST-PH 2.0mm 4P right-angle\"), reviewed once. Can land after the cycle; each label improves the BOM with no code change.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:47","updated_at":"2026-09-02T19:42:47","priority":2,"tags":["bom","footprints","pcb","task-cycle-15"],"parent":"plugins.dct:01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55"}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063b981237741ada82a9e6505006a","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb panel: PCBComponent.to_dict() is silently the UNDO-SNAPSHOT shape — a typed field not added there is erased by the first undo","description":"Found during task-cycle 15 T1 (bug 01a0638f9ccb). ui/model/pcb_component.gd to_dict()/load_from_dict() (~line 1440) is documented as the legacy .minpcb shape but is ALSO the undo-snapshot shape (PCBData.save_to_history). The only statement of that is a comment inside the function body, so any new typed model field (the T1 `value` field needed exactly this) that is not mirrored there round-trips away on the first undo, with no test or doc telling the author. Options: a class-level doc line naming to_dict as the snapshot shape, or split the snapshot shape from the legacy file shape (the legacy shape may die with the minpcb importer in task-cycle 15 T2, which would leave to_dict as the snapshot shape only — rename it accordingly). Oracle for any fix: a model with every typed field set survives save_to_history → undo → redo field-for-field.","created_at":"2026-09-02T20:04:56","updated_at":"2026-09-02T20:04:56","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","panel","pcb","task-cycle-15","undo"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a063fa44cd7190b6affe958ef0f1c8","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb Go codec: an assembly-block refusal names the offending key but not the owning component — prefix the component path/ref like the schema walk does","description":"Found by Codex on task-cycle 15 batch 1. internal/board/schema.go's walk stops at any type that owns its own decoding (ComponentAssembly, AssemblyPlacement, AssemblyOffset), so their refusals (`invalid_component_assembly: unknown assembly field \"mpm\"`) carry no component ref, unlike a schema-walk refusal (`invalid_board_structure: board.components[3] (U3): unknown key \"colour\"`). On a 47-component board the author has to search for the typo. GOAL: every assembly refusal names the component (path + ref) the same way the walk does — either by having the walk preflight the assembly mapping's keys with the entity path before the typed decoder runs (one extra check, same known-key set), or by wrapping the decoder's error with the component label in the component's decode. Both YAML and JSON boundaries. ORACLE: a fixture with `assembly: {mpm: X}` on U3 refuses with a message containing both \"U3\" and \"mpm\" on both boundaries; the existing assembly_test messages keep their code.","created_at":"2026-09-02T21:15:41","updated_at":"2026-09-02T21:15:41","priority":3,"tags":["codec","file-dont-fix","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0640b999e7123b6e745f3326f22b0","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb Go codec: delete the deprecated inline Pin geometry fields (drill_mm/annulus_diameter_mm/pad_width_mm/pad_height_mm/plated) — `override` is the one channel","description":"Task-cycle 15 T3 (bug 01a063a4c716) made a pin entry an override and nothing else: both writers drop a pin that restates the library, and legacy inline fab geometry is folded (redundant → dropped, divergent → typed `override`) by the worker. The Go Pin struct (internal/board/board.go) STILL models the deprecated inline fields as a second home for pad geometry beside `override` (no Go code populates them; TestSMDPadDimsSurviveJSONMarshal guards them). Under the cycle's one-fact-one-key ruling the inline channel should go: the codec refuses `drill_mm` etc. on a pin by name, and an author states a deviation only through `override`. Requires: fixture cleanup with pcb/scripts/prune_restated_pins.py (coupon_jlc1 12 pins, hitl_bench 30; rev B 44 inline-geometry pins — all measured REDUNDANT) under a golden-verified run (source_digest moves; copper must not — gerber goldens + IR/placement parity are the oracle), inverting the Go tests that assert the deprecated channel, and the Python fold keeping its reader for the CLI path or dropping it with the same refusal. Not done in TC15 because the goldens could not be run mid-cycle and the writers already never emit the fields.","created_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:36","updated_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:36","priority":2,"tags":["codec","dry","follow-up","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0640bb1af75efa52cb1aacd5ff7ba","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb worker: two pin readers bypass the resolved pads — route_bridge.board_to_router (dead, test-only) and pad_source.iter_pads' silent pins fallback","description":"Found during task-cycle 15 T3 (pins are overrides only). (1) worker/pcb_worker/route_bridge.py:282 board_to_router iterates comp[\"pins\"] directly and ignores resolved pads; on a pins-pruned board it sees zero pads. It has no production caller (only test_route_bridge.py; the live route path is resolved_board_to_router) — delete it or rebase it onto pad_source.iter_pads. (2) worker/pcb_worker/pad_source.py:213 iter_pads falls back to comp[\"pins\"] when `pads` is absent; on a pruned, un-resolved board that yields zero pads silently instead of refusing — fail-closed (refuse by name: no resolved pads and no authored pads) is the safer answer. (3) resolve.py does not repopulate comp[\"pins\"] from the resolved pads the way the panel's fill_pins_from_pads does, so a Python consumer reading comp[\"pins\"] after a resolve sees only the overrides; no live consumer breaks today but the two sides of the boundary differ. ORACLE for (2): a pruned board compiled without a library resolve refuses naming the component rather than emitting zero lands.","created_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:43","updated_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:43","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","pcb","task-cycle-15","worker"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0640bc4d973f99d0387b0c87ea1db","type":"chore","status":"open","title":"pcb fixtures: prune restated pins with scripts/prune_restated_pins.py under a golden-verified run (coupon_jlc1 12, hitl_bench 30)","description":"Task-cycle 15 T3 checked in pcb/scripts/prune_restated_pins.py (dry run prints the per-pin verdict; --apply rewrites only the pins blocks). The fixtures that carry restated pins (coupon_jlc1.yaml 12, hitl_bench.yaml 30; 15 real pins kept) were deliberately NOT pruned mid-cycle: every one feeds the gerber-golden / IR-parity / determinism gates that could not run at the time, and pruning changes source_digest (stamped into order-package output). Do it with the suites running: apply, confirm copper-identical goldens (the compile-side test test_dropping_restated_pins_moves_no_copper already proves the IR is equal), and inspect any digest-bearing golden that moves for being digest-only.","created_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:48","updated_at":"2026-09-02T21:34:48","priority":3,"tags":["chore","fixtures","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0641e2dd57aa2a989f7c21b0f808d","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb panel: trace `locked` is read by five edit gates but has no writer — no UI or MCP verb can lock a trace; grid pitch changes are journalled but not restored by undo","description":"Found during task-cycle 15 T4. (1) PCBTrace.locked gates edits at pcb_data.gd ~1159/1212/1253, panel_tools.gd ~4712/4797 and pcb_region_describe.gd ~177, but the only assignment is pcb_trace.gd's legacy load_from_dict — nothing lets a user or an agent lock a trace, so the gates are dead code or a missing feature; decide which (an MCP verb + session-state entry beside component locks, or delete the gates). (2) PCBData.set_grid_size journals a change, but _restore_state never restores grid_size, so undo skips grid pitch changes silently. ORACLE for (1): a locked trace refuses a move through the same verb that moved it before; for (2): set grid → undo → grid is back.","created_at":"2026-09-02T21:54:54","updated_at":"2026-09-02T21:54:54","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","panel","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a06425aa83743ab4f06fcc84a2ca40","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb panel God files: PCBPanel.gd (~6,670 lines) and pcb_data.gd (~4,430 lines) — split by concern","description":"Measured during task-cycle 15 T5 (both shrank slightly in this cycle and are still 3-6x the 1-2 KLOC target). PCBPanel.gd mixes the host_owned save/load path, the worker channel round-trips (serialize, deserialize, board_health, zone_fill, mask_view, assembly), the promote gate, the status/lead machinery and the UI wiring; pcb_data.gd mixes the model, undo history, CSV codec, via/hole/zone/cutout/graphic sub-models, groups and the canonical codec. Candidate cuts: PCBPanel → pcb_panel_documents.gd (save/load/session/sidecars), pcb_panel_channels.gd (worker round-trips), pcb_panel_promote.gd; pcb_data → pcb_data_codec.gd (to/from board dict + legacy dict), pcb_data_history.gd, pcb_data_csv.gd. Off-tree rule: no class_name, relative preload, static funcs taking the data/panel. Oracle: every existing GD suite passes unchanged after the split (no behaviour change). panel_tools.gd (11.8 KLOC) is already filed separately (01a0604405ef).","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:03:05","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:03:05","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","god-file","panel","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0642eebfc7aa1a492d174c77b3f12","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb promote design_delta: authored geometry (pads/graphics/pins/refdes_placement) and net pin membership are not diffed","description":"Task-cycle 15 T6 shipped the promote reply's field-level design_delta (pcb/ui/pcb_promote_delta.gd) over the scalar component fields, the assembly block, nets added/removed and per-net trace/via counts. Two known limits, stated in the code and docs: (1) authored geometry keys — pads, graphics, pins (overrides), refdes_placement — are not compared, so a promotion whose only change is a pad geometry edit reports no changed field (~line 26); (2) a net's pin membership (nets[].pins) is not compared, so rewiring a net while keeping its name shows only as copper-count movement (~line 222). GOAL: extend the delta with a per-component `geometry` change marker (or per-key digest compare) and per-net pins added/removed. ORACLE: a one-pin rewire on disk reports that net with the pin ref moved; a pad-geometry-only edit reports the component with a geometry marker; an unchanged board still reports an empty delta.","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:13:11","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:13:11","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","pcb","promote","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0643a51d07d1eb98c6691a10fdd8e","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A --fixup for task N conflicts under autosquash when task N+1 edited the same block — keep it as a named batch-review commit via a GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR todo rewrite, never resolve by hand","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:25:38","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:25:38","tags":["fold","git","gotcha","task-cycle"],"value":"Task-cycle fold rule in practice (TC15, twice): `git commit --fixup <task N sha>` then `GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR=: git rebase -i --autosquash <base>` stops with \"Could not apply <fixup>\" whenever a LATER task commit touched the same lines (T1's status-lead fix sat on T2's rename; T5's promote docstring trim sat on T6's promote edits). Do NOT resolve the conflict inside the rebase (it rewrites the later task). `git rebase --abort`, then either (a) if the fixup is HEAD: `git commit --amend` it into a normal \"fix(pcb): batch-N review — …\" commit and rerun the autosquash (only the remaining fixups fold); or (b) if it is not HEAD: rerun the rebase with `GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR=\"python3 fix_todo.py\"` where the script deletes the autosquashed `fixup <sha>` line and appends `pick <sha>` + `exec git commit --amend -q -F <msgfile>` at the end of the todo — the fixup lands last as a named commit, the others fold. The skill already allows a clearly named batch-review fix commit when attribution cannot fold; record it in the commit body.","component":"task-cycle","key":"fixup-fold-conflicts-across-tasks","confidence":"measured","research_cost":4}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a0645129297511b6f895f83367c5b5","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"pcb GD: test_pcb_hover_card section 5 reads the OWNER's live preference store — pin the hover-card toggle in-process so the suite does not depend on ~/.local/share prefs","description":"Found during task-cycle 15 scoped execution. test_pcb_hover_card.gd section 5 (_run_live_canvas, 7 pointer-driven checks) fails on any machine whose ~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Minerva/plugins/data/pcb/preferences.json holds \"hover_card\": false (the owner's Options toggle): pcb_canvas._update_hover_card reads PcbOptionsPrefs.shared().get_bool(KEY_HOVER_CARD) and raises no card. Sections 1–4 pass. The suite must pin the toggle in-process (the prefs store supports a redirected base dir, or set the pref through the same API the Options menu uses and restore it after) so its verdict is about the canvas, not the developer's settings. ORACLE: the suite passes with the user pref set to false and does not modify that file.","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:50:35","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:50:35","priority":3,"tags":["file-dont-fix","gd-tests","pcb","task-cycle-15"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"01a064518251735e96493f4922fb6113","type":"hint","status":"draft","title":"A scoped test run longer than ~10 minutes must be launched detached (setsid nohup) — the Claude Code background Bash job is killed at the tool timeout","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:50:58","updated_at":"2026-09-02T22:51:11","tags":["claude-code","gd","gotcha","scoped-run","task-cycle"],"value":"TC15: the combined scoped run (pytest ~1300 tests + go test ./... + 22 GD suites) launched with run_in_background was KILLED at the 600 s tool ceiling with only the Python stage complete. Pattern that works: write the run as a script, `setsid nohup bash ./scoped.sh > run.log 2>&1 < /dev/null & disown`, end the script with a sentinel line (GD_RUN_DONE) and wait with a background `until grep -q GD_RUN_DONE run.log; do sleep 15; done`. Also: never chain `git rebase -i --autosquash … | tail -1 && <gates> && codex` — a conflicted rebase leaves the chain running on a half-rebased tree (one Codex pass reviewed garbage and was discarded); run the rebase alone, check `git status`, then continue. GD real-worker preflight for a scoped run: `cd pcb && go build -o pcb-plugin .`, park ~/.local/share/Minerva/plugins/pcb/runtime/<ver> (restore after), `python3 scripts/probe-worker-methods.py`; run suites per-file from ~/github/Minerva/src (`godot --headless --path . --script res://../../minerva-plugins/pcb/tests/gd/<suite>.gd`) and parse the \"=== Results:\" line. test_pcb_hover_card section 5 reads the OWNER's prefs (hover_card toggle) — a red there on a dev box is environment until work item 01a06451292 lands.","component":"task-cycle","key":"scoped-run-long-jobs-detach","confidence":"measured","research_cost":3}
