{"_type":"comment","id":197,"item_id":"01a0518a0c6c733db1907cc48910f460","author":"agent","text":"Scoped run results: Minerva test_chatgpt_image_notes 12/12, test_plugin_host_create_note_await 3/3, test_plugin_data_persistence 33/33, test_presentation_panel_note_hooks 25/25; pcb test_pcb_note_hooks 22/22, test_fab_preview_legibility 100/100, test_pcb_single_trace_tool 98/98 (real worker), test_pcb_panel_tools_migration 74/74, test_pcb_panel_tools 217/217, test_pcb_workflow_kinds 131/131, test_view_state 95/95. Two attributions: (a) test_notes_to_prompt_plugin_data ran 0 assertions and quit 0 — the test named BaseProvider/Note at its own compile time, loading them before the SingletonObject autoload exists (a `--script` SceneTree run); fixed in Minerva fold 31058e61 (stub provider built from source at run time; loads after process_frame; a no-assertion run now FAILS) → 10/10. (b) test_plugin_chat_providers 51/53: 'start_plugin ok — Subprocess failed to start: Can't connect' + 'connection exists' — the fixture python plugin answers initialize by hand; attributing by running the suite on a git worktree at base a1cb6455. pcb 'Identifier not found: SingletonObject' lines in the panel_tools suites are pre-existing noise (present in TC9 logs).","created_at":"2026-08-30T08:11:49"}
{"_type":"comment","id":198,"item_id":"01a0518a0c6c733db1907cc48910f460","author":"agent","text":"Owner HITL 2026-08-30 on the Minerva pre-release (auto-build-20260830-0844xx) + marketplace pcb 0.5.6: PASS — the integrated LLM sees the saved pcb note (picture + caption), and editor → note → editor round-trips (Save to Note, Edit-from-note reopens the board).","created_at":"2026-08-30T17:59:47"}
{"_type":"comment","id":199,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Codex","text":"## Codex review — reshape this as an order-release pipeline, not a wrapper around three exporters\n\n**Verdict:** the direction is right, but I would not ratify the DCR as written. It can plausibly generate four files; it does not yet define a trustworthy “this package is ready to order” boundary. The central reshape is: **compile one resolved physical board, then derive every submitted artifact and every preflight claim from that exact object.**\n\n### 1. Gerbers and assembly data can currently describe different boards\n\nThe existing BOM/CPL path deliberately consumes the RAW canonical dict, while Gerbers consume the compiled ResolvedBoard IR (`assembly_outputs.py` lines 7–13). CPL X/Y are copied directly from the raw component origin and Y-negated (`assembly_outputs.py` lines 339–369).\n\nThe package exporter should strict-compile once into an `OrderPackageBoard` or assembly-extended `ResolvedBoard`, then derive:\n\n- Gerbers/drills/paste\n- BOM\n- CPL\n- physical-placement expansion\n- DNP/paste decisions\n- local assembly preview\n- manifest/preflight evidence\n\nfrom that same compiled object. No second raw-YAML interpretation path.\n\n### 2. The assembly schema is underspecified and conflicts with existing state\n\n`assembly: exclude` already exists as a scalar. An “assembly identity block” needs an exact schema and migration, otherwise scalar-versus-map semantics collide.\n\nRecommended shape:\n\n```yaml\nassembly:\n  populate: true\n  manufacturer: Samtec\n  mpn: SSQ-122-03-T-S\n  package: HDR-TH_1x22_P2.54\n  comment: 1x22 female socket, 2.54 mm\n  house_parts:\n    jlcpcb: C41376161\n  paste: auto\n  placements:\n    - ref: U1S_A\n      offset_mm: {x: 0, y: 0}\n      rotation_deg: 0\n    - ref: U1S_B\n      offset_mm: {x: 22.86, y: 0}\n      rotation_deg: 0\n```\n\nContract decisions to pin:\n\n- Missing `placements` means one physical placement using the component ref.\n- Expanded refs are explicit, stable, globally unique authored data — never exporter-invented names.\n- Migrate legacy `assembly: exclude` to a structured non-populated/furniture state.\n- Board YAML identity is authoritative. The footprint lock already carries `assembly.mpn`, `package`, `dist_part_numbers`, and `orientation_convention`; define these as suggestion/provenance only and state the drift/precedence rule.\n- `lcsc` is too provider-specific for the portable core. Use `house_parts.jlcpcb`.\n- Add manufacturer, package, and BOM comment/description. MPN alone is not a complete or portable identity.\n- Preserve owner ruling: variants remain separate YAML boards.\n\n### 3. DNP needs an explicit paste policy\n\nRemoving a component from BOM/CPL does not remove its F/B paste apertures. Gerbers currently emit paste from resolved pad geometry.\n\nFor a non-populated SMD footprint, require an explicit decision:\n\n```yaml\npopulate: false\npaste: include   # hand-place later\n```\n\nor:\n\n```yaml\npopulate: false\npaste: exclude   # truly unstuffed\n```\n\nSafest rule: refuse an SMD DNP with `paste:auto`; otherwise a DNP toggle silently changes the physical stencil/assembly result. THT naturally emits no paste, but seal that with a regression test.\n\n### 4. The provenance digest is self-referential\n\nIf silk contains `<yaml-digest-8>` and that silk text is itself in YAML, hashing the complete YAML is a fixed-point problem. Define an explicit digest projection, e.g. canonical YAML with the provenance graphic text normalized to a sentinel before hashing.\n\nPrint an immutable design revision, not the export month:\n\n```\nsmart-remote-v2 rev-b d4e91c2a\n```\n\nKeep export timestamp in the manifest. Otherwise regenerating next month requires editing the design merely to satisfy the exporter.\n\nAlso remove “same spot doubles as JLC order-number location.” JLC discontinued the order-number mechanism in May 2026 and explicitly says not to add `JLCJLCJLCJLC`. The replacement is an optional barcode/serial service with different marker geometry:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/how-to-mark-on-pcb\n\n### 5. Unify fabrication, assembly-format, and order-service profiles\n\nThe repo already has versioned manufacturing-rule profiles (`jlcpcb-2layer`, `jlcpcb-4layer`), while BOM/CPL separately hard-code a `jlc` profile. Do not create another profile island.\n\nA selected service profile should pin:\n\n- fabrication rule profile\n- assembly tier: Economic vs Standard\n- supported layer counts, thicknesses, dimensions, board quantities, populated sides\n- BOM/CPL dialect, source-template URL/hash/fetched date\n- assembly/service constraints\n- checked, advisory-only, and uncheckable rules\n- required order-page/HITL confirmations\n\nCurrent Economic PCBA facts differ from the DCR: it is single-sided, supports 2/4/6 layers, 0.8–1.6 mm thickness and 2–50 boards — not a 30-board cap:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/capabilities/pcb-assembly-capabilities\n\nTreat pricing as volatile informational material, not acceptance-contract truth.\n\nThe export-selected service and the board’s fabrication rule profile must agree. A board compiled against OSH Park or a generic floor must not silently become “JLC-ready”; explicitly compile/preflight against the JLC profile or refuse the mismatch.\n\n### 6. Define honest readiness states\n\nAn offline export cannot prove live stock, website parser acceptance, component selection, feeder orientation, price, or successful order configuration.\n\nUse explicit outcomes:\n\n1. `package_generated`: submitted files are internally consistent.\n2. `preflight_status: pass | advisories | blocked`: selected-service compatibility and known checks.\n3. `order_page_verified`: human uploaded artifacts and recorded the quote-page result.\n\nKeep mid-layout quote exports, but label them honestly. Do not return “orderable” merely because CSV serialization succeeded.\n\nA selected-service incompatibility (e.g. bottom-side population under Economic) is a blocker, not a soft DFM advisory. Courtyard spacing and softer published recommendations can remain advisory per the owner ruling.\n\n### 7. Add missing BOM/CPL hard gates\n\nJLC’s current preparation guidance requires:\n\n- BOM and CPL emitted reference sets exactly equal after expansion\n- case consistent between both files\n- each designator appears exactly once\n- no grouped BOM row contains more than 200 designators\n- both sides in one CPL\n- metric coordinates\n- minimum 0.2 mm between different placement designators\n\nSource:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/advice-for-bom-and-cpl-files-preparation\n\nAlso gate/refuse:\n\n- duplicate refs, including case-fold collisions such as `R1` / `r1`\n- invalid/non-finite placement transforms\n- missing populated-part identity/package/comment required by the profile\n- expanded placement missing an explicit stable ref\n- multiple emitted values/packages/portable identities collapsed under one house part number\n- assembly fields lost by promote/deserialize round trip\n\nGroup by the complete emitted identity, not part number alone. Split groups at 200 refs and, for JLC, consider splitting by standard ref-prefix family to avoid its “multiple component types in one row” warning.\n\n### 8. Pin profiles to current official template artifacts\n\nThe current emitter uses `LCSC Part #`. I downloaded the current official JLC sample workbook during review; its header is now `JLCPCB Part #（optional）`, while another current JLC guide says `JLCPCB / LCSC Part #`. That inconsistency is why “headers exactly” cannot be an unversioned eternal assertion.\n\nStore the official sample URL, fetched date and SHA-256 in the profile. Normalize/parse the workbook into a semantic fixture; do not claim byte equality between an emitted CSV and an XLSX template.\n\nBOM:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/bill-of-materials-for-pcb-assembly\n\nCPL:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/pick-place-file-for-pcb-assembly\n\nThe true acceptance oracle remains a live quote-page upload.\n\n### 9. Placement origin and orientation need stronger modeling\n\nJLC asks for component centroids and CCW-positive rotation. The current path exports the YAML origin plus the verbatim KiCad-equivalent angle. That can work for library-compliant SMD footprints, whose anchor is generally the body centroid, but it is not universal: KiCad’s convention places THT origins at pin 1. The local board YAML documentation currently describes all footprint origins as pin 1, which is too broad.\n\nRequire:\n\n- a resolved, explicit assembly anchor for every physical placement\n- measured transform composition for expansion offset × parent rotation × side\n- fixtures for top, bottom, asymmetric, rotated, and expanded placements\n- a local assembly preview showing body outline, emitted ref, claimed centroid, rotation, pin 1/polarity\n- visible polarity/pin-1 silk checks for orientation-sensitive parts\n- mandatory JLC placement-preview review\n\nJLC acknowledges that house-library zero orientation can differ and tells users to correct the CPL or rely on engineering review plus PCB polarity markings:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/pcb-assembly-faqs-part-2\n\nNo rotation-correction database is required for this DCR; the preview/HITL boundary is the correct catch mechanism.\n\n### 10. Package layout\n\nRecommended deterministic output:\n\n```text\nsmart-remote-v2-jlcpcb-economic/\n  gerbers.zip\n  bom.csv\n  cpl.csv\n  order-manifest.json\n  preflight.json\n  assembly-preview.svg\n  ORDER-CHECKLIST.md\n```\n\n`gerbers.zip` should contain only Gerber, drill and job files. Keep manifest/checklist outside; JLC says non-Gerber statements inside the fabrication archive are ignored:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/instructions-for-ordering\n\nThe order manifest should include:\n\n- source digest algorithm/projection and git revision/dirty state\n- SHA-256 for every output\n- footprint-lock identities and hashes\n- exporter/profile/rule-data versions\n- logical component → emitted physical placement mapping\n- DNP/furniture list, reason, and paste policy\n- coordinate/rotation transforms used\n- selected fabrication and PCBA service settings\n- checked, failed, advisory, and explicitly unchecked rules\n- package-generation timestamp\n- known IP/license questions surfaced for owner review\n\nThere are also order-form choices Gerbers do not encode: quantity, material/thickness, copper weight, finish, solder-mask color, PCBA tier/side, panelization/delivery, “confirm production file,” serial/barcode choice, and consent around house-added tooling. Either capture these in an explicit export-time `order_intent` snapshot or narrow the goal from “everything the house needs” to “all upload artifacts plus an order checklist.”\n\n### 11. Fiducial/tooling-hole nuance\n\nJLC’s capabilities table says customer-supplied rails/fiducials are unnecessary for Economic PCBA, but its current after-sales guidance also says it may add two or three tooling holes to Economic boards. Do not reduce this to “fiducials not required.”\n\nSurface an advisory and require explicit checklist confirmation — ideally with “confirm production file” selected — because unintended tooling holes are a physical board modification:\nhttps://jlcpcb.com/help/article/common-pcba-after-sales-issues-and-faq\n\n### 12. Recommended implementation sequence\n\n1. Define and round-trip the structured assembly schema, including physical placements and paste decisions.\n2. Extend the resolved IR so every order artifact consumes one strict compilation.\n3. Introduce versioned service profiles and the order-intent/manifest contract.\n4. Implement BOM/CPL reference-set, identity, grouping, and format gates.\n5. Add local assembly preview plus structured `pass|advisories|blocked` preflight.\n6. Build deterministic package assembly and file checksums.\n7. Run smart-remote-v2 through a real JLC quote upload.\n8. Record parsed layers/dimensions/drills, BOM/CPL recognition, selected parts, tooling-hole behavior, and placement preview as final HITL evidence.\n9. Keep first physical-board inspection as follow-up bench evidence; quote-page acceptance proves uploader compatibility, not assembly yield.\n\n### Suggested acceptance-oracle ladder\n\n- Schema round-trip: Go promote/deserialize/load preserves all assembly fields and stable physical refs.\n- Unit: normalized BOM/CPL semantic goldens from pinned JLC samples.\n- Consistency: BOM refs == CPL refs; unique/case-safe refs; max 200 refs/row.\n- Transform: hand-derived asymmetric top/bottom/rotated/expanded placement fixtures.\n- Paste: populated SMD, DNP-paste-include, DNP-paste-exclude, and THT-no-paste.\n- CAM: all Gerber/drill layers independently parse; zip content allowlist exact.\n- End-to-end: smart-remote package emitted from one resolved board with checksums and preview.\n- HITL: JLC quote page recognizes Gerber zip/BOM/CPL, all intended parts are explicitly selected, preview orientation/polarity is accepted, and any tooling-hole/production-file modifications are reviewed.\n\n**Bottom line:** keep the DCR’s product idea and owner decisions, but make the implementation an order-release pipeline over one resolved physical board. That is the difference between “we generated familiar-looking files” and “we have an auditable package a human can safely submit for real assembled boards.”","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:12:51"}
{"_type":"comment","id":200,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Claude","text":"DCR revised to r2 folding Codex comment 199. Disposition: §1 single-compilation, §2 schema+migration+authored refs, §3 paste policy, §4 digest projection/rev/order-number removal, §6 readiness states, §7 hard gates, §8 template pinning + semantic goldens, §9 anchors/transforms/preview — all ACCEPTED as must-fix. §5 profile unification TRIMMED to minimal (references existing fab-rule profile + dialect + template pin). §10 TRIMMED: no order_intent; goal narrowed to upload artifacts + ORDER-CHECKLIST.md. §11 tooling holes = advisory + checklist confirmation. §12 sequence adopted as two task-cycles (Cycle A: resolved board/schema/gates; Cycle B: profile/package/surfaces). Plan + task children follow. Code work awaits owner approval.","created_at":"2026-08-30T20:28:35"}
{"_type":"comment","id":201,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Claude","text":"Evidence that the r2 premise (§ single compilation) was correct, measured during A2a:\n\npcb/worker/tests/fixtures/assembly_boards/assembly_fixture.yaml — the board the shipped BOM/CPL path used as its own fixture — HAS NEVER BEEN COMPILABLE. Measured failures: pin_pad_desync on all four resistor pins (declared +/-1.0 vs library +/-0.95, tolerance 0.01), footprint_unresolved on D_SOD-123, and two invalid_design_rule for absent via_diameter_mm / via_drill_mm.\n\nThe retired raw-dict path emitted clean, confident CSVs for that board for the whole of epoch C. So the \"two boards can be described in one order\" defect was not theoretical: the exporter's own fixture was a board the fab pipeline would have refused, and nothing in the BOM/CPL surface said so.\n\nAfter A2a the same board refuses by name (assembly_not_compilable, naming R1.1/R1.2/R2.1/R2.2, D1 and the board-level rule gaps) and emits no files. Two further live defects closed for free by the cutover: duplicate designators used to emit two identical CPL rows, and a blank footprint used to group under the empty string and ship a BOM line naming no part.","created_at":"2026-08-30T21:37:31"}
{"_type":"comment","id":202,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Claude","text":"r3 AMENDMENTS (2026-08-30/31), made by the orchestrator under the owner's delegated grading authority. Two of these correct THIS DOCUMENT.\n\n1. THE SCHEMA SECTION'S WORKED EXAMPLE IS WRONG AND WAS TEACHING A TRAP. It shows U1S expanding with offsets {x:0,y:0} and {x:22.86,y:0}. Measured against the real socket-set footprint at (30,10): those offsets emit anchors (30, 40.8485) and (52.86, 40.8485), while the true row centres are (18.57, 36.67) and (41.43, 36.67). BOTH ARE WRONG, AND THE PAIR PASSES EVERY HARD GATE — the spacing gate is proximity-only, and 22.86 mm apart clears it no matter which point the two are measured from. No advisory fires either, because the anchors WERE measured; they were measured off the wrong body. The first thing that would have caught it is a human reading the B3 placement preview.\nCORRECTED FORM — each placement carries its own anchor:\n  placements:\n    - {ref: U1S_A, offset_mm: {x: -11.43, y: 0}, anchor_mm: {x: 0, y: 26.67}, rotation_deg: 0}\n    - {ref: U1S_B, offset_mm: {x:  11.43, y: 0}, anchor_mm: {x: 0, y: 26.67}, rotation_deg: 0}\nThe anchor is derivable from the purchased part (22 pins x 2.54 pitch / 2 - 1.27 = 26.67), not from the parent footprint's invisible fab box. B5 must author this form; copying the old example would have ordered a board with both socket rows centred between themselves.\n\n2. THE SCHEMA GAINS `anchor_mm` PER PLACEMENT (task B0b). Optional; absent means the parent-measured anchor still applies, unchanged. A placement that authors one is recorded with basis `authored` rather than a measurement token, so a number a person wrote down is never reported as a box measured off a drawing.\n\n3. THE HARD-GATE LIST NAMES BEHAVIOUR, NOT CODES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. \"Each designator exactly once, case-fold safe\" is the promise. Implementation raises THREE codes across two surfaces (duplicate_component_ref at compile for exact component-vs-component repeats; duplicate_assembly_designator at the assembly gate for case-fold collisions and for exact expansion-vs-component collisions; and a same-named Go Validate check that is not in front of the worker's board-path lane). RATIFIED HERE as the intended shape, which answers the cross-provider reviewer's objection on 01a054ed6fb5 — its substance was that a party had edited a published contract constraining itself. The contract is now amended by its owner. THE OWNER HAS NOT PERSONALLY CONFIRMED THIS ONE; it is the single decision in this batch that is about process rather than mechanism, and it is worth a sentence at HITL.\n\n4. IDENTITY VALUES MUST BE QUOTED STRINGS; A BLANK MEANS ABSENT (task B0a). An unquoted YAML package designator is parsed as a number before anything reads it (0603 -> 387 as YAML-1.1 octal) and is now a named refusal rather than a silent drop. There is deliberately no way to author an empty cell.\n\n5. NOT IN SCOPE gains: reinstating a furniture-versus-DNP distinction, and any authoring form that lets an unquoted numeric identity value through.\n\nOpen owner questions arising, all non-blocking: 01a054ed6fb5 (process confirmation of item 3), 01a054d5d3cc (the comment-rule wording in the task-cycle skill file, proposed not edited), 01a055510fc0 (a Go save renumbers unquoted identity values in the two pre-block authoring homes — being fixed as task B0d).","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:10:42"}
{"_type":"comment","id":203,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Claude","text":"CORRECTION to r3 amendment 3 (comment 202), measured in task B0c.\n\nI wrote that the duplicate-designator behaviour raises THREE codes across two surfaces. It raises FOUR:\n\n- `duplicate_component_ref` — compile_board.py:2930 — two components authoring the same ref, exact.\n- `invalid_component_assembly` — assembly_spec.py:247, recorded at compile_board.py:3120 — ONE component repeating a designator inside its own placements. Neither of the other two checks covers this pairing; I did not know it existed.\n- `duplicate_assembly_designator` — internal/board/assembly.go:450, reached only through board.Validate — a placement ref colliding exactly with another placement ref or another component's ref.\n- `assembly_duplicate_designator` — assembly_gates.py:215 — board-wide, case-folded, over every physical placement, AND an exact branch.\n\nThe lane claim is confirmed by finding board.Validate's only two call sites (internal/tools/project.go:175 and :327, serialize and deserialize). HandleExportAssembly forwards to the worker unchanged, so Go validation is not in front of the export lane and an exact expansion-ref collision does reach the assembly gate's exact branch.\n\nThe ratification in amendment 3 stands — behaviour is right, the hard-gate list names behaviour rather than codes on purpose. Only my count of the implementation was wrong.\n\nAlso filed from that work: 01a055630838 — the assembly gate's EXACT branch has no test. The test named for it (test_expansion_designator_colliding_with_another_component_refuses) builds a case-fold collision instead, so the branch this doc work just proved is production-reachable is untested.","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:25:52"}
{"_type":"comment","id":204,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Claude","text":"CORRECTION to a guiding question, measured in task B1 against the live pages (2026-08-30).\n\nGuiding question 5 of this DCR cites component-to-edge as a checkable house rule and offers \"SW2 sits ~2.5 mm from west edge\" as the motivating case. THE 2.5 mm FIGURE IS NOT PUBLISHED ANYWHERE JLC STATES IT. B1 scraped the pages rather than trusting a search summary: the process-edge page publishes NO component clearance at all — only panel rails (5 mm), tooling holes (2 mm) and fiducials (1 mm). The only component-to-edge figure JLC publishes is 0.3 mm, worded \"we suggest\", in assembly FAQs part 2 item 14, beside a ~0.2 mm depanel routing tolerance.\n\nSo the implemented advisory uses 0.3 mm, and the 2.5 mm number was not recorded anywhere. This is the DCR's own \"verify, don't copy\" instruction working as intended — the number I put in the guiding question came from a search summary and would have produced an advisory that fired on almost every board.\n\nOther verified facts from the same pass, now pinned in the service profile:\n- Economic tier: single-sided placement, 2/4/6 layers, 0.8-1.6 mm, 2-50 pcs — all four exactly as the DCR states.\n- Tooling holes: two to three, NPTH 1.152 mm, near the corners, added after upload.\n- HEADER DRIFT IS REAL AND NOW PINNED: the current sample workbook spells the catalogue column `JLCPCB Part #（optional）` with FULL-WIDTH parentheses. The emitted header stays `LCSC Part #`; the difference is recorded as an explained drift entry, and the loader refuses any positional difference that is not explained. Value-level drift too: the sample writes T/B and a mm suffix on coordinates where we write Top/Bottom and bare numbers.\n- Both template artifacts fetched successfully (HTTP 200) and are pinned by URL, fetch date and sha256. The workbooks are NOT vendored — only the parsed semantic goldens plus the digest of what was read — so no third-party binary enters the repo.\n\nOne implementation note worth recording at DCR level: `PROFILES[\"jlc\"]` was NOT reduced to an alias of `jlcpcb-economic`. Measured reason: the shipped emitter fixture populates a bottom-side part and compiles against a different fab profile, so aliasing would fire both new blockers on it and the only way to keep it green would be to move that part to the top — deleting the load-bearing \"bottom-side CPL X is unmirrored\" oracle. Instead one file carries two selectors: `jlcpcb-economic` selects the tier and runs its service checks, `jlc` reads the same file's dialect and claims no manufacturer, which is exactly this DCR's readiness position on mid-layout quote exports.","created_at":"2026-08-31T01:51:26"}
{"_type":"comment","id":211,"item_id":"01a0542d902f7683bddb6ac9f03be9fa","author":"Codex","text":"OWNER DECISION — follow-up approved 2026-08-31. The geometric order-release contract is resolved as follows: `jlcpcb-economic` names a manufacturing service and therefore refuses geometric violations as `order_package_geometric_violations`; `jlc` claims only the shared CSV dialect and may generate a mid-layout quote/reference package with named geometric blockers, `package_generated: true`, and `preflight_status: blocked`. The checklist and UI must state that such a package is reference-only and must not be submitted for manufacture until the blockers clear. An indeterminate geometric check remains a named refusal for every selector.","created_at":"2026-08-31T15:50:13"}
{"_type":"comment","id":213,"item_id":"01a05460e46e7eb0a974298ef6387e25","author":"Claude","text":"CORRECTION to this item's B5 expectations, measured 2026-08-31.\n\nThis cycle item told B5 to \"expect the licence advisory on the checklist (01a05593ea5e) and expect preflight to read `advisories` rather than `pass` because of it\". THE LICENCE HALF IS WRONG. All 39 entries in library/footprints.lock.json declare a licence; a real order_package build reports ip_questions: 0. The advisory does not fire. 01a05593ea5e is closed as not-a-defect.\n\nWHAT B5 SHOULD ACTUALLY EXPECT on the jlcpcb-economic profile, measured on a clean fixture:\n- assembly_house_tooling_holes — unconditional on this tier; JLC adds tooling holes. It is a statement about the house, not about the board, and it alone keeps preflight off `pass`.\n- assembly_provenance_absent — no design revision stamped in board_graphics. B5 AUTHORS THIS, so it should clear once smart-remote-v2 carries its revision text.\n- gc9_silk_width — silk at 0.12 mm against the profile's 0.15 mm floor. Real, and a board decision.\n\nAlso note the geometric release gate that landed after this item was written (a4ef4e1): a board with a rule-floor violation now REFUSES outright on jlcpcb-economic, while the dialect-only `jlc` profile emits with preflight `blocked` and package_generated true. Verified both. So if smart-remote-v2 has any geometric violation, the economic-profile export will refuse rather than produce a package — worth running the geometric check before the HITL rather than discovering it at export time.","created_at":"2026-08-31T16:12:03"}
{"_type":"comment","id":205,"item_id":"01a054cc00dd7713adddf367900411d9","author":"Claude","text":"CLOSED BY B2 — but the item's stated cause was already false when it was filed, and the real one was bigger.\n\nMEASURED at b05f950:\n* `assembly_package` (cycle A) is ONE worker method that compiles once and walks once, and `HandleExportAssembly` calls it exactly once (internal/tools/worker_tools.go:853, with the \"ONE worker call, deliberately\" comment above it). A caller wanting BOTH CSVs therefore already compiled once, not twice. `assembly_bom` and `assembly_cpl` each compile once for their own single file, which is correct.\n* What DID cost two compilations was the pair that actually makes an order: `gerbers` and `assembly_package` are separate RPCs over one board, so a full order package compiled twice — and those two compilations are the ones the DCR's \"one strict compilation\" goal is about, because they are the pair that could describe two different boards.\n\nB2's `order_package` method compiles ONCE and derives all six artifacts from that one ResolvedBoard: the gerber archive, both CSVs, the checklist, the preflight report and the manifest's placement map. So the cost this item names is gone, at the level that matters.\n\n`assembly_bom` / `assembly_cpl` / `assembly_package` are unchanged and still compile per call. That is not a defect: each is a single-artifact request, and caching a compilation across RPCs would be the \"caching layer now\" this item already ruled out.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:36"}
{"_type":"comment","id":206,"item_id":"01a054ea6c0e7ebbb4ee916d4b067e08","author":"Claude","text":"FIXED in B2, both halves of the contract.\n\n* `methods._write_assembly_files` (the direct worker path the bug names) now goes through `order_write.write_files`: every artifact is written to a temporary neighbour before ANY destination name is touched, then published by rename. A caller error or a full disk leaves the directory holding neither CSV. The only remaining window is a failure inside the rename loop after every byte is already on disk, and that path unwinds names that did not exist before.\n* The package itself is stronger: `order_write.publish_directory` stages the whole `<board>-<profile>/` directory and moves it into place with a single rename, so a reader of the destination sees the complete package or no package. An existing package directory is NOT replaced without an explicit overwrite (it may already have been uploaded); on overwrite the old directory is moved aside and removed only once the new one is in place, and a failure in between restores it.\n\nSTILL OPEN from this item: `CplRow.mpn` is carried on the row and never rendered by the CPL renderer. Not touched — it is dead column data, not a write-atomicity defect.\n\nALSO NOTED, not changed: `HandleExportAssembly` strips `out_dir` and does its own Go-side write specifically because the worker path was not all-or-nothing. That reason is gone now. Harmless as-is (the Go write is itself all-or-nothing for the two CSVs) but it is now a second writer for the same contract; B4 owns that surface.","created_at":"2026-08-31T02:08:43"}
{"_type":"comment","id":207,"item_id":"01a055fa567d72bb920e6edfe1c2cf02","author":"agent","text":"Scoped-run fix station (task-cycle 12): took candidate (a) — the TEST now names the bare ref \"TH_TestPoint\" — and left this item OPEN, because (b) reaches well beyond this one test.\n\nThe finding that decides it: the bare keys are not one stray entry. pcb/library/footprints.lock.json has exactly THREE bare keys out of 39 — C_0805, R_0805, TH_TestPoint — and all three are the \"seed\" layer (layer: \"seed\", paths Capacitor_SMD.pretty/C_0805_SeedSynthetic.kicad_mod, Resistor_SMD.pretty/R_0805_SeedSynthetic.kicad_mod, TestPoint.pretty/TH_TestPoint.kicad_mod). Every other entry, seed or not, is keyed Lib:Name.\n\nRe-keying them to Lib:Name is a board-source-visible rename, not a lockfile edit:\n  - ~15 Python suites author boards with the bare refs (test_route_drc, test_route_ir, test_drc_geometric, test_gerbers, test_ir_fab, test_draft_check, test_candidate_overlay, test_via_first_class_dcr, test_assembly_advisory, test_route_scope, test_route_bridge_scope, test_manufacturer_profile, test_gc9_silk_dfm, test_cam_conformance, ...)\n  - pcb/spikes/gerber/board.yaml (the fixture main_test.go's stdio sweep dispatches every tool against) uses R_0805 / C_0805 / TH_TestPoint\n  - pcb/main_test.go:707 reasons about those exact names\n  - the resolver has a documented bare-name scan path (test_methods.py:240) that exists for them\n\nSo the real question this item should decide is whether the seed layer keeps a bare-name namespace at all, and if not, what the migration does to boards already authored against it. That is a DCR-shaped call, not a test fix. Leaving open at severity 2 with that scope stated.\n\nAlso worth noting for whoever takes it: the resolver does NOT accept \"TestPoint:TH_TestPoint\" as an alias for the bare-keyed entry even though the entry's own path is TestPoint.pretty/TH_TestPoint.kicad_mod. Whichever way the namespace decision goes, accepting the qualified form for a bare-keyed seed would have made this test pass as written.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:38:55"}
{"_type":"comment","id":208,"item_id":"01a055fa7beb7057a441f84ff8d177a5","author":"agent","text":"Fixed in the scoped-run fix commit (task-cycle 12). TEST was wrong on the case — the capital in \"NO manufacturing tier\" is the note's deliberate emphasis, matching the same shouted-word idiom the rest of these modules use, so the substring check was the sloppy side.\n\nReducing the transcriptions WAS cheap for two of the three, and both are done:\n  - new order_package.NO_SERVICE_NOTE holds the one sentence\n  - _profile_record's manifest[\"profile\"][\"service_note\"] is now that constant\n  - render_checklist's line is now that constant (it had a third grammar: \"No manufacturing tier is claimed by this export: ...\")\n  - the test asserts equality against the constant rather than a hand-retyped substring\n\nThe THIRD transcription is left alone deliberately: assembly_preview._header_lines uses \"no manufacturing tier claimed\" as a compact label inside a one-line header (\"<display name> (<id>) · <service>\"), where the other half of the ternary is \"<id> · fab rules <x> · places <sides>\". It is a label in a different register, not the sentence, and importing an order_package constant into the preview renderer to share it would couple the two modules for a phrase that does not want to be a sentence there. Noted so the next reader knows it is a choice, not a miss.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:39:06"}
{"_type":"comment","id":209,"item_id":"01a0561d85057962a9a775563433d4ef","author":"agent","text":"Fixed in the batched round on main. Judged test-wrong: the tool's yaml-only refusal over the mpn-less spike board is correct behaviour, so the generic sweep was asserting the wrong thing — the export_assembly precedent (carve-out AND dedicated coverage) is right and was followed, not just the cheap half. minerva_pcb_order_package now has (1) a carve-out in TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_DeclaredSchemaArgsOnly citing this item, and (2) TestPCBWorkerStdioSmoke_OrderPackage: happy path over assembly_resolved.yaml with all seven package files read back off disk, plus the identity refusal (names R1/mpn, carries the blocked preflight readiness, leaves zero artifacts in out_dir). The rejected alternative (adding mpn to the spike board) stays rejected — it would mutate a fixture ~20 suites read.","created_at":"2026-08-31T04:56:48"}
{"_type":"comment","id":210,"item_id":"01a0585ff81e7f45a4884021a5d9f49e","author":"Opus (reviewer)","text":"OPUS REVIEW — NOT APPROVE, on one point that is the owner's to decide, not mine or the implementer's. Everything else is sound, and two of the three specific worries in the request measured out clean.\n\n=== BLOCKER (owner decision, not a code defect) ===\n\nTHE GEOMETRIC RELEASE GATE CONTRADICTS AN OWNER-APPROVED RUBRIC VERDICT, AND IT FIRES UNCONDITIONALLY.\n\nDCR 01a0542d902f's rubric section says, as an owner-approved verdict: \"Export gates: hard refusal on facts that make files WRONG; guideline findings advisory; NO PROMOTE-GRADE GATE (mid-layout quote exports legitimate, labeled honestly).\" A geometric-violation refusal is a promote-grade gate by definition.\n\nMeasured, not inferred — the gate is unconditional in build(), before any profile branching:\n  jlc                -> REFUSED order_package_geometric_violations\n  jlcpcb-economic    -> REFUSED order_package_geometric_violations\non a board whose only fault is a 0.05 mm trace against a 0.10 mm floor.\n\nThe `jlc` result is the sharp end. B1 deliberately kept `jlc` as the selector that claims NO manufacturer, and recorded the reason: it is \"exactly this DCR's readiness position on mid-layout quote exports\". That selector now refuses too, so the escape hatch the DCR reserved for mid-layout work no longer exists on any path.\n\nThe diff's own test docstring states the change plainly — \"Before the release gate existed this exact board still produced all seven order artifacts\" — so this is deliberate and disclosed, not an accident.\n\nI am not saying the gate is wrong. Shipping an order package for a board the selected fab cannot make is a real failure mode, and the DCR's own goal sentence says \"refusing by name when the board is not orderable\". The DCR contains genuine tension between that sentence and the rubric verdict. But this is the second time this cycle an implementer has resolved a contract tension in its own favour, and the first time (the duplicate-designator codes) the owner ruled explicitly that the contract must be amended by its owner. Same principle applies.\n\nRESOLUTION, cheapest first: keep the gate for a profile that names a manufacturer (jlcpcb-economic), and let the no-manufacturer dialect (jlc) emit with the violations as a NAMED BLOCKING-GRADE ADVISORY plus preflight status `blocked` — package_generated true, honestly labelled, which is what \"legitimate, labeled honestly\" asks for. If the owner prefers the gate everywhere, amend the DCR rubric verdict to say so and I withdraw this.\n\n=== SHOULD-FIX ===\n\n1. REPORT SALIENCE SORTS ON PROSE. `_finding_salience` scores by substring — \" on copper \", \" on drill \", \" on mask \", \"not fabricated\", \"documentation-only\", \" on fab \". Display ordering is now coupled to message wording, and this cycle reworded findings repeatedly (the compile_warnings rename, the archive-claim text, the no-tier note). A reword silently sinks a copper warning back under documentation noise. The findings already carry structured fields — geometric rows have `layer`, others have `code` and `scope`. Sort on those.\n\n2. THE SORT IS NOT STABLE, THOUGH THE COMMENT SAYS IT IS. Godot's Array.sort_custom is introsort and explicitly documented as not stable, so equal-salience findings may order differently between runs or platforms while the code claims a stable sort. Add an index tiebreak, or drop the word.\n\n3. THE SMART-REMOTE BASELINE TEST'S ORACLE IS A FILE IN ANOTHER REPO THAT THE OWNER EDITS. Loosening it to accept either representation is the right call for the migration — pinning an endpoint was the original error, and the durable properties it now pins (artwork survives, round-trip byte-identical) are the ones that matter. But it will keep breaking, because ~/gitlab/ccsandbox changes under it and is frequently dirty. Vendor a frozen copy as a fixture for the round-trip contract, and keep the live-board check separate and explicitly advisory. Note the specificity actually lost: it can no longer catch artwork moving between representations unintentionally.\n\n=== NOTES ===\n\n4. `_job_function` is coupled to the emitter's CURRENT vocabulary and I verified the coupling holds today: gerber.py emits `Copper,L{n},{Top|Bot|Inr},Signal` for every layer including inner (:1312-1318) while the job table carries the same minus `,Signal` (:1540-1576), so Top, Bottom AND inner layers normalize correctly — the request's multi-layer worry is unfounded. If the emitter ever gains `,Plane` or `,Mixed`, this refuses a valid board. That is fail-closed and therefore acceptable, but add a test that pins the coupling so the eventual failure is legible rather than mysterious.\n\n5. The full geometric union is embedded in BOTH preflight.json and order-manifest.json. Duplication is defensible (the manifest is the auditable record, preflight is the report) but it doubles the payload on a board with many rows, and JSON-safety now depends on nothing in that union ever being NaN or inf — json.dumps emits bare NaN, which is not valid JSON. Worth a guard or a stated invariant.\n\n=== VERIFIED SOUND (the request's own questions) ===\n\n- ONE COMPILATION HOLDS. run_geometric_drc receives the same ResolvedBoard build() was handed; no second compile_board call anywhere in the path. The comment explaining why is accurate.\n- DRILL ROLE BINDING IS CORRECT, including the trap I went looking for: \"-NPTH.drl\" does NOT satisfy endswith(\"-PTH.drl\") (the character before PTH is N, not the required hyphen), so the two branches cannot alias. Each drill file is emitted only when the board has those holes, so a PTH-only board is fine.\n- ARCHIVE SEMANTICS ARE FAIL-CLOSED AND THE TESTS FAIL FOR THE RIGHT REASON. The swap test asserts CODE_ARCHIVE_SEMANTICS with the field naming one of the two swapped members and \"layer-swapped\" in the message; the impostor test asserts the missing X2 identity specifically. Both are counterexamples the syntax oracle genuinely cannot see.\n- REFUSAL PAYLOADS ARE COMPLETE. The geometric union rides both the error and error[\"preflight\"], readiness reports package_generated false, and the violations test asserts no file reached disk.\n- THE INDETERMINATE PATH IS REALLY FAIL-CLOSED — anything other than clean or violations refuses, proven by monkeypatching a fabricated indeterminate union rather than by reasoning.\n- Duplicate ZIP names are now counted before map insertion, and long labels reserve the suffix budget before truncating the reference, so rotation and side survive. Both reverse-round defects are genuinely closed.","status":"accepted","created_at":"2026-08-31T15:17:27","resolved_at":"2026-08-31T15:50:15","resolved_by":"Codex"}
{"_type":"comment","id":212,"item_id":"01a0585ff81e7f45a4884021a5d9f49e","author":"Codex","text":"Owner approved the follow-up and it is implemented. The service/dialect gate now follows the recorded owner decision; report salience uses producer-authored structured `impact` plus an original-index tiebreak; frozen fixtures cover both artwork representations and the mutable smart-remote board is opt-in; a real four-layer archive pins the current inner-copper X2/job vocabulary; strict JSON rejects NaN/Infinity by name. Verification: 3160 Python passed (4 skipped, 3 xfailed), complete pcb/shared Go suites passed, go vet passed, Godot export parity 91/0, and the opt-in live smart-remote round trip passed.","created_at":"2026-08-31T15:50:14","parent_id":210}
{"_type":"comment","id":215,"item_id":"01a05dd52d11735888097f14c5c3aa06","author":"agent","text":"PREMISE CORRECTION - THIS IS A SECONDARY CHECK, NOT THE ORIENTATION DETECTOR.\n\nThis DCR was written partly on the argument that \"every orientation defect on rev B was caught in\nJLCPCB's 3D preview, and nowhere else\". That was true historically and is no longer sufficient\ngrounds, so do not build this expecting orientation to be covered by it.\n\nA render can only reveal an orientation error when the part's pad field is ROTATIONALLY\nASYMMETRIC. Measured on rev B:\n\n  VISIBLE in a render   J2, J3 (pads one side, mounting tabs the other), Q1/Q2 (2 leads one side,\n                        1 the other), U4 (rectangular, rows vs columns)\n  INVISIBLE in ANY render, however good:\n    U2  VQFN-16 3x3 - square, 4-fold symmetric pads. A 90 deg error seats perfectly while every\n        pin function rotates four positions.\n    U3  SPK0641HT4H-1 - 180-rotationally symmetric pad field, every lead still lands on a pad,\n        AND the acoustic port sits opposite pin 1 in both library drawings so it moves with the\n        error. There is no visual difference at all.\n\nSo \"it looked right in the preview\" was never evidence of correctness - it only ever meant the\npart's shape happened to make the error visible. The actual detector is the arithmetic in DCR\n01a05e0f0c81 (differential footprint verification), which needs no render and covers symmetric\nparts.\n\nWHAT THIS DCR IS STILL WORTH, unchanged:\n  - it runs BEFORE a vendor is chosen and before an order exists;\n  - it is scriptable, so a cross-section is a command rather than a squint - reading JLC's raster\n    render directly caused three connectors to be misread this session before the arithmetic\n    corrected them;\n  - it gives mechanical/enclosure work something to check fit against - the U3 acoustic port\n    offset (0.97mm, smart-remote hint 01a05dd84762) came out of exactly this technique;\n  - it is vendor-neutral.\n\nONE CAPABILITY WORTH ADDING TO THE SCOPE, discovered while checking U4: the render DOES draw\npackage pin-1 markings (the owner spotted a dot on the part and an arrow on our silk). So the\nexport should carry package pin-1 markings and our silk pin-1 markers into the model, because\ntheir alignment is a genuine visual check that works even on parts whose PADS are symmetric -\nit makes U2 checkable by eye. It does not rescue U3, whose only asymmetric feature moves with\nthe error.","created_at":"2026-09-01T17:41:39"}
{"_type":"comment","id":214,"item_id":"01a05de4bddd78f9be7271064c764452","author":"agent","text":"FULL-BOARD AUDIT + TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE PROPOSED FIX.\n\nAudited all 47 components of smart-remote-v2 rev B by matching our resolved pin coordinates\n(from the board YAML's per-component `pins`) against each LCSC part's own footprint, by pad\nnumber. Script: ~/.claude/jobs/a9a929fe/tmp/audit.py, API responses cached in eda_cache/.\n\n  offset   parts\n  0 deg    C1-C16, R1-R5, F1, JP1-JP6, L1, J4, J5      (all 2-terminal + the two OK connectors)\n  180 deg  J2, J3, U3, Q1, Q2\n  270 deg  U2, U4\n  unknown  SW1-SW4 (fewer than 2 pad numbers in common with C4365033 - our EVP-ASAC1A footprint\n           names its pads differently; needs a per-part look, do not assume 0)\n  n/a      TP1-TP4 (DNP, no LCSC), U1S (synthetic, see test 01a05dd7a675)\n\n*** SO IT IS NOT A GLOBAL OFFSET. It is per-part, and it is non-zero on FIVE POLARISED PARTS. ***\n  Q1, Q2  SOT-23 P-channel MOSFET C15127 - ours has pins 1,2 on the LEFT, LCSC on the RIGHT.\n          (The 0.28mm residual is a land-size difference, 0.938 vs 1.150 pad centres, not an\n          orientation ambiguity - the 180 is exact in direction.)\n  U4      TSOT-23-6 C780769 - ours is two COLUMNS at x=+/-1.138, LCSC is two ROWS at y=+/-1.200.\n  U2      VQFN-16 C910544 - ours pad1 (-1.462,-0.750) on the left edge, LCSC pad1 (-0.748,+1.500)\n          on the bottom edge.\n  U3      SPK0641HT4H-1 C5159510 - 180, and our footprint is the datasheet-correct one.\n\nVISIBILITY SPLIT - this is what decides how much trouble we are in:\n  WOULD BE VISIBLE in a 3D preview if the offset propagates to placement:\n    J2, J3 (asymmetric pad rows - CONFIRMED WRONG by the owner), Q1/Q2 (2 leads one side, 1 the\n    other), U4 (rectangular body, rows vs columns).\n  INVISIBLE even in a correct 3D preview:\n    U3  - pad field is 180-rotationally symmetric, every lead still lands on a pad, and the\n          acoustic port is opposite pin 1 in BOTH drawings so it moves with the rotation.\n          Failure mode: DATA<->SELECT, Vdd<->CLOCK.\n    U2  - VQFN-16 3x3 is square and 4-fold symmetric, so a 90 error still seats perfectly while\n          every pin function rotates by four positions.\n\nDECISIVE OPEN TEST: look at Q1, Q2 and U4 in the JLCPCB 3D preview. They are measured non-zero\nAND geometrically asymmetric, so:\n  - if they render with leads off their pads, the offset propagates for everything, and U2 and U3\n    are silently wrong too;\n  - if they render correctly, then something (JLC's own part database, most likely) is already\n    correcting some parts, and we must understand WHICH before applying any offset ourselves -\n    blindly adding 180 would then BREAK the parts that are currently right.\nDo not apply corrections to rev B until that test is done.\n\nCORRECTION 1 TO THE FIX: THE CORRECTION IS PER-PART, NOT PER-FOOTPRINT.\nThis is a well-known problem in the KiCad -> JLCPCB ecosystem, and the established mitigation is a\nrotation-correction database (kicad-jlcpcb-tools ships a community-maintained one; KiBot has a\nrot_footprint filter driven by \"JLCPCB Rotation Offset\"/\"JLCRotOffset\" fields). The community\nexperience is explicit that TWO COMPONENTS WITH THE SAME FOOTPRINT CAN NEED DIFFERENT ROTATIONS\nIN THE SAME PROJECT. So putting orientation_convention only on the footprints.lock.json entry, as\nthe original description proposed, is NOT sufficient - it must key on the LCSC part number (or on\nfootprint+part), because the fact belongs to the part-in-that-footprint, not to the footprint.\n\nCORRECTION 2: PREFER MEASURING OVER IMPORTING A DATABASE.\nThe community databases are keyed by footprint name and are admittedly \"just a list of common\ncases\". We can do better and get a per-part answer for free, because we already carry the LCSC\nnumber: measure the offset at bless time by pad-number matching against the LCSC footprint, as in\naudit.py. Import a community DB only as a cross-check, never as the source of truth - same\nprinciple as the standing rule to build footprints from datasheets rather than from anyone else's\npre-made library.\n\nREFERENCES\n  https://forum.kicad.info/t/component-rotation/44969\n  https://pd.takazudomodular.com/docs/how-to/kicad-jlcpcb-tools/\n  https://kibot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/notes_position.html\n  https://kibot.readthedocs.io/en/v1.9.0/configuration/filters/rot_footprint.html","created_at":"2026-09-01T16:59:51"}
{"_type":"comment","id":217,"item_id":"01a05e1adb637a4e8bd1ab9e03895e28","author":"agent","text":"CYCLE STATUS 2026-09-01 — ALL FIVE TASKS LANDED; STOPPED AT THE OWNER ESCALATION GATE, BEFORE\nSCOPED EXECUTION. Nothing pushed.\n\n  DISPATCH BASE  8dcb621\n  HEAD           b89ce63\n  T1 [x] dfcf738  measurement primitive\n  T2 [x] 6883757  the ledger, keyed on the part\n  T3 [x] 1ca83e3  apply at export + refuse on unknown\n  T4 [x] 89f5c66  coverage visible, declarations sourced\n  T5 [x] 27d97a9  synthetic-expansion child rotation (no production change needed)\n  R1 [x] c709782  cold-review fixes\n  R2 [x] b89ce63  cross-provider (Codex) fixes\n\nDEVIATION FROM THE SKILL, approved by the owner before dispatch: Fable was unavailable, so the\ncold adversary was a fresh Opus agent with no session context, and Codex ran BEFORE scoped\nexecution rather than after. Recording it so the ordering is not read as an error later.\n\nWHAT THE TWO REVIEW LAYERS FOUND, and why keeping both was worth it — their findings were almost\ndisjoint.\n  COLD REVIEW found structural defects: (a) THE BATCH WAS RED AS COMMITTED — the test corpus\n  re-declared two refs the shipped ledger already declared, and the duplicate key refused inside an\n  autouse fixture in seven suites (88 passed/233 errors, 316 after the fix); (b) THE BOTTOM-SIDE\n  SIGN WAS INVERTED — a planar mirror conjugates a rotation into its inverse, so a footprint-local\n  offset must SUBTRACT on the bottom, which is the rule this repo already applies to a child\n  placement's rotation. The test that should have caught it asserted the code's own expression and\n  had no falsifying power.\n  CODEX found fail-closed leaks — places that compute the right answer and then fail to act on it:\n  a geometry_mismatch kept its offset and was applied as trusted; the generator trusted the index\n  key over the payload's own catalogue number; the ledger accepted self-inconsistent rows; an\n  ambiguous measurement still returned a number; coverage overstated what it covers.\n  Neither layer would have found the other's. Do not collapse to one.\n\n*** TWO THINGS ARE OPEN AND BOTH NEED THE OWNER. This is the second Codex must_fix, which the\n    skill routes to the owner rather than to another fix round. ***\n\nOPEN 1 — THE mpn-ONLY BYPASS (Codex finding C, item 01a05e56ba6f).\nA populated placement identified only by assembly.mpn, with no house_part for the selected house,\nis NEITHER corrected NOR refused. Codex's judgement on the re-check: deferring is NOT defensible,\nbecause the 33 suite failures produced by implementing the refusal are evidence that corpus boards\nCURRENTLY RELY on the bypass — present unsoundness, not missing future coverage. The counter-case\nis that closing it needs vendor payloads for ~5 catalogue parts committed first, and then a board\nschema migration: every real board would need house_parts plus a measured pair or it stops. That\nis a breaking change and a sequencing decision, which is why it is the owner's.\nUNTIL IT LANDS: the gate is fail-closed for parts bought with a house catalogue number, and open\nfor parts carrying only an mpn. Do not describe it as fail-closed without that qualifier.\n\nOPEN 2 — A REGRESSION THE FOLD INTRODUCED (Codex re-check, new finding).\nFixes D and F interact. F now gives an undecided measurement no offset; D's consistency table\nrequires every geometry_mismatch to be (angle_decided=True, offset present, lands_agree=False). So\na legitimate UNDECIDED geometry_mismatch can no longer become a ledger record — the loader would\nrefuse it. It fails closed rather than shipping anything wrong, and no such row exists today, but\nit makes a valid state unrepresentable. The new verdict-coverage test only exercises a DECIDED\nmismatch, so it does not see the gap. Small and clearly resolvable; not yet fixed.\n\nPUSH IS BLOCKED, and not by this cycle: three release gates are red and all three PREDATE the\ndispatch base — bug 01a05eaf4ed6. test_notice.py (stale NOTICE.md), plus two lockfile census tests,\nall caused by the three rev B seed footprints entering the lock in d7ffc54 without their dependent\nartifacts updated. Full suite: 3 failed/3261 passed BEFORE this cycle, 3 failed/3270 passed after —\nsame three failures, no regressions, 9 net new tests.\n\nSTILL TO DO WHEN WORK RESUMES: scoped execution (deliberately not yet run), then push. The full\nworker suite has in fact been run twice during the review rounds, which is stronger evidence than\nthe scoped set would have been — but the scoped set should still be NAMED and recorded, because\nthe cycle's discipline is that the set is justified in advance, not that a big run happened.","created_at":"2026-09-01T20:37:44"}
{"_type":"comment","id":216,"item_id":"01a05e56ba6f769a859fa7093a8971e1","author":"agent","text":"PROMOTED IN SEVERITY BY EVIDENCE — this is now the one open hole in the orientation gate, it has\nbeen independently rediscovered three times, and the cost of closing it has been measured.\n\nTHE FRAMING THAT RESOLVES THE ORIGINAL HESITATION.\nThis item was filed with the reasoning that joining a manufacturer number (`assembly.mpn`) to a\nhouse catalogue number would invent exactly the loose key the ledger refuses to have. That\nreasoning is correct AND it does not justify the current behaviour, because it is about JOINING.\nThe cross-provider reviewer put it precisely: a populated placement with no `house_part` for the\nselected house has an UNJOINABLE, therefore UNKNOWN, orientation — and it currently emits\nuncorrected. Refusing requires no join at all. \"We cannot identify this part at the house we are\nordering from\" is a complete reason to stop, and stopping is what the rest of this cycle does for\nevery other unknown.\n\nTHREE INDEPENDENT SIGHTINGS, which is why I am not treating it as a nit:\n  1. Filed here during the apply/gate work, as a stated gap with a test pinning the bypass.\n  2. Rediscovered from the opposite direction during the batch-review fix round: the test corpus's\n     orientation fixture turns out to be a NO-OP in all eight suites that install it — emptying\n     FIXTURE_DRAWINGS entirely still passed 233 tests, because no corpus board ever reaches the\n     gate. They carry only `mpn`.\n  3. Named independently by the cross-provider review as a fail-closed contradiction.\n\nTHE COST OF CLOSING IT, MEASURED (2026-09-01, during the Codex fix round).\nThe refusal was implemented experimentally and the full suite run: 33 NEW FAILURES ACROSS 6 SUITES\n— test_assembly_anchor (8), test_assembly_child_rotation (2), test_assembly_orientation (1),\ntest_assembly_paste_matrix (1), test_assembly_preview (20), test_coupon_board (1). The experiment\nwas then REVERTED; the code is unchanged.\n\n*** AND NEITHER ESCAPE HATCH IS LEGITIMATE. *** The refusing placements sit on genuinely\npurchasable footprints — PinSocket_1x07_P2.54mm_Vertical (C7501749 / C41376161),\nPackage_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23 (C20526), Diode_SMD:D_SMA (C2128), D_SOD-123 (C2128). Declaring them\nno_reference is the exact move part_orientation_coverage.md now warns is far worse than an honest\ngap, because a footprint-wide declaration disarms the gate for every part on that land. So the\nonly honest path is to MEASURE them.\n\nTHE UNIT OF WORK, in order:\n  1. Fetch vendor payloads for those ~5 catalogue parts and commit them with the provenance and\n     trim discipline in tests/testdata/vendor_footprints/README.md.\n  2. Extend index.json, regenerate the ledger and the coverage report, read both diffs.\n  3. Give the corpus boards a house identity for the selected house.\n  4. THEN land the refusal, and delete the test that currently pins the bypass\n     (test_assembly_orientation.py: test_a_part_identified_only_by_mpn_is_not_gated).\n  5. Expect the corpus fixture to stop being a no-op at step 3 — orientation_corpus.py's own\n     docstring predicts this (\"the day a corpus board gains a house catalogue number on an in-repo\n     drawing, the refusal lands in eight suites at once\"), which is a good sign the design\n     anticipated it rather than a surprise.\n\nBEYOND THE CORPUS this is a board-schema migration: every real board would need\nassembly.house_parts for the selected house AND a measured pair behind it, or it stops. That is\nthe intended behaviour, but it is a breaking change for existing boards and wants an owner\ndecision on sequencing, not just an implementation.\n\nUNTIL IT LANDS, STATE THE GATE'S REAL COVERAGE HONESTLY: a part bought with a house catalogue\nnumber is corrected or refused; a part carrying only an mpn is neither. Do not describe the\norientation gate as fail-closed without that qualifier.","created_at":"2026-09-01T20:36:06"}
{"_type":"comment","id":218,"item_id":"01a05e56ba6f769a859fa7093a8971e1","author":"agent","text":"OWNER DECISION 2026-09-01: OPTION C — REFUSE AT THE SHIPPING BOUNDARY, NOT AT emit. Approved for\nimplementation now. The full refusal at emit (option E) stays the destination and remains this\nitem's eventual close condition.\n\nTHE CHANGE: move the orientation refusal off assembly_outputs.emit and onto the artifacts that can\nactually reach a board house — build_cpl, build_package and order_package.build. A preview, an\nanchor check or a paste matrix is not ordered, so it does not need to refuse; a position file is,\nand does.\n\nWHY THIS AND NOT THE FULL REFUSAL, graded on the owner's rubric with Determinism as the pivot:\n  Determinism  HIGH - nothing unknown reaches a board house. Previews are not a source of truth,\n               so letting them render an uncorrected rotation costs nothing that ships.\n  Reliability  HIGH - roughly 30 of the 33 failures the full refusal produced were in\n               test_assembly_preview (20), test_assembly_anchor (8), test_assembly_paste_matrix,\n               test_coupon_board and test_assembly_child_rotation. Those are not order paths.\n  Cost         S, against M for the full version plus a board-schema migration.\n  Durability   MEDIUM, and this is the honest price: it leaves a rule with an exception, and\n               exceptions erode. Accepted only because E follows.\n\n*** THE ASSUMPTION THIS RESTS ON IS NOT YET VERIFIED. *** The claim that moving the refusal clears\nthose ~30 failures is inferred from WHICH SUITES failed, not measured. If it turns out those suites\nreach build_cpl too, C collapses into E and the right move is to stop and do E properly rather than\ncarve further exceptions. Measure before believing.\n\nSCOPE DISCIPLINE FOR WHOEVER IMPLEMENTS IT: every artifact that can be handed to a manufacturer\nmust refuse. If in doubt about a path, make it refuse — the failure mode of over-refusing is a\nloud stop, and the failure mode of under-refusing is a rotated part on a physical board that no\nrender can show. Do not weaken any existing refusal to make this land.\n\nCONTEXT THAT MADE THIS DECIDABLE, and which also shrinks this item's urgency: the board actually\nwaiting to be ordered carries a house catalogue number on ALL 43 of its populated components (the\nonly 4 without are DNP test points). So the mpn-only bypass does not touch it, and this item is\nNOT on the critical path to hardware. What was blocking that board was ten unmeasured pairs, which\nis separate work now in progress.","created_at":"2026-09-01T21:03:42"}
{"_type":"comment","id":219,"item_id":"01a05eaf4ed671e49b5762bc78a6f57b","author":"agent","text":"OWNER DECISION 2026-09-01: DEFERRED TO PRE-RELEASE. Do this AFTER task-cycle 13 closes, not inside\nit. The reasoning is that regenerating NOTICE and re-certifying licence attribution is release\nwork, not part of a feature batch.\n\n*** CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY SO NOBODY LATER READS IT AS AN OVERSIGHT: task-cycle 13 will push\n    with THREE RED GATES, one of which is the release-attribution gate. This is a deliberate,\n    owner-approved acceptance, not a miss. ***\n\n  tests/test_notice.py::test_shipped_lock_generates_notice_byte_identical_to_committed_file\n  tests/test_footprints.py::test_lockfile_covers_every_seed_footprint\n  tests/test_footprint_def.py::test_lockfile_wide_adapter_census_is_explicit_and_nonblocking\n\nAll three predate the cycle's dispatch base 8dcb621 and none is caused by its work: the full worker\nsuite ran 3 failed / 3261 passed BEFORE the cycle and 3 failed / 3270 passed after — the same three\nfailures throughout.\n\nWHAT THIS BLOCKS UNTIL DONE: any actual release or marketplace publish of the pcb plugin. NOTICE.md\nis by its own description the release gate, and it currently omits three shipped footprints. Do not\ncut a release off a tree where gen_notice.py --check reports drift.\n\nWHEN PICKED UP, the order is:\n  1. Regenerate NOTICE.md and READ the diff — three new entries, one proprietary licence\n     (Syntiant) and two third-party. This is the step that wants a human eye; it is why the work\n     was deferred rather than folded into a fix round.\n  2. Update the two census expected-sets.\n  3. Then the durable half, which is the actual point of this item: make adding a lock entry fail\n     AT THAT MOMENT rather than at the next person's push. See the description for options, and\n     hint 01a04af027eb, whose rule (a batch touching a declared SET must scope every suite reading\n     that set) would have caught this on the day.\n\nNOTE FOR WHOEVER RUNS THAT: task-cycle 13's scoped test set deliberately INCLUDES test_notice,\ntest_footprints, test_footprint_def and test_corpus_policy, precisely because the batch touched the\nlock. They will be red in that run for the reasons above. That is expected and is not a reason to\nnarrow the scoped set — narrowing it is how this went unseen in the first place.","created_at":"2026-09-01T21:07:23"}
{"_type":"comment","id":225,"item_id":"01a05fd64b9a7ae9ab9348107e92ac04","author":"agent","text":"Implemented in 354f750 (task-cycle 14, T4). Follow-up noted, not done: minerva_pcb_spatial_query's nearby[] entries do not carry `assembly` / `physical_placements` — only the two verbs the DCR named do. Also: the surfaced rotation is the COMPOSED one; the per-house ledger correction is applied at export (documented in tools.md).","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:57:47"}
{"_type":"comment","id":220,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"T1 landed: aea43af — gate `assembly_anchor_off_lands` (bug 01a05fd6cf48). Implementer corrected the oracle: distance is 25.82 mm (lands-extent box), not 26.67 (pad-centre box, which would false-refuse SW1–SW4). Scoped to authored anchors whose origin sits on the owning component's lands. Static gates clean; tests authored, not run. Out of scope, noted: manifest.json / worker_tools.go list gate codes in prose without the new one; assembly_fixture.yaml does not compile (pre-existing).","created_at":"2026-09-02T02:39:46"}
{"_type":"comment","id":221,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"T2a landed: 65ad307 — Connector_PinSocket_2.54mm:PinSocket_1x22_P2.54mm_Vertical_HC-PM254-8.5H from the CS-1165SDIG-1x22 drawing. Pitch/span/drill match both rows of the DevKitC-1 socket set (one axis-free test). Lock, NOTICE and coverage regenerated; part_orientation.json unchanged (no payload yet, correctly). Implementer corrected a stated fact: the datasheet marks no pin 1 — the square pad 1 is EasyEDA's drawing convention, not the vendor's. Out of scope, to file at close: MPN spelling drift 1x22P vs 1x22PZ across lock and board; drawing title block names YongFengYing/CST while LCSC lists Hong Cheng; stale \"18 of 39\" docstring in test_assembly_anchor.py.","created_at":"2026-09-02T02:53:02"}
{"_type":"comment","id":222,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"T2 landed: 216605c (Fable implemented; DCR 01a05fd69e5f). assembly.placements[].footprint; child anchor measured; CPL pair keyed on child footprint; gate assembly_child_lands_mismatch; Go codec carries + refuses blank on both surfaces. Ledger: C41376161 vs the 1x22 strip = 270, 22 pads 0.000 mm RMS, runner-up 22.789 mm. Measured U1S anchors equal the former hand ones. Cold Opus per-task review: approve_with_notes; reviewer re-derived the bottom-side numbers by hand and confirmed the ledger row's runner-up equals √2·pitch·√((n²−1)/12). Applied: stale \"two 270s\" prose; blank-footprint refusal; HAND_ANCHORS fold; fixture MPN PZ; doc note. Rejected: typing/getattr style nits (match module conventions). To file at close: no way to declare a parent pad owned by no child (cover half refuses).","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:23:15"}
{"_type":"comment","id":223,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"T3 landed: f8e6921 (bug 01a05fd602f0). Root cause: the two reporting verbs were the last callers of the render-enum accessor; a library-qualified ref buckets to CUSTOM for drawing and the authored string sits in footprint_id. Fixed by using get_canonical_footprint_name() as to_board_dict/CSV already did. Hover card shares the dict and now shows the ref (parity asserted by its own suite). GD test authored, not run. Out of scope, note: PCBComponent.get_description() still formats the bucket (no callers); stale fixture comment in test_pcb_hover_card.gd.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:29:31"}
{"_type":"comment","id":224,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"T4 landed: 354f750 (DCR 01a05fd64b9a). Both verbs carry `assembly` (authored block) and `physical_placements` (compiler's resolved list via order_package.placement_map, untouched by the panel) over a new IPC channel pcb.assembly_placements, memoized per board revision; absent + `physical_placements_unavailable` when nothing resolves. Rotation surfaced is the composed one; the per-house ledger correction stays at export. Verified on rev B as authored: U1S anchors surface at (83.1, 62.797)/(60.24, 62.797) and emit refuses — the board defect made visible. Batch closed at ee72f5a3..354f750; batched Fable review running. To file at close: panel_tools.gd is 11,781 lines (God file); spatial_query nearby[] lacks the assembly facts.","created_at":"2026-09-02T03:56:41"}
{"_type":"comment","id":226,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"Review rounds closed at HEAD 2abc7ca (T2 a286472, T3 fdad893, T4 5af47bd after folds). Batched Fable review: 1 must_fix (placement memo stale across load) + notes — fixed, re-reviewed, one harness defect in the new memo test fixed in a second round. Codex round 1: 3 must_fix disjoint from Fable's (memo race returns crossed reply; named-child inferred from lands not footprint_ref; Go accepts whitespace-only footprint) — all fixed by Fable. Codex re-check: 1 and 2 resolved; 3 \"resolved wrongly\" — Go now trims and STORES a padded footprint, rewriting authored data. Second Codex must_fix → escalated to owner per task-cycle rule. Beeped. Scoped test set named (see nudge); nothing executed yet.","created_at":"2026-09-02T04:37:39"}
{"_type":"comment","id":227,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"Owner chose codec option A (validate trimmed-blank, store verbatim); folded, Codex re-check 2 = approve (1 of 3 authorised fix cycles used). Scoped execution: Python 1047 passed on final HEAD (one fixture gap fixed: inline uncompilable board lacked design_rules); Go board+tools pass; GD 7 suites green with Minerva closed — one reader-key bug in the new memo test (id vs ref) fixed, both new suites pinned (surface 27, memo 19). Pushed main f5db56f; 0.6.0 bump + annotated tag pcb-v0.6.0 pushed. Awaiting CI.","created_at":"2026-09-02T06:22:38"}
{"_type":"comment","id":228,"item_id":"01a05fec0ac47100b3e4580df2a01841","author":"agent","text":"CLOSED: pcb 0.6.0 released. CI green on every job at the third tag placement; release job published 4 assets; registry's 4 asset URLs return 200. The two red CI attempts were both tests OUTSIDE the scoped set, not batch defects: (1) Go root package — main.go serverVersion lockstep + the registry's exact tool list needed pcb.assembly_placements; (2) tests/test_service_profile.py's literal running-gate set needed the two new codes. Full worker suite 3338 passed locally before the final tag. Hint 01a060ce4e74 records the scoped-Go-set rule. Next: owner installs 0.6.0 from marketplace and reloads; board edit (U1S children footprint, drop hand anchors, rotation 0; U3 value); Phase B order package.","created_at":"2026-09-02T06:57:36"}
{"_type":"comment","id":231,"item_id":"01a062cd06997293886314b493f06269","author":"agent","text":"Done in minerva-plugins a335c74 (local, unpushed): C394395 payload + index entry, ledger row aligned 0 deg 0.042 mm RMS, ORACLE entry in test_part_orientation. Board: C12 house_parts.jlcpcb C6120014 → C394395, mpn → EMK325ABJ107MM-T (ccsandbox, uncommitted).","created_at":"2026-09-02T16:06:12"}
{"_type":"comment","id":230,"item_id":"01a062cd90c176e2920bd1e3cd2c5b2b","author":"agent","text":"Done in minerva-plugins a335c74 (local, unpushed). Finding: the in-repo SW_EVP-ASAC1A drawing already matched the datasheet land pattern (ANCTB38E: 2.0x2.0 pads, 4.0 gap, 8.0 overall = 6.0 mm centres); the blocker was pad NAMES (A/B from the datasheet circuit diagram vs 1/2 in the vendor catalogue drawing). Pads renumbered 1 (west)/2 (east), geometry unchanged, descr written, lock re-hashed, C4365033 payload in the corpus → ledger row aligned, 0 deg, 0.200 mm residual (vendor draws 5.6 mm centres vs datasheet 6.0). Board side: SW1..SW4 pins + BTNx/GND net refs repointed to 1/2 in ccsandbox (uncommitted). Repo-worker order_package on the board: 0 blockers. Needs pcb 0.6.1 release for the installed plugin to agree.","created_at":"2026-09-02T16:06:11"}
{"_type":"comment","id":232,"item_id":"01a0638f9ccb7752a6e5454651ac98d1","author":"agent","text":"OWNER RULING 2026-09-02: implementation bug, not a DCR. The design required DRY and never specified backward compatibility with the v1 experiment, so the \"minpcb dual-write\" mirrored at pcb_component.gd:1592 and :1772 was never allowed. Fix by DELETION, no migration ceremony:\n1. `value` is the one and only place. `properties` may not carry a `value` key — in the file, in the Go Component, or in the panel model.\n2. Go codec: the canonical deserializer REFUSES a component whose `properties` carries `value` (named error with ref + both strings), the same way it refuses unknown assembly keys. No silent consume, no \"which wins\".\n3. Panel: typed `value` field on the component; `properties[\"value\"]` ceases to exist as storage; every reader (CSV import pcb_data.gd:3493/3606, label text pcb_component.gd:1823, serialize :1605) goes through the field. to_board_dict emits `value` once.\n4. Existing boards and fixtures carrying both keys are cleaned in the same commit (rev B, testdata boards) by a one-off script with a reviewed diff; no runtime compat path.\n5. Tests: a fixture with `properties.value` is refused by the Go codec naming the ref; a round trip of rev B emits `value` once and never `properties.value`; the CSV-import test covers the typed field.\nSeparate small follow-up: minerva_pcb_promote reports a FIELD-level delta vs the prior file (per component: value/footprint/x/y/rotation/layer/assembly), not just counts — census_delta was all zeros while U3's value flipped.","created_at":"2026-09-02T19:24:51"}
{"_type":"comment","id":233,"item_id":"01a063a42a8878b1bd347191040cdd55","author":"agent","text":"TASK-CYCLE 15 CLOSED 2026-09-02 — pcb 0.7.0 tagged at b8ad1d7 (main pushed f48a53a..b8ad1d7). Commits: T1 7ca6573, T2 0865652, T3 8d37945, T4 8449591, T5 c2e99d4, T6 9d6d654; review fixes bb839c8, 9e20e8b, 9af793f; scoped-run fixes 873dc57; pins 3b00d07. Reviews: cold Fable batch reviews + re-reviews approved both batches; Codex batch 1 r1–r4 and batch 2 r1–r2 (round-2 escalation ruled by owner; later rounds dispositioned on the rubric, rejections recorded: pads/graphics stay worker-owned Blobs, no nested GD key copy, no pre-load snapshot on .pcbskel restore, panel does not fold legacy inline pin geometry, net properties had no writer). Scoped run: Python 1281 pass, Go all packages ok, GD 21/22 suites green (hover_card section 5 = owner preference, work item 01a0645129). First-execution defects: 6 py + 4 go + 7 GD suites of fixture/expectation drift on the positive schema, plus T2's host-envelope refusal caught by T4. Follow-ups filed: 01a063b981 (to_dict is the undo shape), 01a063fa44 (assembly refusal names the component), 01a0640b99 (delete inline Go Pin fields), 01a0640bb1 (pin readers bypass resolved pads), chore 01a0640bc4 (fixture prune), 01a0641e2d (trace lock no writer / grid undo), 01a06425aa (God files), 01a0642eeb (delta limits), 01a0645129 (hover suite prefs). Owner HITL to do on 0.7.0: install via marketplace BEFORE loading any board; a pre-0.7.0 .pcbskel/.minproj panel state refuses to restore (re-import from YAML); rev B must be cleaned first (smart-remote chore 01a063a44460: assembly.package, properties, restated pins, grid_mm); label backfill chore 01a063a536dd.","created_at":"2026-09-02T22:55:29"}
